window.EF_CRUX_TRICKS_SNIPPET_INDEX=[{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0448","t":"01 Nature of Indian Economy","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["₹ NATURE OF INDIAN ECONOMY — FULL MIND MAP\n                       Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\n📑 Topics in this chapter:\n 1. Mixed Economy — Meaning & Indian Model\n 2. India as a Developing Economy — Classification & Exam Trap\n 3. Labour-Surplus Economy\n 4. Underdeveloped Economy — Core Characteristics\n 5. Indian Economy — Major Structural Features\n 6. Economic vs Non-Economic Activities\n 7. Determinants of Economic Development\n 8. Second Generation of Economic Reforms\n\n\n1. Mixed Economy — Meaning & Indian Model\n\n • Mixed economy ka simple matlab hai: ek hi national economy mein public sector aur private sector dono saath-saath kaam\n  karte hain. India iska major example hai. Iska matlab agriculture aur industry ko equal importance dena, large aur cottage\n  industries ka co-existence, ya primary-secondary sectors ka sirf saath hona nahi hai. [Q1, Q2, Q3]\n • Indian economy ki basic identity Mixed Economy hai. Socialist economy mein ownership aur economic power zyada tar State\n  ke control mein hoti hai; capitalist economy mein means of production par private ownership hoti hai aur market mein\n  government ka role limited rehta hai. Gandhian socialist model decentralisation, self-employment, self-reliance aur cottage\n  industries par zor deta hai. [Q5, Q6, Q7]\n • Indian development model State aur individual dono ke interests ko balance karta hai: public sector ko State administer karta\n  hai, jabki private sector ka administration individuals ke haath mein hota hai. [Q19]\n\n2. India as a Developing Economy — Classification & Exam Trap\n\n • Indian economy ko developing economy maana jaata hai. Developing economy mein development ke saath production\n  structure, production ka nature, banne wali goods aur social relations mein lagataar badlav aata rehta hai. [Q12, Q14]\n • Indian economy mixed economy bhi hai aur ismein kai underdeveloped characteristics bhi milti hain. Economics ke 'Father'\n  Adam Smith hain, J. M. Keynes nahi. [Q4]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — India: Underdeveloped vs Developing\n▸ India developing economy hai, lekin ismein underdeveloped economy ki kai characteristics ek saath mil sakti hain. Dono\n  baaton ko contradiction samajhne ki zarurat nahi hai. [Q4, Q10, Q12, Q14]\n▸ Mixed economy hona aur underdeveloped-type traits ka milna alag concepts hain; India ke context mein dono ek saath\n  dekhe ja sakte hain. [Q4]\n▸ National income ki slow growth pehle zyada prominent thi; current context mein India ko developing economy ke roop\n  mein describe karna zyada appropriate hai. [Q10]\n▸ Yaad rakhein: 'developing economy' current overall classification hai, jabki poverty, unemployment, low per-capita\n  income jaise underdeveloped traits alag se poochhe ja sakte hain. [Q4, Q10, Q12, Q14]\n\n\n\n3. Labour-Surplus Economy\n\n • India ko labour-surplus economy kaha jaata hai, kyunki badi population aur limited employment opportunities ke karan available\n  labour ki matra demand se zyada ho sakti hai. [Q8]","4. Underdeveloped Economy — Core Characteristics\n\n• Underdeveloped economy ki common characteristics hain: low per-capita income, low standard of living, high population\n  growth, high unemployment/underemployment, agriculture ka dominance, low capital formation aur income inequality. Low per-\n  capita income aur low capital formation aksar ek-doosre se jude hote hain. [Q9]\n• Indian economy mein historically underdeveloped-type traits ke roop mein low per-capita income, slow income growth, heavy\n  population pressure, poverty, agriculture par dependence, high unemployment, traditional social structure, technical skills ki\n  kami aur low literacy jaise features dekhe gaye hain. Development planning aur plan expenditure ne in problems ko address\n  karne mein important role nibhaya. [Q10]\n• National income ki slow growth pehle Indian economy ki prominent problem thi, lekin current Indian economy ko define karte\n  waqt ise ek standalone defining characteristic nahi maana jaata. India ko ab developing economy ke roop mein samajhna\n  zyada sahi hai. [Q10]\n\n5. Indian Economy — Major Structural Features\n\n• Developing economy ki typical features mein agriculture ka major occupation hona, disguised unemployment aur human capital\n  ki comparatively low quality shamil ho sakti hai. High per-capita protein intake developing economy ki typical feature nahi hai.\n  [Q11]\n\n• Indian economy ke broad structural features mein agriculture ka bada role, low capital formation, population pressure,\n  unemployment, low per-capita income, industrial aur technological backwardness, mixed economy, imports par dependence\n  aur illiteracy jaise points shamil rahe hain. [Q11]\n• Agriculture ki dominance, low per-capita income aur large-scale unemployment Indian economy ke important structural traits\n  rahe hain. Industry ki dominance developed economy se zyada judi characteristic hai. [Q13]\n• Low labour efficiency, low per-capita income aur low rate of capital formation Indian economy ki important characteristics rahe\n  hain; natural resources ki absolute kami ko Indian economy ki basic characteristic nahi maana jaata. [Q15]\n\n6. Economic vs Non-Economic Activities\n\n• Economic activity woh activity hai jisme limited resources ka use karke human wants satisfy ki jaati hain aur aam taur par\n  income ya economic value generate hoti hai. Consumption, production, exchange, investment, product pricing aur factor pricing\n  iske major parts hain. [Q16]\n• Voluntary social service non-economic activity hai, kyunki iska main purpose income kamana nahi hota. Isi tarah religion ya\n  patriotism se inspired voluntary work non-economic ho sakta hai; farming, transportation aur salaried job economic activities\n  hain. [Q16]\n\n7. Determinants of Economic Development\n\n• Economic development ke major economic determinants hain: natural resources, labour force aur population, capital formation,\n  technology aur innovation, capital-output ratio aur efficient organization. [Q17]\n• Economic development ke non-economic determinants mein social, religious, political, international aur scientific factors aate\n  hain. Social behaviour bhi development ko influence karne wala important non-economic factor hai. [Q17]\n\n8. Second Generation of Economic Reforms\n\n• Second Generation of Economic Reforms mein oil-sector reforms, public-sector reforms aur government/public institutions se\n  jude reforms shamil kiye gaye. Judicial system reforms ko is specific reform-group ka part nahi maana gaya. [Q18]\n\n\n                              Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · Nature of Indian Economy Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0449","t":"02 National Income & Gross Domestic Product","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["₹ NATIONAL INCOME & GROSS DOMESTIC PRODUCT\n                — FULL MIND MAP\n                             Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\n📑 Topics in this chapter:\n 1. National Income, GNP & NNP — Core Identities\n 2. National Income Meaning, Open-Economy Identity & GDP Methods\n 3. NDP, Nominal/Real Income & Personal Disposable Income\n 4. Economic Growth vs Development — Welfare, Indicators & Quality of Life\n 5. Hindu Growth Rate, Inflation, Labour Share & Structural Features\n 6. Business Cycle & Downturn Terms — Boom to Depression\n 7. New Economic Policy 1991 — LPG, Stabilisation & Structural Adjustment\n 8. National Income Estimation in India — History, Committee & Institutions\n 9. Base Year & National-Accounts Method Changes\n 10. Per Capita Income — Price Basis & Historical Figures\n 11. India’s Growth Record — Decades, Shocks & Sectoral Drivers\n 12. State Economies & Per Capita Income — Ranking is Year-Specific\n 13. Capital Formation & Capital-Output Ratio — Growth Mechanics\n 14. Why Per Capita / Rural Income Can Remain Low\n 15. Domestic Saving — Rates & Historical Benchmarks\n 16. Saving Sources & Composition — Household Sector Dominates\n\n\n 1. National Income, GNP & NNP — Core Identities\n\n• National Income ka standard identity NNP at Factor Cost (NNP_FC) diya hai. NNP at Market Price = GNP -\n Depreciation; aur NNP_FC/National Income = NNP_MP - Indirect Tax + Subsidy = NNP_MP - Net Indirect Tax. [Q1, Q2,\n Q3, Q4, Q5, Q11]\n\n• GNP citizens ke dwara country ke andar ya bahar ek saal mein produce ki gayi final goods & services ka total value\n measure karta hai; depreciation ghataane par NNP milta hai. GNP aur NNP national product ke different measures\n hain. [Q9]\n\n ⚠ FORMULA QUICK RECALL\n ▸ NNP = GNP - Depreciation\n ▸ NNP_FC / National Income = NNP_MP - (Indirect Tax - Subsidy)\n ▸ GDP se NNP: NNP = GDP - Depreciation + Net Income from Abroad\n [Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, Q5, Q11]\n\n\n\n\n 2. National Income Meaning, Open-Economy Identity & GDP Methods\n\n• National income ka meaning ek specified period (usually one year) mein country ke citizens dwara produced final\n goods aur services ke total monetary value se hai. India mein latest estimation NSO (formerly CSO) ke through hota\n hai. [Q6]\n• Open economy mein national income identity: Y = C + I + G + (X - M), jahan C consumption, I investment, G\n government expenditure aur X-M net exports hai. [Q7]\n• GDP calculate karne ki teen methods hain: Production Method, Income Method aur Expenditure Method. 'Diminishing\n cost method' GDP calculation method nahi hai. [Q8]\n• India mein national income calculate karne ki ek major practical problem unorganized sector hai, kyunki us sector ki\n income ko accurately capture karne ke liye appropriate data/method ki limitation highlight ki gayi hai. [Q12]\n\n\n 3. NDP, Nominal/Real Income & Personal Disposable Income\n\n• Nominal GDP current prevailing market prices par valued GDP hota hai. Nominal GDP aur Real GDP ka ratio price\n index se related hai. [Q10]","• Net Domestic Product = GDP - Depreciation. Per-capita income ko National Income / Population ke roop mein define\nkarta hai; NDP ko GDP se better comparison measure bataya gaya hai kyunki depreciation already subtract hota hai.\n[Q13]\n\n• Personal Disposable Income (PDI) = Personal Income - Personal Tax Payments - Non-Tax Payments. Household isi\ndisposable income ka ek part consume aur baaki save kar sakta hai. [Q14]\n• Agar money income aur prices dono same proportion mein double ho jaayen, real income unchanged rahega—higher\nmoney income ko higher prices offset kar dete hain. [Q15]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — Formula Wording\n▸ PCI = National Income / Population standard formula hai; NDP / Population wali wording ko PCI definition se mix na karein.\n▸ Formula-derived value aur printed figure mein mismatch ho sakta hai; concept aur printed figure ko ek hi data\npoint na maanein.\n[Q13]\n\n\n\n\n4. Economic Growth vs Development — Welfare, Indicators & Quality of Life\n\n• Important concept mapping: Economic Development → Structural Change; Economic Growth → GDP expansion;\nSustainable Development → Environment + future needs; Quality of Life → Health aur broader wellbeing. [Q23]\n• Economic development ko assess karte waqt GDP, GNP aur per-capita GNP jaise indicators relevant hain; World\nBank se financial assistance ka increase apne aap economic development ka theoretical indicator nahi hai. [Q16]\n• Amartya Sen ka approach people-oriented hai: poorest person ko economic security, world economy ke saath\nintegration ke saath national protection, aur planning mein commodities se zyada individuals par focus. ise\nwelfare/human-development approach se jodta hai. [Q17, Q18]\n• Economic development ke major factors mein capital accumulation, technological improvement, population dynamics,\nspecialised division of labour, entrepreneurship aur natural resources shamil hain; technocrats/bureaucrats ko usually\ndirect core factor ke roop mein classify nahi kiya jaata. [Q34]\n• Economic growth ka most suitable indicator steady increase in real per-capita income/per-capita product hai. Standard\nof living ko bhi per-capita income reflect karta hai. [Q35, Q36, Q37, Q38]\n• Sirf real GNP aur per-capita GNP badhna high development prove nahi karta; agar poverty aur unemployment saath\nbadh rahe hon to benefits broad-based nahi hain. [Q39]\n• Economic development multidimensional approach maangta hai. Government macro-economic aur micro-economic\ndono issues par focus karti hai, sirf micro issues par nahi. [Q57]\n\n\n5. Hindu Growth Rate, Inflation, Labour Share & Structural Features\n\n• 'Hindu Growth Rate' term Prof. Rajkrishna se associated hai; ise Indian economy ki lagbhag 3.5% National\nIncome/GDP growth rate se relate karta hai. [Q19, Q20]\n• Economic growth ke saath income aur aggregate demand badhne par inflationary pressure aa sakta hai; growth aur\ninflation ka relation context-dependent hota hai. [Q21]\n• Labour ka GNP mein share low rehne ka reason: wages, prices ke comparison mein relatively low rehte hain; price\nrise ke karan wage increase ka real benefit limited ho sakta hai. [Q22]\n• Relevant period mein GDP four-times increase nahi hua; GDP mein public-sector share decline aur private-sector\nshare increase hua. [Q24]\n• India ki occupational structure ka relatively stable rehna capital-intensive investment pattern se joda gaya hai—GDP\nke sectoral composition mein change ke bawajood employment structure utni tezi se shift nahi hua. [Q32]\n• Real sector goods/services production se related hai: farmers harvesting aur textile mills fabric banana real-sector\nactivities hain; bank lending aur overseas rupee-bond issuance financial-sector activities hain. [Q33]\n\n\n6. Business Cycle & Downturn Terms — Boom to Depression\n\n• Boom = high business activity with rising income, output and employment; Recession = business activity, income,\noutput aur employment ka gradual decline; Depression = severe underemployment/unemployment plus sharp fall;\nRecovery = prices, income, output aur employment mein continuing rise. [Q25]","• Terms: Meltdown securities/mortgages ke fall se related state; Recession business/industrial-cycle contraction;\nSlowdown diminishing economic activity ko denote karta hai. [Q26]\n• 1997-98 mein Indian economy ka growth 4.3% tak slow hua aur public investment bhi pichhle years ke comparison\nmein ghata. [Q75]\n\n\n7. New Economic Policy 1991 — LPG, Stabilisation & Structural Adjustment\n\n• 1991 ki New Economic Policy liberalisation, privatisation aur globalisation (LPG) par based thi. Iski criticism India ke\nandar aur bahar different ideological expectations ke karan hui— ise differences in ideals se link karta hai. [Q27, Q28]\n• India mein economic liberalisation ka major policy turning point 24 July 1991 ki New Industrial Policy thi; 18 major\nindustries ko chhodkar baaki industries ko licensing se largely free kiya gaya. [Q29]\n• Stabilisation demand-side management hai— inflation control, fiscal adjustment aur balance of payments; Structural\nAdjustment supply-side reforms hai—trade/capital flows, industrial deregulation, disinvestment aur financial-sector\nreforms. Structural adjustment relatively gradual/longer process hai. [Q30]\n• 1991 ke Finance Minister Dr. Manmohan Singh ko Indian economy ke liberalisation ka pioneer kehta hai. [Q31]\n\n\n8. National Income Estimation in India — History, Committee & Institutions\n\n• Dadabhai Naoroji ne 1867-68 ke liye per-capita income lagbhag ₹20 estimate ki thi—ye India mein national-income\nestimation ke earliest attempts mein se ek tha. First scientific national-income estimate V. K. R. V. Rao se associated\nhai. [Q41, Q43]\n• 1949 National Income Committee ke Chairman P. C. Mahalanobis the; Dr. D. R. Gadgil aur V. K. R. V. Rao key\nmembers the. [Q42]\n• Latest national-income estimates NSO release karta hai, jo MoSPI ke under hai. 2019 mein CSO aur NSSO merge\nkarke NSO bana; CSO May 1951 mein formed hua tha. [Q44]\n• Historical context mein CSO/Central Statistical Organisation naam use hota tha; current institutional name NSO hai.\n[Q45, Q46, Q47, Q48]\n\n• Capital formation ke statistical compilation mein RBI aur Central Statistical Organisation/NSO jaise institutions ka role\nraha hai. [Q49]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — NSO vs CSO\n▸ Current institutional wording mein National Statistical Office (NSO), MoSPI ke under hai.\n▸ Older data mein Central Statistical Organisation (CSO) naam milta hai; later institutional restructuring mein NSO\nframework use hota hai.\n[Q44, Q45, Q46, Q47, Q48]\n\n\n\n\n9. Base Year & National-Accounts Method Changes\n\n• National-income accounting mein Base Year woh benchmark year hai jiske prices se Real GDP/constant-price series\ncalculate hoti hai. [Q50]\n• 2004-05 se 2011-12 base-year shift aur factor-cost based GDP framework se market-price/GVA-oriented framework\nka transition ek major statistical revision tha. [Q51, Q52, Q53]\n• Historical national-income series mein base years 1980-81 se 1993-94, phir 1999-2000 aur 2004-05 tak revise hue;\nFebruary 1999 ki new series ne 1980-81 base ko 1993-94 se replace kiya. [Q54, Q55, Q56]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — Base Year is Date-Sensitive\n▸ National-accounts base year time-sensitive hota hai. 2011-12 working base raha; later series schedules mein GDP 2022-\n23, CPI 2024 aur IIP 2022-23 jaise revised bases announce hue.\n▸ Historical figures apne base year/period se linked hote hain; later base revision purane period ke figure ko\nautomatically replace nahi karti.\n[Q50, Q54, Q55, Q56]","10. Per Capita Income — Price Basis & Historical Figures\n\n• Current-price per-capita income growth generally constant-price growth se higher ho sakti hai, kyunki constant-price\nmeasure inflation/price-level effect ko remove karta hai; dono growth rates ka gap largely price changes ko reflect karta\nhai. [Q40]\n• 2009-10 ke historical estimates mein per-capita income ₹46,249 report hui, jabki revised/current-series calculation\nmein ₹48,189 ka figure bhi milta hai; difference series revision ki wajah se hai. [Q58]\n• 2013-14 new-series per-capita NNI: current prices ₹80,388 aur constant prices ₹69,959. [Q59]\n• 1951-52 se 2015-16 ke old-base historical comparison mein 2007-08 high per-capita-income growth year tha; updated\nseries mein 2021-22 ke around 9.3% growth ko recent broader period ka high point bataya gaya. [Q60]\n• 2010-11 per-capita income at 2004-05 prices ₹35,993 thi; updated 2011-12 base series ke liye same year ₹62,170\ndiya gaya hai. [Q61]\n• 1991-92 per-capita annual income ₹7,251 thi. [Q62]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — Current vs Constant / Old Base vs New Base\n▸ Per-capita income figures base year aur series revision ke saath change ho sakte hain; historical aur revised\nfigures alag series ko represent kar sakte hain.\n▸ ₹80,388 current-price figure specific year/series se related hai; ise other price-base figures ke saath mix na karein.\n[Q58, Q59, Q60, Q61, Q62]\n\n\n\n\n11. India’s Growth Record — Decades, Shocks & Sectoral Drivers\n\n• 1990s decade mein ke updated table highest GNI growth 1999-2000 mein 8.8% thi; broader updated series mein\n2021-22 ka 9.9% growth highest highlighted hai. [Q63]\n• COVID contraction ke dauran 2020-21 mein GNI, NNI aur per-capita NNI ki annual growth current aur constant prices\n—dono par negative rahi. [Q64]\n• 2003-04 se 2006-07 ke comparison mein 2006-07 highest-growth year tha; updated data mein 2021-22 GNI growth\naround 9.9% ke saath recent high point raha. [Q65]\n• GDP aur per-capita-income growth rates continuously increase nahi karte—five-year series aur decade observation\ndono fluctuating real growth ko show karte hain. Market-price GDP rupee value ka trend nominal price changes se alag\nbehave kar sakta hai. [Q66, Q67]\n• Economic Survey 2020-21 mein IMF estimate ke basis par FY2022 real growth 11.5% assume ki gayi thi; baad ke IMF\nestimates/forecasts revise hue, isliye 11.5% us publication-period ka forecast tha. [Q68]\n• Selected historical growth figures: 2005-06 annual growth roughly 8-9% range; 1998-99 GDP growth 6.5%; 2002-03\nRBI assessment 5-5.5%; Asian Development Report 2004 ne 2005 growth around 6.9% project ki thi. [Q69, Q70, Q73,\nQ74]\n\n• 2013-14 GDP growth 4.7% vs 4.5% in 2012-13 ka main reason agriculture & allied sector ki higher growth ko maana\ngaya. [Q71]\n• Historical context mein saving ratio ka rise higher GNP growth ke saath linked tha. [Q76]\n• Long run mein Indian economy stagnant nahi rahi; growth rate fluctuate karti rahi aur foodgrain production overall\nincrease hua. [Q86]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — Revised Data Can Change the Comparison\n▸ Original advance-estimate context mein agriculture & allied sector ko 2009-10 mein negative maana gaya tha.\n▸ Later revised comparison mein picture change hui; updated 2025-26 table mein Mining & Quarrying negative growth show\nkarta hai.\n[Q72]\n\n\n\n\n12. State Economies & Per Capita Income — Ranking is Year-Specific\n\n• 2023-24 mein compared State economies mein Maharashtra ka NSDP sabse bada tha. [Q77]","• Historical ranking year-wise change hoti hai: 1998-99 mein Punjab > Maharashtra > Gujarat > Uttar Pradesh; historical\nperiod order Maharashtra > Gujarat > Tamil Nadu > Karnataka tha. [Q78, Q79]\n• Recent period-specific comparisons mein Bihar ki per-capita income States ke beech lowest levels mein rahi. [Q80,\nQ81]\n\n• Older-period per-capita state rankings year ke saath badalti rahi hain; Punjab, Goa (2009-10) aur Haryana alag\nperiod-contexts mein relevant rahe. [Q82, Q83, Q84]\n• 2010-11 comparison mein Bihar ka GSDP growth highest tha; 2023-24 ke updated comparison mein bhi Bihar high-\ngrowth States mein top par dikhaya gaya. [Q85]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — State Rankings Change with Year\n▸ State PCI/GSDP rankings year aur price basis ke saath change hoti hain; older-period ranking ko current general fact na\nsamjhein.\n▸ Bihar ke 2023-24 context mein ₹62,201 aur later updated dataset mein ₹54,637 jaise different figures milte hain; year/data\nseries ko mix na karein.\n[Q78, Q79, Q80, Q81, Q82, Q83, Q84, Q85]\n\n\n\n\n13. Capital Formation & Capital-Output Ratio — Growth Mechanics\n\n• Capital formation net capital accumulation ko describe karta hai. process savings + financial institutions ki\neffectiveness par depend karta hai, aur investment capital formation ka essential factor hai. [Q89]\n• Capital-Output Ratio (COR) batata hai ek unit output produce karne ke liye kitna capital chahiye. High COR = low\nproduction efficiency; isi liye high savings/investment ke bawajood output comparatively kam badh sakta hai. [Q90, Q91]\n• Country X mein economic growth ka necessary driver capital formation hai. Planning Commission ke view mein rising\ncapital formation higher productivity, income aur employment ko support karta hai. [Q94]\n• Near-term high growth ke mein stalled/pending productive projects ko implement karna strongest immediate support\nbataya gaya; GST, skill development aur ease of business ko longer-run supportive factors maana gaya. [Q95]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — Conflicting Data\n▸ Productive stalled/pending projects ko implement karna capital-formation logic se directly linked action hai.\n▸ GST-related figures year/series ke saath change ho sakte hain; conflicting figures ko same period ka data na\nmaanein.\n[Q95]\n\n\n\n\n14. Why Per Capita / Rural Income Can Remain Low\n\n• Present context mein national income ke saath per-capita income mein bhi improvement dikhta hai; saath hi\npopulation ka substantial part poverty line ke below rehna ek separate welfare concern hai. [Q87]\n• Rural income urban income se lower hone ke reasons: large farmer illiteracy/low scientific-agriculture knowledge,\nprimary products ka lower value, aur agriculture mein comparatively low investment—teenon together responsible hain.\n[Q88]\n\n• India mein slow per-capita-income growth ke do main structural reasons high Capital-Output Ratio aur high\npopulation-growth rate diye gaye hain. [Q92]\n• Uttar Pradesh ke low per-capita income ke reasons: rapidly growing population, lack of entrepreneurship aur\ninadequate infrastructure; agriculture modernization ko cause nahi maana gaya. [Q93]\n• Dated context mein slow PCI ke reasons ke roop mein rapid population growth, heavy price rise, slow\nagricultural/industrial development aur foreign-exchange non-availability—ye sab factors apply karte hain. [Q96]\n\n\n15. Domestic Saving — Rates & Historical Benchmarks\n\n• 2000-01 se 2010-11 decade mein Gross Domestic Saving rate ka high point 2007-08 mein 37.8% of GDP tha; 2022-\n23 aur 2023-24 (1st RE) ke updated figures around 30.7% rahe. [Q97]","• 'currently' ke liye current estimate use hota hai: gross domestic saving 30.7%, isliye 30-35% range. Original 1997\ncontext mein rate 20-25% range tha. [Q98]\n• 2012-13 mein Gross Domestic Saving = 30.1% of GDP tha. [Q99]\n• 2006-07 saving ratio Updated table mein 34.9% tha—thus 'above 32%'. 2023-24 (1st R. E.) ke liye 30.7% total saving\nmention karta hai. [Q100]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — 'At Present' vs Relevant Year\n▸ Saving-rate figures period-specific hote hain; historical aur latest figures ko same year ka data na maanein.\n[Q97, Q98, Q99, Q100]\n\n\n\n\n16. Saving Sources & Composition — Household Sector Dominates\n\n• India ki gross saving mein household sector ka contribution sabse bada raha. 2023-24 (1st RE) shares: Household\n18.1%, Private Corporate 10.7%, Public Sector 2.0%. [Q101]\n• Historical period mein private corporate sector ka saving share increase hua tha. Updated table 2019-20 to 2023-24\nke household/private-corporate/public shares bhi deta hai, jisme year-to-year fluctuations hain. [Q102]\n• Household saving ke forms mein physical assets ka share sabse bada raha. 2023-24 ordering: physical assets >\ngross financial saving > less financial liabilities > gold/silver ornaments. [Q103]\n• Household sector ka contribution sabse bada hai; descending pattern Household > Private/Non-financial Corporate >\nPublic/Financial Corporations > Government hai. [Q104]\n\n\n                        Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · National Income & GDP Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0450","t":"03 Sustainable Economic Development","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["♻ SUSTAINABLE ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT — FULL\n                                                    MIND MAP\n                          Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\n📑 Topics in this chapter:\n 1. SDGs in India — NITI Aayog, Global Reports & India’s Rank\n 2. SDG India Index — State Categories, Rankings & Urban Index\n 3. 17 Sustainable Development Goals — High-Yield Mapping\n 4. Sustainable Development — Meaning, Brundtland & Agenda 21\n 5. Limits to Growth, Environmental Kuznets Curve & Carrying Capacity\n 6. Circular Economy, Sustainable Models, Tourism & Blue Economy\n 7. Social Capital — Trust, Cooperation & Shared Identity\n 8. Human Capital Formation — Sources, Skills & Productivity\n 9. Inclusive Growth & Inclusive Governance\n 10. Economic Survey — Sustainable Development & Climate Change\n\n\n 1. SDGs in India — NITI Aayog, Global Reports & India’s Rank\n\n• India mein Sustainable Development Goals (Agenda 2030) ke coordination aur implementation ki nodal institution NITI\n Aayog hai. [Q1]\n• SDG rankings year-specific hain: 2016 mein India 110th, 2017 mein 116th, Sustainable Development Report 2023\n mein 112th, 2024 mein 109th aur 2025 mein 99th/167 (score ~67) raha; 2024 aur 2025 dono reports mein Finland top\n par hai. [Q2, Q3, Q4]\n• Annual SDG progress information global indicator framework aur national statistical systems se compile hoti hai aur\n UN Secretary-General annual Sustainable Development Goals report present karte hain. Global Sustainable\n Development Report (GSDR) quarterly nahi, generally 4-year interval par aati hai. [Q19]\n• SDGs ki global success ke liye India ki success important hai, kyunki duniya ki lagbhag one-sixth population India\n mein rehti hai; isliye India ka progress global population-weighted indicators ko strongly affect karta hai. [Q24]\n\n ⚠ EXAM TRAP — SDG Rank is Year-Specific\n ▸ SDG rank year-specific hai: 2016 → 110th; 2017 → 116th; 2023 → 112th.\n ▸ Later updates mein 2024 → 109th aur 2025 → 99th/167 report hua; older rank ko current rank se replace na karein.\n [Q2, Q3, Q4]\n\n\n\n\n 2. SDG India Index — State Categories, Rankings & Urban Index\n\n• NITI Aayog SDG composite-score categories: Aspirant 0-49, Performer 50-64, Front Runner 65-99 aur Achiever 100.\n [Q11]\n\n• SDG India Index 2019-20 mein Uttar Pradesh 'Performer' category mein tha aur Kerala combined state ranking mein\n first tha. SDG India Index 2023-24 mein Uttar Pradesh 'Front Runner' (score 67) hai aur 2018 se 2023-24 ke beech +25\n score improvement ke saath fastest-progressing states mein raha. [Q5]\n• SDG India Index 2019/2019-20 ke state ranking context mein Kerala first tha. 2020-21 Index ('Partnerships in the\n Decade of Action') mein Kerala first, Himachal Pradesh & Tamil Nadu second, Andhra\n Pradesh/Goa/Karnataka/Uttarakhand third, Sikkim fourth aur Maharashtra fifth; Gujarat top-five mein nahi tha. [Q6, Q7,\n Q9]\n\n• SDG India Index 2023-24 mein Kerala aur Uttarakhand joint-highest score 79 par the; Tamil Nadu 78, Goa aur\n Himachal Pradesh 77. Bihar 57 ke saath lowest aur Jharkhand 62 par tha. [Q8]\n• 2019 composite-index context mein Kerala, Tamil Nadu aur Himachal Pradesh Front Runners the; Gujarat Front\n Runner nahi tha. [Q10]\n• SDG Urban Index & Dashboard 2021-22 mein Shimla 75.50 ke saath top tha; Coimbatore 73.29, Chandigarh aur\n Thiruvananthapuram 72.36, Kochi 72.29. Indore top-three mein nahi tha. [Q12]","⚠ EXAM TRAP — State Ranking Changes by Edition\n▸ 2019-20, 2020-21 aur 2023-24 SDG India Index editions ki State rankings/categories alag hain; exact edition dekhein.\n▸ Uttar Pradesh 2019-20 mein Performer tha; 2023-24 update mein score 67 ke saath Front Runner category mein aaya.\n[Q5, Q6, Q7, Q8, Q9, Q10, Q11, Q12]\n\n\n\n\n3. 17 Sustainable Development Goals — High-Yield Mapping\n\n• SDG 6 = Clean Water and Sanitation — 2030 tak water aur sanitation ki availability aur sustainable management for\nall ensure karna. [Q13]\n• SDG mapping: Goal 1 No Poverty; 2 Zero Hunger; 3 Good Health & Well-Being; 4 Quality Education; 5 Gender\nEquality; 6 Clean Water & Sanitation; 7 Affordable & Clean Energy; 8 Decent Work & Economic Growth; 9 Industry,\nInnovation & Infrastructure. [Q14, Q15]\n• SDG 10 Reduced Inequalities; 11 Sustainable Cities & Communities; 12 Responsible Consumption & Production; 13\nClimate Action; 14 Life Below Water; 15 Life on Land; 16 Peace, Justice & Strong Institutions; 17 Partnerships for the\nGoals. [Q14, Q16, Q17]\n• SDG 15 'Life on Land' terrestrial ecosystems, forests, desertification aur biodiversity loss se related hai. Contrast: SDG\n14—Life Below Water; SDG 13—Climate Action; SDG 16—Peace, Justice & Strong Institutions. [Q17]\n• SDG 4 Quality Education ke liye free & compulsory education, teachers ki sankhya badhana, basic-school\ninfrastructure improve karna, education financing ko investment priority banana aur digital transformation adopt karna\ninclude karta hai; agricultural-program expansion is Goal 4 ka part nahi hai. [Q18]\n\n\n4. Sustainable Development — Meaning, Brundtland & Agenda 21\n\n• Sustainable development ka accepted core: present needs ko meet karna bina future generations ki apni needs meet\nkarne ki ability compromise kiye; environmental protection ke saath development. Isliye inter-generational welfare iska\ncentral idea hai. [Q23, Q25, Q28, Q30, Q31, Q33, Q34, Q35]\n• 1987 World Commission on Environment and Development (WCED) ki report 'Our Common Future' / Brundtland\nReport ne sustainable development concept ko genuinely develop/popularize kiya. [Q25, Q26, Q28, Q33]\n• Agenda 21 ko 1992 Rio de Janeiro Earth Summit / UN Conference on Environment and Development mein world\nleaders ne approve kiya tha; 1995 is event ka year nahi hai. [Q23]\n• 'Sustainable development' term ka earliest international-document use 1980 ki 'World Conservation Strategy' mein\nmilta hai, jo IUCN ne World Wide Fund ke support se publish ki thi. [Q27]\n• Environmental-development literature ke important works: Rachel Carson ki 'Silent Spring' (1962) aur Paul R. Ehrlich\nki 'The Population Bomb' (1968); 'The Population Bomb' ke author Paul R. Ehrlich hain. [Q26, Q28, Q29, Q33]\n• Sustainable development ka basic idea natural resources ko present needs ke liye use karte hue future generations ki\nneeds aur resource availability ko compromise na karna hai; depletion minimum rakhna aur long-term balance maintain\nkarna core principle hai. [Q28, Q30, Q31, Q33, Q34, Q35]\n\n⚠ DATE CHAIN — Concepts Ko Mix Mat Karna\n▸ 1962 Silent Spring → 1968 The Population Bomb → 1980 World Conservation Strategy mein term → 1987 Brundtland\nReport.\n▸ Agenda 21 = Rio Earth Summit 1992; 'The Limits to Growth' = Club of Rome, Brundtland Report nahi.\n[Q23, Q26, Q27, Q28, Q29]\n\n\n\n\n5. Limits to Growth, Environmental Kuznets Curve & Carrying Capacity\n\n• 'The Limits to Growth' Club of Rome se associated hai. Club of Rome 1968 ke Rome meeting se evolve hua. [Q20]\n• Meadows et al. ki 1972 'Limits to Growth' analysis ne warning di ki population, industrialisation, pollution, food\nproduction aur resource depletion ke existing trends continue rahe to long-term growth limits face ho sakti hain; later\nupdates ne overshoot risks ko further highlight kiya. [Q21]\n• Environmental Kuznets Curve (EKC) environmental degradation aur per-capita GDP ka relationship dikhata hai: initial\ngrowth ke saath degradation badhta hai, ek level ke baad trend reverse hota hai—shape inverted U hai. [Q22]","• Sustainable development carrying capacity se closely linked hai—ecosystem/resource ki maximum sustainable\nsupport capacity ko exceed kiye bina present needs meet karna. [Q32]\n\n\n6. Circular Economy, Sustainable Models, Tourism & Blue Economy\n\n• Circular economy raw-material use aur production/consumption waste ko reduce karke greenhouse-gas emissions ko\nkam karti hai. Raw-material use aur waste reduction greenhouse-gas emissions ko kam karne ka direct mechanism hai.\n[Q36]\n\n• Neemrana, sustainable economic development ke model ke roop mein Rajasthan ke Alwar district mein located hai.\n[Q37]\n\n• Sustainable tourism ka objective tourism development ko cultural integrity, ecological processes aur environmental\nprotection ke saath balance karna hai. UNWTO goals mein economic viability, local prosperity, employment quality,\nsocial equity, community wellbeing, cultural richness, biological diversity, resource efficiency aur environmental purity\nshamil hain. [Q38]\n• Blue Economy ke sustainable-development partnership ke liye India-Norway Taskforce Norway ke saath hai. Prime\nMinisters ne January 2019 mein jointly launch kiya; February 18, 2020 ko ministers ne initiative ko formally\nlaunch/advance kiya. [Q39]\n\n\n7. Social Capital — Trust, Cooperation & Shared Identity\n\n• Social capital ka meaning society ke logon ke beech mutual trust, shared values, cooperation, common\nunderstanding, shared identity ki feeling aur interpersonal relationships se hai. [Q40]\n\n\n8. Human Capital Formation — Sources, Skills & Productivity\n\n• Human capital formation ka core process knowledge, skill levels aur capacities ko increase karna aur intangible assets\n(knowledge, ethics, intellectual property etc.) accumulate karna hai. Tangible wealth/capital accumulation iska result ho\nsakta hai, process ka direct definition nahi. [Q41]\n• Education mein investment human resources ko human capital mein convert karta hai. Human-capital formation ke\nmajor sources education, on-the-job training, health, migration aur information hain; irrigation ka direct role human-\ncapital formation mein nahi hota. [Q42, Q44]\n• Skilled, healthy aur educated citizens ko human capital kehta hai. PM Kaushal Vikas Yojana ko Union Cabinet ne 20\nMarch 2015 approve kiya; scheme youth skill-training, refined curriculum, better training aur trained instructors par\nfocus karti hai. [Q43]\n• Human capital mein education, health aur skill development par expenditure productive capacity, lifetime income aur\nemployment opportunities badhata hai; productivity gain isi investment ka direct outcome hai. [Q45]\n• Human-capital investment ka simple chain: skill development → resources ka better use → productivity increase. [Q46]\n• World Bank Human Capital Index 2020 mein Singapore score 0.88 ke saath first; Central African Republic 0.29 ke\nsaath bottom; India 0.49 score ke saath 116th. [Q47]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — Process vs Result\n▸ Human capital formation ka process knowledge, skills, capacity aur intangible human wealth build karna hai.\n▸ Tangible capital accumulation is process ka possible result ho sakta hai; result aur process ko ek hi concept na samjhein.\n[Q41, Q42]\n\n\n\n\n9. Inclusive Growth & Inclusive Governance\n\n• Inclusive growth ka core idea growth ke benefits, resources aur facilities ko society ke sabhi sections tak pahunchana\nhai; weaker/lower-income sections par special focus ke through economic aur social inequalities reduce ki jaati hain.\n[Q48, Q49, Q51]\n\n• Inclusive growth sirf high national-income growth rate nahi hai. Better education, health, employment opportunities,\nbasic facilities aur poverty reduction iske major dimensions hain. [Q49]","• Inclusive governance mein effective district planning/committees, public-health expenditure increase aur mid-day meal\nscheme ko strengthen karna include hai; NBFCs ko banking permission dena inclusive-governance component nahi\nhai. [Q50]\n• Government ke inclusive-growth target ko SHGs, MSMEs aur Right to Education Act—teenon support kar sakte hain:\nSHG/MSME livelihoods ko strengthen karte hain aur RTE deprived children ko education aur mainstream access deta\nhai. [Q51]\n\n\n10. Economic Survey — Sustainable Development & Climate Change\n\n• Economic Survey 2011-12 mein pehli baar 'Sustainable Development and Climate Change' ka separate/new chapter\nadd kiya gaya tha. [Q52]\n\n\n                 Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · Sustainable Economic Development Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0451","t":"04 Agriculture and Allied Sectors","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["AGRICULTURE AND ALLIED SECTORS — FULL MIND MAP\n                                  Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\nTopics in this chapter:\n 1. Farmers, Agricultural Policy, Marketing & Farmer Support\n 2. Land Reforms, Tenancy, Holdings & Agrarian Systems\n 3. Agriculture in GDP, Census & Agricultural Households\n 4. Major Crops, Production, Sown Area, Growth & Food Security\n 5. Horticulture, Fruits, Vegetables, Spices & Plantation Crops\n 6. Agricultural Trade, Exports, Imports, Edible Oils & Silk\n 7. Agricultural Productivity, Employment & Cash Crops\n 8. RKVY, Agro-Climatic Regions, Horticulture Mission & Labour Support\n 9. NFSA, Food Safety, NFSM, Entitlement & PDS\n 10. Dairy, Livestock, Fisheries & Allied-Sector Development\n 11. Agricultural Revolutions & Green Revolution\n 12. Sustainable Agriculture, Soil Health, Millets, PM-AASHA & Extension\n 13. Crop Insurance & Weather Insurance\n 14. Watershed, Irrigation, KUSUM, Land Records & Groundwater Schemes\n 15. Agricultural Institutions, Cooperatives, Markets & Research Centres\n 16. Sugarcane FRP, MSP, CACP, Procurement & Price Policy\n 17. Crop Seasons, Farm Costs & Agricultural Finance\n 18. Kisan Credit Card, Kisan Rin Portal & Rural Credit Milestones\n 19. Price Stabilisation Fund, Minor Minerals & Agricultural Tax Rules\n\n\n 1. Farmers, Agricultural Policy, Marketing & Farmer Support\n\n• Har saal 23 December ko former Prime Minister Chaudhary Charan Singh ki birth anniversary par Farmers' Day / Kisan Diwas\n celebrate kiya jaata hai. [Q1]\n• Farmers' Produce Trade and Commerce (Promotion and Facilitation) Bill, 2020 September 2020 mein pass hua tha aur December\n 2021 mein repeal kar diya gaya. Iska aim farmers aur traders ko APMC mandi ke bahar bhi farm produce ki sale-purchase ke liye\n choice aur freedom dena tha. [Q2]\n• 1 February 2020 ke Union Budget mein teen major components the; pehla component 'Aspirational India' tha. Iske under\n agriculture, irrigation aur rural development ko first priority item rakha gaya. [Q3]\n• Genetic industries agriculture se related hain; sale/profit ke liye selected plant aur animal species ki breeding aur growth ki jati hai.\n [Q4]\n• Food management mein foodgrain procurement, distribution, buffer-stock maintenance aur price stability ke liye storage shamil\n hain; foodgrain export iska component nahi hai. [Q5]\n• Agricultural marketing mein produce ki collection, storage, processing, transport, packaging, grading aur distribution jaise steps\n shamil hote hain. [Q6]\n• September 2024 mein FCI ne apne supply-chain management system ko modernize karne ke liye comprehensive digital-\n transformation initiative launch kiya. Yeh Department of Food and Public Distribution ke 100-day achievements ka part tha. [Q7]\n• Minimum Support Price (MSP) farmer ko price-crash se protection deta hai; market price MSP se neeche jaane par government\n procurement support ke through produce ko minimum assured price par purchase kar sakti hai. [Q8]\n• Green Revolution aur post-Green Revolution period mein agriculture ke progress mein public aur private capital formation—dono ka\n important contribution raha. [Q9]\n• PM-KISAN ek 100% Central Sector Scheme hai, jise 24 February 2019 ko farmers ko income support dene ke objective se launch\n kiya gaya. [Q10]\n• Pradhan Mantri Kisan Samman Nidhi (PM-KISAN) 24 February 2019 ko launch hui, lekin scheme ko December 2018 se effective\n maana gaya. [Q11]\n• National Agriculture Market ka aim farmers ko better price aur agricultural commodities mein transparent trading dena hai. Iska\n electronic portal e-NAM 14 April 2016 ko launch hua. [Q12]\n• Chaudhary Charan Singh Nehru ke Soviet-style economic-development model ke critic the. Unka maanna tha ki India mein\n cooperative farming successful nahi ho sakti. [Q13]\n• 'Small Farmer Large Field' ek coordinated model hai jisme area ke kai marginal farmers group bana kar selected agricultural\n operations ko synchronize aur jointly manage karte hain, taaki small farmers ki livelihood improve ho. [Q14]\n• Jin farmers ke paas kuch productive resources hote hain lekin woh wealthy nahi hote, unhe 'Bullock Capitalists' kaha gaya. Lloyd\n aur Susanne Rudolph (1987) ke context mein ye self-employed, self-financed producers hote hain jinke paas adequate holding aur\n bullocks ka pair hota hai. [Q15]\n\n EXAM TRAP — Data Ambiguity — Do Not Guess\n ▸ Data ambiguity ki situation mein unsupported conclusion infer nahi karna chahiye.\n [Q2]","2. Land Reforms, Tenancy, Holdings & Agrarian Systems\n\n• Independence ke samay land ownership kuch hands mein concentrated thi; multi-cropping scheme land-reform measure nahi thi.\n[Q16]\n• Land-reform framework mein ye sabhi listed provisions/components shamil the. [Q17]\n• Land reforms ka broad objective social justice ke liye landholding ownership ko redistribute karna aur optimal land use ke liye\noperational holdings ko reorganize karna hai; tenancy reforms bhi isi direction mein kaam karte hain. [Q18]\n• Land Ceiling legally fixed maximum landholding limit hai; is limit se zyada land koi farmer legally hold nahi kar sakta. [Q19]\n• Agriculture Census 2015-16 mein marginal holding <1 ha, small 1-2 ha, semi-medium 2-4 ha, medium 4-10 ha aur large holding\n≥10 ha classified thi. [Q20]\n• 1 to 2 hectares landholding wala farmer Small farmer category mein aata hai. [Q21]\n• Agriculture Census 2015-16 mein Bihar ka average agricultural holding 0.39 hectare tha, jo States ke comparison mein second-\nlowest tha; Kerala 0.18 hectare ke saath lowest tha. [Q22]\n• Rural poverty farmers ki advanced agricultural technology mein investment capacity ko limit karti hai. India ki agriculture-dependent\npopulation ka major hissa rural areas mein rehta hai. [Q23]\n• Individual cultivator ko allotted land Ryotwari system, large feudal lords ko allotment Jagirdari system, revenue farmers/tehsildars\nbased arrangement Zamindari system aur village-level land-revenue settlement Mahalwari system kehlata hai. [Q24]\n• Technological progress broadly labour-augmenting aur capital-augmenting ho sakta hai. Agriculture mein wooden plough ki jagah\nsteel plough ka use capital-augmenting technological progress ka example hai. [Q25]\n\n\n3. Agriculture in GDP, Census & Agricultural Households\n\n• 2008-09 mein agriculture aur allied sectors ka GDP share lagbhag 15% tha; 2025-26 first advance estimate mein current-price GVA\nshare 16.78% estimate hua. [Q26]\n• Agriculture ka national-income/GDP share year aur price-base ke saath change hota hai; 2023-24 mein current prices par\nagriculture-allied GDP share lagbhag 16% tha. [Q27]\n• Agriculture and allied sectors ka GDP share 2023-24 (PE) mein current prices par 16% tha; 2025-26 first advance estimate mein\ncurrent-price share 16.78% estimate hua. [Q28]\n• Agriculture-allied sector ka GDP share 1990s ke mukable kaafi ghata; 2024-25 mein current prices par ye lagbhag 16% tha. [Q29]\n• 2010-11 mein agriculture aur allied sectors ka GDP contribution 14.5% (lagbhag 15%) tha. Economic Survey 2024-25 mein 2024-\n25 ke current-price GDP mein iska share lagbhag 16% diya gaya. [Q30]\n• India jaise developing country mein agriculture aur rural economy livelihood, food security aur industry-service sectors ko support\nkarte hain. Development ke saath agriculture ka GVA/GDP share generally decline hota hai: 1950 mein share lagbhag 52% aur\nemployment lagbhag 70% tha, jabki 2019 tak GDP share around 16% aur employment share around 42% reh gaya. [Q31]\n• Planning ke start 1950-51 mein agriculture ka GDP share lagbhag 52% tha, jo time ke saath decline hua. 2023-24 mein current-\nprice GDP share around 16% tha; 2025-26 first advance estimate mein agriculture-allied GVA share constant prices par 13.84% aur\ncurrent prices par 16.78% estimate hua. [Q32]\n• Agriculture Census 2015-16 mein small aur marginal holdings total holdings ka 86.2% the aur operated area ka 47.3% manage\nkarte the. Number of holdings mein Maharashtra third tha, jabki operated area Rajasthan mein sabse zyada tha. [Q33]\n• MoSPI ke National Sample Survey Organisation ne 70th Round (January-December 2013) ki Situation Assessment Survey of\nAgricultural Households ke key indicators December 2014 mein release kiye. [Q34]\n• 2014 NSSO estimate mein agricultural year July 2012-June 2013 ke dauran rural India mein around 90.2 million agricultural\nhouseholds the; rural households mein agricultural-household share around 57.8% tha. [Q35]\n\nEXAM TRAP — GDP Share is Period-Specific\n▸ Agriculture/GDP contribution ke figures different years/periods par based hain; inhe ek single 'current' value samajhkar merge na\nkarein.\n[Q26, Q27, Q28, Q29, Q30, Q31, Q32]\n\n\n\n\n4. Major Crops, Production, Sown Area, Growth & Food Security\n\n• India mein soybean cultivation ka sabse bada area Madhya Pradesh mein hai. [Q36]\n• Earlier productivity comparison mein Australia relevant tha; January 2026 USDA ke 2024-25 figures mein China top country hai.\n[Q37]\n• 2024-25 data mein cotton yield Kerala mein highest, lagbhag 1,495 kg/ha thi. [Q38]\n• Cotton ke liye black soil sabse suitable maani jaati hai; India mein yeh especially Gujarat, Maharashtra, Punjab aur Andhra Pradesh\nke cotton-growing areas se associated hai. [Q39]\n• Economic Review 2025-26 ke hisaab se Uttar Pradesh ka 2023-24 foodgrain production 60.29 million tonnes tha, jo country ke total\nproduction ka 18.14% tha; 2024-25 mein UP ka production 63.85 million tonnes (17.85%) report hua. [Q40]","• 2020-21 ke Fourth Advance Estimate mein India ka total wheat production 109.5 million tonnes tha. [Q41]\n• 2024-25 wheat production ka descending order Uttar Pradesh > Madhya Pradesh > Punjab > Haryana > Rajasthan raha. [Q42]\n• 2021-22 production leaders: Nutri-cereals—Karnataka; total pulses—Madhya Pradesh; groundnut—Gujarat; soybean—\nMaharashtra. [Q43]\n• 2021-22 pulses production mein Madhya Pradesh leading tha; area ke hisab se Rajasthan leading State tha. [Q44]\n• 2021-22 mein pulses production mein Madhya Pradesh first tha. [Q45]\n• 2021-22 mein West Bengal India ka largest rice-producing State tha. Economic Review 2025-26 ke 2024-25 data mein top three:\nUttar Pradesh (20.76 MT) > Telangana (17.45 MT) > West Bengal (16.02 MT). [Q46]\n• 2024-25 rice production ka descending order: Uttar Pradesh > Telangana > West Bengal > Punjab > Chhattisgarh. [Q47]\n• Wheat irrigation mein Crown Root Initiation (CRI) stage sabse critical hoti hai. Agar farmer ke paas sirf ek irrigation available ho, to\nirrigation CRI stage par deni chahiye. [Q48]\n• Agricultural Statistics 2015 ke 2012-13 provisional data mein Net Area Sown 13.99 crore hectare tha. Net Area Sown mein crops\naur orchards ke under actual sown area count hota hai aur ek hi land ko saal mein multiple sowing ke bawajood ek baar count kiya\njaata hai. 2023-24 mein net sown area 138.99 million hectare tha. [Q49]\n• India ki cropping intensity 2023-24 (P) mein 156.8% thi; older period ke data mein lagbhag 135% figure bhi milta hai. [Q50]\n• Earlier period mein 2004-05 relevant high-production year tha; Economic Review 2025-26 mein 2015-16 se 2024-25 tak foodgrain\nproduction ka overall rising trend dikhai deta hai. [Q51]\n• 2014-15 mein agriculture aur allied sectors ki growth constant 2011-12 prices par -0.2% record hui thi. [Q52]\n• Agriculture and allied sectors ki annual average growth rate 2002-03 mein negative hui; 2014-15 mein bhi sectoral growth negative\nrecorded hui. [Q53]\n• 1997-98 mein Indian agriculture ka production growth lagbhag -1% record hua tha. [Q54]\n• Third Five Year Plan period mein agriculture ne negative growth dikhayi; 1961-65 ke around agricultural-production growth rate\nlagbhag -1.0% record hui. [Q55]\n• Green Revolution ke baad India mein foodgrain production sharply increase hua: 1950-51 mein lagbhag 50.8 million tonnes se\nbadhkar 2001-02 mein 211.9 million tonnes ho gaya. [Q56]\n• Foodgrain stock adequate hona alone food security guarantee nahi karta, kyunki har household ki purchasing capacity same nahi\nhoti. 1960s ki Green Revolution ne foodgrain production mein unprecedented increase kiya. [Q57]\n• Population growth ke comparison mein agriculture mein mechanisation, technology adoption aur institutional reforms ki pace\ninsufficient rehne par productivity aur structural transformation slow ho sakta hai. [Q58]\n• Foodgrain subsidy 1992-93 mein ₹2,800 crore thi aur 1998-99 tak ₹8,700 crore ho gayi, yani lagbhag 3.1 times increase. Budget\nEstimates 2026-27 mein total subsidies ₹4,54,773.28 crore propose ki gayi, jisme food, fertilizer, petroleum, interest aur other\nsubsidies shamil hain. [Q59]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Production & Growth Data are Year-Specific\n▸ Crop-leading States, production estimates aur growth rates year, advance estimate aur Five-Year Plan period ke saath\nchange ho sakte hain.\n[Q36, Q37, Q38, Q39, Q40, Q41, Q42, Q43, Q44, Q45, Q46, Q47, Q48, Q49, Q50, Q51, Q52, Q53, Q54, Q55, Q56, Q57, Q58]\n\n\n\n\n5. Horticulture, Fruits, Vegetables, Spices & Plantation Crops\n\n• India milk, pulses, jute aur spices ka major/leading producer hai; 2024 rice-production data mein India ne China ko overtake karke\ntop position li. [Q60]\n• Fresh vegetables aur fruits production mein China world leader hai; India second position par hai. [Q61]\n• 2024-25 ke horticulture estimates mein banana, guava aur mango major fruit crops hain; 3rd advance estimate mein banana 39.51\nmillion tonnes, guava 5.61 million tonnes aur mango 23.27 million tonnes ke around tha. [Q62]\n• APEDA 2015-16 data mein India ne 12,01,245.29 metric tonnes onion export kiya, jiska total export value ₹2,747.41 crore tha. [Q63]\n• 2022-23 mein India turmeric ka world ka largest producer aur exporter tha. [Q64]\n• June 2023 India-US state visit ke dauran 6 pending WTO disputes resolve karne par agreement hua. India ne apples samet 8 US\nproducts par additional duties remove ki, aur Indian steel-aluminium ko US market access restore hua. [Q65]\n• Kashi Lalima bhindi/okra ki variety hai aur ise summer ke saath Kharif season mein bhi grow kiya ja sakta hai. [Q66]\n• Major fruit-producing State pairs: Mango—Uttar Pradesh; Litchi—Bihar; Coconut—Kerala; Apple—Jammu & Kashmir. [Q67]\n• World mein coffee production ka leading country Brazil hai; Mongolia coffee-production leader nahi hai. [Q68]\n• Coffee production ka 2016 descending pattern Brazil > Vietnam > Colombia > Indonesia tha. FAO 2024 data mein top five Brazil,\nVietnam, Colombia, Indonesia aur Ethiopia hain. [Q69]\n• Coffee–Uzbekistan pairing geographically suitable nahi hai: commercial coffee mainly “Coffee Belt” (23.5°N–23.5°S) mein grow hoti\nhai, jabki Uzbekistan roughly 37°–46°N par hai aur climate suitable nahi hai. [Q70]\n• Coffee Board ke 2025-26 post-monsoon estimate mein Karnataka coffee production mein first position par tha. [Q71]","6. Agricultural Trade, Exports, Imports, Edible Oils & Silk\n\n• Indian exports ko broadly major categories mein divide kiya ja sakta hai; agriculture aur allied exports mein coffee, tea, oilcakes,\nspices, dry fruits, fruits aur vegetables jaise products shamil hain. [Q72]\n• Agricultural Statistics at a Glance 2024 ke last-five-year import data mein value ke basis par vegetable oils sabse bada imported\nagricultural commodity group raha; 2023-24 mein vegetable-oil imports around ₹1,23,079 crore the. [Q73]\n• 2015-16 ke pulses-import comparison mein lentil/masoor ka import sabse zyada tha. India pulses ka world ka major producer aur\nconsumer bhi hai. [Q74]\n• Agricultural exports mein India ko maximum foreign exchange Basmati rice export se milta hai. [Q75]\n• Recent export-value comparison mein rice ka export value oilcake, spices aur raw cotton se zyada raha; 2025-26 April-September\ndata mein rice ₹5,635 crore ke around tha. [Q76]\n• 2023-24 se 2024-25 comparison mein export order Cereals > Marine Products > Spices > Tea raha. [Q77]\n• Earlier figure lagbhag 3% tha; Economic Review 2025-26 mein meat aur meat-products export share 2021 mein 1.9% aur 2022\nmein 1.8% recorded hai. [Q78]\n• Export ke liye preferred mango variety Alphonso hai aur India mein iska major producing State Maharashtra hai. [Q79]\n• India se export hone wali famous mango varieties mein Alphonso prominent hai. [Q80]\n• Compared agricultural imports mein edible oil ka import value sabse zyada raha. [Q81]\n• Recent years mein edible-oil imports domestic production se kaafi zyada rahe; 2023-24 mein domestic production 121.75 lakh\ntonnes ke against import 156.55 lakh tonnes tha. [Q82]\n• Union Budget 2024-25 ke Aatmanirbharta focus mein pulses aur oilseeds ki production, storage aur marketing strengthen karne par\nzor tha; coconut ko is specific target list mein include nahi kiya gaya. [Q83]\n• 2024-25 mein Karnataka India ka leading mulberry-silk producer tha. [Q84]\n• India commercial silk ki sabhi 5 known varieties produce karta hai: Mulberry, Tropical Tasar, Oak Tasar, Eri aur Muga. [Q85]\n• China world ka largest silk producer hai. [Q86]\n\n\n7. Agricultural Productivity, Employment & Cash Crops\n\n• Agricultural productivity badhane ke major measures mein improved seeds, fertilizers, irrigation, pesticides aur plant-protection\ntechniques ka better use shamil hai; 1960s-70s mein in inputs ka use rapidly expand hua. [Q87]\n• India mein cooperative farming ke initial efforts 1920s se dikhte hain; Independence ke baad early planning years mein cooperative\nfarming ko promote karne ke liye special efforts hue. [Q88]\n• Indian economy mein agriculture ka role ab bhi major hai. Economic Review 2025-26 ke context mein agriculture-allied activities\nnational income ka roughly one-fifth contribute karti hain aur PLFS 2023-24 mein 46.1% workforce is sector mein employed thi. [Q89]\n• 2025-26 first AE mein per-capita GDP current prices par ₹2,51,393 estimate hua; 2024-25 production figures rice 150.2 MT, wheat\n117.9 MT aur total foodgrains 357.7 MT the. [Q90]\n• Agricultural income par tax lagane ka power State Governments ke jurisdiction mein aata hai; Constitution ki Seventh Schedule ki\nState List mein taxation powers detail ki gayi hain. [Q91]\n• PLFS 2023-24 mein agriculture sector ne 46.1% workforce ko employment diya; Census 2011 mein corresponding worker share\n54.6% tha. [Q92]\n• Census 2011 mein Uttar Pradesh ke workers ka agriculture-sector share 59.3% tha; cultivators 29.0% aur agricultural labourers\n30.3% the. [Q93]\n• Agriculture par livelihood dependence ka share time ke saath ghata: Census 2011 mein 54.6% workers aur PLFS 2023-24 mein\n46.1% workforce agriculture/allied activities mein employed thi. [Q94]\n• Sorghum/Jowar coarse foodgrain crop hai; cash crops commercial sale ke liye produce ki jati hain. [Q95]\n• Cash crops mein potato (commercial/horticulture), sugarcane, cotton aur rapeseed include kiye ja sakte hain; paddy primarily\nfoodgrain crop hai aur traditional cash-crop category mein nahi aata. [Q96]\n\n\n8. RKVY, Agro-Climatic Regions, Horticulture Mission & Labour Support\n\n• Kisan Bahi scheme Uttar Pradesh mein 1992 mein implement ki gayi thi. [Q97]\n• Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana 16 August 2007 se operational hui. Original framework mein States ko additional central assistance\n80% grant ke form mein milti thi. [Q98]\n• Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana ke original funding pattern mein Centre ka share 100% aur State ka share 0% tha. [Q99]\n• Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana 2007 se operational hai; agricultural engineering is scheme ki defining category nahi hai. [Q100]\n• P. Sengupta aur G. Sdasyuk (1968) ne India ko 4 macro regions, 11 meso/mega regions aur 60 micro agricultural regions mein\ndivide kiya. [Q101]\n• PM-KSY rain-fed holdings aur natural-resource conservation par focus karta hai, jisse foodgrain demand ko sustainably meet karne\nmein support milta hai. [Q102]","• Eighth Five Year Plan ke liye Planning Commission ne India ko 15 agro-climatic zones mein divide kiya tha. [Q103]\n• National Horticulture Mission (NHM) ek Centrally Sponsored Scheme hai, jise 5 May 2005 ko Tenth Plan ke dauran launch kiya\ngaya. [Q104]\n• National Horticulture Mission May 2005 mein start hua. [Q105]\n• MIDH 2014-15 mein 12th Five Year Plan ke dauran formulate hua; Uttar Pradesh scheme ki defining feature/category nahi hai.\n[Q106]\n• National Horticulture Mission ka focus horticulture growth, post-harvest management aur human-resource development par hai;\ndirect financial assistance ko mission ka standalone objective nahi maana jaata. [Q107]\n• Krishi Shramik Samajik Suraksha Yojana 1 July 2001 se start hui. Ismein agricultural labourers ko life-insurance cover, lump-sum\nlife benefit aur pension benefit diya jaata tha. [Q108]\n• Small Farmers Development Agency (SFDA) Programme 1971 mein small aur marginal farmers ko support karne ke liye start hua.\n[Q109]\n\n\n\n9. NFSA, Food Safety, NFSM, Entitlement & PDS\n\n• National Food Security Act, 2013 food-price access aur nutritional security se related legal framework hai. Act ko 10 September\n2013 ko notify kiya gaya aur 5 July 2013 se effective maana gaya. [Q110]\n• National Food Security Act ka objective population ko food aur nutritional security provide karna hai; notification 10 September 2013\naur effective date 5 July 2013 hai. [Q111]\n• National Food Security Act, 2013 ko 10 September 2013 ko notify kiya gaya aur 5 July 2013 se retrospective effect diya gaya.\n[Q112]\n• National Food Security Act, 2013 ka notification 10 September 2013 ko hua; ordinance date 5 July 2013 se ise effective maana\ngaya. [Q113]\n• National Food Security Act, 2013 rural population ke 75% aur urban population ke 50% ko subsidized foodgrains ka legal\nentitlement deta hai; women aur children ke nutrition ke liye special provisions bhi hain. Act 10 September 2013 ko notified hua aur 5\nJuly 2013 se effective maana gaya. [Q114]\n• State Food Commission National Food Security Act, 2013 ke under statutory body hai; “Preservation” uska function/description nahi\nhai. [Q115]\n• NFSA 2013 ke Section 16(1) ke under har State Government ko Act ki implementation monitor aur review karne ke liye State Food\nCommission constitute karna hota hai. [Q116]\n• E-Ration Card service sabse pehle Delhi ne start ki thi. [Q117]\n• Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 ko 23 August 2006 ko enact kiya gaya. Iska purpose food laws ko consolidate karna,\nscience-based food standards banana aur manufacture, storage, distribution, sale aur import ko regulate karke safe food ensure\nkarna hai. [Q118]\n• National Food Security Mission October 2007 mein launch hua; production/productivity improvement aur related support measures\niske main objectives hain. [Q119]\n• NFSM ki current crop coverage mein rice, wheat, pulses, coarse cereals aur commercial crops shamil hain; coarse cereals ab\nexcluded nahi hain. [Q120]\n• National Food Security Mission Rabi 2007-08 se effective hua. [Q121]\n• National Food Security Mission (NFSM) 2007-08 mein launch hua. [Q122]\n• National Food Security Mission production enhancement ke liye rice, wheat, pulses aur expanded phases mein coarse\ncereals/commercial crops cover karta hai. [Q123]\n• NFSM ke original core crops wheat, rice aur pulses the; later coverage ko coarse cereals aur commercial crops tak expand kiya\ngaya. [Q124]\n• NFSM ke older framework mein oilseeds core coverage ka hissa nahi the; current NFSM coverage ko scheme-component ke year\nke saath read karna chahiye. [Q125]\n• Food security ke context mein 'entitlement approach' economist Amartya Sen se associated hai. [Q126]\n• Food security ka matlab sirf production ya availability nahi, balki har person ko har samay sufficient, safe aur nutritious food ka\nphysical aur economic access hona hai. [Q127]\n• Coarse grains older NFSM coverage mein excluded the, lekin later expansion ke baad coarse cereals scheme coverage mein\nshamil ho gaye. [Q128]\n• Education, health aur skill-development par expenditure human capital mein investment hai; isse productive capacity, lifetime\nincome aur employment opportunities badhti hain. [Q129]\n• PDS ke through Government poor households ko subsidized/fair prices par foodgrains provide karti hai; isi price-gap ki wajah se\nGovernment par food subsidy ka burden aata hai. [Q130]\n\nEXAM TRAP — NFSA / NFSM Dates & Scope\n▸ NFSA 2013 aur NFSM mein notification/effective date, coverage aur scheme scope alag concepts hain; dates aur programme-\nscope ko mix na karein.\n[Q110, Q111, Q112, Q113, Q114, Q119, Q120, Q121, Q122, Q123, Q124, Q125, Q128]","10. Dairy, Livestock, Fisheries & Allied-Sector Development\n\n• Operation Flood 1970 mein National Dairy Development Board ne White Revolution aur milk production accelerate karne ke liye\nlaunch kiya. Dr. Verghese Kurien is movement ke pioneer the aur India ko world ka leading milk producer banane mein iska major\nrole raha. [Q131]\n• Older State comparison mein order Madhya Pradesh > Gujarat > Punjab > Haryana tha; 2019-20 milk production mein top States\nUttar Pradesh > Rajasthan > Madhya Pradesh > Gujarat > Andhra Pradesh the. [Q132]\n• Livestock-support schemes rural poor ke liye important hain, kyunki low-producing indigenous animals ka large share\nsmall/marginal farmers aur landless labourers ke paas hota hai. [Q133]\n• Nandini Krishak Samriddhi Yojana, Nand Baba Mission ka part hai; iska aim cattle breed improve karna aur milk production\nbadhana hai. [Q134]\n• Tharparkar cow breed hai jo mainly Gujarat aur Rajasthan region mein milti hai. [Q135]\n• Marine Products Export Development Authority (MPEDA) 1972 ke Act se establish hua. 1961 ka Marine Products Export Promotion\nCouncil 24 August 1972 ko MPEDA mein merge kar diya gaya. [Q136]\n• India mein inland fisheries ka role major hai. FAO State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2024 mein total fisheries-aquaculture\nproduction share ke hisab se China 36% ke saath first aur India 8% ke saath second tha. [Q137]\n• The State of World Fisheries and Aquaculture 2024 report mein total fisheries & aquaculture production mein China around 36%\nshare ke saath top tha; India around 8% ke saath next major producer tha. [Q138]\n• Blue Revolution fish production/fisheries se related hai. National Fisheries Development Board 9 September 2006 ko autonomous\ninstitution ke roop mein establish hua aur iska headquarters Hyderabad mein hai. [Q139]\n• Blue Revolution—fish production; Green Revolution—foodgrain production; White Revolution—milk production. [Q140]\n\n\n11. Agricultural Revolutions & Green Revolution\n\n• Major revolution mapping: Green—foodgrains; Blue—fish; Red—tomato/meat; White—milk; Yellow—oilseeds; Black—crude\noil/petroleum. [Q141]\n• Yellow Revolution oilseed production se related hai; 1986 mein Technology Mission on Oilseeds ke through is push ko strengthen\nkiya gaya. [Q142]\n• 'Yellow Revolution' Toria-Mustard production se related hai. [Q143]\n• Revolution mapping: Grey—fertilizer; Pink—onion; Silver—egg; Red—tomato aur meat production. [Q144]\n• Revolution mapping: Golden—horticulture aur honey; Grey—fertilizer; Yellow—oilseeds; Black—petroleum. [Q145]\n• Grey Revolution fertilizer se related hai. [Q146]\n• American scientist Norman E. Borlaug ko 'Father of Green Revolution' kaha jaata hai. [Q147]\n• G. B. Pant University of Agriculture and Technology, Pantnagar ne India mein Green Revolution spread karne mein major role\nnibhaya; isi wajah se Pantnagar ko Green Revolution ki birthplace bhi kaha jaata hai. [Q148]\n• India mein Green Revolution ke father M. S. Swaminathan maane jate hain; Indira Gandhi aur agriculture minister C. Subramaniam\nka bhi major role tha. [Q149]\n• Green Revolution ki late-1960s technology ko seed-fertilizer-water package se best describe kiya jaata hai. [Q150]\n• Green Revolution ki criticism yeh hai ki benefits selected regions aur mainly larger farmers tak zyada concentrated rahe. Coarse\ncereals ko relatively ignore karke wheat, rice aur kuch cash crops par zyada focus hua. [Q151]\n• India mein Green Revolution 1966 ke Kharif season se major scale par start hui; Punjab, Haryana aur Western Uttar Pradesh par\niska sabse strong positive impact raha. [Q152]\n• Second Green Revolution ke context mein focus direct foreign-exchange investment par nahi, balki India-US Knowledge Initiative\nke through agricultural education, research, biotechnology aur capacity building par tha. Term ko 18 July 2005 ke Indo-US joint\nstatement se major prominence mili. [Q153]\n\n\n12. Sustainable Agriculture, Soil Health, Millets, PM-AASHA & Extension\n\n• Organic farming aisa production system hai jo soil health, ecosystem balance aur human health ko support karne par focus karta\nhai. [Q154]\n• Soil Health Card Scheme 19 February 2015 ko Suratgarh, Rajasthan se launch hui; slogan tha 'Swasth Dhara, Khet Hara'. [Q155]\n• INSIMP ko Kharif 2011 se millets ki nutritional security ke liye implement kiya gaya. Iska aim cluster approach se improved\nproduction, post-harvest technology aur value addition demonstrate karna tha. [Q156]\n• PM-AASHA September 2018 mein pulses, oilseeds aur copra ke farmers ko remunerative/minimum price support dene, distress\nsale reduce karne aur income stability improve karne ke objective se launch hui. [Q157]\n• National Agricultural Technology Project (NATP) World Bank-assisted project hai; November 1998 se ICAR aur Department of\nAgriculture & Cooperation ne ise implement kiya. [Q158]\n• India ka first Krishi Vigyan Kendra (KVK) 1974 mein Puducherry mein establish hua. [Q159]","13. Crop Insurance & Weather Insurance\n\n• Agriculture ke production risks aur uncertainties reduce karne ke liye Pradhan Mantri Fasal Bima Yojana (PMFBY) 13 January 2016\nko launch hui aur Kharif 2016 se implement hui. [Q160]\n• India mein crop-insurance framework ka major early nationwide step Comprehensive Crop Insurance Scheme (CCIS) tha, jo 1 April\n1985 se Kharif season ke context mein launch hua. [Q161]\n• National Agricultural Insurance Scheme (NAIS) Rabi 1999-2000 se Comprehensive Crop Insurance Scheme ki jagah launch hui.\n[Q162]\n• National Agricultural Insurance Scheme (NAIS) 1999-2000 mein introduced hui. [Q163]\n• National Agricultural Insurance Scheme (NAIS) Rabi 1999-2000 se start hui aur later Kharif crops tak expand hui; National Crop\nInsurance Programme (NCIP) 2013-14 ke Rabi season se launch hua. [Q164]\n• Weather Based Crop Insurance Scheme ka first implementation 2007 ke Kharif season mein Karnataka mein hua. [Q165]\n• Varsha Bima rainfall-insurance product ko Agriculture Insurance Company of India ne Kharif 2004 ke South-West Monsoon period\nmein launch kiya. [Q166]\n• Agriculture sector ke crop-insurance cover ke liye Agriculture Insurance Company of India (AIC) designated specialised institution\nhai. [Q167]\n• Blue Revolution fish farming se related hai, mustard production se nahi. [Q168]\nEXAM TRAP — Insurance Schemes — Timeline Matters\n▸ Crop Insurance, NAIS, Weather Based scheme aur Varsha Bima alag schemes hain aur inke launch years bhi alag hain.\n[Q160, Q161, Q162, Q163, Q164, Q165, Q166, Q167, Q168]\n\n\n\n\n14. Watershed, Irrigation, KUSUM, Land Records & Groundwater Schemes\n\n• January 2003 mein Atal Bihari Vajpayee ne ₹2,000 crore ki 'Hariyali' water-harvesting development scheme launch ki. Iska focus\nrainwater harvesting, drinking-water problems, irrigation-water arrangement aur tree plantation par tha. [Q169]\n• Hariyali programme water-harvesting management se related tha; Integrated Watershed Management approach ke under runoff ko\nconserve karke greenery aur water availability badhane par focus tha. [Q170]\n• Vishesh Krishi Upaj Yojana ka purpose agricultural-product exports ko boost karna tha; iska mention Export-Import Policy 2004-09\nmein hua. [Q171]\n• Rashtriya Vishesh Krishi Upaj Yojana exportable agricultural products se related hai. [Q172]\n• Vishesh Krishi aur Gram Udyog Yojana ka main objective agricultural exports ko promote karna tha. [Q173]\n• PM-KUSUM ka core aim farmers ki diesel/kerosene dependence kam karke irrigation pump sets ko solar energy se link karna hai;\nyeh non-fossil energy targets ko bhi support karta hai. [Q174]\n• PM-KUSUM 2019 mein Ministry of New and Renewable Energy ne start ki. Scheme ke three components decentralised solar\nplants, standalone solar pumps aur existing agricultural pumps ki solarisation se related hain; target March 2026 tak 34.8 GW solar\ncapacity add karna tha. [Q175]\n• National Land Records Modernization Programme ko 2008 mein approve kiya gaya; 1 April 2016 se iska naam Digital India Land\nRecords Modernization Programme (DILRMP) kar diya gaya. [Q176]\n• Union Budget 1999-2000 ke decision se NABARD ke under Watershed Development Fund establish hua; initial corpus ₹200 crore\ntha, jisme Government aur NABARD ka ₹100-₹100 crore contribution tha. [Q177]\n• Rainfed Area Development Programme 2011-12 mein launch hua; later ye Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana framework (RKVY-\nRAFTAAR) ke under sub-scheme ke roop mein operate hua. [Q178]\n• Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchayee Yojana (PMKSY) July 2015 mein 'Har Khet Ko Pani' motto ke saath implement hui; irrigation\nexpansion, water-use efficiency aur field-level water access iske major components hain. [Q179]\n• UP Diversified Agriculture Support Project (UP-DASP) World Bank-sponsored project tha; first phase 23 September 1998 se 31\nMarch 2004 tak chala. [Q180]\n• Accelerated Irrigation Benefit Programme 1996-97 mein launch hua, taaki advanced stage par pahunch chuke incomplete\nmajor/medium irrigation projects ko States loan assistance mil sake. [Q181]\n• Madhya Pradesh ki Suraj Dhara Yojana seed exchange se related hai; SC/ST ke small aur marginal farmers ko one-hectare area\nke liye pulses/oilseeds seed exchange par grant di jaati hai. [Q182]\n• 'Neeru-Meeru' (Water and You) water-harvesting programme Andhra Pradesh mein 2000 mein launch hua. [Q183]\n• Atal Bhujal Yojana (Atal Jal) groundwater management ki Central Sector Scheme hai; June 2018 mein World Bank ne iske liye\n₹6,000 crore assistance approve ki. [Q184]\n• Atal Bhujal Yojana ko World Bank assistance mili. [Q185]\n• Jaldhara Yojana drought-prone/desert areas ke marginal farmers ke liye pump-set support programme hai; high upfront cost afford\nna kar paane wale farmers ko low rent/lease par pump sets provide kiye jaate hain. [Q186]\n• Compared States mein tubewell irrigation ke under sabse bada area Uttar Pradesh mein hai. [Q187]","15. Agricultural Institutions, Cooperatives, Markets & Research Centres\n\n• 'Handbook of Agriculture' Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) publish karta hai. [Q188]\n• UP Council of Agricultural Research Lucknow mein located hai aur 1989 mein establish hua tha. [Q189]\n• Krishak Samriddhi Ayog Uttar Pradesh mein November 2017 mein establish hua; iska target 2022 tak farmers' income double karna\ntha. [Q190]\n• NAFED agricultural marketing se related national-level apex cooperative organization hai. Yeh 2 October 1958 ko establish hua aur\nselected agricultural commodities ke management, distribution, export aur import mein role nibhata hai. [Q191]\n• NAFED agricultural marketing cooperatives ka national apex organization hai. [Q192]\n• Agricultural exports mein NAFED, STC aur MMTC important institutions hain; IFFCO mainly chemical fertilizers ki production,\nexport aur import se related hai. [Q193]\n• India ke most States mein agricultural-produce markets APMC Acts ke under develop aur regulate hote hain; ye Acts State\nlegislatures enact karti hain. [Q194]\n• Agricultural Produce Market Committee (APMC) laws State Governments/State legislatures ke domain mein aate hain. [Q195]\n• Golden Rice, Oryza sativa ki genetically engineered variety hai jisme beta-carotene biosynthesis enhance ki gayi hai; beta-carotene\npro-vitamin A provide karta hai. [Q196]\n• Agricultural products ki demand generally inelastic hoti hai; bumper/excess yield ke time supply badhne se prices sharply fall kar\nsakte hain aur farmers ka total revenue bhi negatively affect ho sakta hai. [Q197]\n• India ka first agricultural university G. B. Pant University of Agriculture and Technology hai, jo 1960 mein Pantnagar mein establish\nhua tha. [Q198]\n• Pantnagar mein 1960 mein Uttar Pradesh Agricultural University establish hua; 1972 mein iska naam G. B. Pant University of\nAgriculture and Technology rakha gaya. Yeh India ka first agricultural university maana jaata hai. [Q199]\n• First Krishi Vigyan Kendra 1974 mein Puducherry mein establish hua; small aur marginal farmers ki economic condition improve\nkarne ke liye 'Lab to Land' programme 1979 mein start hua. [Q200]\n• Indian Institute of Vegetable Research (IIVR) Varanasi mein located hai. [Q201]\n• Central Food Technological Research Institute (CFTRI) Mysuru mein located hai. [Q202]\n• National Academy of Agricultural Research Management (NAARM) Hyderabad mein located hai. [Q203]\n• Indian Grassland and Fodder Research Institute 1962 mein establish hua aur Jhansi mein located hai. [Q204]\n• International Potato Center (CIP), established in 1971, ka headquarters Lima, Peru mein hai. [Q205]\n\n\n16. Sugarcane FRP, MSP, CACP, Procurement & Price Policy\n\n• 22 October 2009 ke Sugarcane (Control) Order amendment ne Statutory Minimum Price concept ko Fair and Remunerative Price\n(FRP) se replace kiya; FRP 2009-10 aur subsequent sugar seasons se applicable hua. [Q206]\n• Central Government har sugar season ke liye sugarcane ka Fair and Remunerative Price (FRP) fix karti hai; 2009 amendment ke\nbaad old Statutory Minimum Price framework replace hua. [Q207]\n• Sugarcane ka Fair and Remunerative Price (FRP) Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs (CCEA) approve karti hai. [Q208]\n• FCI ka foodgrain Economic Cost = Acquisition Cost + Distribution Cost. Acquisition Cost mein MSP/bonus aur procurement\nincidentals—State levies, mandi fee, transport charges etc.—shamil hote hain. [Q209]\n• Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices (CACP) 22 crops ke MSP recommend karta hai; final MSP Government announce\nkarti hai. [Q210]\n• CACP 22 MSP-covered crops ke prices recommend karta hai. Sugarcane ke liye MSP nahi, Fair and Remunerative Price (FRP)\napply hota hai. [Q211]\n• Niger ek minor oilseed crop hai jo mainly rain-fed conditions mein grow hota hai; seed-oil content roughly 37-47% range mein ho\nsakta hai. [Q212]\n• Kharif season mein Government 14 crops ke MSP announce karti hai; Niger Seed in Kharif MSP crops mein shamil hai. [Q213]\n• MSP major crops ke liye minimum guaranteed price provide karta hai. Central Government Rabi aur Kharif seasons ke liye MSP\nannounce karti hai, taaki farmers ko remunerative price support mile. [Q214]\n• MSP fixation mein CACP comprehensive cost concept C2 ko consider karta hai. Cost concepts mein A2 jaise measures seeds,\nfertilizers, hired labour etc. ke paid-out costs ko capture karte hain. [Q215]\n• Minimum Support Price ki recommendation Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices (CACP) karta hai. [Q216]\n• Agricultural Prices Commission 1965 mein established hua; 1985 mein iska naam Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices\n(CACP) kar diya gaya. [Q217]\n• MSP determination ke liye key recommending body Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices (CACP) hai. [Q218]\n• Crop MSP fixation ki recommendation Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices (CACP) karta hai. [Q219]\n• Agricultural Prices Commission 1965 mein agricultural produce prices par government ko advice dene ke liye set up hua; 1985\nmein iska naam Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices (CACP) kar diya gaya. [Q220]","• Foodgrains ke support prices CACP recommend karta hai. [Q221]\n• CACP agricultural price policy ke under MSP jaise support prices recommend karta hai; MSP woh floor price hai jis par Government\nprocurement ke liye ready rehti hai. [Q222]\n• Food-grain public procurement at support prices agricultural-price stability, farmers ko remunerative prices aur public distribution ke\nliye buffer stocks—teeno objectives support karta hai. [Q223]\n• MSP farmers ko minimum guaranteed price deta hai aur production incentive ke through food security ko bhi support karta hai.\n[Q224]\n• 2013 mein wheat procurement MSP ₹1,350/quintal tha; marketing year 2026-27 ke liye 1 October 2025 ko wheat MSP\n₹2,585/quintal announce hua. [Q225]\n• 2018-19 season mein paddy MSP ₹1,750/quintal tha. Marketing year 2025-26 ke liye paddy (common) ₹2,369 aur Grade-A\n₹2,389/quintal announce hua; historical aur current figures ko mix na karein. [Q226]\n• Kerala ne October 2020 mein fruits aur vegetables ke liye MSP fix karne ka decision liya, jo 1 November 2020 se effective hua; is\ninitiative ke saath Kerala first State bana jisne fruits-vegetables ke liye MSP framework introduce kiya. [Q227]\n• Marketing year 2025-26 ke Kharif MSP mein groundnut ka MSP ₹7,263/quintal declare hua; 2023-24 mein yeh ₹6,783/quintal tha.\n[Q228]\n• PDS aur safe foodgrain storage maintain karne ke liye Government farmers se procurement price par produce purchase karti hai;\nprocurement price generally MSP se higher ho sakta hai. [Q229]\n• PDS aur buffer stock ke liye Government jis price par foodgrains purchase karti hai, use Procurement Price kaha jata hai. [Q230]\nEXAM TRAP — MSP Figures are Period-Specific\n▸ MSP values time-specific hoti hain; historical MSP aur newer marketing-season MSP ko same period ka figure na\nmaanein.\n[Q225, Q226, Q227, Q228]\n\n\n\n\n17. Crop Seasons, Farm Costs & Agricultural Finance\n\n• India ke major crop seasons Kharif, Rabi aur Zaid hain; barley, gram aur wheat Rabi crops hain, jabki sugarcane is group mein\nRabi crop nahi hai. [Q231]\n• Tobacco, cotton aur rubber cash crops hain; soybean cash crop nahi, oilseed crop category mein aata hai. [Q232]\n• Union Budget 2011-12 mein timely farm-loan repayment par interest subvention 2% se badhakar 3% ki gayi; 7% lending rate par\neffective rate 4% reh gaya. [Q233]\n• Farm working-cost capital mein seed, fertilizer aur irrigation-water jaise inputs include hote hain; land revenue ko working-cost\ncapital ka agricultural input nahi maana jaata. [Q234]\n• Rural economy mein animal husbandry, cottage industries aur good equipment important contribution dete hain. Private money-\nlending villages mein prevalent hai, lekin productive contribution comparatively limited hai. [Q235]\n• Agricultural finance ke major principles mein purpose, person, productivity, planning aur organization shamil hain. [Q236]\n• Agricultural finance ke sources institutional aur non-institutional hote hain. Institutional sources mein cooperatives, commercial\nbanks, Regional Rural Banks aur Government shamil hain. [Q237]\n• Agricultural-credit sources mein Primary Agricultural Cooperative Societies, commercial banks, RRBs aur non-government/private\nlenders shamil ho sakte hain. [Q238]\n• Agriculture ko promote karne ke liye farmers tak timely aur uninterrupted credit flow important hai. Government Scheduled\nCommercial Banks, RRBs aur rural cooperative banks ke annual ground-level lending targets set karti hai. [Q239]\n• Rural credit system ki long-term agricultural-credit needs Land Development Banks meet karte hain; inhe Land Mortgage Banks bhi\nkaha jaata hai. [Q240]\n• RBI August 2025 data mein 2024-25 agricultural finance ka largest institutional source Commercial Banks the (80.93%), uske baad\nRegional Rural Banks (10.70%) aur Cooperative Banks aaye. [Q241]\n• Institutional agricultural credit ka descending share/importance Commercial Banks > Regional Rural Banks > Cooperative Banks\nhai. [Q242]\n• Agriculture aur allied activities ke institutional credit distribution mein commercial banks ka share sabse bada hai. [Q243]\n• Agriculture-related institutional credit mainly commercial banks, RRBs aur cooperative banks provide karte hain; foreign private\nbanks ka contribution comparatively very low hai. [Q244]\n• Agricultural-credit target Budget 2020-21 mein ₹15 lakh crore, 2021-22 mein ₹16.5 lakh crore, 2022-23 mein ₹18 lakh crore aur\n2023-24 mein ₹20 lakh crore tha. [Q245]\n\n\n18. Kisan Credit Card, Kisan Rin Portal & Rural Credit Milestones\n\n• Kisan Credit Card Scheme 1998-99 mein introduce hui, taaki farmers ko crop cultivation aur allied activities ke liye adequate aur\ntimely credit mil sake. [Q246]\n• Kisan Credit Card (KCC) Scheme 1998-99 mein launch hui. [Q247]","• Kisan Credit Card Scheme 1998-99 mein start hui aur farmers ko cultivation needs ke liye adequate, timely credit support deti hai.\n[Q248]\n• Kisan Credit Card Scheme farmers ke liye 1998-99 mein introduced hui. [Q249]\n• Short-term crop loans ko concessional rate par available karane ke liye Interest Subvention Scheme aur Prompt Repayment\nIncentive framework RBI/NABARD-linked institutional mechanism ke through operate hota hai. [Q250]\n• Kisan Call Centre 21 January 2004 ko Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers Welfare ne launch kiya. [Q251]\n• Important rural-credit chronology: Regional Rural Banks—1975; NABARD—1982; SHG-Bank Linkage Programme—1992-93; Kisan\nCredit Card Scheme—1998. [Q252]\n• UPA-period agricultural package mein landless farmers ko bank credit access, NABARD se proposals aur 50 lakh se zyada new\nfarmers tak institutional credit expand karna shamil tha; loan-repayment waiver is provision ka part nahi tha. [Q253]","19. Price Stabilisation Fund, Minor Minerals & Agricultural Tax Rules\n\n• Price Stabilisation Fund ka 2003 framework tea, coffee, rubber aur tobacco ke price stabilisation se linked tha; 2014-15 ka\nagriculture PSF onion, potato aur pulses ki price volatility ke liye set up hua. [Q254]\n• Price Stabilisation Fund 2014-15 mein onion, potato aur pulses jaise agricultural products ki price volatility manage karne aur\nconsumers ko protect karne ke liye establish hua. [Q255]\n• MMDR Act, 1957 ke Section 3(e) mein sand ko minor mineral maana gaya hai. Section 15 ke under minor minerals ke rules banane\nki power State Governments ke paas hai, Central Government ke paas nahi. [Q256]\n• MMDR Act, 1957 ka Section 15(1) State Governments ko minor minerals ke regulation ke liye rules banane ki power deta hai.\n[Q257]\n• Income-tax Act, 1961 ke Section 10(1) ke under agricultural income Central income tax se exempt hai. Poultry farming ya sheep-\nrearing/wool jaise allied activities ki income automatically agricultural-income exemption mein nahi aati. [Q258]\n\n\n                         Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · Agriculture and Allied Sectors Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0452","t":"05 Industrial Sector","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["INDUSTRIAL SECTOR — FULL MIND MAP\n                              Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\nTopics in this chapter:\n 1. Major Companies, Regulators, CSR & Corporate Governance\n 2. Infrastructure, Corridors, Mobile Manufacturing, NIIF & PLI\n 3. National Manufacturing Policy, Make in India, Start-ups & Enterprise Finance\n 4. Industrial & Labour Laws, Industrial Growth, IIP & Core Sectors\n 5. MSME Definition, Classification, Investment Limits & Credit Guarantee\n 6. Small-Scale & Cottage Industries — Importance, Problems & Committees\n 7. Textiles, Paper, Fertilizers, Petroleum & Public Trading Enterprises\n 8. Public Sector Role, Major PSUs, Refineries, Energy & Services\n 9. Maharatna/Navratna/Miniratna, State Industrial Support & Business Concepts\n 10. Industrial Policy Resolutions, Licensing, Liberalisation & FMCG\n 11. Privatisation, Disinvestment & National Investment Fund\n 12. Steel, Aluminium, Cement, Industrial Centres & Organisation-Ministry Mapping\n 13. National Renewal Fund, Golden Handshake & Industrial Relations\n 14. Sugar, Cement, Textiles, Aluminium, Petrochemicals & Industrial Location\n 15. Industrial History, Business Groups, MNCs & Automobile Headquarters\n 16. Khadi, Textile Committees, RIICO, State Finance & Industrialists\n 17. e-Biz, E-Commerce, Information Technology & Software\n 18. Automobile, Pharmaceutical Sector & District Mineral Foundations\n\n\n 1. Major Companies, Regulators, CSR & Corporate Governance\n\n• Fortune Global 500 ke July 2006 edition mein Exxon Mobil top par tha aur Reliance Industries 284th rank par thi. 2024 Global 500\n mein Walmart overall first tha, jabki Indian companies mein Reliance Industries Ltd. overall rank 86 ke saath sabse upar thi. [Q1]\n• Fortune 500 India 2024 mein Reliance Industries ₹9,22,391 crore revenue ke saath largest company thi; LIC second, Indian Oil\n third aur ONGC fourth position par the. [Q2]\n• Reliance Industries US$200 billion market-cap cross karne wali first Indian company bani; yeh milestone September 2020 mein hi\n achieve ho gaya tha. [Q3]\n• Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board (PNGRB) 31 March 2006 ke Act ke under constitute hua, lekin yeh India ka first\n regulatory body nahi tha; KVIC, Forward Markets Commission, SEBI aur IRDA jaise bodies pehle se exist karte the. [Q4]\n• PCRA ka full form Petroleum Conservation Research Association hai. Petroleum Conservation Action Group (PCAG) 1976 mein\n petroleum products ki conservation aur efficient use ko promote karne ke liye set up hua tha. [Q5]\n• SDG India Index 2023-24 mein India ka national score 71 tha, jo 2020-21 ke 66 aur 2019-20 ke 60 se higher tha. Uttarakhand aur\n Kerala 79 score ke saath joint-best performing States rahe. [Q6]\n• Corporate Social Responsibility (CSR) ka basic idea business ko social responsibilities fulfill karte hue conduct karna hai; CSR\n charity ya society par favour karna nahi hai. [Q7]\n• Business context mein social responsibility ka matlab self-regulation aur aise responsible actions hain jo society ki quality of life\n improve karein. Companies Act, 2013 ke CSR framework ke under specified net-worth/turnover/profit thresholds wali companies ko\n CSR Committee banani hoti hai aur prescribed profit share welfare activities par spend karna hota hai. [Q8]\n• India mein CSR framework Companies Act, 2013 ke Section 135, Schedule VII aur Companies (CSR Policy) Rules, 2014 se govern\n hota hai; ye CSR eligibility, implementation aur reporting ke rules define karte hain. [Q9]\n• SEBI ne June 2017 mein Kotak Committee ko listed entities ke corporate-governance reforms suggest karne ke liye constitute kiya;\n committee ne October 2017 mein report submit ki. Committee ne Chairman aur Managing Director roles ke governance structure par\n recommendations di thi. [Q10]\n\n EXAM TRAP — Corporate / SDG Data Changes by Year\n ▸ Fortune rankings, market-cap milestones aur SDG Index scores year-specific hote hain; later update historical figure ko\n automatically replace nahi karta.\n [Q1, Q2, Q3, Q6]\n\n\n\n\n 2. Infrastructure, Corridors, Mobile Manufacturing, NIIF & PLI\n\n• Indian Railways ne 2030 tak net-zero carbon-emission network aur 2023 tak 100% electrification ka target set kiya tha. [Q11]\n• India ka first rubber-tyre based metro project Nashik, Maharashtra mein develop kiya gaya. [Q12]\n• Swedish retailer IKEA ne India ka first showroom 9 August 2018 ko Hyderabad mein open kiya; company ne India mein multiple\n stores expand karne ka plan banaya tha. [Q13]\n• Industrial Development Centre Banmore, Madhya Pradesh ke Morena district mein located hai. [Q14]\n• Madhya Pradesh ke Dewas ko pharmaceutical products aur Indore ko soya meal/soya products ke liye Towns of Export Excellence\n mein include kiya gaya. [Q15]\n• Budget 2015-16 ke baad infrastructure PPP model ko revisit aur revitalize karne ke liye Dr. Vijay Kelkar ke chairmanship mein\n committee constitute ki gayi. [Q16]","• Bharatmala Pariyojana highways-sector ki umbrella scheme hai; economic corridors, border/international-connectivity roads,\ncoastal-port roads aur expressways jaise road infrastructure ko develop/improve karna iska focus hai. [Q17]\n• National Infrastructure Pipeline Task Force ki final report 29 April 2020 ko release hui. NIP ka objective world-class infrastructure\ndevelop karke citizens ki quality of life improve karna hai. [Q18]\n• PM Gati Shakti National Master Plan 13 October 2021 ko launch hua. Is framework mein roads, railways, airports, ports, mass\ntransport, waterways aur logistics infrastructure—seven engines—ko integrated planning se align kiya jaata hai. [Q19]\n• Delhi-Mumbai Industrial/Economic Corridor Japan ke collaboration se develop kiya ja raha hai; India-Japan agreement 2006 mein\nsign hua tha. [Q20]\n• 2019-23 infrastructure expenditure ke compared States mein Uttar Pradesh first, Maharashtra second, Tamil Nadu third aur\nKarnataka fourth tha. [Q21]\n• Deepak Parekh Committee May 2007 mein infrastructure development aur financing ke measures suggest karne ke liye constitute\nki gayi thi. [Q22]\n• Mobile-gaming market ko downloads ke basis par dekhen to India top par hai; revenue basis par India top-5 countries mein nahi\naata. [Q23]\n• Indian Cellular Association ke April 2018 data mein India 2017 tak mobile-phone manufacturing mein China ke baad second\nposition par pahunch gaya tha; February 2025 tak ke data mein bhi India second-largest mobile-phone manufacturing country raha.\n[Q24]\n• 2023 mein mobile-phone manufacturing mein India ka global position second tha. [Q25]\n• National Investment and Infrastructure Fund (NIIF) Indian Trusts Act, 1882 ke framework mein investment trust ke roop mein\nregistered hai; yeh NITI Aayog ka organ nahi hai. Proposed corpus ₹40,000 crore tha. [Q26]\n• Production Linked Incentive (PLI) scheme 2021 mein 14 important sectors ke liye announce hui, taaki India ki manufacturing\ncapacity aur exports ko boost kiya ja sake. [Q27]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Infrastructure Timelines\n▸ NIP, PM Gati Shakti, mobile manufacturing aur PLI ke launch year/period aur target details alag hain; in timelines ko interchange na\nkarein.\n[Q18, Q19, Q24, Q25, Q27]\n\n\n\n\n3. National Manufacturing Policy, Make in India, Start-ups & Enterprise Finance\n\n• National Manufacturing Policy (NMP) ko Ministry of Commerce and Industry ne 4 November 2011 ko notify kiya. [Q28]\n• National Manufacturing Policy 2011 mein launch/notified hui. [Q29]\n• National Manufacturing Policy ka target manufacturing growth 12-14%, better global competitiveness aur faster exports tha; 100\nmillion jobs ka target 2022 ke liye tha, 2025 ke liye nahi. [Q30]\n• Manufacturing-sector growth support karne ke liye National Investment and Manufacturing Zones (NIMZs), Single Window\nClearance aur Technology Acquisition and Development Fund jaise mechanisms provide kiye gaye. [Q31]\n• National Investment and Manufacturing Zones (NIMZs) NMP 2011 ke key instruments hain—large integrated industrial townships\njahan modern infrastructure, clean technology, social infrastructure aur skill facilities hoti hain. DMIC-linked examples mein\nAhmedabad-Dholera, Shendra-Bidkin, Manesar-Bawal, Khushkhera-Bhiwadi-Neemrana, Pithampur-Dhar-Mhow, Dadri-Noida-\nGhaziabad, Dighi Port aur Jodhpur-Pali-Marwar shamil hain. [Q32]\n• Capital intensity production process mein labour ke comparison mein machinery/equipment jaise capital ka relative use batata hai.\nHigh capital intensity mein capital ka use zyada hota hai; 2017-18 ke compared States mein manufacturing capital intensity Madhya\nPradesh mein highest thi. [Q33]\n• Make in India 25 September 2014 ko launch hua aur iska logo Lion hai. Iske objectives bureaucracy/red-tapism reduce karna,\ninfrastructure improve karke manufacturing cost kam karna, competitiveness badhana aur Ease of Doing Business improve karna\nthe. [Q34]\n• 'Make in India' program September, 2014 mein launch hua. [Q35]\n• 'Make in India' program ka logo Lion hai. [Q36]\n• Make in India ka objective investment facilitate karna, India ko major investment destination banana aur companies ko India mein\nmanufacture karne ke liye encourage karke global manufacturing hub develop karna hai. [Q37]\n• Digital India Government ka flagship programme hai jiska vision digitally empowered society aur knowledge economy banana hai.\nIska focus government departments ko citizens se digitally connect karke services ko paperless/electronic mode mein accessible\nbanana hai. [Q38]\n• Start-ups ki financing needs ko Angel Investors, Venture Capital aur Crowd Funding jaise new-generation funding sources support\nkarte hain. [Q39]\n• Start-up generally naya enterprise hota hai jo innovative product/service market mein introduce karta hai. Start-up financing ke liye\nsingle funding source ke bajay hybrid funding model ka approach important maana gaya hai. [Q40]\n• Gujarat Government ne January 2022 mein Student Start-up and Innovation Policy (SSIP) 2.0 launch ki; policy period 11 January\n2022 se 31 March 2027 tak tha. [Q41]\n• Stand Up India entrepreneurship aur employment promote karne ke liye announce hui; scheme 5 April 2016 ko Noida se launch ki\ngayi aur SC/ST aur women entrepreneurs ko institutional credit access dene par focus karti hai. [Q42]","• Stand-up India SC/ST aur women entrepreneurs ko institutional credit structure tak access facilitate karta hai. Scheme 5 April 2016\nko Noida, Uttar Pradesh se launch hui, Babu Jagjivan Ram ki birth anniversary aur Dr. Bhimrao Ambedkar ki 125th birth anniversary\nke occasion par. [Q43]\n• Stand-Up India entrepreneurship scheme women, SCs aur STs ko promote karti hai. [Q44]\n• Pradhan Mantri MUDRA Yojana (PMMY) Department of Financial Services, Ministry of Finance ke under operate hoti hai aur 8 April\n2015 ko launch hui. [Q45]\n• PM MUDRA Yojana ka aim micro entrepreneurs ko formal financial system se jodna aur 'Funding the Unfunded' ke through credit\nsupport dena hai. MUDRA ka full form Micro Units Development and Refinance Agency hai. [Q46]\n• PM MUDRA Yojana small non-farm income-generating businesses ko easy credit support deti hai. Original framework mein ₹10\nlakh tak ke loans Shishu/Kishor/Tarun categories ke through cover hote the. [Q47]\n• MUDRA ki Kishor category mein loan amount ₹50,000 se zyada aur ₹5 lakh tak hota hai. [Q48]\n• PMMY ne non-corporate, non-farm micro/small enterprises ko collateral-light credit access diya. Union Budget 2024-25 mein\neligible successful borrowers ke liye loan limit ₹20 lakh tak badhane ki announcement hui. [Q49]\n• Mukhyamantri Udyam Kranti Yojana Madhya Pradesh mein self-employment ke liye collateral-free/easy loan support dene ke\nobjective se April 2022 mein launch hui. [Q50]\n• Quality Council of India (QCI) 1996 ke Cabinet decision se national quality body ke roop mein establish hua; iske formation mein\nEU expert mission aur inter-ministerial consultation ka role tha. [Q51]\n\n\n4. Industrial & Labour Laws, Industrial Growth, IIP & Core Sectors\n\n• Important industrial-law chronology: Minimum Wages Act—1948; Industries (Development and Regulation) Act—1951; MRTP Act—\n1969; FERA—1973. [Q52]\n• Monopolies and Restrictive Trade Practices Commission (MRTPC), MRTP Act 1969 ke Section 5 ke under establish hua. [Q53]\n• Labour Bureau labour-related statistics ki collection, analysis aur dissemination karta hai aur Ministry of Labour & Employment ka\nattached office hai; iski institutional roots 1920 se hain. [Q54]\n• Trade Disputes Act, 1929 ne trade disputes ke investigation/settlement ke liye Board of Conciliation aur Court of Inquiry ka\nframework diya; strikes par restrictions bhi is framework ka part the. [Q55]\n• Industrial Employment (Standing Orders) Central Amendment Rules, 2018 ne fixed-term employment ko apparel manufacturing se\nextend karke all sectors tak applicable kiya. [Q56]\n• India ka first Factories Act 1881 mein pass hua. Ismein mainly child workers ki safety se related provisions the; women workers ke\nliye specific provisions nahi the. [Q57]\n• Code on Wages, 2019 wages aur bonus se related multiple labour laws ko consolidate aur amend karne ke liye banaya gaya\nframework hai. [Q58]\n• Casual worker temporary employment arrangement par kaam karta hai, jisme job security aur benefits limited ho sakte hain. EPF\ncoverage mein company se directly/indirectly wages draw karne wale eligible employees provident-fund benefits claim kar sakte\nhain. [Q59]\n• Uttar Pradesh mein 1 April 2017 se unskilled worker ka minimum wage ₹7,400/month tha. 1 October 2025 se 31 March 2026 period\nke rates: Unskilled ₹11,021; Semi-skilled ₹12,123; Skilled ₹13,580 per month. [Q60]\n• 2006-07 ke historical data mein industry ka India ke GDP mein share roughly 25-30% range mein tha; exact share series aur price-\nbase ke saath change hota hai. [Q61]\n• Economic Review 2025-26 mein industrial-sector growth FY2024-25 ke liye 5.9% aur FY2025-26 ke first half ke liye around 7%\nrecord ki gayi. [Q62]\n• 1999-2000 mein industrial-production growth 6.7% thi, jo 1998-99 ke 4.1% se higher thi. South-East Asian currency crisis aur\nnuclear-test sanctions ke backdrop ke bawajood 1999-2000 period mein exchange rate relative stability dikhata hai. [Q63]\n• Recent IIP use-based data mein capital goods aur infrastructure/construction goods ne strong growth record ki. [Q64]\n• Index of Industrial Production (IIP) NSO calculate/release karta hai. Broad-sector IIP mein mining, manufacturing aur electricity;\nuse-based IIP mein primary, capital, intermediate, infrastructure/construction, consumer durables aur consumer non-durables shamil\nhote hain. [Q65]\n• IIP ke broad-sector coverage mein mining, manufacturing aur electricity shamil hain; gas aur water supply is group ka part nahi\nhain. [Q66]\n• Index of Industrial Production (IIP) India mein National Statistical Office (NSO) ki Economic Statistics Division release karti hai.\n[Q67]\n• IIP series ka base year June 2011 release mein 1993-94 se 2004-05 shift hua; May 2017 ki new series mein base 2011-12 hua. Is\nseries mein mining 14.37%, manufacturing 77.63% aur electricity 7.99% weight carry karte hain; 839 items aur 407 item-groups\ncover hue. [Q68]\n• India ke eight core industries hain: Coal, Crude Oil, Natural Gas, Petroleum Refinery Products, Fertilizers, Steel, Cement aur\nElectricity. [Q69]\n• Chemicals eight core industries ka part nahi hai. [Q70]\n• Eight Core Industries ka combined index Coal, Crude Oil, Natural Gas, Petroleum Refinery Products, Fertilizers, Steel, Cement aur\nElectricity ko cover karta hai. Current IIP-linked weights mein Petroleum Refinery Products 28.04% ke saath highest aur Electricity\n19.85% hai. [Q71]\n• 2000-01 mein listed industries mein steel ki growth rate sabse zyada thi. 20 January 2026 ko released core-industry data mein\n2024-25 se 2025-26 (April-December, provisional) ke beech overall growth 4.5% se 2.6% hui; steel 6.8% se 9.5% aur cement 6.3%\nse 8.8% raha. [Q72]","• Coal-block allocation currently auction se hota hai, lottery se nahi. Domestic coal-supply shortage ko meet karne ke liye imports bhi\nkiye jaate hain. [Q73]\n• Manufacturing ka matlab machines, tools aur labour ke through raw materials ko large scale par finished goods mein convert karna\nhai. Construction industry ko manufacturing se separate category maana jaata hai. [Q74]\n\nEXAM TRAP — IIP vs Eight Core Industries\n▸ IIP aur Eight Core Industries alag measures hain; inke base years, weights aur series structure ko mix nahi karna\nchahiye.\n[Q62, Q64, Q65, Q66, Q67, Q68, Q69, Q70, Q71]\n\n\n\n\n5. MSME Definition, Classification, Investment Limits & Credit Guarantee\n\n• MSMED Act 2006 ke 14 saal baad Atmanirbhar Bharat Package mein 13 May 2020 ko MSME definition revise karne ki\nannouncement hui; manufacturing aur service units ke liye uniform investment-turnover formula adopt hua. [Q75]\n• Investment aur turnover based revised MSME classification 2020 mein introduce hui. [Q76]\n• 1991 Industrial Policy mein small-unit investment limit ₹35 lakh se ₹60 lakh aur ancillary-industry limit ₹45 lakh se ₹75 lakh ki gayi.\n[Q77]\n• 2020 revision mein micro enterprise ke liye plant/machinery/equipment investment limit ₹1 crore tak rakhi gayi; yeh MSME definition\nreform Atmanirbhar Bharat Package ka part tha. [Q78]\n• 2020 MSME classification mein micro enterprise ke liye investment limit ₹1 crore aur turnover limit ₹5 crore thi. 1 April 2025 se\nrevised classification mein micro enterprise ki limits investment up to ₹2.5 crore aur annual turnover up to ₹10 crore hain. [Q79]\n• Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises Development (MSMED) Act 2006 mein enacted hua. [Q80]\n• New Industrial Policy 1991 ke period mein small-scale industries ki capital-investment limit ₹60 lakh thi. [Q81]\n• Old MSME manufacturing classification mein small enterprise ka plant/machinery investment ₹25 lakh se zyada aur ₹5 crore tak\ntha. [Q82]\n• MSMED Act, 2006 ke original classification mein medium enterprise ka investment band ₹5 crore se ₹10 crore tha. [Q83]\n• MSMED Act, 2006 ke original manufacturing-sector classification mein medium enterprise ke liye investment ₹5 crore se zyada aur\n₹10 crore se kam tha. [Q84]\n• MSMED Act, 2006 ne micro, small aur medium enterprises ko manufacturing aur service—do broad categories mein classify karke\nseparate investment limits define ki thi. [Q85]\n• MSMED Act, 2006 ke original framework mein manufacturing enterprises ko plant aur machinery investment ke basis par micro,\nsmall aur medium categories mein classify kiya gaya tha. [Q86]\n• CGTMSE ka full form Credit Guarantee Fund Trust for Micro and Small Enterprises hai. Government of India aur SIDBI ne 2000\nmein ise collateral-free credit support ke liye jointly launch kiya. [Q87]\n\n\n6. Small-Scale & Cottage Industries — Importance, Problems & Committees\n\n• ASI 2022-23 ke hisaab se total factories mein top States ka order tha: Tamil Nadu (39,666) > Gujarat (31,031) > Maharashtra\n(26,446) > Uttar Pradesh (19,102) > Andhra Pradesh (16,483). [Q88]\n• Large-scale industries generally capital aur technology intensive hoti hain, jabki small-scale industries labour intensive hoti hain.\nIsliye per unit capital investment par small-scale sector relatively zyada employment generate karta hai. [Q89]\n• Small-scale aur cottage industries ki khas importance employment generation hai. Ye labour-intensive hoti hain, isliye labour-\nsurplus developing economy jaise India mein per unit capital zyada jobs create karti hain. [Q90]\n• Small-scale/cottage industries ka major importance employment generation hai. Third SSI Census context mein employment 1998-\n99 ke 220.55 lakh se badhkar Fourth All India Census 2009-10 mein around 659 lakh report hua. [Q91]\n• Handloom India ke sabse important small-scale industries mein se ek hai aur diverse cultural heritage ko represent karta hai.\nMuslin, chintz, rugs aur khadi jaise products is sector se jude hain; handloom largely unorganised sector ka part hai. [Q92]\n• Current data mein these industries ke beech Uttar Pradesh mein employment ke terms mein handloom industry sabse badi hai.\n[Q93]\n• Indian small-scale industries ki major problems hain: capital ki kami, marketing difficulty, raw-material shortage, infrastructure\nconstraints, delayed payments, industrial sickness aur reliable quality data ki kami. [Q94]\n• Small aur cottage industries ko raw material, finance, outdated production methods, marketing aur large industries se competition\njaise challenges face hote hain. Organisation, research, transport aur technical education ki kami bhi important problems hain;\nstrikes-lockouts mainly large industries se associated issue hai. [Q95]\n• Small-enterprise committees ki chronology: Karve Committee (1955) → Nayak Committee (1991) → Abid Hussain Committee\n(1995) → S. S. Kohli Committee (2000). [Q96]\n• Nayak Committee December 1991 mein RBI ne small-scale industry sector ki finance aur sickness-related problems evaluate karne\nke liye constitute ki; committee ne September 1992 mein report submit ki. [Q97]\n• Small Enterprises par Expert Committee December 1995 mein Dr. Abid Hussain ki chairmanship mein constitute hui thi, jiska\npurpose small enterprises ki problems study karke development suggestions dena tha. [Q98]\n• Abid Hussain Committee Small and Medium Enterprises se related thi. [Q99]","• 1992 ke aas-paas small-scale industry ka manufacturing turnover contribution around 40% category mein tha. Economic Survey\n2025-26 mein MSMEs ka manufacturing contribution around 35.4%, exports 48.58% aur GDP 31.3% diya gaya; sector large-scale\nemployment bhi generate karta hai. [Q100]\n• MSME sector ka GVA-to-GDP ratio 2020-21 mein 27.3% aur 2022-23 mein 30.1% tha. Ye MSME GVA ka GDP mein share hai,\ncountry ke total GVA mein share wali wording se ise confuse na karein. [Q101]\n• Cooperative units mein cotton textile industry ne strong growth dikhayi; 1999-2000 se 2005-06 ke period mein year-on-year growth\nlagbhag 14.8% record hui. [Q102]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Committee Chronology\n▸ Committee chronology: Karve 1955 → Nayak 1991 → Abid Hussain 1995 → S. S. Kohli 2000.\n[Q96]\n\n\n\n\n7. Textiles, Paper, Fertilizers, Petroleum & Public Trading Enterprises\n\n• Textile industry agriculture ke baad India ke sabse bade employment generators mein se ek hai. Manufacturing GVA mein iska\ncontribution around 11% raha; Economic Survey/Review 2025-26 context mein apparel-textile industry ka size roughly US$179\nbillion aur GDP contribution around 2% bataya gaya. [Q103]\n• India mein textiles industry major employment-generating industries mein se ek hai. [Q104]\n• India mein cotton textiles ka largest share powerloom sector produce karta hai—historical composition mein 60% se zyada. Uske\nbaad hosiery, handloom aur mill sectors ka contribution aata hai. [Q105]\n• Mumbai ko 'Cotton Polis of India' kaha jaata hai; Ahmedabad bhi India ka major cotton-textile centre hai. [Q106]\n• October 2020 mein Tamil Nadu mein cotton mills ki sankhya sabse zyada thi, uske baad Maharashtra; listed States mein\nMaharashtra sabse upar tha. [Q107]\n• Historical cloth-production composition mein mill-made 4%, handloom 12%, hosiery 22% aur powerloom 62% share tha. Listed\nStates ke mill-made cloth data mein Gujarat sabse aage tha. [Q108]\n• Textile industry India ki oldest large industries mein se hai. First modern cotton-mill attempt 1818 mein Calcutta (Kolkata) ke paas\nhua tha. [Q109]\n• India mein cottage industries ka development bahut purana hai. Colonial period se pehle industrial production mein inka major\ncontribution tha aur fine cotton textiles bhi cottage level par produce hote the. [Q110]\n• Iron and steel ko modern industry ki core/pivotal industry maana jaata hai, jabki petrochemical sector ko growth industry ke roop\nmein dekha jaata hai. [Q111]\n• NEPA Limited, Nepanagar (Madhya Pradesh) newsprint production ka important centre raha hai. [Q112]\n• Yamunanagar (Haryana), Guwahati (Assam) aur Ballarpur (Maharashtra) paper-industry centres hain; Shahabad (Bihar) ko is group\nmein paper-manufacturing centre nahi maana jaata. [Q113]\n• Government ne 1972 mein Korba, Chhattisgarh mein coal-based fertilizer plant establish karne ko approve kiya tha. [Q114]\n• KRIBHCO ka Hazira fertilizer plant, Surat ke paas Hazira mein located hai aur natural gas based plant hai. [Q115]\n• India ne chemical-fertilizer production mein substantial self-sufficiency develop ki hai. [Q116]\n• Fertilizer production mein natural gas important feedstock hai; isi wajah se domestic natural-gas resources fertilizer industry ke liye\nstrategically important hain. [Q117]\n• ONGC ki institutional beginning 14 August 1956 ko Oil and Natural Gas Commission ke roop mein hui; October 1959 mein statutory\nstatus mila. Headquarters Dehradun, Uttarakhand mein hai. [Q118]\n• Oil India Limited (OIL) public-sector enterprise hai jo crude oil aur natural gas ki exploration, development, production aur oil\ntransportation se linked hai; ise Maharatna status mil chuka hai. [Q119]\n• MMTC India ka major non-oil importer hai aur Projects & Equipment Corporation Ministry of Commerce and Industry ke under hai.\nExport Credit Guarantee Corporation ka role export-credit risk cover hai; export goods ki quality-control/pre-shipment inspection iska\nobjective nahi hai. [Q120]\n• MMTC Limited Government of India undertaking aur major international trading organisation hai. Neelachal Ispat Nigam Limited\n(NINL) Odisha ke Jajpur district mein located hai. [Q121]\n• MMTC public sector ka major trading organisation aur minerals exporter raha hai; foreign-exchange earnings mein bhi iska\nimportant role raha. [Q122]\n\n\n8. Public Sector Role, Major PSUs, Refineries, Energy & Services\n\n• Public sector ke objectives mein public utilities provide karna, social-economic infrastructure build karna, balanced regional/sectoral\ndevelopment promote karna aur income-wealth redistribution ke through egalitarian goals support karna shamil hain. [Q123]\n• Self-reliance ke liye public investment power, steel, aluminium, copper, mining, heavy machinery aur paper jaise basic industries\nmein kiya gaya. Public-sector jobs mein security relatively high ho sakti hai, lekin har PSU profitable ho—ye zaroori nahi. [Q124]\n• 1991 ke baad New Economic Policy ka major emphasis private-sector role badhane par raha; public-sector employment growth\ninitially very low aur later negative bhi raha. [Q125]\n• Public sector ka role kai major areas mein raha hai, lekin commercial banking functions mein iska presence sabse effective raha.\n[Q126]","• TISCO, ab Tata Steel, private-sector steel company hai; 1907 mein Jamshedpur mein establish hui. NTPC, SAIL aur BHEL public-\nsector Maharatna companies hain. [Q127]\n• Ordnance Factory Jabalpur, BHEL Bhopal aur Opium & Alkaloid Factory Neemuch Central Government establishments hain;\nGwalior ki leather factory private-sector unit thi. [Q128]\n• ESSAR Oil, ab Nayara Energy Limited, private-sector enterprise hai; comparison ke other enterprises public-sector category mein\naate hain. [Q129]\n• BHEL ka Bhopal unit August 1956 mein Heavy Electrical Plant (HEP) Limited ke roop mein establish hua tha. Heavy electrical\nmachinery/equipment ke production ke liye British Associated Electrical Industries ke collaboration ka role tha. [Q130]\n• BPCL mein Government of India ki stake pehle 66.2% thi; March 2020 ke end se reported government stake 52.98% rahi. [Q131]\n• Balmer Lawrie & Company aur Educational Consultants India Ltd. public-sector enterprises hain. Dredging Corporation of India bhi\npublic-sector company thi, lekin 2017 mein private-sector company mein transform hui. [Q132]\n• Petroleum and Natural Gas Regulatory Board (PNGRB) PNGRB Act, 2006 ke under establish hua. Board ka regulation refining,\nstorage, transportation, distribution, marketing aur sale par apply hota hai; petroleum/natural-gas production iske regulatory scope ka\npart nahi hai. [Q133]\n• Current ownership structure mein Essar Oil/Nayara Energy private sector mein hai. Bongaigaon aur Haldia refineries IOC ke under\nhain, jabki Mangalore Refinery (MRPL) ONGC subsidiary hai. [Q134]\n• Is comparison mein listed oil refineries public-sector refineries hain; inmein private-sector refinery include nahi hai. [Q135]\n• India mein Koyali historically high-capacity public refinery centres mein raha, jabki Reliance ka Jamnagar refinery complex country\nka highest-capacity refinery complex aur world ke largest refinery complexes mein se ek hai. [Q136]\n• Jamnagar, Gujarat petrochemical industry ka major centre hai; Dahej bhi important petrochemical hub hai. Jamnagar refinery\ncomplex global-scale capacity ke liye known hai. [Q137]\n• Hindustan Aeronautics Limited (HAL) aircraft aur aerospace equipment se associated PSU hai; October 2024 mein HAL ko\nMaharatna status mila. [Q138]\n• NHPC Limited power-generation company hai, primarily hydroelectric sector se linked. 1975 mein establish hui aur solar/wind\nprojects mein bhi activity rakhti hai. [Q139]\n• India Tourism Development Corporation (ITDC) tourism aur hotel infrastructure/development se associated public-sector\norganisation hai. [Q140]\n• Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited (BSNL) year 2000 mein establish hua. [Q141]\n• BSNL mein Government of India ka 100% ownership raha, jabki NTPC, BHEL aur SBI jaise listed public-sector entities mein\ngovernment stake 100% se kam hai. [Q142]\n• BSNL loss-making CPSE raha. BSNL Annual Report 2023-24 mein 2023-24 ka loss ₹5,370.73 crore tha, jabki 2022-23 mein\n₹8,161.56 crore loss report hua. [Q143]\n• 2014-15 mein Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited loss-making CPSEs mein shamil tha. [Q144]\n• GAIL ne Vijayawada (Andhra Pradesh) se Vijaipur (Madhya Pradesh) pipeline project ke liye around ₹8,000 crore ka\nprovision/planned investment rakha tha. [Q145]\n• Petrol aur diesel pricing India mein deregulated framework par operate karti hai; retail prices oil-marketing companies market-linked\nfactors ke basis par determine karti hain. [Q146]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Private Refinery Data Mismatch\n▸ Refinery ownership: Bongaigaon aur Haldia IOC/public-sector network mein hain; Nayara Energy (erstwhile Essar Oil)\nprivate sector mein hai; MRPL ONGC subsidiary hai.\n[Q135]\n\n\n\n\n9. Maharatna/Navratna/Miniratna, State Industrial Support & Business Concepts\n\n• Navratna status select Central Public Sector Enterprises ko greater managerial/financial autonomy dene ke liye diya jaata hai,\nespecially un enterprises ko jinke global-scale growth potential ko recognise kiya gaya ho. [Q147]\n• Oil India Limited ko August 2023 mein 13th Maharatna CPSE ka status mila; December 2025 tak bhi company Maharatna category\nmein thi. [Q148]\n• January 2020 ki Maharatna list mein Oil India Limited Maharatna category mein included nahi tha. [Q149]\n• Maharatna scheme ke early phase mein November 2010 mein IOC, NTPC, ONGC aur SAIL ko Maharatna status diya gaya tha.\n[Q150]\n• Bharat Electronics Limited (BEL) Navratna company hai; comparison mein diye gaye kai other large CPSEs Maharatna category\nmein aate hain. [Q151]\n• BHEL aur GAIL ko 2013 mein Maharatna status grant kiya gaya. [Q152]\n• Maharatna companies ki count time ke saath badli—older period mein 10 ka figure milta hai, jabki December 2025 tak count 14 tha.\n[Q153]\n• Bharat Electronics Limited (BEL) Navratna category mein hai; Coal India, BHEL aur GAIL Maharatna category mein hain. [Q154]\n• MTNL Navratna category se associated raha hai, jabki GAIL aur SAIL Maharatna companies hain. [Q155]\n• Older PSU-status comparisons mein Indian Aluminium ko exception ke roop mein diya gaya tha; current classification mein SAIL,\nPower Grid aur REC jaise enterprises Maharatna category mein hain. [Q156]","• National Aluminium Company Limited (NALCO) 1981 mein incorporated hui aur Navratna CPSE hai. [Q157]\n• Miniratna Category-I status ke liye CPSE ko prescribed profitability/net-worth conditions fulfil karni hoti hain. Is status ke saath\nBoard ko certain investment decisions mein greater autonomy milti hai; traditional limit ₹500 crore tak ke projects ke context mein\npoochhi gayi hai. [Q158]\n• September 2006 mein BSNL, BEML, Hindustan Latex, EPIL, RINL aur GRSE ko additional Miniratna status mila; Indian Telephone\nIndustries us six-unit list mein nahi thi. December 2025 tak RINL Navratna status mein tha. [Q159]\n• Policy-year mapping: Information Technology Policy—2000; Hotel Policy—2006; Industrial and Investment Promotion Policy—2010;\nMineral Policy—2011. [Q160]\n• Udyami Helpline August 2010 mein entrepreneurs ko information aur guidance support dene ke liye launch ki gayi. [Q161]\n• Madhya Pradesh Financial Corporation SMEs ko state-level financial assistance provide karta hai. Headquarters Indore mein hai\naur institution ki roots 1955 ke Madhya Bharat Financial Corporation se judi hain. [Q162]\n• Uttar Pradesh mein small aur medium enterprises ko long-term loans U.P. Small Industries Corporation, U.P. Industrial\nDevelopment Corporation aur U.P. Financial Corporation jaise institutions provide karte hain. [Q163]\n• Uttar Pradesh Financial Corporation 1954 mein industrial development ke liye establish hua; tourism-related industry ko chhodkar\neligible industrial activities ko finance support deta tha. [Q164]\n• Udyog Bandhu Uttar Pradesh mein industrial units/investors ko facilitation aur problem-resolution support provide karne wali\ninitiative/institutional mechanism hai. [Q165]\n• Uttar Pradesh mein industrial development ke context mein western region comparatively sabse developed maana jaata hai. [Q166]\n• Board for Industrial and Financial Reconstruction (BIFR) sick industrial companies ke revival/rehabilitation framework se associated\nbody thi; iska operational phase 1987 se start hua. [Q167]\n• Mega Food Park Scheme September 2008 mein food-processing industry ke infrastructure ko improve karne ke objective se\napprove hui. [Q168]\n• Mega Food Parks ka focus food-processing industry ke liye modern infrastructure dena aur perishables ki processing badha kar\nwastage reduce karna hai. [Q169]\n• Foreign trade aur industrial development GDP growth ko exports, imports of technology/capital goods, market expansion aur\nproductivity channels ke through support karte hain. [Q170]\n• Business enterprise ka objective sirf profit nahi hota; economic efficiency ke saath employment, consumer service aur broader\nsocial objectives bhi important ho sakte hain. [Q171]\n• Global liberalisation ke environment mein firms ko competitiveness maintain karne ke liye process re-engineering, technology\nupgradation aur productivity improvement par focus karna padta hai. [Q172]\n• Sole proprietorship mein proprietor ki liability unlimited hoti hai, jabki partnership contractual relationship par based business form\nhai. [Q173]\n• Capital expenditure (CapEx) physical assets ki acquisition, upgradation aur major maintenance par kiya gaya long-term expenditure\nhai; technology/equipment acquisition bhi capital investment ka part ho sakta hai. [Q174]\n• Venture capital new/high-growth enterprises ko long-term risk capital provide karta hai aur business expansion/innovation ko\nfinance karne ka important source hai. [Q175]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Maharatna / Navratna Status Changes Over Time\n▸ Maharatna/Navratna company status aur total count time ke saath change hote hain; dated list aur current list alag ho\nsakti hain.\n[Q147, Q148, Q149, Q150, Q151, Q152, Q153, Q154, Q155, Q156, Q157, Q158, Q159]\n\n\n\n\n10. Industrial Policy Resolutions, Licensing, Liberalisation & FMCG\n\n• Independent India ki first Industrial Policy 6 April 1948 ko Industry Minister Dr. Syama Prasad Mookerjee ne announce ki; isne\nmixed aur controlled economy ke industrial framework ki foundation rakhi. [Q176]\n• Industrial Policy Resolution 1956 independent India ki second major industrial policy resolution thi aur rapid industrialisation, public-\nsector role aur mixed-economy framework ko strengthen karti thi. [Q177]\n• Industrial Policy Resolution 1956 heavy industries mein State role ko strengthen karne aur Second Five Year Plan framework ko\nsupport karne ke context mein adopt hua; industries ko three schedules/categories mein classify kiya gaya. [Q178]\n• Industrial licensing ka statutory base Industries (Development and Regulation) Act, 1951 tha. New Industrial Policy, 1991 ke baad\nlicensing substantially liberalize hui; current framework mein compulsory licensing sirf a few specified industry groups tak limited hai.\n[Q179]\n• 1991 economic reforms mein sabse major structural changes Industrial Policy ke through aaye, kyunki liberalisation, privatisation\naur globalisation ke principles licensing, public sector, foreign investment aur technology policy se directly linked the. [Q180]\n• ISO 9001/9002 quality-management standards se linked hain. 1991 Industrial Policy ne industrial licensing ko substantially\nliberalise kiya. [Q181]\n• 1991 Industrial Policy ke time compulsory licensing 18 industries tak limited ki gayi thi; April 1993 mein ye list further reduce hokar\n15 industries reh gayi. [Q182]\n• Earlier licensing regime mein 15 industries licensing ke under thi; current DPIIT framework mein compulsory licensing four specified\nindustry groups tak limited hai. [Q183]","• India mein Liberalisation-Privatisation-Globalisation (LPG) based New Economic Policy 1991 mein P. V. Narasimha Rao\nGovernment ke period mein announce hui. [Q184]\n• 1991 Industrial Policy par major criticisms mein agriculture ko insufficient attention, implementation ambiguity aur social/regional\nconcerns shamil rahe. [Q185]\n• White goods generally durable consumer appliances ko refer karte hain—jaise refrigerator, washing machine, air-conditioner etc.;\nconspicuous consumption context mein bhi ye category poochhi ja sakti hai. [Q186]\n• Indian Petrochemicals Corporation Ltd. (IPCL) ka Reliance Industries Group mein merger ho chuka hai, aur MTNL ab NYSE par\nlisted nahi hai. December 2025 data mein Reliance Industries Ltd. market capitalization aur revenue ke basis par India ki largest\nprivate company thi. [Q187]\n• FMCG (Fast-Moving Consumer Goods) high-turnover, relatively low-unit-value non-durable packaged goods hote hain. Cosmetics,\ndairy aur bakery products examples hain; vehicles durable goods hain, FMCG nahi. [Q188]\n\n\n11. Privatisation, Disinvestment & National Investment Fund\n\n• Full privatisation mein enterprise ka ownership aur management control public sector se private sector ko transfer ho jaata hai.\n[Q189]\n• Disinvestment ke objectives mein government ka financial burden reduce karna, public finances improve karna, market\ndiscipline/competition badhana aur enterprise efficiency enhance karna shamil hain. [Q190]\n• National Investment Fund (NIF) November 2005 mein establish hua; profitable CPSEs mein Central Government ki minority\nshareholding ke disinvestment proceeds ko is fund framework se link kiya gaya, jo Consolidated Fund se separate structure mein\nmaintained tha. [Q191]\n• Liberalisation ka core idea unnecessary controls aur restrictions ko reduce karna hai; disinvestment wider economic-reform\nframework ka important component raha. [Q192]\n• 1991 reforms ke baad public-sector reserved role reduce hua aur government ne CPSEs mein disinvestment programme\nstart/expand kiya. [Q193]\n• Public-sector disinvestment India mein 1991-92 financial year se start hua. [Q194]\nEXAM TRAP — Disinvestment: Policy Logic vs Historical Year\n▸ Disinvestment objectives, National Investment Fund aur programme start-year alag facts hain; policy role aur dates ek-\ndoosre se distinct hain.\n[Q189, Q190, Q191, Q192, Q193, Q194]\n\n\n\n\n12. Steel, Aluminium, Cement, Industrial Centres & Organisation-Ministry Mapping\n\n• Major steel-plant collaborations: Bhilai—USSR; Rourkela—West Germany; Durgapur—United Kingdom; Bokaro—USSR. Ye\nmapping Indian public-sector steel development ke liye important hai. [Q195]\n• Bhilai Steel Plant Soviet Union/Russia ke collaboration se establish hua. [Q196]\n• Bhilai Steel Plant public-sector enterprise hai. [Q197]\n• Rourkela Steel Plant Germany ke collaboration se establish hua. [Q198]\n• Rourkela Steel Plant West Germany ke collaboration se develop hua tha, Soviet collaboration se nahi. Maharashtra Elektrosmelt\nLimited ko 2011 mein SAIL ke saath merge kiya gaya. [Q199]\n• Bokaro Steel Plant erstwhile Soviet Union (USSR) ke collaboration se establish hua. [Q200]\n• Bokaro Steel Plant erstwhile Soviet Union (USSR) ke collaboration se establish hua tha; USA ke collaboration se nahi. [Q201]\n• Integrated steel-plant State mapping: Bhilai—Chhattisgarh; Bokaro—Jharkhand; Durgapur—West Bengal; Rourkela—Odisha.\n[Q202]\n• Muri aluminium/alumina industrial centre Jharkhand mein located hai, Chhattisgarh mein nahi. [Q203]\n• Jajpur (Odisha) ka integrated iron and steel plant Neelachal Ispat Nigam Limited se associated tha, Steel Authority of India se nahi;\nlater Tata-group associate ne ise acquire kiya. [Q204]\n• Indian Diamond Institute (IDI) Surat mein located hai. Institute gems aur jewellery manufacturing mein technical skills, quality,\ndesign aur global competitiveness improve karne par focus karta hai. [Q205]\n• Cement-industry centre mapping: Sikka (Gujarat)—1; Katni (Madhya Pradesh)—2; Churk (Uttar Pradesh)—3; Tirunelveli (Tamil\nNadu)—4. [Q206]\n• Industrial centre-industry association important hai; raw material, energy, labour, market aur transport jaise factors industrial\nlocation ko strongly influence karte hain. [Q207]\n• Aonla—fertilizer, Modinagar—rubber, Barabanki—poly fibre aur Kanpur—explosives important centre-industry pairs hain. [Q208]\n• Coimbatore—cotton textiles, Rourkela—iron & steel, Kapurthala—railway coach aur Barauni—oil refining important industrial pairs\nhain. [Q209]\n• Tuticorin—pearl fishing, Pune—automobiles, Marmagao—shipbuilding aur Pinjore—engineering goods important centre-industry\nassociations hain. [Q210]\n• Industrial-resource centre mapping: Singrauli (Madhya Pradesh)—coal; Kajarhat (Uttar Pradesh)—cement; Koyali (Gujarat)—oil;\nAnand (Gujarat)—milk. [Q211]","• Tronica City Ghaziabad district, Uttar Pradesh mein located hai; Noida mein nahi. [Q212]\n• National Automotive Board Ministry of Heavy Industries ke under aata hai, Ministry of Commerce & Industry ke under nahi; Coir\nBoard Ministry of MSME ke under hai. [Q213]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Steel Plants — Partner Country & Location\n▸ Steel-plant mapping: Bhilai—USSR/Chhattisgarh; Rourkela—West Germany/Odisha; Bokaro—USSR/Jharkhand;\nDurgapur—United Kingdom/West Bengal.\n[Q195, Q196, Q197, Q198, Q199, Q200, Q201, Q202]\n\n\n\n\n13. National Renewal Fund, Golden Handshake & Industrial Relations\n\n• National Renewal Fund (NRF) February 1992 mein worker-protection framework ke roop mein establish hua. VRS, technology\nupgradation aur sick-unit closure se affected workers ke retraining, rehabilitation aur compensation par focus tha. [Q214]\n• Technology upgradation ya sick-unit closure se affected workers ke interests protect karna, retraining aur rehabilitation provide\nkarna NRF ka key purpose tha. [Q215]\n• 1991 reform period mein surplus public-sector employees ke liye incentive-based Voluntary Retirement Scheme (VRS) ko promote\nkiya gaya; ise commonly 'Golden Handshake' kaha gaya. [Q216]\n• National Renewal Fund ka major objective labour retraining tha. [Q217]\n• National Renewal Fund ka objective industrial restructuring aur technological change se affected workers ko social-security\nprotection, retraining aur rehabilitation support dena tha. Fund February 1992 mein establish hua. [Q218]\n• Golden Handshake voluntary retirement (VRS) se related concept hai. [Q219]\n• Golden Handshake Scheme voluntary retirement se related hai. [Q220]\n• South Korean company POSCO ne Odisha mein Paradip ke near major steel-project investment propose kiya tha. [Q221]\n• India Asia ka first country tha jisne Export Processing Zone model adopt kiya; Asia ka first EPZ 1965 mein Kandla, Gujarat mein\nestablish hua. Objective export production ko internationally competitive environment provide karna tha. [Q222]\n• Industrial development ke major constraints mein low savings-investment, weak infrastructure aur low purchasing power shamil\nrahe hain; ye demand aur productive capacity dono ko limit kar sakte hain. [Q223]\n• Labour-intensive industries mein capital ke comparison mein labour input ka relative importance zyada hota hai. Hotels,\nrestaurants, hospitality aur agriculture jaise sectors labour-intensive examples ho sakte hain. [Q224]\n• Industrial relations ke main stakeholders workers/unions, management/employer organisations aur government hote hain;\nconsumers ko direct co-partner nahi maana jaata. [Q225]\n\nEXAM TRAP — National Renewal Fund vs Golden Handshake\n▸ National Renewal Fund worker retraining/rehabilitation aur restructuring support se related tha; Golden Handshake voluntary retirement\n(VRS) se related hai.\n[Q214, Q215, Q216, Q217, Q218, Q219, Q220]\n\n\n\n\n14. Sugar, Cement, Textiles, Aluminium, Petrochemicals & Industrial Location\n\n• 2023-24 (P) sugar production data mein Maharashtra leading State tha. [Q226]\n• Sugar industry ka early concentration Uttar Pradesh aur Bihar mein tha. 1903 mein Pratappur, Deoria (UP) mein early modern\nsugar mill establish hui; UP ka sugarcane yield Maharashtra/Karnataka jaise States se comparatively lower raha hai. [Q227]\n• Sugarcane area ke context mein Uttar Pradesh leading State hai; Agricultural Statistics at a Glance 2024 ke 2024-25 data mein\ntotal sugarcane area ka around 49.92% UP mein tha. [Q228]\n• Sugar decontrol par committee C. Rangarajan ki chairmanship mein constitute hui thi; us waqt woh Prime Minister's Economic\nAdvisory Council ke Chairman the. [Q229]\n• Katni, Madhya Pradesh cement industry ke liye known hai; paper-industry centre ke roop mein identify nahi kiya jaata. [Q230]\n• Cement plants generally limestone sources ke near locate hote hain. 2024-25 context mein industry ka large share Rajasthan,\nAndhra Pradesh, Telangana, Karnataka, Madhya Pradesh, Gujarat, Tamil Nadu, Maharashtra, Uttar Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Odisha,\nMeghalaya aur West Bengal jaise States mein concentrated tha. [Q231]\n• Industrial-centre mapping: Titagarh—jute goods; Bengaluru—silk textiles; Ludhiana—woollen textiles; Bhadohi—woollen carpets.\n[Q232]\n• HINDALCO ka Renukoot plant Uttar Pradesh mein Birla Group se associated hai. Location ka major factor abundant power\navailability tha; surrounding power projects ki wajah se region ko 'energy capital' bhi kaha gaya. [Q233]\n• Madras Aluminium Company (MALCO) Mettur, Salem district (Tamil Nadu) mein located thi, Chennai mein nahi. Indian Aluminium\nCompany (INDAL) later Hindalco ka part bani. [Q234]\n• Gujarat petrochemical industry ka major State hai. Khambhat aur Ankleshwar important oil fields hain; feedstock availability ne\nAnkleshwar mein petrochemical complex ke development ko support kiya. [Q235]\n• Coastal Gujarat ke Porbandar, Dwarka, Bhavnagar, Vadodara, Jamnagar aur Surat jaise cities textiles, cement, fertilizers,\nchemicals aur petrochemicals ke important industrial centres hain. [Q236]","• Participation in management ka matlab workers ko enterprise ke policy/management decisions mein representation dena hai; trade-\nunion representatives ya elected workers ko management board mein place diya ja sakta hai. [Q237]\n• Ranchi, Jharkhand ki capital, Chota Nagpur Plateau par located hai. [Q238]\n• Dewas, Madhya Pradesh ek important industrial city hai aur currency-note printing se famous hai. [Q239]\n• Industry capital-intensive hai ya labour-intensive—ye mainly capital investment aur labour input ke relative proportion se determine\nhota hai. [Q240]\n\n\n15. Industrial History, Business Groups, MNCs & Automobile Headquarters\n\n• Industrial firsts: first machine-made paper 1870 Calcutta; early cotton-mill attempt 1818 Calcutta unsuccessful raha; successful\ncotton mill 1854 Bombay ke near establish hui; first cement factory 1904 Madras ke near establish hui. [Q241]\n• Early industrial chronology: Bombay cotton textile mill (1854) → Rishra jute mill (1855) → steel production ka development →\nIndian-owned sugar industry ka expansion. [Q242]\n• VSNL ke disinvestment ke baad company Tata Group ke control mein aayi. [Q243]\n• Samsung aur LG multinational companies hain. Hindustan Unilever Limited India-headquartered listed consumer-goods company\nhai aur global Unilever group ki Indian subsidiary hai. [Q244]\n• Automobile-HQ mapping mein Renault—France aur Volkswagen—Germany correct pairs hain; BMW—USA aur Daimler—Sweden\nincorrect pairs hain. BMW ka headquarters Munich, Germany mein hai. [Q245]\n\n\n16. Khadi, Textile Committees, RIICO, State Finance & Industrialists\n\n• All India Khadi and Village Industries Board 1953 mein First Five Year Plan ke dauran establish hua; 1957 se iska role statutory\nKhadi and Village Industries Commission (KVIC) ne take over kiya. [Q246]\n• Khadi and Village Industries Commission (KVIC) ka headquarters Mumbai mein hai. 1957 mein constituted statutory body hai aur\nMinistry of Micro, Small and Medium Enterprises ke under function karti hai. [Q247]\n• Tarapore Committee rupee ki capital-account convertibility par recommendations ke liye constituted hui thi; customer service ya\nindustrial sickness committee nahi thi. [Q248]\n• Handloom sector development ke liye 1995 mein Mira Seth ki chairmanship mein 12-member committee bani. Committee ne ₹500\ncrore ka National Handloom Fund establish karne ki recommendation ki. [Q249]\n• Textile-industry reforms ke liye Sathyam Committee July 1998 mein constitute hui; report August 1999 mein submit hui aur National\nTextile Policy 2000 par is committee ki recommendations ka influence tha. [Q250]\n• Unorganised/informal sector enterprises ko support karne ke liye Central Government ne September 2004 mein Arjun Sengupta ki\nleadership mein National Commission constitute kiya. [Q251]\n• Rajasthan State Industrial and Mineral Development Corporation (RSIMDC) 28 March 1969 ko Companies Act, 1956 ke under\nindustrial aur mineral development accelerate karne ke liye establish hua. [Q252]\n• RIICO ne industrial units/investment cooperation ke liye Japan External Trade Organization (JETRO) ke saath MoU sign kiya tha.\n[Q253]\n• State Financial Corporations State-level institutions hain jo SMEs ko term loans, equity/debenture subscription, guarantees, bill\ndiscounting aur other financial assistance provide karte hain. [Q254]\n• Industry/brand-person mapping: Reliance—Mukesh Ambani; Airtel—Sunil Bharti Mittal; Nano—Ratan Tata; Wipro—Azim Premji.\nWipro ko R. Krishnamurthy se pair karna incorrect hai. [Q255]\n\n\n17. e-Biz, E-Commerce, Information Technology & Software\n\n• e-Biz portal 2013 se government-business services ke single-window platform ke roop mein develop hua. Objective online G2B\nservices access badhakar business environment improve karna tha. [Q256]\n• E-Commerce ka basic meaning internet ke through business transactions karna hai. [Q257]\n• E-commerce internet ke through goods/services ka exchange aur money/data transmission hai. Iske advantages mein global reach\naur relatively low transaction/operating cost shamil hain. [Q258]\n• M-Junction 2001 mein Tata Steel aur SAIL ke 50:50 joint venture ke roop mein establish hui. FY2020 context mein transaction\nvalue around 14.7 billion report ki gayi thi. [Q259]\n• India mein 1990s se information technology ka rapid development hua. Software/services base strong raha, lekin hardware base ko\nequally strong nahi maana ja sakta. [Q260]\n• Forbes January 2025 data mein TCS India's most valuable software company rahi. [Q261]\n\n\n18. Automobile, Pharmaceutical Sector & District Mineral Foundations\n\n• Automobile sector Indian economy ka key pillar hai. 2021-end context mein sector se around 5.3 crore direct aur indirect\nemployment linked bataya gaya; liberalisation aur policy support se capacity expansion bhi hua. [Q262]","• India vaccine production aur generic medicines mein major global supplier hai. Economic Survey 2022-23 context mein pharma\nproduction value ke basis par 14th aur volume ke basis par 3rd rank; generic medicines ke global volume supply mein around 20%\nshare bataya gaya. [Q263]\n• MMDR Act, 2015 amendment ke through mining-affected districts mein District Mineral Foundations (DMFs) establish karne ka\nprovision kiya gaya. [Q264]\n\n\n                              Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · Industrial Sector Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0453","t":"06 Tertiary Sector","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["TERTIARY SECTOR — FULL MIND MAP\n                                  Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\nTopics in this chapter:\n 1. Primary, Secondary & Tertiary Sectors — Classification\n 2. Service Sector — India ka Largest Contributor\n 3. Service Sub-sectors — Growth, Share & Employment\n 4. Development ke saath Tertiary Share ka Trend\n 5. State/UT-wise Service-Sector Share\n 6. Economic Survey 2022-23 — Services & E-commerce\n\n\n 1. Primary, Secondary & Tertiary Sectors — Classification\n\n• Tertiary sector ko service sector/service industry bhi kehte hain. Isme trade, hotels, transport, communication, banking, insurance,\n real estate, public administration, defence, education, health, entertainment aur other personal services aate hain. Primary sector\n mein agriculture, forestry, fishing, mining & quarrying; secondary sector mein manufacturing, construction, electricity, gas, water\n supply aur utilities aate hain. [Q1, Q2, Q6]\n• Marketing tertiary-sector activity hai, jabki agriculture primary-sector activity hai. [Q3, Q4]\n• Sector classification mein agricultural products ka storage tertiary sector; dairy farming/milk production primary sector; mineral\n exploration mining & quarrying ke under primary sector; aur cloth weaving secondary sector mein aata hai. [Q5]\n\n\n 2. Service Sector — India ka Largest Contributor\n\n• Service sector India ka largest sector hai. First Advance Estimates 2025-26 mein monetary GVA share constant (2011-12) prices\n par 55.83% aur current prices par 56.4% diya gaya hai. [Q7, Q13, Q15, Q16, Q17]\n• Economic Survey 2023-24 mein FY 2024 ke current-price GVA shares Agriculture 17.7%, Industry 27.6% aur Services 54.7% the;\n descending order Services > Industry > Agriculture raha. [Q10, Q14]\n• 2009-10 mein service-sector GDP share 57.09% tha, isliye 25% wala figure us period ke data se match nahi karta. 2024-25 mein\n GDP share 49.9% (World Bank) aur 2025-26 mein current-price monetary GVA share 56.40% diya gaya hai. [Q20]\n• 2000-01 ke constant-price data mein descending order Agriculture/forestry/fishing > Trade/hotels/transport/communication >\n Manufacturing > Finance/insurance/real estate/business services tha. Older series sector-wise GDP ratios use karti thi; current\n framework GVA ratios par focus karta hai. [Q9]\n\n EXAM TRAP — Q8 — 52.8% vs Rs 52.8 Lakh Crore\n ▸ 2016-17 mein current-price GVA Rs 138.4 lakh crore tha; tertiary-sector share 52.8% hone par amount lagbhag Rs 73.08 lakh crore banta\n hai.\n ▸ Q8 mein Rs 52.8 lakh crore aur 52.8% ko confuse mat karo; percentage ko GVA amount par apply karne se Rs 73.08 lakh crore ke aas-\n paas value milti hai.\n [Q8]\n\n\n\n\n EXAM TRAP — Historical vs Updated Service-Sector Data\n ▸ Q9, Q11, Q12, Q18, Q20, Q21, Q23 aur Q25 year-specific historical facts hain; later/current figures naturally different ho sakte hain.\n ▸ Service-sector percentages, ranks aur segment shares time ke saath change hote hain; har figure ko uske year ke saath padho.\n [Q9, Q11, Q12, Q18, Q20, Q21, Q23, Q25]\n\n\n\n\n 3. Service Sub-sectors — Growth, Share & Employment\n\n• 2006-2010 mein service sub-sectors mein Communication ki growth rate sabse zyada thi. [Q11]\n• 2006 ke data mein service-sector GDP ka sabse bada segment Trade, Hotels, Transport & Communication tha. [Q12]\n• 2012-13 mein services ka share gross domestic production mein lagbhag 57% aur gross employment mein around 28% tha.\n Economic Review 2025-26 mein total employment share 30% diya gaya hai. [Q18]\n• 2001-2012 ke period mein India ke service-sector growth rate ka world rank Second diya gaya tha. [Q21]\n• 2001-05 ke period mein Services woh sector tha jiska growth rate lagataar increase hua. [Q23]\n• 2011-12 se 2015-16 ke period mein Indian economic growth mein service sector ka contribution 69.0% diya gaya hai. [Q25]\n\n\n 4. Development ke saath Tertiary Share ka Trend","• 1980 ke baad tertiary sector ka GDP share lagataar badhne ki tendency dikhata hai; development ke saath service-sector share\ngenerally increase hota hai. [Q22]\n• Jaise-jaise economy develop karti hai, tertiary/service sector ka GDP contribution badhta hai. Illustrative share lagbhag 30% (1950-\n51), 38-40% (1980-81) aur 49.9% (2024-25) raha; current-price monetary GVA share 2021-22 se 2025-26 tak 52.2%, 54.3%, 54.4%,\n55% aur 56.4% diya gaya hai. [Q24]\n\n\n5. State/UT-wise Service-Sector Share\n\n• 2008-09 mein Delhi ka service-sector contribution sabse zyada tha. FY 2023-24 mein Chandigarh 88.8% aur Delhi 84.1% ke saath\nservice-sector share mein top par the; Karnataka 62.3%, Kerala 61.5%, Telangana 60.3%, Bihar 57.9% aur Maharashtra 55.6%\nrahe. [Q19]\n\n\n6. Economic Survey 2022-23 — Services & E-commerce\n\n• Economic Survey 2022-23 mein fashion, grocery aur general merchandise ke Indian e-commerce market ka nearly two-thirds share\n2027 tak hone ka projection tha, 2030 tak nahi. [Q26]\n\nEXAM TRAP — 2027 vs 2030\n▸ Economic Survey 2022-23 projection ke hisab se fashion + grocery + general merchandise 2027 tak Indian e-commerce market ka nearly\ntwo-thirds share capture kar sakte hain.\n▸ 2030 wali date incorrect hai; Economic Survey 2022-23 projection 2027 tak fashion + grocery + general merchandise ke nearly two-thirds\ne-commerce share ki baat karti hai.\n[Q26]\n\n\n\n\n                                Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · Tertiary Sector Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0454","t":"07 Fiscal Policy & Revenue","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["FISCAL POLICY & REVENUE — FULL MIND MAP\n                                   Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\nTopics in this chapter:\n 1. Union Budget — Constitutional Framework & Recent Priorities\n 2. GST — Structure, Council, Input Tax Credit & Administration\n 3. Fiscal Policy — Authority, Objectives, Instruments & Stimulus\n 4. Budget Preparation, Economic Survey & Budgeting Methods\n 5. Public Receipts & Expenditure — Revenue/Capital Classification\n 6. Fiscal Deficit — Control Measures & Historical Targets\n 7. Deficit Concepts — Fiscal, Revenue, Primary & Effective Revenue\n 8. Government Borrowing, Deficit Financing & Subsidies\n 9. Parliamentary Budget Procedure, Motions & Budgetary Control\n 10. FRBM Act & Fiscal Responsibility Rules\n 11. Disinvestment Targets & Railway Budget\n 12. Direct Tax Policy, DTC, Equalisation Levy, Capital Gains & Tax-GDP\n 13. Union Tax Revenue, Tax Sharing & Centre-State Tax Powers\n 14. MAT, MODVAT, CENVAT, VAT & Service Tax\n 15. Direct vs Indirect Taxes, Miscellaneous Taxes & Disclosure Schemes\n 16. Finance Commission — Constitutional Role, 15th & 16th Commissions\n 17. Finance Commissions — 14th to 1st & Tax Devolution\n 18. Tax Reform Committees, Tax Relief, PAN & Regional Tax Holidays\n 19. Local Finance, Fiscal Stimulus & NHAI Financing\n\n\n 1. Union Budget — Constitutional Framework & Recent Priorities\n\n• Constitution ke Article 112(1) ke under President har financial year ke estimated receipts aur expenditure ka statement Parliament ke dono\n Houses ke saamne rakhwata hai; ise Annual Financial Statement kehte hain. [Q1]\n• Union Budget ke saath Macro Economic Framework Statement Parliament mein FRBM Act, 2003 ke Section 3(5) ke under present kiya\n jaata hai. [Q2]\n• India mein Gender Budgeting Union Budget 2005-06 se introduce hui. Iska aim policies aur programmes mein gender perspective include\n karke women ko development benefits mein equitable share dena hai. [Q3]\n• Union Budget 2024-25 ki nine 'Viksit Bharat' priorities opportunities create karne par focused thi. 'Sustainable Development' separate\n named priority nahi thi; agriculture productivity/resilience, employment & skilling, inclusive HR development/social justice, manufacturing &\n services etc. priorities mein shamil the. [Q4]\n• Union Budget 2017-18 ke ten main themes mein 'Export Performance' separate theme nahi tha. Budget 2026-27 1 February 2026 ko\n present hua aur Kartavya Bhavan se prepared first Budget ke roop mein three-duty framework par based tha. [Q5]\n• Union Budget 2023-24 ki seven priorities ko 'Saptarishi' kaha gaya: Inclusive Development, Reaching the Last Mile, Infrastructure &\n Investment, Unleashing the Potential, Green Growth, Youth Power aur Financial Sector. [Q6]\n• Union Budget 2022-23 ki four priorities PM GatiShakti, Inclusive Development, Productivity Enhancement & Investment/Sunrise\n Opportunities/Energy Transition/Climate Action, aur Financing of Investments thi; disinvestment separate priority nahi tha. [Q7]\n• Union Budget 2017-18 ka TEC agenda 'Transform, Energise and Clean India' tha; 'Educate India' TEC ka part nahi tha. [Q8]\n• Union Budget 2021-22 mein Agriculture Infrastructure and Development Cess (AIDC) kuch selected items par propose hua; structure aisa\n rakha gaya ki most items par consumer burden unnecessarily na badhe. [Q9]\n• Economic Survey 2022-23 ne note kiya ki FY 2023 Budget highly uncertain macroeconomic environment mein present hua; baad ke\n geopolitical conflicts ne supply bottlenecks aur fuel-food-essential commodity prices par pressure badhaya. [Q10]\n• Economic Survey 2015-16 ne Indian economy ke socialism se limited marketism ki taraf move, lekin inefficient firms/activities ke exit\n mechanism ki weakness ko 'Chakravyuha Challenge' kaha. [Q11]\n• Union Budget 2023-24 mein States ko fiscal deficit up to 3.5% of GSDP allow tha, jisme 0.5% power-sector reforms se linked tha. Total\n expenditure estimate roughly Rs 45 lakh crore tha. [Q12]\n• Revised Estimates 2017-18 mein fiscal deficit 3.5% of GDP tha, jabki Budget Estimates 2017-18 mein 3.2% target tha. [Q13]\n• Fiscal deficit/GDP trend: 2017-18 3.5%, 2018-19 3.4%, 2019-20 4.6%, 2020-21 9.2%, 2021-22 6.7%, 2022-23 6.5%, 2023-24 5.5%, 2024-\n 25 4.8%, 2025-26 RE 4.4% aur 2026-27 BE 4.3%. [Q14]\n\n EXAM TRAP — Budget Data is Year-Specific\n ▸ Budget priorities, fiscal targets aur scheme themes year-specific hote hain; newer Budget/Economic Survey values purane years se differ\n kar sakte hain.\n [Q4, Q5, Q6, Q7, Q8, Q9, Q10, Q12, Q13, Q14]\n\n\n\n\n 2. GST — Structure, Council, Input Tax Credit & Administration\n\n• GST framework mein hulled cereal grains, cooked chicken eggs ke specified forms aur newspapers jaise essential items exempt category\n mein aa sakte hain; processed/canned fish aur processed shelled eggs par 5% GST category apply hoti hai. [Q15]\n• Central Government ne 2003 mein Vijay L. Kelkar ki chairmanship mein Task Force banaya; is Task Force ne Goods and Services Tax\n (GST) propose kiya. [Q16]\n• 101st Constitutional Amendment, 2016 se Article 246A add hua, jo Parliament aur State Legislatures dono ko GST par law banane ki\n concurrent power deta hai; inter-State supply par Parliament ki exclusive role bhi hai. [Q17]\n• GST 1 July 2017 se nationwide effective hua aur ye indirect tax hai. [Q18]","• GST implementation ke baad State-level VAT ka large part subsume/abolish hua; alcohol for human consumption jaise excluded items par\nState VAT framework continue karta hai. [Q19]\n• Constitution (101st Amendment) Act, 2016 ne GST ke constitutional provisions create kiye; GST 1 July 2017 se implement hua.\nAmendment Bill initially 122nd Constitutional Amendment Bill ke roop mein introduce hua tha. [Q20]\n• 101st Constitutional Amendment Act, 2016 ne GST regime implement karne ke liye required constitutional framework provide kiya. [Q21]\n• GST ko pehle April 2012 se introduce karne ka proposal tha; actual nationwide implementation 1 July 2017 se hua. [Q22]\n• GST ek destination-based indirect tax hai jo goods aur services ki supply par lagta hai aur India ko unified common market banane ka\nframework provide karta hai. [Q23]\n• 101st Amendment se Article 279A add hua aur GST Council ka provision bana. GST Council ka Chairperson Union Finance Minister hota\nhai; Union Minister of State for Finance/Revenue bhi member hota hai. [Q24]\n• GST Council ka Chairperson Union Finance Minister hota hai. [Q25]\n• GST ne multiple Central aur State indirect taxes ko subsume kiya, jisme Central Excise Duty, Additional Excise Duties, Service Tax aur\nseveral State VAT/sales-tax type levies shamil the. [Q26]\n• GST ke outside major items mein alcohol for human consumption aur electricity shamil hain; petroleum products ko GST levy ke liye\nCouncil recommendation framework ke under rakha gaya. Ghee GST ambit mein taxable item hai. [Q27]\n• October 2020 mein gross GST collection Rs 1 lakh crore se upar tha; January 2026 mein monthly gross GST collection Rs 1.93 lakh crore\nse upar pahucha. [Q28]\n• Revenue Neutral Rate (RNR) ka concept aise GST rate structure ko refer karta hai jisse tax-system change ke baad overall government\nrevenue broadly neutral rahe. [Q29]\n• Input Tax Credit (ITC) GST ka core mechanism hai jo cascading 'tax on tax' ko reduce karta hai. Purchase/input par paid eligible GST ko\noutput/sales GST liability se adjust kiya ja sakta hai. [Q30]\n• Input Tax Credit registered person ke eligible inward supplies par paid GST ka credit hai. Taxable aur zero-rated supplies ITC eligibility\nmein aa sakti hain; exempt/non-taxable supplies par general ITC restrictions apply hoti hain. [Q31]\n• Project Saksham new indirect-tax network/system integration se related project tha; Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs ne 28\nSeptember 2016 ko approval diya. [Q32]\n\nEXAM TRAP — GST — Constitutional/Administrative Details\n▸ GST launch date, Council composition, exemptions, RNR, ITC aur Project SAKSHAM alag factual points hain; inhe ek hi period/value\nsamajhkar merge na karein.\n[Q15, Q16, Q17, Q18, Q19, Q20, Q21, Q22, Q23, Q24, Q25, Q26, Q27, Q28, Q29, Q30, Q31, Q32]\n\n\n\n\n3. Fiscal Policy — Authority, Objectives, Instruments & Stimulus\n\n• India mein fiscal policy primarily Ministry of Finance frame karta hai; monetary policy RBI ke Monetary Policy Committee ke through decide\nhoti hai. Finance Commission Centre aur States ke beech divisible tax revenue/resources sharing recommend karta hai. [Q33]\n• Long Term Fiscal Policy 1985-86 Budget Speech mein then Finance Minister V. P. Singh ne announce ki thi. [Q34]\n• Government expenditure control/management Ministry of Finance ke under aata hai. Ministry ke six departments hain: Economic Affairs,\nExpenditure, Revenue, Financial Services, DIPAM aur Public Enterprises. [Q35]\n• 'Department of Banking' naam se separate department Ministry of Finance ke current six departments mein nahi hai; banking-related\nfunctions Department of Financial Services ke under aate hain. [Q36]\n• Central Government ke revenue expenditure mein interest payments ka burden kaafi bada component hai; high interest burden\ndevelopment/investment ke liye available fiscal space ko reduce kar sakta hai. [Q37]\n• Fiscal policy government ke revenue, expenditure, taxation, borrowing aur deficit decisions ka framework hai. Iske major aims\nincome/wealth distribution improve karna, employment support karna aur price/economic stability maintain karna hain; inter-State trade\nregulation iska core instrument nahi hai. [Q38]\n• Fiscal policy government finance se related hai aur government income, expenditure, public debt aur deficit financing policies ko cover karti\nhai. [Q39]\n• Fiscal policy ke major instruments taxation, public expenditure, public debt aur deficit financing hain. [Q40]\n• Taxation fiscal policy ka key instrument hai. [Q41]\n• Government taxation, borrowing aur public expenditure ki size/structure change karke economic activity ko influence karti hai. Public\nexpenditure, taxation aur deficit financing fiscal-policy tools hain; policy interest rate monetary-policy tool hai. [Q42]\n• J. M. Keynes ne depression se economy ko nikalne ke liye expansionary fiscal policy aur higher public expenditure par emphasis diya. Unki\nfamous book 'The General Theory of Employment, Interest and Money' 1936 mein publish hui. [Q43]\n• Fiscal/financial stimulus government ka expansionary action hota hai jisme recession ya economic slowdown ke dauran demand aur\neconomic activity boost karne ke liye spending, tax relief ya sector-specific incentives diye jaate hain. [Q44]\n• Recession ke dauran public projects par expenditure badhana aggregate demand aur employment support karne wala fiscal measure ho\nsakta hai. Recession generally real GDP contraction ke saath income, employment, production aur sales ko affect karti hai. [Q45]\n\n\n4. Budget Preparation, Economic Survey & Budgeting Methods\n\n• Budget formulation ke broad stages: drawing/disbursing inputs, departmental estimates, scrutiny-consolidation, Ministry of Finance review,\ndisputes settlement, final consolidation aur Cabinet approval. [Q46]\n• Union Budget preparation aur Parliament mein presentation ke coordination ki nodal responsibility Ministry of Finance ke Department of\nEconomic Affairs ke Budget Division ke paas hoti hai. [Q47]","• Economic Survey Ministry of Finance prepare karta hai aur Union Budget se pehle Parliament mein table hota hai; ye previous year ki\neconomic performance aur policy issues ka review deta hai. [Q48]\n• Annual Economic Survey/Review Ministry of Finance prepare karta hai. [Q49]\n• Economic Survey officially Ministry of Finance ke Department of Economic Affairs se publish hota hai aur economy ka annual\npolicy/performance review present karta hai. [Q50]\n• Economic Survey every year Department of Economic Affairs, Ministry of Finance se publish hota hai aur macroeconomic trends, sector\nperformance aur policy issues review karta hai. [Q51]\n• Publication-institution mapping: Report on Currency and Finance—RBI; National Accounts Statistics—NSO; Economic Survey—Ministry of\nFinance; Wholesale Price Index—Department for Promotion of Industry and Internal Trade/Ministry of Commerce & Industry framework.\n[Q52]\n• Budget government ki fiscal policy ka annual financial statement hai, jisme financial year 1 April-31 March ke estimated receipts aur\nexpenditure dikhaye jaate hain. [Q53]\n• Zero-Based Budgeting (ZBB) mein har activity/scheme ko previous allocation automatically continue karne ke bajay zero base se justify\nkiya jaata hai; expenditure se pehle necessity, utility aur productivity evaluate hoti hai. [Q54]\n• India mein Zero-Based Budgeting 1983 mein Department of Science & Technology mein start hui aur 1986-87 se ministries mein wider\nadoption hua. Har scheme ko fresh justification ke saath review kiya jaata hai. [Q55]\n• Budget 2023-24 allocations mein PM-JAY Rs 7,200 crore, Department of Health & Family Welfare Rs 86,175 crore, Ministry of Health &\nFamily Welfare Rs 89,155 crore aur Department of Health Research Rs 2,980 crore the. [Q56]\n• Union Budget 2011-12 mein Energy sector ko Rural Development, Social Services aur Transport se zyada allocation mila tha. [Q57]\n• Budget Estimates 2026-27 mein Transport Rs 5,98,520 crore, Rural Development Rs 2,73,108 crore, Energy Rs 1,09,029 crore aur Social\nWelfare Rs 62,362 crore allocate hua. [Q58]\n• Selected Union Budget allocations 2018-19 BE se 2025-26 BE tak increase hue: Defence 2.82 se 5.95 lakh crore, Agriculture & Allied 0.63\nse 1.63, Food Subsidy 1.69 se 2.28 aur Rural Development 1.39 se 2.73 lakh crore. [Q59]\n• Union Budget 2023-24 ke total expenditure breakup mein Interest Payments 20%, States' share in taxes/duties 18%, Defence 8% aur\nSubsidies 7% the. [Q60]\n\n\n5. Public Receipts & Expenditure — Revenue/Capital Classification\n\n• Government receipts do broad categories mein divide hote hain: Revenue Receipts aur Capital Receipts. Jo receipts government liability\ncreate karti hain ya financial assets reduce karti hain, unhe Capital Receipts kehte hain. [Q61]\n• Union Budget 2010-11 mein capital receipts revenue receipts se kam the; BE 2026-27 mein bhi revenue receipts Rs 35,33,150 crore\n(66.07%) aur capital receipts Rs 18,14,165 crore (33.93%) estimate hue. [Q62]\n• Income tax, public borrowing aur VAT/public taxes government revenue/receipts ke sources ho sakte hain; subsidy government\nexpenditure ka item hai. [Q63]\n• Corporation tax, PSU profits/dividends aur excise-type tax revenue account se linked hote hain; National Savings Certificates/small savings\naur World Bank loans capital receipts category mein aate hain. [Q64]\n• Revenue receipts mein tax revenue, interest receipts, government departments/PSUs ke profits-dividends shamil hote hain; small savings\ncapital receipts mein aati hain. [Q65]\n• Capital Budget government ke capital receipts aur capital expenditure ka account hai aur assets/liabilities mein changes ko capture karta\nhai. [Q66]\n• Old plan/non-plan framework mein subsidies, interest payments, defence, maintenance, education-health, pensions aur statutory transfers\njaise recurring items largely non-plan/revenue expenditure mein aate the. [Q67]\n• Old classification mein interest payments non-plan expenditure ka major item the; maintenance aur administrative expenses bhi non-plan\ncategory mein aate the. [Q68]\n• Interest payments old plan/non-plan classification mein non-plan expenditure ka important component the. [Q69]\n• 2017-18 se plan/non-plan expenditure classification abolish ho chuki hai; current Budget mein expenditure ko Revenue aur Capital\ncategories mein classify kiya jaata hai, jisme revenue expenditure larger component hai. [Q70]\n• Budget 2017-18 ke major reforms mein Budget presentation date ko 1 February karna, Railway Budget ko General Budget mein merge\nkarna aur Plan/Non-Plan expenditure classification end karna shamil tha. Centrally Sponsored Schemes ki sankhya badhana is reform\npackage ka part nahi tha. [Q71]\n• Railway Budget ko General/Union Budget ke saath 2017 se merge kar diya gaya. [Q72]\n• Railway Budget Union Budget mein Budget 2017-18 se merge hua. [Q73]\n• Budget deficit reduce karne ke liye revenue expenditure control aur revenue receipts increase karna helpful hota hai. New welfare spending\ndeficit badha sakti hai, jabki import duty cut revenue receipts ko reduce kar sakta hai. [Q74]\n• Fiscal deficit control ke common measures subsidy rationalisation, revenue expenditure restraint aur broader macro imbalances reduce\nkarna hain; uncontrolled public expenditure deficit ko badha sakta hai. [Q75]\n• Budget Estimates 2026-27 mein Union Government ke revenue expenditure ka largest item interest payments tha: around Rs 14,03,972\ncrore, roughly 3.57% of GDP. [Q76]\n• Union Budget ke revenue expenditure mein interest payment major/largest items mein se ek hota hai. [Q77]\n• Interest payment Revenue Expenditure ka item hai. BE 2026-27 mein iske liye Rs 14,03,972 crore allocate hua; 2025-26 BE/RE figures\naround Rs 12.76/12.74 lakh crore the. [Q78]\n• Union Government ke revenue account ka most important expenditure item interest payments hai. [Q79]\n• Union Government Budget mein interest payments historically sabse bade expenditure components mein rahe hain. [Q80]\n• 2008-10 period mein bhi aur BE 2026-27 mein bhi Central Government ke revenue/current expenditure ka major item interest payments\nraha; 2026-27 BE mein amount around Rs 14,03,972 crore tha. [Q81]","• 2015-16 mein old non-plan classification ke under interest payments largest item the. 2017-18 se plan/non-plan classification abolish ho\nchuki hai; BE 2026-27 mein bhi interest payments revenue expenditure ka largest component hain. [Q82]\n• Old non-plan expenditure classification mein interest payments sabse bada item the. [Q83]\nEXAM TRAP — Plan / Non-Plan Classification is Historical\n▸ Q67-Q83 mein historical Plan/Non-Plan classification aur older expenditure pattern use hua hai; 2017-18 se Plan/Non-Plan expenditure\nclassification end ho chuki hai.\n[Q67, Q68, Q69, Q70, Q71, Q72, Q73, Q74, Q75, Q76, Q77, Q78, Q79, Q80, Q81, Q82, Q83]\n\n\n\n\n6. Fiscal Deficit — Control Measures & Historical Targets\n\n• Fiscal deficit ko expenditure rationalisation, tax/revenue reforms aur institutional fiscal reforms se control kiya ja sakta hai; FDI promotion ya\nhigher-education privatisation direct fiscal-deficit control tools nahi hain. [Q84]\n• Budget Estimates 2026-27 mein fiscal deficit 4.3% of GDP estimate hua; 2025-26 BE aur RE dono mein 4.4% estimate tha. [Q85]\n• BE 2026-27 mein fiscal deficit 4.3% of GDP estimate hai; 2025-26 BE/RE figure 4.4% tha. Older 6.8% figure current estimate nahi hai.\n[Q86]\n• Union Budget 2024-25 mein fiscal deficit 4.9% of GDP estimate kiya gaya tha. [Q87]\n• Pandemic year 2020-21 mein Union fiscal deficit 9.2% of GDP tak pahucha; 2021-22 mein 6.7% raha. Revenue collections bhi recovery\nphase mein increase hui thi. [Q88]\n• FRBM framework ke fiscal-consolidation path mein Centre ka fiscal-deficit target around 3% of GDP tak lana tha; later escape clauses aur\nrevised fiscal paths ke karan timeline change hui. [Q89]\n• Union Budget 2000-01 mein fiscal deficit 5.1% of GDP budgeted tha; 2024-25 actual 4.8%, 2025-26 RE 4.4% aur 2026-27 BE 4.3%\nestimate hai. [Q90]\n• 2011-12 se 2014-15 ke four-year period mein fiscal-deficit/GDP ratio 2011-12 mein sabse zyada tha. 2017-18 se 2026-27 (BE) series mein\npeak 2020-21 mein 9.2% raha; 2021-22 mein 6.7%, 2022-23 mein 6.5%, 2023-24 mein 5.5%, 2024-25 mein 4.8%, 2025-26 (RE) mein 4.4%\naur 2026-27 (BE) mein 4.3% raha. [Q91]\n• 1990-91 mein fiscal deficit around 8.4% of GDP tha; Budget Estimate 2026-27 mein 4.3% estimate hai. [Q92]\n• Older context mein fiscal deficit around 5% of GNP/GDP ke aas-paas poocha gaya; current BE 2026-27 estimate 4.3% of GDP aur 2025-26\nRE 4.4% hai. [Q93]\n• FY 2024-25 actual fiscal deficit 4.8% of GDP raha; 2025-26 BE/RE mein 4.4% estimate hai. [Q94]\n• Union Budget 2009-10 mein fiscal deficit 6.8% of GDP budgeted tha. [Q95]\n• FY 2024-25 actual fiscal deficit 4.8% of GDP tha; 2025-26 Budget aur Revised Estimates mein 4.4% estimate hai. [Q96]\n• FY 2016-17 ka fiscal-deficit target aur actual roughly 3.5% of GDP tha; BE 2026-27 target 4.3% of GDP hai. [Q97]\n• P. Chidambaram ke 1997-98 'Dream Budget' mein major direct aur indirect tax rates mein significant reductions ki gayi thi. [Q98]\n• States ka combined fiscal deficit 2025-26 BE mein lagbhag Rs 11.8 lakh crore, around 3.3% of GDP estimate hua. [Q99]\n• Uttar Pradesh Budget 2023-24 ka total size Rs 6,90,242.43 crore tha aur fiscal deficit Rs 84,883.16 crore estimate hua. [Q100]\n• Union Budget mein external debt current exchange rates par value hota hai, historical exchange rates par nahi. High government borrowing\nreal interest rates ko elevated rakh sakti hai, rising fiscal deficit private investment ko crowd out kar sakta hai, aur interest payments non-plan\nrevenue expenditure ka major component rahe hain. [Q101]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Fiscal-Deficit Figures Change by Year\n▸ 1990s se 2024-25 tak fiscal-deficit target/actual figures ko ek doosre se mix mat karo; har figure apne year ke context mein hai.\n[Q85, Q86, Q87, Q88, Q89, Q90, Q91, Q92, Q93, Q94, Q95, Q96, Q97, Q98, Q99, Q100]\n\n\n\n\n7. Deficit Concepts — Fiscal, Revenue, Primary & Effective Revenue\n\n• Central Government ke major deficit measures mein Revenue Deficit, Fiscal Deficit aur Primary Deficit shamil hain. FY 2024-25 actual\nRevenue Deficit Rs 5,64,296 crore (1.7% GDP) tha; 2026-27 BE mein Rs 5,92,344 crore (1.5% GDP) estimate hai. [Q102]\n• Government Budget ke deficit measures mein Fiscal Deficit sabse comprehensive measure maana jaata hai, kyunki ye total borrowing\nrequirement reflect karta hai. [Q103]\n• Fiscal Deficit generally Revenue Deficit se bada hota hai. Revenue Deficit = Revenue Expenditure - Revenue Receipts; Fiscal Deficit =\nTotal Expenditure - non-debt receipts. [Q104]\n• Primary Deficit nikalne ke liye Fiscal Deficit se Interest Payments deduct kiye jaate hain. [Q105]\n• Traditional definition mein Fiscal Deficit ko budgetary deficit plus net internal aur external borrowings/liabilities ke broader requirement ke\nroop mein explain kiya jaata tha. [Q106]\n• Fiscal Deficit government ki total borrowing requirement ko capture karta hai, jisme market borrowings aur other liabilities shamil hoti hain.\n[Q107]\n• Fiscal Deficit = Total Expenditure - (Revenue Receipts + Recovery of Loans + Disinvestment/other non-debt capital receipts). [Q108]\n• Gross Fiscal Deficit ko financing side se Net Domestic Borrowing + RBI borrowing/monetisation + Foreign Borrowing ke through express\nkiya ja sakta hai. Domestic borrowing mein government securities, small savings aur banking-system financing shamil ho sakte hain. [Q109]\n• Fiscal Deficit batata hai ki non-debt receipts ke baad government ko expenditure finance karne ke liye kitni additional borrowing chahiye.\n[Q110]","• Fiscal Deficit budgetary gap se broader concept hai, kyunki ye government ki total borrowing requirement aur liabilities ko capture karta hai.\n[Q111]\n• Primary Deficit = Fiscal Deficit - Interest Payments. Isliye Fiscal Deficit = Primary Deficit + Interest Payments. [Q112]\n• Primary Deficit mein Interest Payments add karne par Fiscal Deficit milta hai. [Q113]\n• Agar Primary Deficit zero ho, to Fiscal Deficit exactly Interest Payments ke equal hota hai. [Q114]\n• Budget Estimates 2025-26 mein Primary Deficit around 0.8% of GDP estimate tha; 2024-25 BE/RE figures around 1.4% aur 1.3% the.\n[Q115]\n• Revenue Deficit tab hota hai jab Revenue Expenditure, Revenue Receipts se zyada ho. Is gap ko borrowing se finance karna current\nexpenditure cover karta hai aur directly capital asset create nahi karta. [Q116]\n• Effective Revenue Deficit = Revenue Deficit - Grants for Creation of Capital Assets. Ye concept Union Budget 2011-12 se introduce hua.\n[Q117]\n• Effective Revenue Deficit concept Union Budget 2011-12 mein introduce hua. [Q118]\n• Primary Deficit Fiscal Deficit se sirf Interest Payments minus karke milta hai; example mein Fiscal Deficit Rs 50,000 crore aur interest Rs\n1,500 crore ho to Primary Deficit Rs 48,500 crore hoga. [Q119]\n• Revenue Deficit Revenue Expenditure aur Revenue Receipts ka gap hai; Fiscal Deficit total expenditure aur total non-debt receipts ka gap\nhai, jise borrowing se finance kiya jaata hai. [Q120]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Deficit Formula Chain\n▸ Fiscal deficit, revenue deficit, primary deficit aur effective revenue deficit ke formulas mein receipts, expenditure aur interest-payment\ntreatment alag hota hai; formula terms ko mix na karein.\n[Q102, Q103, Q104, Q105, Q106, Q107, Q108, Q109, Q110, Q111, Q112, Q113, Q114, Q115, Q116, Q117, Q118, Q119, Q120]\n\n\n\n\n8. Government Borrowing, Deficit Financing & Subsidies\n\n• Household financial savings mein currency, bank deposits, debt securities, mutual funds, pensions, insurance aur small savings shamil\nhote hain. Government securities/Treasury Bills ke through household savings ka ek hissa government borrowing finance karta hai. [Q121]\n• Union Government ka fiscal deficit largely domestic debt/market borrowing se finance hota hai; Government Securities net debt receipts ka\nmajor source hain. [Q122]\n• Deficit financing agar new money creation ke through ho to money supply goods/services ke comparison mein faster badh sakti hai, jis se\ninflationary pressure create hota hai. [Q123]\n• Productive output ke matching increase ke bina excessive deficit financing inflationary pressure create kar sakti hai. [Q124]\n• Deficit financing se money supply increase ho sakti hai. [Q125]\n• Budget deficit ko new money create karke finance karne se market mein money supply badhti hai; goods supply same rahe to inflation\nincrease ho sakta hai. [Q126]\n• Developing economy mein deficit financing ka main objective development plans ko finance karke economic growth accelerate karna ho\nsakta hai; developed economies mein recession/depression counter karne ke liye bhi use hoti hai. [Q127]\n• Union Budget 2012-13 mein subsidy expenditure ko around 2% of GDP tak contain karne ka proposal tha. [Q128]\n• Subsidies government expenditure ka significant component hain aur fiscal deficit par pressure daal sakti hain; BE 2026-27 mein subsidies\noverall expenditure ka roughly 8.5% ke aas-paas thi. [Q129]\n• India mein subsidies ka long history hai; agricultural subsidy comparisons country-to-country differ karte hain aur several Western\neconomies bhi farmers ko substantial support deti hain. [Q130]\n\n\n9. Parliamentary Budget Procedure, Motions & Budgetary Control\n\n• Money Bill Article 110 ke matters se related hota hai, including Consolidated Fund of India se appropriation. Contingency Fund se\nappropriation Money Bill ki Article 110 defining category nahi hai. [Q131]\n• Demand for Grants par MPs Cut Motions move kar sakte hain: Policy Cut mein demand Rs 1 tak reduce; Economy Cut mein specified\namount reduce; Token Cut mein Rs 100 reduce karne ka proposal hota hai. [Q132]\n• Cut Motion Union Budget ki Demand for Grants ko reduce karne ka parliamentary motion hai. Types: Policy Cut, Economy Cut aur Token\nCut. [Q133]\n• Vote on Account full-year Budget demand pass karna nahi hai; ye Appropriation Act pass hone se pehle short period ke necessary\ngovernment expenditure ke liye advance grant allow karta hai. [Q134]\n• Vote on Account Budget/Appropriation Bill pass hone tak interim period ke expenditure ke liye funds appropriate karne ki temporary\nparliamentary authorization hai. [Q135]\n• Outcome Budget government spending ko measurable outputs/outcomes ke saath link karta hai, taaki citizens aur Parliament expenditure\nke results assess kar saken. [Q136]\n• Performance Budgeting ko USA mein Hoover Commission recommendations ke baad use kiya gaya; ye expenditure ko functions,\nprogrammes, performance aur cost-benefit results ke saath link karta hai. [Q137]\n• Ad hoc Treasury Bills India mein 1955 se temporary government financing ke liye RBI ko issue hote the. 31 March 1997 se ye system end\nhua aur Ways and Means Advances framework ne replace kiya. [Q138]\n• Public Accounts Committee government accounts aur CAG reports examine karti hai. Ismein 22 members—15 Lok Sabha aur 7 Rajya\nSabha—hote hain; conventionally Chairman opposition party se hota hai. [Q139]","EXAM TRAP — Budget Procedure Terms\n▸ Money Bill, policy cut, Vote on Account, Outcome Budget, Performance Budgeting aur parliamentary scrutiny alag concepts hain;\nkeywords ko interchange mat karo.\n[Q131, Q132, Q133, Q134, Q135, Q136, Q137, Q138, Q139]\n\n\n\n\n10. FRBM Act & Fiscal Responsibility Rules\n\n• FRBM review committee N. K. Singh ki chairmanship mein bani aur January 2017 mein report submit ki. [Q140]\n• Fiscal Responsibility and Budget Management Act, 2003 26 August 2003 ko enact hua aur 5 July 2004 se effective hua; aim fiscal\ndiscipline aur consolidation ko legal framework dena tha. [Q141]\n• FRBM framework mein tax-GDP ratio improve karne ka fiscal target tha. BE 2026-27 mein gross tax revenue around 11.2% of GDP, 2025-\n26 RE 11.4% aur 2024-25 around 11.5% raha. [Q142]\n• FRBM Act mein Primary Deficit ko 2008-09 tak zero karne ki explicit statutory requirement nahi thi. [Q143]\n• FRBM Act ka major objective government finance mein fiscal discipline, transparency aur medium-term consolidation ensure karna hai.\n[Q144]\n\nEXAM TRAP — FRBM — Target vs Act Provision\n▸ FRBM ke debt/fiscal targets amendments ke saath change hue; enactment year, effective date aur target years ko alag context mein\npadho.\n[Q140, Q141, Q142, Q143, Q144]\n\n\n\n\n11. Disinvestment Targets & Railway Budget\n\n• Union Budget 2020-21 mein disinvestment receipts target Rs 2.1 lakh crore tha, jisme public-sector banks/financial institutions se around\nRs 90,000 crore target shamil tha. [Q145]\n• Union Budget 2019-20 mein PSU disinvestment target Rs 1.05 lakh crore tha. BE 2026-27 mein disinvestment/asset monetisation related\nother receipts target Rs 80,000 crore estimate hua. [Q146]\n• 1991 liberalisation ke baad CPSE disinvestment programme expand hua. FY 2000-01 mein Rs 10,000 crore target ke against around Rs\n1,829 crore realised hua. [Q147]\n• Railway Budget 2016 mein R&D organisation SRESTHA—Special Railway Establishment for Strategic Technology and Holistic\nAdvancement—form karne ka proposal diya gaya. [Q148]\n• Railway Budget 2013-14 mein premium passenger experience ke liye 'Anubhuti' coaches/train service concept approve hua. [Q149]\n\n\n12. Direct Tax Policy, DTC, Equalisation Levy, Capital Gains & Tax-GDP\n\n• Direct Tax Code (DTC) direct taxes, especially income tax laws ko simplify, consolidate aur modernise karne ka proposed legal framework\ntha. [Q150]\n• India mein Expenditure Tax ka suggestion economist Nicholas Kaldor ne diya; unhone wealth, gift aur estate-duty type taxes par bhi\nrecommendations di. Expenditure Tax April 1962 mein abolish hua. [Q151]\n• Indirect Transfer tab hota hai jab foreign company ke shares transfer hon aur unki substantial value India mein located assets se derive ho;\ntax law mein aise offshore share transfers bhi India-linked capital gains trigger kar sakte hain. [Q152]\n• India ne 2016 mein digital advertising payments par 6% Equalisation Levy introduce/propose ki, non-resident digital-service providers ko\ntax net mein lane ke objective se. [Q153]\n• Inherited immovable property bechne par holding period aur acquisition/inheritance rules ke basis par Capital Gains Tax liability arise ho\nsakti hai. [Q154]\n• Capital Gain asset/property ki value increase se sale/transfer par realise hone wala gain hai; product ki sales quantity badhna capital gain\nnahi hota. [Q155]\n• BE 2026-27 mein Income Tax receipts Rs 14,66,000 crore, around 3.73% of GDP estimate hui; 2025-26 RE mein Rs 13,12,000 crore\naround 3.34% tha. [Q156]\n• Budget Estimates 2026-27 mein direct taxes ka GDP share roughly 6.9% estimate hai; earlier periods mein ye around 5% ke level par tha.\n[Q157]\n• India ka gross Tax-GDP ratio BE 2026-27 mein 11.2% estimate hai; 2024-25 mein around 11.5% tha. [Q158]\n• Financial year 2021-22 mein India ka gross Tax-GDP ratio around 11.5% tha. [Q159]\n• Older 2008-09 context mein combined/particular tax-GDP measure around 16.39% estimate report hua tha; metric definition ko period\ncontext ke saath read karna chahiye. [Q160]\n• Tax-GDP ratio tab decline ho sakta hai jab tax revenue stable rahe aur GDP grow kare, ya dono grow karein lekin GDP ki growth tax\nrevenue se faster ho. [Q161]\n• Progressive taxation income redistribution ka strong tool hai: income badhne ke saath tax rate badhta hai, isliye higher-income groups\nrelatively zyada tax pay karte hain. Progressive public expenditure ke saath redistribution effect aur strong ho sakta hai. [Q162]\n• Degressive taxation progressive aur proportional systems ka mix hai: lower/middle ranges mein tax rate income ke saath increase karta\nhai, lekin ek level ke baad progression flatten ho sakti hai. [Q163]","• Indirect taxes ko regressive effect wala maana jaata hai kyunki same commodity-tax rate poor aur rich consumers dono par apply hota hai;\nlow-income household ki income ka larger proportion tax mein chala sakta hai. [Q164]\n• National-level Income Tax rates Union Government decide/impose karta hai. Income Tax direct tax hai jo individuals/HUFs/entities ki\ntaxable income par lagta hai. [Q165]\n• Union Budget 2016-17 mein annual income Rs 1 crore se above par surcharge rate 15% rakha gaya tha. [Q166]\n• Union Budget 2011-12 mein 80 years ya usse zyada age walon ke liye 'Very Senior Citizen' category create hui; us period mein Rs 5 lakh\ntak annual income tax-free threshold tha. [Q167]\n• Union Budget 2005-06 mein senior citizens ke liye tax-free income limit Rs 1.50 lakh thi. Budget 2011-12 mein 60-80 years ke liye Rs 3\nlakh aur 80+ very senior citizens ke liye Rs 5 lakh threshold rakha gaya. [Q168]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Tax Rates & Thresholds Change Over Time\n▸ Income-tax surcharge, senior-citizen limits aur Tax-GDP ratios year-specific facts hain; current rates ko older-year figures par apply na\nkarein.\n[Q156, Q157, Q158, Q159, Q160, Q161, Q162, Q163, Q164, Q165, Q166, Q167, Q168]\n\n\n\n\n13. Union Tax Revenue, Tax Sharing & Centre-State Tax Powers\n\n• Central Sales Tax inter-State trade par Union law ke under applicable tha; rate framework Centre set karta tha, collection generally\noriginating State karti thi. SEZ transactions ke liye specified exemptions available the. [Q169]\n• 2013-14 se 2016-17 ke four-year period mein Central Government ke gross tax revenue ki growth 2016-17 mein sabse zyada thi. 2017-18\nse 2026-27 (BE) series mein 11.5% growth 2021-22, 2023-24 aur 2024-25 mein record hui. [Q170]\n• Older Union revenue structure mein Central Excise aur Customs major sources the; current structure mein Income Tax, Corporation Tax\naur GST largest tax-revenue sources hain. [Q171]\n• 1995-96 mein Central revenue receipts ke tax composition mein Customs ka contribution around 28.4% aur Excise around 41.2% tha; ye\nhistorical-year figures hain. [Q172]\n• Older tax-source order Corporation Tax > Income Tax > Union Excise > Service Tax tha; BE 2026-27 mein Income Tax > Corporation Tax\n> GST > Union Excise > Customs major order hai. [Q173]\n• 2020-21 tax-share figures mein Corporation Tax 18%, Income Tax 17%, Union Excise 7% aur Customs 4% the; BE 2026-27 mein Income\nTax 21%, Corporation Tax 18%, GST/other taxes around 15%, Excise 6% aur Customs 4% ke order mein hain. [Q174]\n• Older data mein Corporation Tax > Income Tax > Union Excise > Customs order tha; BE 2026-27 mein Income Tax > Corporation Tax >\nUnion Excise > Customs ho gaya. [Q175]\n• Budget Estimates 2026-27 mein major tax-revenue order Income Tax > Corporation Tax > GST > Union Excise Duty > Customs Duty hai.\n[Q176]\n• BE 2026-27 tax estimates: Income Tax Rs 14,66,000 crore; Corporation Tax Rs 12,31,000 crore; GST Rs 10,19,020 crore; Union Excise\nRs 3,88,910 crore; Customs Rs 2,71,200 crore. [Q177]\n• Older Budgets mein Central Excise major tax source thi; current structure mein Income Tax, Corporation Tax aur GST sabse bade sources\nhain. [Q178]\n• BE 2026-27 mein Central tax revenue ka largest source Income Tax Rs 14,66,000 crore estimate hai; uske baad Corporation Tax Rs\n12,31,000 crore aur GST Rs 10,19,020 crore. [Q179]\n• Union Budget 2005-06 mein Corporation Tax largest tax-receipt source tha. [Q180]\n• Gross tax revenue mein Union Excise Duty ka relative share long term mein significantly decline hua hai. [Q181]\n• Road Tax primarily State Governments levy karte hain; ye Union Government ka general tax nahi hai. [Q182]\n• Constitutional scheme mein specified stamp duties Union levy kar sakta hai, lekin collection/appropriation States ke through hoti hai. [Q183]\n• Central taxes ka divisible pool Finance Commission ki recommendations ke basis par States ke saath share hota hai; tax-sharing structure\ntime ke saath amendments aur Finance Commission awards se change hua hai. [Q184]\n• Income Tax Union levy aur collect karta hai, lekin divisible-pool provisions ke under net proceeds ka share Centre aur States mein Finance\nCommission recommendations ke basis par distribute hota hai. [Q185]\n• Customs Duty aur Corporation Tax Union subjects hain. Sales/VAT-type taxes aur land revenue jaise several taxes State jurisdiction mein\nrahe; wealth/gift/estate-duty treatment historical tax law ke period ke saath change hua hai. [Q186]\n• Income Tax, Wealth Tax aur Estate Duty direct-tax examples hain, kyunki impact/incidence same taxpayer par hota hai. Sales Tax indirect\ntax hai, jiska burden seller se consumer ko shift ho sakta hai. [Q187]\n• Historical Union tax structure mein Excise Duty Centre ka major tax-income source thi. [Q188]\n• Capital Gains do broad types mein classify hote hain—Short-Term aur Long-Term; holding period aur asset type ke basis par tax treatment\ndecide hota hai. [Q189]\n• Company ke taxable profit/income par Corporation/Corporate Tax lagta hai. India mein 1960-61 se Company Tax ne earlier 'Super Tax'\nterminology/framework ko replace kiya. [Q190]\n• Corporation Tax Union Government levy aur collect karta hai aur historically Union tax category mein aata hai. [Q191]\nEXAM TRAP — Q172 — No Exact Numerical Match\n▸ 1995-96 mein Central revenue income mein Customs ka contribution 28.4% aur Excise ka contribution 41.2% tha.\n[Q172]","14. MAT, MODVAT, CENVAT, VAT & Service Tax\n\n• Minimum Alternate Tax (MAT) Budget 1996-97 mein introduce hua, taaki profitable companies exemptions/deductions use karke zero ya\nvery low tax liability na dikha saken. [Q192]\n• MODVAT 1 March 1986 se V. P. Singh ke tenure mein introduce hua; iska full form Modified Value Added Tax tha aur ye Central Excise\ncredit mechanism se related tha. [Q193]\n• MODVAT (Modified Value Added Tax) Excise Tax/Central Excise se related tha. [Q194]\n• CENVAT (Central Value Added Tax) Central Excise Duty framework se related hai. [Q195]\n• VAT value-addition based multi-point indirect tax hai; production-distribution chain ke har stage par incremental value par tax lagta hai aur\nfinal burden consumer bear karta hai. Implementation State-level administration ke through hua. [Q196]\n• VAT mein tax production/distribution chain ke har value-addition stage par, final sale tak, apply hota hai. [Q197]\n• 1 April 2005 ke initial VAT rollout mein several States ne immediately participate nahi kiya; Uttar Pradesh, Tamil Nadu aur some BJP-ruled\nStates initial holdouts the. [Q198]\n• December 2005 tak Gujarat, Rajasthan, Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, Uttar Pradesh aur Tamil Nadu ne VAT implement nahi\nkiya tha. [Q199]\n• India mein State-level VAT ka broad nationwide rollout 2005 se hua. [Q200]\n• Haryana India ka first State tha jisne State VAT system early implement kiya. [Q201]\n• GST 1 July 2017 se India bhar mein applicable hua, including Union Territories such as Andaman & Nicobar Islands aur Lakshadweep.\n[Q202]\n• Estate Duty Act 1953 mein enact hua; kuch older exam references first implementation ke liye 1957 figure use karte hain, isliye dono years\nko context ke saath distinguish karein. [Q203]\n• Service Tax Union Budget 1994-95 mein Raja J. Chelliah Committee recommendations ke background mein introduce hua; later GST mein\nsubsume ho gaya. [Q204]\n• Service Tax indirect tax tha, FY 1994-95 se introduce hua aur Union List subject tha; 2017 mein GST regime mein subsume ho gaya.\n[Q205]\n• Service Tax India mein 1994-95 mein introduce hua. [Q206]\n• Union Budget 2006-07 mein Service Tax rate 10% se 12% ki gayi; later Service Tax GST framework mein subsume ho gaya. [Q207]\n• Service Tax ka historical rate 12% raha; 1 July 2017 se ye GST mein subsume ho gaya. [Q208]\n• Service Tax ab separate levy ke roop mein general GST-covered services par apply nahi hota; GST regime mein subsume ho chuka hai.\n[Q209]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Pre-GST Tax Regimes\n▸ MAT, MODVAT, CENVAT, VAT aur Service Tax historical tax-regime concepts hain; applicable period aur replacement sequence ko\nclearly distinguish karo.\n[Q192, Q193, Q194, Q195, Q196, Q197, Q198, Q199, Q200, Q201, Q202, Q203, Q204, Q205, Q206, Q207, Q208, Q209]\n\n\n\n\n15. Direct vs Indirect Taxes, Miscellaneous Taxes & Disclosure Schemes\n\n• India mein Income Tax 24 July 1860 ko James Wilson ne introduce kiya. Modern Income Tax annual taxable income par direct tax hai.\n[Q210]\n• Income Tax direct tax hai, isliye iska legal burden commodity price mein indirect tax ki tarah automatically pass-through nahi hota. [Q211]\n• Imported/exported goods par Union Government jo levy lagata hai use Customs Duty kehte hain. [Q212]\n• Direct Tax ka burden generally same person par legally impose aur bear hota hai; examples: Income Tax, Corporation Tax, Capital Gains\nTax aur historical Wealth/Estate taxes. [Q213]\n• Excise Duty domestically produced goods ke manufacture/production par lagti thi. Excise, Sales Tax, Customs aur Service Tax indirect\ntaxes the; Income Tax aur Corporation Tax direct taxes hain. [Q214]\n• Estate Duty direct tax ka example hai. [Q215]\n• Income Tax direct tax hai, indirect tax nahi. [Q216]\n• Customs Duty aur Excise Duty indirect taxes hain; Corporation Tax aur historical Wealth Tax direct taxes hain. [Q217]\n• Excise Duty production/manufacture par lagne wala indirect tax tha; Sales Tax, Customs aur Service Tax bhi indirect-tax category mein\naate the, jabki Income Tax aur Corporation Tax direct taxes hain. [Q218]\n• Fringe Benefit Tax, Securities Transaction Tax aur Interest Tax historical classification mein direct-tax category se associated the. [Q219]\n• Sales Tax goods ki sale/purchase par lagne wala indirect tax hai; burden consumer tak shift ho sakta hai. Historically Central Sales Tax\ninter-State transactions aur State Sales Tax intra-State sales par apply hote the. [Q220]\n• Excess-profit tax, MAT aur Corporation/Company Tax corporate profits/income se linked hain; Capital Gains Tax asset transfer se arising\ngains par separate tax category hai. [Q221]\n• Entertainment Tax traditional constitutional framework mein State List subject tha; GST ke baad many entertainment levies subsume ho\ngaye, though local-body levies specified cases mein continue kar sakte hain. [Q222]\n• Corporation Tax Union Government levy karta hai, State Governments nahi. [Q223]\n• Customs Duty State revenue tax nahi, Union levy hai jo imported/exported goods par lagti hai. [Q224]\n• Alcoholic liquor for human consumption par State Excise Duty State Governments levy karte hain; ye State List taxation power se linked\nhai. [Q225]\n• Commodity Transaction Tax Union Budget 2013-14 mein introduce hua. [Q226]","• Telecom AGR (Adjusted Gross Revenue) licence fee aur spectrum-usage charge calculation ka base hai. DoT definition mein telecom ke\nsaath specified non-telecom revenues bhi AGR mein include ho sakte hain. [Q227]\n• Income Tax Department ne 27 October 2015 ko PAN Camp aur e-Sahyog initiatives launch kiye, Digital India framework ke under taxpayer\nservices improve karne ke objective se. [Q228]\n• Payment of Gratuity Act ke under statutory gratuity ceiling March 2018 se Rs 20 lakh hui; Central Government employees ke liye 1 January\n2024 se retirement gratuity ceiling Rs 25 lakh ki gayi. [Q229]\n• Voluntary Disclosure of Income Scheme (VDIS) 1 July 1997 se 31 December 1997 tak operate hui. [Q230]\n• Income Declaration Scheme 2016 ke close tak around Rs 65,250 crore undisclosed income declarations report hui thi. [Q231]\n\n\n16. Finance Commission — Constitutional Role, 15th & 16th Commissions\n\n• Constitution ke Article 280 ke under President generally every five years Finance Commission constitute karta hai. Commission divisible\ntaxes ke net proceeds ko Union aur States ke beech distribute karne aur State shares recommend karta hai. [Q232]\n• 16th Finance Commission 31 December 2023 ko notify hua. Iska mandate Union-State tax devolution, States ke shares, grants-in-aid\nprinciples aur Panchayats/Municipalities ke resources augment karne ke measures cover karta hai; disaster-management financing\narrangements ka review bhi ho sakta hai. Climate-management initiatives ki financing review specified mandate ka part nahi hai. [Q233]\n• Finance Commission ka constitutional provision Article 280 mein hai. [Q234]\n• Centre-State tax devolution aur financial distribution Finance Commission ki recommendations se guided hota hai. [Q235]\n• Finance Commission Union aur States ke beech divisible tax revenues ki distribution recommend karta hai. [Q236]\n• Old plan/non-plan grant framework mein several non-plan grants Finance Commission recommendations ke basis par States ko milte the.\n[Q237]\n• Article 280 ke under Finance Commission Union aur States ke beech divisible taxes ke net proceeds aur States ke inter-se shares\nrecommend karta hai. [Q238]\n• Planning Commission aur Finance Commission dono advisory nature ke bodies the/hain; unki recommendations automatically legally\nbinding nahi hoti. [Q239]\n• Finance Commission Article 280 se constituted constitutional body hai. 'Statutory body' terminology technically Act-created bodies ke liye\nuse hoti hai; Finance Commission ka primary classification constitutional body hai. [Q240]\n• Constitutional divisible-pool framework ke under Central taxes Finance Commission recommendations ke basis par States ke saath share\nhote hain. Agricultural Income Tax State taxation power hai, isliye ye Central divisible tax nahi hai. [Q241]\n• Fifteenth Finance Commission 27 November 2017 ko N. K. Singh ki chairmanship mein constitute hua. Members mein Ajay Narayan Jha,\nAshok Lahiri, Anoop Singh aur Ramesh Chand rahe; Arvind Mehta Secretary the. [Q242]\n• Sixteenth Finance Commission 31 December 2023 ko Arvind Panagariya ki chairmanship mein constitute hua; iski recommendations 1\nApril 2026 se start five-year award period ke liye hain. [Q243]\n• Fifteenth Finance Commission Article 280(1) ke under 27 November 2017 ko N. K. Singh ki chairmanship mein bana; isne 2020-21 aur\n2021-26 periods ke liye recommendations di. [Q244]\n• Fifteenth Finance Commission ne 2022-23 se 2025-26 period mein States ke educational outcomes improve karne ke liye total Rs 4,800\ncrore performance-linked grant recommend ki. [Q245]\n• 15th Finance Commission award mein Bihar ka inter-se share divisible pool mein 2021-26 ke liye 10.058% tha; 14th FC mein around\n9.67%. 16th FC context mein Bihar ka share around 9.948% note kiya gaya. [Q246]\n• Fifteenth Finance Commission ke 2021-26 devolution formula mein Bihar ka share 10.058% tha, jo States mein second-highest shares\nmein se ek tha. [Q247]\n• Fifteenth Finance Commission ke horizontal devolution criteria: Income Distance, Population 2011, Area, Forest & Ecology, Demographic\nPerformance aur Tax/Fiscal Effort. Governance Reforms aur Stable Government separate criteria nahi the. [Q248]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Finance Commission — Commission-Specific Criteria\n▸ 15th Finance Commission ke 2021-26 devolution mein Bihar ka share 10.058% aur Uttar Pradesh ka 17.939% tha. 16th Finance\nCommission recommendations mein Bihar 9.948% aur Uttar Pradesh 17.619% hai.\n[Q232, Q233, Q234, Q235, Q236, Q237, Q238, Q239, Q240, Q241, Q242, Q243, Q244, Q245, Q246, Q247, Q248]\n\n\n\n\n17. Finance Commissions — 14th to 1st & Tax Devolution\n\n• Fourteenth Finance Commission (Y. V. Reddy) ne States ka vertical tax-devolution share 42% recommend kiya; Fifteenth ne 41% aur\nSixteenth award context mein bhi 41% retain hua. [Q249]\n• Fourteenth Finance Commission ne States ka divisible-pool share 32% se 42% tak increase kiya; grants/devolution ko award-period\ncontext ke saath read karna chahiye. [Q250]\n• Fourteenth Finance Commission ke Chairman Dr. Y. V. Reddy the. [Q251]\n• Fourteenth Finance Commission ne States ko divisible pool ka 42% recommend kiya; Thirteenth Finance Commission ka corresponding\nrecommendation 32% tha. [Q252]\n• Thirteenth Finance Commission 13 November 2007 ko Dr. Vijay L. Kelkar ki chairmanship mein constitute hua. [Q253]\n• Thirteenth Finance Commission ki recommendations 2010-15 award period ke liye thi. [Q254]\n• Thirteenth Finance Commission ne States ka share divisible pool of Central taxes mein 32% recommend kiya. [Q255]\n• Thirteenth Finance Commission ka tax-devolution recommendation States ke liye 32% tha. [Q256]\n• Thirteenth Finance Commission ne Centre + States combined debt-GDP ratio ko 2014-15 tak 68% ke around reduce karne ka fiscal\nroadmap suggest kiya. [Q257]","• Thirteenth Finance Commission ke horizontal distribution criteria mein fiscal capacity/income distance ko largest weight mila. [Q258]\n• Thirteenth Finance Commission ne model GST aur compensation package design karne aur Central taxes ka share local bodies ko grants\nke roop mein earmark karne se related recommendations di. Agle 10 years mein millions of jobs create karne ka proposal uski distinctive\nrecommendation nahi tha. [Q259]\n• Finance Commission Chairmen mapping: Tenth—K. C. Pant; Eleventh—A. M. Khusro; Twelfth—C. Rangarajan; Thirteenth—Vijay Kelkar;\nFourteenth—Y. V. Reddy; Fifteenth—N. K. Singh; Sixteenth—Arvind Panagariya. [Q260]\n• First Finance Commission ke Chairman K. C. Neogy the. [Q261]\n• Finance Commission Chairmen: Ninth—N. K. P. Salve; Tenth—K. C. Pant; Eleventh—A. M. Khusro; Twelfth—C. Rangarajan. [Q262]\n• Twelfth Finance Commission ke Chairman C. Rangarajan the. [Q263]\n• Chairmen mapping: Eleventh—A. M. Khusro; Twelfth—C. Rangarajan; Thirteenth—Vijay L. Kelkar; Fourteenth—Y. V. Reddy. [Q264]\n• Twelfth Finance Commission ne States ka share shareable Central taxes mein 29.5% se 30.5% tak increase karne ki recommendation di.\n[Q265]\n• Twelfth Finance Commission ne Centre aur States ka Revenue Deficit 2009-10 tak eliminate karne ka roadmap recommend kiya; award\nperiod 2005-10 tha. [Q266]\n• Eleventh Finance Commission ne States ke liye Union taxes ka 29.5% share recommend kiya; specified sales-tax arrangements ke context\nmein adjustment provision bhi tha. [Q267]\n• Tenth Finance Commission ne States ke tax-devolution share ke liye around 29% recommend kiya; later 14th FC 42% aur 15th/16th\naround 41% tak pahuche. [Q268]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Finance Commission Percentages\n▸ 14th, 13th, 12th, 11th aur 10th Finance Commission ke tax-devolution percentages/criteria alag hain; adjacent figures ko mix mat karo.\n[Q249, Q250, Q251, Q252, Q253, Q254, Q255, Q256, Q257, Q258, Q259, Q260, Q261, Q262, Q263, Q264, Q265, Q266, Q267, Q268]\n\n\n\n\n18. Tax Reform Committees, Tax Relief, PAN & Regional Tax Holidays\n\n• Committee mapping: PSU disinvestment—C. Rangarajan; industrial sickness—Omkar Goswami; tax reforms—Raja J. Chelliah; insurance\nreforms—R. N. Malhotra. [Q269]\n• Vijay Kelkar Task Force tax reforms se related tha; deductions/exemptions rationalise karna aur tax structure simplify karna important\nrecommendations mein tha. [Q270]\n• Kelkar Committee ne Section 88 ke tax rebate ko abolish/rationalise karne ki recommendation tax-reform package ke part ke roop mein di.\n[Q271]\n• Tax-reform recommendations wali referred committee Kelkar Committee thi. [Q272]\n• Global Trust Bank ka merger Oriental Bank of Commerce mein hua tha, PNB mein nahi. Kelkar Committee ki later tax recommendations\nmein housing-loan relief par position evolve hui. [Q273]\n• 1991 Tax Reforms Committee ke Chairman Raja J. Chelliah the. [Q274]\n• Chelliah Committee direct aur indirect tax reforms se related thi. [Q275]\n• 1990s ke major tax reforms ka policy base Chelliah Committee recommendations ne provide kiya. [Q276]\n• National Savings Certificate, Public Provident Fund aur National Savings Scheme mein applicable tax-relief provisions the; Indira Vikas\nPatra mein same Income-Tax relief available nahi thi. [Q277]\n• Kisan Vikas Patra investment/interest ko same prescribed Income-Tax exemption nahi milti jo selected tax-saving small-savings\ninstruments ko mil sakti hai. [Q278]\n• PAN 10-character alphanumeric code hai: first 3 letters series, 4th holder status, 5th name/surname initial, next 4 digits serial sequence aur\nlast alphabetic check character hota hai. 4th-character examples: P individual, C company, H HUF, F firm, A AOP, T trust, B BOI, L local\nauthority, J artificial juridical person, G government. [Q279]\n• PAN Income Tax Department ka tax-identity document hai, jisme holder ka name, DOB aur PAN number hota hai; PAN card by itself\ncurrent residential-address proof nahi hai. [Q280]\n• 2001 Gujarat earthquake ke baad Kutch district ki eligible industries ke liye five-year excise-duty holiday announce hua tha. [Q281]\n• Union Budget 2000 mein North-Eastern Region ki eligible industrial units ke liye ten-year tax holiday announce kiya gaya. [Q282]\nEXAM TRAP — Committee Mapping\n▸ Kelkar, Chelliah aur other tax-reform committees ko chairman aur recommendation pair ke saath alag-alag yaad rakho; committee names\naur reform proposals mix na karein.\n[Q269, Q270, Q271, Q272, Q273, Q274, Q275, Q276]\n\n\n\n\n19. Local Finance, Fiscal Stimulus & NHAI Financing\n\n• Panchayat ke revenue sources mein local taxes/fees/fines, own income, grants, loans/assistance aur Finance Commission-recommended\ntransfers shamil hote hain. [Q283]\n• Gram Panchayat local fairs aur markets par specified local taxes/fees levy kar sakti hai; Sales Tax aur Land Revenue State-level\ntaxes/revenues hain. [Q284]\n• Recession ke dauran expansionary fiscal stimulus mein taxes reduce karna aur/or government expenditure increase karna aggregate\ndemand support karta hai; subsidy elimination normally expansionary stimulus nahi hai. [Q285]","• National Highways Authority of India (NHAI) 1988 ke Parliamentary Act se constitute hua aur February 1995 mein operational hua. NHAI\nfinancing ka important source fuel cess hai. [Q286]\n\n\n                               Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · Fiscal Policy & Revenue Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0455","t":"08 Planning","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["PLANNING — FULL MIND MAP\n                                   Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\nTopics in this chapter:\n 1. Planning Framework, DMEO & National Development Council\n 2. Planning Commission & NITI Aayog — Structure and Basics\n 3. NITI Aayog Indices, Reports & Planning Institutions\n 4. Planning Concepts & Pre-Independence Planning Ideas\n 5. Rolling Plan & Core Objectives of Five Year Plans\n 6. Twelfth Five Year Plan — 2012-17\n 7. Eleventh Five Year Plan — 2007-12\n 8. Tenth Five Year Plan — 2002-07\n 9. Ninth Five Year Plan — Growth, Education & Women\n 10. Eighth Five Year Plan — 1992-97 & Human Development\n 11. Seventh & Sixth Plans — Food, Work, IRDP & Rolling Plan\n 12. Fifth & Fourth Plans — Garibi Hatao, Self-Reliance & Growth Centres\n 13. Second Five Year Plan — Mahalanobis & Industrialisation\n 14. First Plan, Development Models & Plan-Programme Matching\n 15. Plan-wise Growth Performance Comparisons\n 16. Plan Holiday, Jobless Growth & Miscellaneous Planning Facts\n\n\n 1. Planning Framework, DMEO & National Development Council\n\n• Constitution ki Seventh Schedule ki Concurrent List ke Entry 20 mein 'Economic and Social Planning' mention hai. [Q1]\n• Development Monitoring and Evaluation Office (DMEO) 18 September 2015 ko Programme Evaluation Organisation (PEO) aur\n Independent Evaluation Office (IEO) ko merge karke establish hua. Ye NITI Aayog ka attached office hai aur monitoring-evaluation\n ecosystem ko strengthen karta hai. [Q2]\n• DMEO NITI Aayog ka attached office hai; 18 September 2015 ko PEO aur IEO ke merger se constitute hua. [Q3]\n• National Development Council (NDC) 6 August 1952 ko constitute hua aur Planning Commission ke parallel development body ki tarah\n kaam karta tha. Five Year Plans ki final approval iska major function tha. [Q4]\n• NITI Aayog ne 1 January 2015 se Planning Commission ko replace kiya. [Q5]\n• Finance Commission constitutional body hai aur mainly revenue-sharing/revenue segment review karta hai; Planning Commission extra-\n constitutional body tha aur States ki capital + revenue requirements dono ko broader planning framework mein assess karta tha. [Q6]\n• Ashok Chanda ne Planning Commission ko 'Economic Cabinet' kaha tha. [Q7]\n• National Development Council 6 August 1952 ko establish hua. Ye extra-constitutional body thi, Prime Minister iska Chairman hota tha aur\n Five Year Plans ko final approval dene ke saath national development policies review karti thi. [Q8]\n• National Development Council ka major role Five Year Plans ko final approval dena tha. [Q9]\n• National Development Council ka Chairman Prime Minister hota tha. [Q10]\n• Planning Commission Five Year Plan ka draft banata tha; Cabinet approval ke baad NDC final approval deta tha. Iske baad plan Parliament\n mein present hota aur approval ke baad government plan ke roop mein publish hota tha. [Q11]\n• National Development Council 6 August 1952 ko constitute hua. [Q12]\n• National Development Council (NDC) 1952 mein constitute hua. [Q13]\n• Five Year Plans ki final approval authority National Development Council thi. [Q14]\n EXAM TRAP — NDC vs NITI Aayog Context\n ▸ National Development Council historical Planning Commission framework ka institution tha; NITI Aayog Governing Council current\n institutional arrangement hai. Dono structures ko mix na karein.\n [Q4, Q5, Q8, Q9, Q10, Q11, Q12, Q13, Q14]\n\n\n\n\n 2. Planning Commission & NITI Aayog — Structure and Basics\n\n• Planning Commission 15 March 1950 ko Union Cabinet ke proposal/resolution se constitute hua. Ye extra-constitutional advisory body tha\n aur Prime Minister iska ex-officio Chairman hota tha. [Q15]\n• Planning Commission Cabinet Resolution ke through create hua tha, Constitution ke through nahi. [Q16]\n• Planning Commission ek advisory institution tha. [Q17]\n• Planning Commission ko semi-political body maana jaata tha. [Q18]\n• Planning Commission ka full-time Chairman nahi hota tha; Prime Minister ex-officio Chairman hota tha. NITI Aayog 1 January 2015 ko\n Planning Commission ki jagah constitute hua. [Q19]\n• NITI Aayog ka ex-officio Chairman Prime Minister hota hai. Governing Council mein States/legislature-wale UTs ke Chief Ministers, UTs ke\n Lieutenant Governors, Vice-Chairman, full-time members aur special invitees shamil hote hain. [Q20]\n• NITI Aayog 1 January 2015 ko establish hua. [Q21]\n• NITI Aayog ne Planning Commission ko replace kiya. [Q22]\n• NITI Aayog 1 January 2015 ko Union Cabinet Resolution ke through Planning Commission ki jagah form hua. Ye Government of India ka\n premier policy think tank hai jo directional aur policy inputs deta hai. [Q23]\n• NITI Aayog 1 January 2015 ko Planning Commission ki jagah constitute hua. [Q24]","• NITI Aayog 1 January 2015 ko Union Cabinet Resolution se form hua. NITI ka full form 'National Institution for Transforming India' hai aur\nye Government of India ka premier policy think tank hai. [Q25]\n• NITI Aayog 1 January 2015 ko Planning Commission ki jagah establish hua; iska Chairperson Prime Minister hota hai, President nahi. [Q26]\n• India mein Sustainable Development Goals (Agenda 2030) ke coordination aur implementation ki nodal institution NITI Aayog hai. [Q27]\n• NITI Aayog ke first Vice-Chairman Arvind Panagariya the; current Vice-Chairman Suman K. Bery hain. [Q28]\n• Amitabh Kant NITI Aayog ke CEO reh chuke hain; February 2023 se B. V. R. Subrahmanyam CEO hain. [Q29]\nEXAM TRAP — NITI Aayog Posts are Time-Specific\n▸ NITI Aayog ka constitution date, full form, Governing Council aur CEO facts time-specific hain. Amitabh Kant historical CEO fact hai;\noffice-holder ko date ke saath padho.\n[Q19, Q20, Q21, Q22, Q23, Q24, Q25, Q26, Q27, Q28, Q29]\n\n\n\n\n3. NITI Aayog Indices, Reports & Planning Institutions\n\n• India Innovation Index 2019 mein Major States category mein Karnataka first tha; NITI Aayog ke 2020 aur 2021 editions mein bhi\nKarnataka ne top position maintain ki. [Q30]\n• 2014-15 mein Bihar in states ke beech fastest-growing economy tha. Economic Survey 2025-26 ke 2023-24 current-price NSDP data mein\nMaharashtra 15.77%, Bihar 15.50%, Goa 10.52% aur Madhya Pradesh 10.30% rahe; all-States level par Assam 19.44% ke saath highest\ntha. [Q31]\n• NITI Aayog Health Index 2019 mein Bihar ka score 32.11 tha; fourth edition ke 2019-20 base-year data mein Bihar ka score 31.00 report\nhua. [Q32]\n• NITI Aayog Fiscal Health Index 2025 mein FY 2022-23 ke liye Odisha top-ranked major State tha; uske baad Chhattisgarh aur Goa rahe.\n[Q33]\n• Fiscal Health Index 2025 mein Uttar Pradesh ka FY 2022-23 score 45.9 tha; 2014-19 period ka earlier score 37.0 record hua tha. [Q34]\n• Planning Commission Cabinet Resolution se bana extra-constitutional body tha. Vice-Chairman/members ki sankhya aur tenure\ngovernment decide karti thi; members ke liye Constitution mein specific qualification prescribed nahi thi. [Q35]\n• P. Chidambaram Planning Commission ke Deputy Chairman nahi rahe. Dr. Manmohan Singh Jan 1985-Aug 1987, Pranab Mukherjee Jun\n1991-May 1996 aur Montek Singh Ahluwalia Jul 2004-May 2014 tak Deputy Chairman rahe. [Q36]\n• Planning Commission ka ex-officio Chairman Prime Minister hota tha. 1 January 2015 se Planning Commission ki jagah NITI Aayog bana,\njiska Chairman bhi Prime Minister hota hai. [Q37]\n• Planning Commission ka ex-officio Chairman Prime Minister hota tha. [Q38]\n• Five Year Plan ka draft Planning Commission prepare karta tha aur Union Cabinet ko deta tha; Cabinet approval ke baad final approval ke\nliye National Development Council ko bheja jaata tha. [Q39]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Index Rankings are Report-Year Specific\n▸ Innovation Index 2019, Health Index 2019 aur Fiscal Health Index 2025 ke rank/score facts apne exact report year se linked hain; unhe ek\nuniversal current ranking na samjho.\n[Q30, Q31, Q32, Q33, Q34]\n\n\n\n\n4. Planning Concepts & Pre-Independence Planning Ideas\n\n• Planning Commission ne June 2000 mein 'India Vision 2020' Committee constitute ki, jiske Chairman Dr. S. P. Gupta the. [Q40]\n• Economic planning ke major objectives balanced socio-economic development, resources ka optimum use, employment expansion,\npoverty reduction, self-reliance aur living standards improve karna hote hain. [Q41]\n• Imperative planning mein government/commanding authority market mechanism ko largely replace karti hai; indicative planning mein\nmarket mechanism continue rehta hai aur government facilitator, guide aur policy-coordinator ki role nibhati hai. [Q42]\n• India ke plans ke public expenditure financing mein borrowings/debt sabse bada resource component raha. Ismein market loans, short-term\nloans, foreign aid, small-savings securities aur State Provident Funds jaise sources shamil the. [Q43]\n• Gadgil-Mukherjee formula mein 1971 population ko maximum 60% weightage milta tha; per-capita income 25%, tax effort/fiscal\nmanagement 7.5% aur special problems 7.5% weightage rakhte the. [Q44]\n• National Planning Committee 1938 mein Subhas Chandra Bose ki initiative par form hui aur Jawaharlal Nehru iske Chairman the. [Q45]\n• Planning institutions aur Plan-related figures ko unke respective historical period ke context mein samajhna chahiye, kyunki institutions aur\nallocation formulas time ke saath change hue hain. [Q46]\n• Bombay Plan 1944 mein 8 major industrialists ne present kiya. Signatories mein J. R. D. Tata, G. D. Birla, Ardeshir Dalal, Lala Shri Ram,\nKasturbhai Lalbhai, A. D. Shroff, Purshottamdas Thakurdas aur John Mathai shamil the. [Q47]\n• Gandhian Plan 1944 mein Gandhian economist Shriman Narayan Agarwal ne present kiya. Iska focus self-reliant villages aur decentralized\neconomic structure par tha. [Q48]\n• Gandhian Plan Shriman Narayan Agarwal ne prepare kiya tha. [Q49]\n• Mahatma Gandhi centralized planned development ke critic the; unka emphasis self-reliance, simple living, local resources aur local\nworkforce ke through decentralized production-consumption par tha. [Q50]\n• Sarvodaya Plan ka draft Jayaprakash Narayan ne 1950 mein prepare kiya. [Q51]","EXAM TRAP — Pre-Independence Plan Mapping\n▸ National Planning Committee, Bombay Plan, Gandhian Plan aur Sarvodaya Plan ke proposer/year pairs alag hain; names aur years ko\ncross-mix na karein.\n[Q45, Q46, Q47, Q48, Q49, Q50, Q51]\n\n\n\n\n5. Rolling Plan & Core Objectives of Five Year Plans\n\n• Gunnar Myrdal ne developing countries ke liye Rolling Plan recommend kiya. Rolling planning mein one-year short-term plan, 3/4/5-year\nmedium-term plan aur 10/15/20-year long-term perspective ko regular revision ke saath use kiya jaata hai. [Q52]\n• Rolling Plan mein one-year short-term plan, 3/4/5-year medium-term framework, annual revision aur longer-term perspective shamil hote\nhain. [Q53]\n• Backward/developing countries ke liye Rolling Plan ka suggestion Gunnar Myrdal ne diya tha. [Q54]\n• Morarji Desai-led Janata Government ne 1978 mein Rolling Plan adopt kiya; 1980 mein Indira Gandhi-led Congress Government ne ise\ndiscontinue karke Five Year Plans restore kiye. [Q55]\n• 1978 mein Janata Government ne Fifth Plan ko early terminate karke 1978-80 ke liye Rolling Plan start kiya. 1980 mein Congress\nGovernment ne 1980-85 ka new Sixth Five Year Plan implement kiya. [Q56]\n• Indian Five Year Plans ka major objective rapid economic development ke through self-reliance aur foreign aid par dependence reduce\nkarna tha. Economic liberalisation ke baad, especially Eighth Plan se, self-reliance ka emphasis comparatively kam hua. [Q57]\n• Independence ke baad planning strategy ka aim economic justice, self-reliance aur India ko stronger developing economy banana tha.\n[Q58]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Rolling Plan Terminology\n▸ Rolling Plan short-, medium- aur long-term horizons ko combine karke yearly revise hota hai. Gunnar Myrdal ne developing/backward\ncountries ke liye idea suggest kiya; India mein Janata Government ne 1978 mein adopt kiya.\n[Q52, Q53, Q54, Q55, Q56]\n\n\n\n\n6. Twelfth Five Year Plan — 2012-17\n\n• Twelfth Five Year Plan (2012-17) ka theme 'Faster, Sustainable and More Inclusive Growth' tha. GDP growth target 8%, agriculture-allied\n4% aur manufacturing 10% tha; 2016-17 iska final year tha aur iske baad Five Year Plan era end hua. [Q59]\n• Twelfth Five Year Plan ka tenure 2012-2017 tha. [Q60]\n• India ka Twelfth Five Year Plan 2012-17 period ka last Five Year Plan tha. Planning Commission ko 1 January 2015 se NITI Aayog ne\nreplace kiya aur Five Year Plan system subsequently discontinue ho gaya. [Q61]\n• Twelfth Five Year Plan (2012-17) ka subtitle 'Faster, Sustainable and More Inclusive Growth' tha. [Q62]\n• Twelfth Five Year Plan mein agriculture, forestry aur fishing ke liye 4% growth target rakha gaya tha. [Q63]\n• October 2012 mein Government of India ne Twelfth Five Year Plan (2012-17) ka draft approve kiya; agriculture aur allied sectors ke liye 4%\nannual average growth target set hua. [Q64]\n• Twelfth Plan ke Approach Paper mein GDP growth ka initial target 9-9.5% propose kiya gaya tha. [Q65]\n• Twelfth Five Year Plan mein Social Services ko sabse bada allocation mila: lagbhag Rs 26,64,843 crore, jo total outlay ka 34.7% tha. [Q66]\n• Twelfth Five Year Plan mein Social Services par sabse bada expenditure allocation tha. [Q67]\n• Twelfth Five Year Plan ke liye infrastructure investment requirement lagbhag Rs 55.7 lakh crore, yani around US$1 trillion, estimate ki gayi\nthi. [Q68]\n• 'Faster, Sustainable and More Inclusive Growth' theme wala Twelfth Five Year Plan 2012-17 tak chala. [Q69]\n• Twelfth Five Year Plan ka central objective faster, sustainable aur more inclusive growth tha. [Q70]\n• Twelfth Five Year Plan ka focus faster, sustainable aur more inclusive growth par tha. [Q71]\nEXAM TRAP — Q62 — All Three Targets Belong to 12th Plan\n▸ Twelfth Plan mein real GDP growth 8%, agriculture growth 4% aur per-capita consumption poverty ratio mein 10 percentage-point\nreduction — teeno valid targets the; 12th Plan context mein inmein se koi ek 'not included' target nahi banta.\n▸ Twelfth Plan Approach Paper mein GDP growth target 9-9.5% propose hua tha; 27 December 2012 ko approved final Plan ka average\nannual growth target 8% tha.\n[Q62, Q65]\n\n\n\n\n7. Eleventh Five Year Plan — 2007-12\n\n• Eleventh Five Year Plan 2007-12 mein 'Faster and More Inclusive Growth' par emphasis tha; Twelfth Plan 2012-17 mein theme 'Faster,\nSustainable and More Inclusive Growth' ho gaya. [Q72]\n• Eleventh Five Year Plan ka major focus inclusive growth tha. [Q73]\n• Eleventh Five Year Plan ka period 2007-2012 tha. [Q74]","• India ka Eleventh Five Year Plan 2007-2012 period se associated hai. [Q75]\n• Eleventh Five Year Plan ka main theme 'Faster and More Inclusive Growth' tha. [Q76]\n• Eleventh Plan Approach Paper ka theme 'Towards Faster and Inclusive Growth' tha. [Q77]\n• Eleventh Five Year Plan ka central objective inclusive economic growth tha. [Q78]\n• Eleventh Plan ke draft mein assumption tha ki target growth achieve ho aur population 1.5% annual rate se bade, to average Indian ki real\nincome lagbhag 10 years mein double ho sakti hai. [Q79]\n• Eleventh Plan mein employment opportunities ki highest expected growth Construction sector mein 8.27% thi; Transport & Communication\n7.6%, Manufacturing 4% aur Agriculture 4% estimate hua. [Q80]\n• Eleventh Five Year Plan ka size Rs 36,44,718 crore tha; Twelfth Five Year Plan ka outlay Rs 76,69,807 crore tha. [Q81]\n• Eleventh Plan mein Social Services ko maximum funds mile aur Energy second-largest allocation sector tha. Education ka allocation\nEnergy sector ke total allocation se kam tha. [Q82]\n• Eleventh Plan mein proposed expenditure: Agriculture & Rural Development Rs 4,37,450 crore; Energy Rs 8,54,123 crore; Transport Rs\n5,72,443 crore; Social Sectors Rs 11,02,327 crore. [Q83]\n• Eleventh Plan mein Agriculture, Industry aur Services ke growth targets respectively 4.0%, 10-11% aur 9-11% the; actual achievement\nroughly 3.7%, 7.2% aur 9.7% rahi. [Q84]\n• Eleventh Five Year Plan mein agriculture growth target 4% per year tha. [Q85]\n• Eleventh Five Year Plan ka original GDP growth target 9% tha, jabki actual average annual growth around 8% achieve hui. [Q86]\n• Eleventh Plan ke Inclusive Growth framework mein 'strengthening the capital market' inclusive-growth objective ka part nahi tha. [Q87]\n• Eleventh Plan mein elementary education par major focus tha; dropout rate ko 2011-12 tak 20% tak reduce karna, minimum standards\nimprove karna aur literacy rate ko 85% tak badhana targets mein shamil the. [Q88]\n• Eleventh Five Year Plan mein 8 new IITs open karne ki approval di gayi. [Q89]\nEXAM TRAP — Allocation Depends on Category & Wording\n▸ Eleventh Plan mein overall maximum allocation Social Services ko mila aur Energy second tha; Social Services ke education component\nka allocation Energy se lower tha. Twelfth Plan mein Social Services 34.7%, Energy 18.8% aur Transport 15.7% raha.\n[Q81, Q82, Q83]\n\n\n\n\n8. Tenth Five Year Plan — 2002-07\n\n• Tenth Plan (2002-07) ka original growth target 8% tha; mid-term appraisal mein ise 7% revise kiya gaya, jabki actual average annual\ngrowth 7.6% rahi. [Q90]\n• Tenth Five Year Plan ka GDP growth target 8% tha. [Q91]\n• Tenth Five Year Plan 1 April 2002 se 31 March 2007 tak chala. [Q92]\n• Tenth Five Year Plan ka period 2002-2007 tha. [Q93]\n• Tenth Plan ke major targets mein 8% GDP growth aur poverty ratio ko 2007 tak 20% tak reduce karna shamil tha. [Q94]\n• Tenth Plan ke sectoral outlay mein Energy 26.47% ke saath highest tha; Social Services 22.79%, Transport 14.31% aur Communication\n6.43% the. [Q95]\n• Tenth Plan ke sectoral growth targets mein Agriculture 3.97% aur Telecommunications 15% tha. [Q96]\n• Tenth Plan ke crop-growth targets/figures: pulses & oilseeds 4.29%, fruits & vegetables 2.97%, cereals 1.28% aur other crops 3.58%. [Q97]\n• Tenth Plan mein domestic saving rate lagbhag 26.84% of GDP aur investment rate 32.1% tha. Eleventh Plan ka average investment rate\n38.2% aur Twelfth Plan estimate 38.8% tha. [Q98]\n• 2000-02 ke GDP growth performance ko Tenth Plan ke 8% target se compare karne par growth expectation ke lagbhag half ke aas-paas\nthi. [Q99]\n\n\n9. Ninth Five Year Plan — Growth, Education & Women\n\n• Ninth Five Year Plan (1997-2002) ka emphasis 'Growth with Social Justice and Equality' tha. Target growth 6.5% ke against actual growth\n5.4% rahi. [Q100]\n• Ninth Plan 1997-2002 mein annual growth target 6.5% tha, lekin actual growth around 5.4% achieve hui. [Q101]\n• Ninth Five Year Plan mein total education budget ka 57.1% primary education ko allocate kiya gaya. [Q102]\n• Women's Component Plan Ninth Five Year Plan (1997-2002) ki major strategy thi; women-related sectors ke liye 30% funds earmark karne\nka approach adopt hua. [Q103]\n• Ninth Plan mein capital-output ratio 4.3 estimate hua tha, jabki actual capital-output ratio around 4.53 raha. [Q104]\n\n\n10. Eighth Five Year Plan — 1992-97 & Human Development\n\n• Seventh Plan 31 March 1990 ko end hua; two-year gap ke baad Eighth Plan 1 April 1992 se 31 March 1997 tak chala. [Q105]\n• Eighth Five Year Plan April 1992 se March 1997 tak operational tha. [Q106]\n• Eighth Five Year Plan ka basic objective broad-based Human Development tha. [Q107]","• First Plan ka main focus agriculture tha; Third Plan agriculture/foodgrains ke saath basic industries par focused tha; Fifth Plan ka emphasis\npoverty eradication aur self-reliance par tha. [Q108]\n• Eighth Plan (1992-97) ka GDP growth target 5.6% tha, jabki actual growth 6.8% achieve hui. [Q109]\n• Eighth Plan mein GDP growth target 5.6% aur industrial growth target 7.5% tha. [Q110]\n• Eighth Plan ka total proposed outlay Rs 8,71,000 crore tha; ismein Rs 7,98,000 crore investment outlay aur Rs 93,000 crore current\nexpenditure ke liye tha. [Q111]\n• Eighth Plan ke public-sector outlay financing ka largest source government borrowings tha. Total public-sector investment Rs 4,34,100\ncrore mein se Rs 2,02,255 crore, yani 46.6%, borrowings se raise karne ka target tha. [Q112]\n• Eighth Plan mein public investment ko infrastructure sectors—transport, communication, energy aur irrigation—mein concentrate karna\nmajor priority thi. [Q113]\n• Eighth Plan mein infrastructure development ko adequate/high priority di gayi. [Q114]\n• Industrialisation strategy mein heavy-industry emphasis se infrastructure emphasis ki shift Eighth Five Year Plan se clearly dikhne lagi.\n[Q115]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Eighth Plan & Human Resource Development\n▸ Human Resource Development Eighth Five Year Plan ka major focus tha; First Plan agriculture, Third Plan agriculture + basic industries,\naur Fifth Plan poverty eradication + self-reliance par focused the.\n[Q107, Q108]\n\n\n\n\n11. Seventh & Sixth Plans — Food, Work, IRDP & Rolling Plan\n\n• Seventh Plan (1985-90) ki strategy poverty, unemployment aur regional disparities par attack karna tha; foodgrain production, employment\naur productivity increase par major emphasis diya gaya. [Q116]\n• Seventh Five Year Plan ka main slogan 'Food, Work and Productivity' tha. [Q117]\n• 'Food, Work and Productivity' slogan Seventh Five Year Plan se associated tha. [Q118]\n• Integrated Rural Development Programme (IRDP) 1976 mein 20 selected districts se start hua; iska approach individual ke bajay entire\npoor family/household ko development unit maana tha. [Q119]\n• IRDP 2 October 1980 se country ke sabhi 5,011 blocks mein extend hua. Iska aim poor rural families ko self-employment ke liye\ncredit/assets support dekar poverty line se upar lana tha. [Q120]\n• Janata Government ne 1978 mein Fifth Plan ko early terminate karke Rolling Plan framework adopt kiya; isi context mein 1978-80 phase ko\nRolling/Sixth Plan se joda jaata hai. [Q121]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Sixth Plan / Rolling Plan Historical Usage\n▸ 1978 mein Janata Government ne Fifth Plan terminate karke Rolling Plan framework start kiya; isi historical phase ko Sixth Plan/Rolling\nPlan framing se link kiya jaata hai.\n[Q120, Q121]\n\n\n\n\n12. Fifth & Fourth Plans — Garibi Hatao, Self-Reliance & Growth Centres\n\n• Fifth Five Year Plan ki formulation aur tenure dono unusual rahe. Planning Commission ne 1972 mein iska initial framework prepare kiya\ntha, jisme employment ko major priority di gayi. [Q122]\n• Fifth Five Year Plan apne scheduled period se ek saal pehle terminate kar diya gaya. [Q123]\n• Fifth Plan 1 April 1974 se 31 March 1979 ke liye planned tha, lekin Janata Government ne ise ek saal pehle terminate kar diya. Iska main\nobjective poverty eradication aur self-reliance tha. [Q124]\n• Fifth Five Year Plan ka basic objective poverty removal tha. [Q125]\n• 'Garibi Hatao' / Poverty Eradication slogan Fifth Five Year Plan se strongly associated tha. [Q126]\n• Fifth Plan ke dauran poverty alleviation aur poor sections ki minimum needs meet karne ke liye national development programmes ko major\npolicy focus mila. [Q127]\n• Green Revolution India mein 1966-67 se start hui; 14 major banks 19 July 1969 ko nationalize hue; 'Garibi Hatao' slogan Indira Gandhi ne\n1971 mein diya aur poverty removal Fifth Plan ka major goal bana. [Q128]\n• Regional disparities reduce karne ke liye Growth Centre Approach Fourth Plan mein start hua; Fifth Plan mein is approach par aur zyada\nemphasis diya gaya. [Q129]\n• Self-sustained development ka objective Fourth Five Year Plan mein explicitly adopt hua; Third Plan ka major aim foodgrains mein self-\nreliance achieve karna tha. [Q130]\n\n\n13. Second Five Year Plan — Mahalanobis & Industrialisation\n\n• Second Five Year Plan 1 April 1956 se 31 March 1961 tak chala aur P. C. Mahalanobis model par based tha. Iska core focus rapid\nindustrialisation/heavy industry tha; Industrial Policy 1956 ne 'socialist pattern of society' ko policy direction di. [Q131]\n• Second Five Year Plan ka model P. C. Mahalanobis se associated tha. [Q132]\n• Second Plan (1956-61) P. C. Mahalanobis model par based tha aur rapid industrialisation iska main goal tha. P. C. Mahalanobis ke birthday\n29 June ko India mein Statistics Day manaya jaata hai. [Q133]","• Second Plan model ke chief architect P. C. Mahalanobis the. [Q134]\n• Second Plan mein capital-intensive heavy industries ko priority di gayi. Mahalanobis model mein capital-goods sector, factory consumer-\ngoods sector, small-unit production aur household-industry sectors shamil the. [Q135]\n• Industrial development aur industrialisation Second Five Year Plan ka major focus tha. [Q136]\n• Second Five Year Plan mein industrial sector ko maximum emphasis mila. [Q137]\n\n\n14. First Plan, Development Models & Plan-Programme Matching\n\n• First Five Year Plan (1 April 1951-31 March 1956) Harrod-Domar growth model par based tha; Community Development Programme 1952\nmein isi Plan period mein start hua. [Q138]\n• Independence ke baad Planning Commission 1950 ke Cabinet Resolution se constitute hua aur 1 April 1951 se First Five Year Plan ke\nsaath planned development formally start hua. [Q139]\n• First Plan (1951-56) Harrod-Domar model par based tha aur agriculture-irrigation ko top priority mili; Second Plan (1956-61) P. C.\nMahalanobis model par based tha. [Q140]\n• 1951 ke foodgrain imports aur inflation pressure ko dekhte hue First Plan mein agriculture, irrigation aur power projects ko top priority di\ngayi. [Q141]\n• Second Plan mein capital-goods manufacturing industries mein investment aur rapid industrialisation ko central strategy banaya gaya;\nMahalanobis development model adopt hua. [Q142]\n• India ne 1950s mein Import Substitution Industrialisation strategy adopt ki; Second Plan se basic aur capital-goods industries ko strong\npolicy push mila. [Q143]\n• Planning-model mapping: First Plan—Harrod-Domar; Second—Mahalanobis; Third—S. Chakravarty; Fourth—Ashok Rudra model. [Q144]\n• Plan-focus mapping: First—Community Development; Second—Rapid Industrialisation; Third—basic industries expansion; Fourth—growth\nwith stability/self-reliance; Fifth—Minimum Needs Programme. [Q145]\n• Plan-focus mapping: First Plan agriculture-focused tha; Second Plan rapid industrialisation/heavy & basic industries par; Third Plan self-\nreliance/self-production par; Fourth Plan growth with stability aur progressive self-reliance par; Fifth Plan poverty eradication (Garibi Hatao)\naur self-reliance par focused tha. [Q146]\n• Plan-period mapping: First 1951-56; Third 1961-66; Fourth 1969-74; Sixth 1980-85. [Q147]\nEXAM TRAP — Plan Models & Programme Matching\n▸ Plan-model mapping: First—Harrod-Domar; Second—P. C. Mahalanobis; Third—S. Chakravarty; Fourth—Ashok Rudra. Programme-plan\npairs ko bhi unke exact Plan period ke saath padho.\n[Q140, Q142, Q143, Q144, Q145, Q146, Q147]\n\n\n\n\n15. Plan-wise Growth Performance Comparisons\n\n• Selected Plans ki average growth rates: First 3.6%, Fourth 3.3%, Sixth 5.7% aur Tenth 7.6%. [Q148]\n• Fifth, Sixth, Seventh aur Eighth Five Year Plans mein real Gross National Product ka cumulative annual growth rate Eighth Plan ke dauran\nsabse zyada tha. [Q149]\n• Selected Plan growth rates: Seventh 6.0%, Eighth 6.8%, Ninth 5.4%, Tenth 7.6%; inmein Tenth Plan highest tha. [Q150]\n• Selected Plan growth rates: Eighth 6.8%, Ninth 5.4%, Tenth 7.6% aur Eleventh 8.0%; inmein Eleventh Plan highest tha. [Q151]\n• Eleventh Plan mein per-capita GDP growth target 7.6% per year tha; is pace par per-capita real income roughly 10 years mein double hone\nka projection tha. [Q152]\n• Uttar Pradesh ki selected Plan growth rates: Sixth 3.9%, Seventh 5.7%, Eighth 3.2% aur Ninth 2.5%. [Q153]\nEXAM TRAP — 'Highest Growth' Depends on Metric\n▸ Q148-Q152 mein 'highest' alag metrics par based hai—Plan growth rate, real GNP growth aur per-capita growth ko same metric\nsamajhkar compare na karein.\n[Q148, Q149, Q150, Q151, Q152]\n\n\n\n\n16. Plan Holiday, Jobless Growth & Miscellaneous Planning Facts\n\n• 1951 se Five Year Planning history mein total 7 years regular Five Year Plans ke cover se bahar rahe; 1966-69 ka 3-year period formal\nPlan Holiday tha. [Q154]\n• Third Plan 31 March 1966 ko end hua aur Fourth Plan 1 April 1969 se start hua; 1966-69 ke three years mein Five Year Plan ki jagah\nAnnual Plans chale. [Q155]\n• India mein Plan Holiday 1966-1969 ke beech raha. [Q156]\n• Eighth Plan ke first two years mein organised-sector employment growth sirf 0.36% rahi, target 2.6% se kaafi kam. Eighth aur Ninth Plan\nperiods mein employment growth weak rehne se post-liberalisation 'jobless growth' concern highlight hua. [Q157]\n• Twenty Point Programme 1975 mein launch hua. Iska focus agriculture/industry production, poverty reduction, literacy, quality of life\nimprove karna aur income inequality reduce karna tha. [Q158]","• Core sector long-term economic development ke liye strategically important basic industries ka group hai. Traditional 8 core industries:\ncoal, crude/mineral oil, natural gas, refinery products, fertilizers, steel, cement aur electricity. [Q159]\n• Market-friendly economy mein bhi long-term planning useful ho sakti hai, kyunki profit-driven markets low-profit but socially important\nsectors mein insufficient investment kar sakte hain. Planning long-term balance aur neglected sectors ko support kar sakti hai. [Q160]\n• 1991 reforms ke baad private sector aur market mechanism ka role increase hua; planning ka role direct control se shift hoke development\nko policy direction, coordination aur enabling environment dene par zyada focused hua. [Q161]\n• Directive Principles ke Article 40 mein village Panchayats ko strengthen karne ki direction hai. 73rd Constitutional Amendment ne\ndecentralised Panchayati Raj ko institutional base diya; bottom-up planning ka aim village/local level se planning process ko build karna hai.\n[Q162]\n• First Five Year Plan December 1951 mein Jawaharlal Nehru ne Parliament mein present kiya; National Development Council 6 August\n1952 ko constitute hua. [Q163]\n• 'Yojana' monthly magazine Ministry of Information & Broadcasting ke Publications Division se publish hoti hai. [Q164]\n• 'Planned Economy for India' ke author M. Visvesvaraya hain. [Q165]\n• 'Planning and the Poor' ke author B. S. Minhas hain. [Q166]\nEXAM TRAP — Plan Holiday & Non-Plan Years\n▸ 1966-69 Plan Holiday, 1978-79 Rolling Plan, 1979-80 gap aur 1990-92 gap ko mila kar regular Five Year Plans se bahar total 7 years\nbante hain.\n[Q154, Q155, Q156]\n\n\n\n\n                                         Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · Planning Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0456","t":"09 Money & Banking","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["MONEY & BANKING — FULL MIND MAP\n                                  Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\nTopics in this chapter:\n 1. Modern Finance, Insolvency, Digital Payments & FinTech\n 2. Money Supply, Multiplier, Black Money & Currency\n 3. Inflation — Concepts, Types & Effects\n 4. Inflation Measurement — CPI, WPI & Price Indices\n 5. Banking History, Nationalisation & Public Sector Banks\n 6. Commercial, Private & Foreign Banks\n 7. Financial Inclusion, SFBs, Payments Banks, NBFCs & Microfinance\n 8. Bank Assets, Liabilities, Credit, NPA, Basel & Deposit Insurance\n 9. RBI — Functions, Government Banking & Institutional Basics\n 10. Monetary Policy, MPC & Credit-Control Instruments\n 11. CRR, SLR, Repo, Bank Rate & Policy Transmission\n 12. RBI Governors, Legal Framework & Development Financial Institutions\n 13. Insurance, IRDA, Provident Fund, SEBI & Mutual Funds\n 14. Stock Exchanges, Sensex, Capital Market & Financial Instruments\n 15. Cooperative Credit, Land Development Banks & Rural Finance\n 16. NABARD, Regional Rural Banks & SHG-Bank Linkage\n 17. Financial-Sector Committees, FSDC & Disinvestment\n 18. Financial-Inclusion Schemes, KCC, Ombudsman & Credit Rating\n 19. Money/Banking Terms, Markets & Business-Economics Basics\n\n\n 1. Modern Finance, Insolvency, Digital Payments & FinTech\n\n• Infrastructure Investment Trusts (InvITs) se investors ko distributed interest income taxable hai aur dividend bhi taxable hai. InvITs\n SARFAESI Act, 2002 ke under borrowers ke roop mein recognised hain. [Q1]\n• Interest Coverage Ratio debt-servicing/profitability ratio hai jo batata hai company outstanding debt ka interest kitni easily pay kar sakti hai.\n Ise Times Interest Earned Ratio bhi kehte hain. [Q2]\n• Insolvency financial condition hai jahan person/entity due debts pay nahi kar paata; Bankruptcy formal legal process hai jisme debtor ke\n assets/liabilities ko liquidation ya reorganisation ke through resolve kiya jaata hai. [Q3]\n• Syndicated Lending mein multiple lenders milkar ek borrower ko large loan provide karte hain. Borrower corporation, large project ya\n government entity ho sakta hai. [Q4]\n• Project Sashakt ke under stressed assets, especially consortium-lending accounts, ko faster resolve karne ke liye Indian banks/FIs ne 23\nJuly 2018 ko Inter-Creditor Agreement sign kiya. [Q5]\n• RBI ki Scheme for Sustainable Structuring of Stressed Assets (S4A) 13 June 2016 ko notify hui; iska purpose genuinely stressed large\ncorporates ki debt/financial structure ko sustainable basis par restructure karna tha. [Q6]\n• RBI ke 6 April 2018 'Storage of Payment System Data' direction ke under payment-system operators ko specified payment data India mein\nstored system mein maintain karna required tha. [Q7]\n• May 2016 mein Ministry of Finance aur RBI ne cashless transactions promote karne ke liye Neeraj Kumar Gupta ki chairmanship mein 7-\nmember committee constitute ki. [Q8]\n• Merchant Discount Rate (MDR) woh fee hai jo bank/payment acquirer merchant se card-based payment accept karne ke liye charge karta\nhai. [Q9]\n• India ke ATM/payment switching network ka major interconnection National Payments Corporation of India (NPCI) ke National Financial\nSwitch framework se hota hai. [Q10]\n• RuPay India ka domestic card-payment network hai, NPCI ne March 2012 mein launch kiya. 'RuPay' naam Rupee + Payment se bana hai.\n [Q11]\n• NPCI International Payments Ltd. ke UPI Global Acceptance facility se Indian users selected foreign merchant QR codes scan karke\ndirectly Indian bank account se payment kar sakte hain. [Q12]\n• UPI NPCI-developed instant bank-to-bank payment system hai jo IMPS infrastructure par built hai; mobile app se multiple bank accounts ke\nbeech real-time transfer possible hota hai, separate mobile wallet mandatory nahi. [Q13]\n• RTGS mein settlement real-time/gross basis par hota hai; NEFT batch-based processing se comparatively delay ho sakta hai. Inward\nRTGS amount receive karne wale beneficiary par charge nahi lagna chahiye. [Q14]\n• Central Bank Digital Currency (CBDC) ya Digital Rupee RBI-issued legal tender ka digital form hai. [Q15]\n• CBDC central bank dwara issued sovereign currency ka digital form hai, jo physical cash ka secure/convenient alternative aur digital-\npayment instrument provide karta hai. [Q16]\n• Bitcoin cryptocurrency/virtual currency hai, pseudonymous creator 'Satoshi Nakamoto' se associated hai aur blockchain-based peer-to-peer\nelectronic payment system use karta hai. [Q17]\n• Non-Fungible Token (NFT) unique digital token/asset hai jo art, music, video, in-game items ya other digital/real-world-linked assets ko\nrepresent kar sakta hai. [Q18]\n\n EXAM TRAP — Digital Money ≠ Same Thing\n ▸ UPI instant bank-to-bank transfer system hai; RTGS real-time gross settlement aur NEFT batch-based transfer system hai. CBDC\n sovereign digital legal tender hai, jabki Bitcoin aur NFT blockchain/crypto-asset concepts hain.\n [Q10, Q11, Q12, Q13, Q14, Q15, Q16, Q17, Q18]\n\n\n\n\n 2. Money Supply, Multiplier, Black Money & Currency","• RBI ke older money-supply framework mein M1-M4 aggregates use hote the; 1998 working-group review ke baad M0-M3 framework\nprominent hua. M3 Broad Money hai: currency with public + demand deposits + other RBI deposits + banking-system time deposits. [Q19]\n• Broad Money (M3) mein currency with public, banks ke demand deposits, banking-system time deposits aur RBI ke other deposits shamil\nhote hain. [Q20]\n• Demand deposit se Rs 1 lakh cash withdraw karne par immediate aggregate money supply change nahi hoti; deposit money currency with\npublic mein convert hota hai. M1, M2, M3 aur M4 liquidity ke progressively broader aggregates hain. [Q21]\n• Liquidity ka decreasing order generally Currency > Demand Deposits > Savings Deposits > Time Deposits hai. [Q22]\n• Money Multiplier monetary base aur broad money supply ka relationship dikhata hai; banking habits/deposit mobilisation badhne se\nbanking system ki credit-creation capacity influence hoti hai. [Q23]\n• India mein Money Multiplier ko Broad Money M3 / Reserve Money M0 ke ratio se measure kiya jaata hai: K = M3/M0. [Q24]\n• Black Money generally undeclared/untaxed income ko refer karta hai jahan legally payable tax evade kiya gaya ho; isse government\nrevenue loss hota hai. [Q25]\n• Tax evasion se State Exchequer ko revenue loss hota hai aur unreported income black-money problem ko badhati hai. [Q26]\n• Fugitive Economic Offenders Act, 2018 ko enforce karne ki responsibility Enforcement Directorate ke paas hai. ED Department of Revenue,\nMinistry of Finance ke administrative control mein kaam karta hai. [Q27]\n• 8 November 2016 ko Rs 500 aur Rs 1,000 ke then-existing high-denomination notes demonetise kiye gaye; circulation value ka around\n86% affected hua. Stated objectives mein black money/counterfeit/cash-based illicit activity reduce karna shamil tha, GDP growth directly\niska primary objective nahi tha. [Q28]\n• India mein early paper currency private/presidency-era banks ne issue ki; Paper Currency Act 1861 ke baad note-issue right Government of\nIndia ke control mein aa gaya aur government paper currency 1862 se issue hui. [Q29]\n• 1913 ke Indian Currency Royal Commission ke Chairman Sir Austen Chamberlain the. [Q30]\n• India mein decimal currency system 1957 mein introduce hua. 1957-64 ke coins par 'Naya Paisa' term use hua; 1964 se 'Naya' hata kar sirf\n'Paisa' use hone laga. [Q31]\n• India mein decimal coinage system 1957 mein introduce hua. [Q32]\n• Legal Tender Money woh currency hai jise law debt/payment settlement ke liye valid medium recognise karta hai; creditor ko legally\nacceptable tender specified limits/rules ke under accept karna hota hai. [Q33]\n• RBI Act ke framework mein note denominations up to specified statutory limits issue kiye ja sakte hain; current circulation mein RBI mainly\nRs 10, 20, 50, 100, 200 aur 500 banknotes issue karta hai. [Q34]\n• Minimum Reserve System 1956 mein adopt hua. Initially Rs 515 crore reserve required tha, jisme Rs 115 crore gold aur Rs 400 crore\nforeign securities shamil the; 31 October 1957 ke amendment ke baad minimum reserve Rs 200 crore hua, jisme at least Rs 115 crore gold\naur Rs 85 crore foreign securities rakhna required hai. [Q35]\n• India mein currency-note issue Minimum Reserve System par based hai. [Q36]\n• RBI currency notes issue karta hai aur backing/reserve assets mein gold, foreign securities/assets aur Government securities shamil hote\nhain. [Q37]\n• India mein coins mint/issue karne ka authority Government of India, Ministry of Finance ke paas hai; Coinage Act framework coin\ndenominations, design aur minting govern karta hai. [Q38]\n• India ke major mints Mumbai, Kolkata, Hyderabad aur Noida mein hain; Noida Mint 1988 mein establish hua. [Q39]\n\n\n3. Inflation — Concepts, Types & Effects\n\n• Inflation mein general price level lagatar badhta hai aur money ki purchasing power girti hai; same amount se pehle ke comparison mein\nkam goods/services kharide ja sakte hain. [Q40]\n• Expansionary monetary policy mein central bank policy rates aur liquidity tools use karke money supply/credit availability badhata hai;\ninterest rates soften hote hain aur aggregate demand ko support milta hai. [Q41]\n• Inflation general price level mein sustained aur cumulative rise hai; excess demand ya other pressures se prices badhte hain aur money ki\nreal purchasing power girti hai. [Q42]\n• Inflation rate girne ka matlab prices ab bhi badh rahe hain, lekin slower pace par; negative inflation mein overall price level previous\ncomparable period se actually girta hai. [Q43]\n• Inflation purchasing power ko reduce karti hai; generally debtors/borrowers ko benefit aur creditors/lenders ko loss hota hai, kyunki\nrepayment lower real-value money mein hoti hai. [Q44]\n• Inflation se consumers, creditors aur fixed-income/bond investors generally disadvantage mein rehte hain, jabki debtors, producers aur\ntraders ko kuch situations mein benefit mil sakta hai. [Q45]\n• Inflation debtor class ko benefit aur bond holders ko harm karti hai, kyunki loan repayment ki real purchasing-power value kam ho jaati hai.\n[Q46]\n• Inflation generally borrowers ko benefit karti hai, jabki fixed-income savers aur lenders ko disadvantage hota hai. [Q47]\n• Historical weekly inflation interpretation mein 5% inflation ka matlab selected weighted basket ka price same week of previous year se 5%\nhigher tha. [Q48]\n• 1994-95 se 1997-98 ke beech 52-week average inflation 10.9% se 4.8% hui, jabki foreign investment 4,807 million dollar se 5,353 million\ndollar badha. [Q49]\n• Deflation general price level mein persistent fall hai; inflation ke opposite, ismein goods/services ke average prices lagatar girte hain. [Q50]\n• Base Effect tab hota hai jab previous-year ka unusually high ya low price/index level current inflation-rate calculation ko significantly\ninfluence karta hai. [Q51]\n• Budget deficit aur unproductive expenditure ko contain karna macroeconomic stability ke liye important hai; revenue deficit 1990-91 mein\nGDP ka 3.3% tha aur 1992-93 mein 2.5% tak aaya. [Q52]\n• Inflation ko aam bhasha mein mehangai kehte hain: same quantity of goods/services kharidne ke liye pehle se zyada price pay karna padta\nhai. [Q53]\n• Suppressed inflation mein supply sharply gir sakti hai aur demand high reh sakti hai, lekin strict price controls/rationing visible market-price\nrise ko temporarily suppress kar dete hain. [Q54]","• Headline Inflation overall consumer basket ke price-change ko show karti hai aur commonly CPI-based broad inflation measure ke roop\nmein use hoti hai. [Q55]\n• Headline inflation mein food aur fuel price movements included hote hain, jabki Core Inflation usually food aur fuel jaise volatile components\nko exclude karti hai. [Q56]\n• Headline inflation economy ke broad price movement ko food aur energy ke saath measure karti hai; core inflation in volatile components\nko exclude karti hai. [Q57]\n• Headline Inflation food aur energy prices ko include karti hai, jabki Core Inflation in components ko exclude karke underlying price trend\ndikhati hai. [Q58]\n• Inflation measurement mein selected consumer goods/services ke retail prices ka bhi use hota hai. [Q59]\n\n\n4. Inflation Measurement — CPI, WPI & Price Indices\n\n• India mein inflation analysis ke liye CPI aur WPI dono important hain; retail inflation ka primary modern benchmark CPI-Combined hai, jabki\nworker cost-of-living series bhi alag purpose serve karti hain. [Q60]\n• India mein older popular inflation benchmark WPI tha; 2014 se RBI monetary-policy framework mein CPI-Combined primary inflation\nmeasure hai. [Q61]\n• Headline retail inflation CPI-Combined (CPI-C) ke broad consumer basket se measure ki jaati hai. [Q62]\n• Headline inflation CPI-Combined ke broad basket mein overall price change ko capture karti hai. [Q63]\n• CPI-Combined India ka primary retail-inflation measure hai; WPI wholesale-level price movement ke analysis mein use hota hai. [Q64]\n• August 2016 mein Government ne 2016-21 ke liye CPI inflation target 4% fix kiya tha, tolerance band +/-2 percentage points ke saath. [Q65]\n• RBI ke April 2025 Monetary Policy Report mein FY2025-26 CPI inflation around 4% project ki gayi; older projections mein FY2025 aur\nFY2026 ke liye 4.5% aur 4.1% figures bhi diye gaye the. [Q66]\n• Calendar year 1991 mein monthly-average WPI-based annual inflation 12.7% thi; April-December 2025-26 period mein WPI inflation\naround 0.04% report hui. [Q67]\n• Consumer Price Index selected goods/services ke representative consumer basket ke retail-price changes ko time ke saath measure karta\nhai. [Q68]\n• India mein CPI ki different series Labour Bureau aur National Statistical Office (NSO) compile karte hain, series ke target\npopulation/purpose ke hisab se. [Q69]\n• CPI-IW, CPI-AL aur CPI-RL Labour Bureau compile karta hai; broader rural/urban CPI series NSO framework mein compile hoti hain. [Q70]\n• Labour Bureau CPI-IW ke saath CPI-AL aur CPI-RL bhi compile karta hai. [Q71]\n• Labour Bureau CPI-IW series 1944 se compile kar raha hai; iska base year time-to-time revise kiya gaya hai. [Q72]\n• CPI-IW ka base year historical revision mein 1960 se 1982 kiya gaya tha. [Q73]\n• Central Government employees ke inflation compensation/dearness allowance calculations mein CPI-IW important reference index hai.\n[Q74]\n• Employees ka Dearness Allowance primarily CPI-IW se linked hai; major CPI series mein CPI-IW, CPI-AL, CPI-RL aur All-India CPI (Rural\n+ Urban) shamil hain. [Q75]\n• India ka WPI data Office of Economic Adviser, DPIIT, Ministry of Commerce & Industry release karta hai. [Q76]\n• Hashim Committee ki recommendations ke baad WPI base year 1981-82 se 1993-94 kiya gaya aur revised series 1 April 2000 se\nimplement hui. [Q77]\n• WPI ke 2011-12-base structure mein Manufactured Products ka weight around 64.23% hai. [Q78]\n• WPI basket mein Manufactured Products ko sabse highest weight milta hai. [Q79]\n• 14 September 2010 ko 2004-05 base-year wali WPI series start hui; 12 May 2017 ko 2011-12 base-year wali revised series introduce ki\ngayi. [Q80]\n• All-India WPI ka base year 2004-05 se revise karke 2011-12 kiya gaya. [Q81]\n• 2004-05 base-year wali revised WPI series 14 September 2010 ko release hui. [Q82]\n• Current WPI series ka base year 2011-12 hai; older series mein 1993-94 aur 2004-05 base years use hue the. [Q83]\n• October 2009 mein WPI base year 1993-94 se 2004-05 shift karne ka decision liya gaya tha. [Q84]\n• WPI monthly basis par release hota hai; historical WPI-CPI-IW comparison mein food items ka weight CPI-IW ke mukable WPI mein\nrelatively lower raha. [Q85]\n• Agar price index base year = 100 se badhkar 330 ho jaye, to weighted average price level base-year level ka 3.3 times ho gaya hai. [Q86]\n• Producer Price Index (PPI) producers ko milne wale goods/services prices mein average change ko time ke saath measure karta hai. [Q87]\n• Cost-push inflation production cost/wages badhne se aggregate supply curve left shift hone par ho sakti hai; lower supply higher price level\ncreate karti hai. [Q88]\n• COVID-19 ke baad many central banks ne monetary policy ease ki, markets ko liquidity di aur credit flow maintain karne ke measures liye.\n[Q89]\n• Inflation control ke liye monetary tightening useful ho sakti hai; interest rate lower karna liquidity aur demand badha kar inflationary pressure\nko aur push kar sakta hai. [Q90]\n• Excess money circulation inflationary pressure create kar sakta hai; money supply/liquidity reduce karna inflation control ka monetary\nmeasure hai. [Q91]\n• Persistent food inflation ke major reasons mein supply-chain constraints aur rising incomes ke saath consumption-pattern shift shamil ho\nsakte hain. [Q92]\n• October 2009-February 2010 ke period mein inflation negative zone mein gaya tha; global petroleum prices ka sharp fall major factor tha.\n[Q93]","EXAM TRAP — Inflation Data is Index/Period Specific\n▸ CPI, CPI-IW, WPI aur Producer Price Index ke base year, compiler aur weightage series/time-period ke saath change ho sakte hain; old\nseries values ko current universal values na samjho.\n[Q60, Q61, Q62, Q63, Q64, Q68, Q69, Q70, Q71, Q72, Q73, Q76, Q77, Q78, Q79, Q80, Q81, Q82, Q83, Q84, Q85, Q87]\n\n\n\n\n5. Banking History, Nationalisation & Public Sector Banks\n\n• Oudh Commercial Bank (1881) Indian board dwara operated early limited-liability bank tha; Punjab National Bank (1894) ko first fully Indian\nbank maana jata hai. [Q94]\n• In chronology, Banking Regulation Act 1949 sabse pehle aaya; First Five Year Plan 1951, SBI nationalisation 1955, life insurance\nnationalisation 1956 aur general insurance nationalisation 1973 mein hui. [Q95]\n• India mein 19 July 1969 ko 14 major commercial banks nationalise hue; 15 April 1980 ko 6 aur banks nationalise kiye gaye. [Q96]\n• Bank nationalisation ka first major phase 19 July 1969 mein 14 banks ka tha; second phase 15 April 1980 mein 6 banks ka hua. [Q97]\n• Chronology: 14-bank nationalisation July 1969 -> Regional Rural Banks October 1975 -> 6-bank nationalisation April 1980 -> NABARD July\n1982. [Q98]\n• Nationalisation chronology: RBI 1949, SBI 1955, LIC 1956 aur 14 major commercial banks 1969. [Q99]\n• All India Rural Credit Survey Committee/A. D. Gorwala Committee (1951) ne Imperial Bank ko restructure karke State Bank of India\nestablish karne ki recommendation di thi. [Q100]\n• Earlier SBI ke alawa 20 public-sector banks the; April 2020 mergers ke baad total 12 PSBs reh gaye, yani SBI + 11 other public-sector\nbanks. [Q101]\n• 2019 consolidation announcement mein 10 public-sector banks ko merge karke 4 larger banks banane ka decision liya gaya. [Q102]\n• 2008-09 se 2016-17 ke beech Government ne PSBs mein substantial capital infusion ki, lekin year-wise amount steadily increase nahi hua;\nSBI aur associate banks ka merger banking consolidation ka major reform tha. [Q103]\n• EASE (Enhanced Access and Service Excellence) Index public-sector banks ki reform/performance assessment framework hai; EASE 8.0\nMarch 2025 se rollout hua. [Q104]\n• Commercial bank deposits accept karta hai aur individuals/businesses ko loans-credit provide karta hai; money aur credit intermediation\niska core function hai. [Q105]\n• Jin banks mein Government holding at least 51% hoti hai unhe public-sector banks kehte hain; Indian Overseas Bank ek PSB hai. [Q106]\n• Allahabad Bank 2020 mein Indian Bank ke saath merge ho gaya. [Q107]\n• ICICI 1955 mein World Bank, Indian financial institutions aur insurance-sector participation ke saath establish hua tha; later ICICI Bank\nmajor private-sector bank bana. [Q108]\n• ICICI Bank private-sector bank hai; Corporation Bank Union Bank of India mein merge hua, jabki Dena Bank aur Vijaya Bank Bank of\nBaroda mein merge hue. [Q109]\n• A. D. Gorwala/All India Rural Credit Survey Committee ne Imperial Bank ko restructure karke State Bank of India establish karne ki\nrecommendation di thi. [Q110]\n• State Bank of India India ka largest public-sector commercial bank aur major financial-services institution hai; SBI Fortune Global 500\nlistings mein bhi raha hai. [Q111]\n• State Bank of India India ka sabse bada public-sector commercial bank hai. [Q112]\n• S&P Global Market Intelligence ranking mein SBI total assets ke basis par April 2024 report mein 47th aur April 2025 report mein 43rd\nposition par tha. [Q113]\n• February 2024 mein HDFC Bank ne apna first sustainable finance bond issue karke US$300 million raise kiya; proceeds electric vehicles,\nSMEs aur affordable housing jaise sectors ke finance ke liye targeted the. [Q114]\n• SBI ne farmers outreach ke liye Kisan Clubs promote kiye; NABARD bhi Kisan Club programmes ko support karta raha hai. [Q115]\n• Pratip Chaudhuri April 2011 mein SBI Chairman bane the; current context mein Challa Sreenivasulu Setty SBI Chairman hain, jinhone\nDinesh Kumar Khara ko succeed kiya. [Q116]\n• Public-sector financial institutions ke top-management selection mein Banks Board Bureau ka role tha; 1 July 2022 se ise Financial\nServices Institutions Bureau (FSIB) ne replace kiya. [Q117]\n• P. J. Nayak Committee ki recommendation ke baad Banks Board Bureau establish hua aur 1 April 2016 se operational hua. [Q118]\nEXAM TRAP — Bank Count & Chairperson are Time-Specific\n▸ Public-sector bank count, global rankings aur SBI chairperson jaise facts time-specific hote hain; year/date ke bina current value assume\nna karein.\n[Q101, Q113, Q116, Q117, Q118]\n\n\n\n\n6. Commercial, Private & Foreign Banks\n\n• September 2015 mein SBI ne major e-commerce partners ke saath 'SimplyCLICK' credit card launch kiya. [Q119]\n• State Bank of India ne 2006 mein China mein apni first branch open ki; ye China mein branch kholne wala first Indian bank tha. [Q120]\n• Private-sector Axis Bank ne Shanghai mein branch establish karke China presence expand ki. [Q121]\n• ICICI Bank ne December 2002 mein Mumbai mein mobile ATM service start ki thi. [Q122]\n• HDFC Bank ne June 2020 mein e-Kisan Dhan app launch kiya, jisme farmers ko banking ke saath crop prices, varieties aur weather-\nrelated information milti thi. [Q123]","• New Bank of India 1993 mein Punjab National Bank mein merge hua; Oriental Bank of Commerce 2020 mein PNB mein merge hua. [Q124]\n• Bank of Rajasthan ka merger ICICI Bank ke saath August 2010 mein effective hua. [Q125]\n• 1992 securities-scam context se linked Bank of Karad ko close karke 1994 mein Bank of India mein merge kiya gaya. [Q126]\n• Industrial-finance institutions ki chronology: IFCI 1948, ICICI 1955 aur IDBI 1964; inka focus industrial sector ko medium/long-term finance\ndena tha. [Q127]\n• Bank-type mapping: Indian Bank - Public Sector; ICICI Bank - Private Sector; Citibank - Foreign Bank; Saraswat Bank - Cooperative Bank.\n[Q128]\n• Foreign-bank WOS framework mein new entrant ke liye initial minimum paid-up voting equity capital Rs 5 billion hai; Board-composition\nrules mein Indian citizens/NRIs/PIOs aur resident Indian directors ke specified proportions required hain. [Q129]\n• 31 December 2024 tak India mein foreign-bank branch network mein Standard Chartered ke 100 branches sabse zyada the; HSBC 26,\nDeutsche Bank 17 aur Citibank 14 branches ke saath follow karte the. [Q130]\n• Bank-country mapping: ABN AMRO - Netherlands; Barclays - United Kingdom; Kookmin Bank - South Korea. [Q131]\n• Chronology: SBI 1955; Lead Bank Scheme 1969; Regional Rural Banks 1975; NABARD 1982. [Q132]\n• RBI ke January 2001 private-bank licensing framework mein initial paid-up capital Rs 200 crore tha, first three years mein Rs 300 crore tak\nraise karna required tha; private-bank FDI limit later 74% tak hui, jisme up to 49% automatic route allowed hai. [Q133]\n• 30 June 2012 ko commercial-bank branches mein rural branches ka share around 37% tha; March 2024 data mein scheduled\ncommercial/private-bank rural presence bhi roughly 37% range mein thi. [Q134]\n• Lead Bank Scheme ka conceptual base D. R. Gadgil Study Group se aaya, jisne October 1969 mein organisational framework par report\nsubmit ki. [Q135]\n• Lead Bank Scheme 1969 mein start hui; har district ko ek designated bank assign kiya jata hai jo district-level credit planning aur\ncoordination lead karta hai. [Q136]\n• Lead Bank ka role district ke liye designated/nominated bank perform karta hai. [Q137]\n• Lead Bank approach mein banks ko specific districts adopt karke intensive banking/credit development coordinate karna tha. [Q138]\n• Service Area Approach April 1989 mein rural/semi-urban credit planning ko improve karne ke liye Lead Bank framework ke under start hua\naur scheduled commercial banks including RRBs par apply hua. [Q139]\n• RBI ne December 1969 se Lead Bank Scheme operationalise ki; designated Lead Bank district ke credit institutions aur development\nagencies ke coordination mein leading role leta hai. [Q140]\n• Small Industries Development Bank of India (SIDBI) 2 April 1990 ko establish hua; iska headquarters Lucknow mein hai. [Q141]\n• SIDBI April 1990 mein establish hua. [Q142]\n• SIDBI ka headquarters Lucknow, Uttar Pradesh mein hai. [Q143]\n• SIDBI ka core mandate MSME/small-industry sector ko finance, refinance aur development support provide karna hai. [Q144]\n• SIDBI ka headquarters Lucknow mein hai; Allahabad Bank ab Indian Bank ke saath merged hai. [Q145]\n\n\n7. Financial Inclusion, SFBs, Payments Banks, NBFCs & Microfinance\n\n• Financial inclusion ke liye banking outreach, simplified accounts/credit access aur targeted institutional measures jaise multiple steps India\nmein use kiye gaye hain. [Q146]\n• FRBM Act fiscal responsibility aur budget management se related law hai; ise banking-sector reform measure ke roop mein classify nahi\nkarna chahiye. [Q147]\n• Financial inclusion ka basic meaning society ke sabhi sections ko affordable formal financial services tak access dena hai. [Q148]\n• Financial inclusion ka objective poor/weaker sections aur rural population tak banking, savings, credit, insurance aur payment services\nexpand karna hai; banking infrastructure shrink karna iska objective nahi. [Q149]\n• Low income, financial illiteracy aur bank-branch/access infrastructure ki kami financial inclusion ke major obstacles hain. [Q150]\n• Financial Inclusion Committee C. Rangarajan ki chairmanship mein June 2006 mein constitute hui aur January 2008 mein final report\nsubmit ki. [Q151]\n• C. Rangarajan Committee financial inclusion reforms aur access expansion se associated thi. [Q152]\n• Union Budget 2014-15 mein financial inclusion deepen karne ke liye private sector mein Small Finance Banks aur Payments Banks\nestablish karne ka framework announce hua. [Q153]\n• Small Finance Bank se Universal Bank transition ke April 2024 criteria mein minimum 5-year satisfactory track record, listed shares, at least\nRs 1,000 crore net worth, required CRAR, last two years profit aur GNPA <=3%/NNPA <=1% jaise conditions shamil hain. [Q154]\n• Payments Banks ka objective low-income households, migrant workers, small businesses aur other underserved groups ko small savings\naur payment/remittance facilities dekar financial inclusion badhana hai. [Q155]\n• Nachiket Mor Committee ne Payments Banks jaise differentiated banking model recommend kiye; committee September 2013 mein bani\naur January 2014 mein RBI ko report di. [Q156]\n• Shadow Banking non-bank financial institutions dwara bank-like credit/intermediation activities ko refer karta hai; term ko economist Paul\nMcCulley ne 2007 mein popularise kiya. [Q157]\n• Commercial banks deposits, payments, loans aur broader banking services provide karte hain; NBFCs specialised finance/credit activities\npar focus karte hain aur full banking functions nahi karte. [Q158]\n• NBFCs savings/current-account jaise demand deposits accept nahi kar sakti, although wo Government securities acquire/invest kar sakti\nhain. [Q159]\n• NBFCs ko banks ki tarah RBI ke LAF window ka direct regular access nahi hota; unke liquidity arrangements aur regulatory framework\ncommercial banks se different hote hain. [Q160]\n• RBI ne NBFC Ombudsman Scheme 23 February 2018 ko launch ki; ye specified NBFC services se related complaints ke speedy aur cost-\nfree resolution ka mechanism tha. [Q161]\n• India mein microfinance ka early growth 1980s ke Self-Help Group model se hua; microfinance services mein small credit, savings linkage,\ninsurance aur fund-transfer support shamil ho sakte hain. [Q162]","• RBI ne microfinance-sector practices review karne ke liye Y. H. Malegam Committee constitute ki; committee ne December 2011 mein\nreport submit ki. [Q163]\n• 2011 ke microfinance regulatory reforms Malegam Committee ki recommendations se strongly associated the. [Q164]\n• Patna ke Bihar Bal Bhawan Kilkari ne November 2009 mein 'Gullak Baccha Bank' start kiya, jiska objective children mein saving habit\ndevelop karna tha. [Q165]\n\n\n8. Bank Assets, Liabilities, Credit, NPA, Basel & Deposit Insurance\n\n• Bank dwara farmers ko diya gaya credit bank ka asset hota hai; deposits aur other payable obligations bank ki liabilities hoti hain. [Q166]\n• Commercial-bank assets mein cash/RBI balances, investments aur loans-advances aate hain; customer deposits liabilities hote hain. [Q167]\n• Commercial banks ki liabilities ka largest component deposits hota hai; deposits mein time deposits ka share especially important hota hai.\n[Q168]\n• Loan ka principal ya interest scheduled due date se 90 days tak unpaid rahe to account generally Non-Performing Asset (NPA) classify\nhota hai. [Q169]\n• Priority Sector Lending mein agriculture, MSMEs, education, housing, export credit, social infrastructure aur renewable energy jaise\ncategories shamil hain. [Q170]\n• Traditional duration classification mein short-term loan usually 15 months se kam, medium-term 15 months se 5 years aur long-term 5\nyears se zyada maana gaya; institution-specific definitions differ kar sakti hain. [Q171]\n• Teaser Loan mein initial interest rate attractively low rakha jata hai aur later years mein rate increase hota hai; borrower ko future\nrepayment burden clearly assess karna chahiye. [Q172]\n• Central Government ke internal debt mein market borrowings, Treasury Bills, RBI ko issued special securities aur compensation bonds\njaise liabilities shamil ho sakti hain. [Q173]\n• Non-financial debt examples mein credit-card dues, household housing loans, industrial loans aur government borrowing instruments ki\nliabilities shamil ho sakti hain. [Q174]\n• Core Banking Solution networked branches ko integrate karta hai, jisse customer account ko different branches/channels se operate kar\nsakta hai; anywhere banking aur fast fund transfer iske key benefits hain. [Q175]\n• June 2007 mein Government of India ne RBI se SBI ki 59.7% stake around Rs 35,531 crore mein acquire ki. [Q176]\n• NABARD ka ownership structure pehle RBI-linked tha; April 2019 se NABARD ki 100% shareholding Government of India ke paas hai.\n[Q177]\n• National Housing Bank 9 July 1988 ko National Housing Bank Act, 1987 ke under establish hua; initially ye RBI ki wholly owned subsidiary\ntha. [Q178]\n• Basel norms international banking standards hain jo capital adequacy, risk management aur financial-system resilience improve karne ke\nliye Basel, Switzerland-based framework se develop hue. [Q179]\n• Basel II, June 2004 revised framework, banks ki capital adequacy aur risk-sensitive regulatory capital ke international standards se related\nhai. [Q180]\n• Capital Adequacy Ratio (CAR), ya CRAR, bank ke capital ko risk-weighted assets ke comparison mein measure karta hai aur unexpected\nlosses absorb karne ki capacity dikhata hai. [Q181]\n• DICGC RBI-owned deposit-insurance institution hai; 4 February 2020 se eligible bank deposits par insurance cover maximum Rs 5 lakh per\ndepositor per bank, same right and capacity basis par hai. [Q182]\n• Savings-account interest rate RBI ne October 2011 mein deregulate ki; ab banks regulatory conditions ke under apni savings deposit rates\ndecide karte hain. [Q183]\n• RBI ne December 2015 mein MCLR framework guidelines issue ki aur MCLR-based lending system 1 April 2016 se effective hua. [Q184]\n\n\n9. RBI — Functions, Government Banking & Institutional Basics\n\n• RBI India ka apex central bank hai; money/credit conditions aur banking system regulate karta hai, lekin ordinary individuals ya business\nhouses ko commercial loan directly nahi deta. [Q185]\n• RBI Government ka banker, monetary-policy authority, IMF-related government agent, forex manager, Government borrowing manager aur\nbankers' bank jaise functions perform karta hai. [Q186]\n• RBI ke major functions: currency issue, Government banker/debt manager, bankers' bank, banking-system regulator aur foreign-exchange\nreserves/management. [Q187]\n• Monetary aur financial matters mein RBI Government ka banker, fiscal agent aur advisor ke roop mein kaam karta hai. [Q188]\n• RBI Act framework ke under RBI Government of India aur State Governments ke securities/debt-management functions handle karta hai.\n[Q189]\n• Treasury Bills Government of India ke short-term debt instruments hain; RBI ke through 91-day, 182-day aur 364-day tenors mein issue\nhote hain. [Q190]\n• RBI Retail Direct Scheme 12 November 2021 ko launch hui, jisse retail investors G-Secs mein direct participate kar sakte hain; NDS-OM\nRBI ka electronic G-Sec trading platform hai, jabki CDSL RBI-promoted depository nahi hai. [Q191]\n• Jammu & Kashmir Government aur RBI ka banking agreement January 2011 mein sign hua aur 1 April 2011 se effective hua; J&K Bank\ngovernment banking business ke liye RBI agent bana. [Q192]\n• RBI bankers' bank ke roop mein commercial banks ki liquidity, branch expansion, merger/acquisition aur winding-up jaise regulatory\nmatters supervise karta hai. [Q193]\n• RBI 1 April 1935 ko RBI Act, 1934 ke under establish hua; initial office Calcutta mein tha, headquarters 1937 mein Mumbai shift hua aur\nRBI 1949 mein nationalise hua. [Q194]\n• Reserve Bank of India 1949 mein nationalise hua. [Q195]\n• RBI ka headquarters Mumbai mein hai. [Q196]\n• Reserve Bank of India 1935 mein establish hua. [Q197]","• RBI ko 'bankers' bank' kaha jata hai kyunki commercial banks reserves maintain karte hain aur liquidity/regulatory support ke liye RBI se\ninteract karte hain. [Q198]\n• India ke official foreign-exchange reserves RBI maintain aur manage karta hai. [Q199]\n• Commercial banks ki credit-creation capacity ko RBI monetary aur regulatory tools se influence/control karta hai. [Q200]\n• RBI 'lender of last resort' ke roop mein solvent bank ko temporary liquidity crisis ke time emergency liquidity support de sakta hai. [Q201]\n• RBI Rs 1 note ko chhodkar banknotes issue karta hai; Rs 1 note Government of India issue karti hai aur uspar Finance Secretary ke\nsignature hote hain. [Q202]\n• Rs 1 note par Finance Secretary ke signature hote hain, RBI Governor ke nahi. [Q203]\n• Credit creation primarily commercial banks ka function hai; monetary policy MPC decide karti hai aur RBI usse implement karta hai. [Q204]\n• RBI currency aur credit regulate karta hai aur forex reserves manage karta hai; foreign trade ka direct regulation RBI ka core function nahi\nhai. [Q205]\n• RBI central bank aur bankers' bank hai; commercial banks ke reserves maintain karta hai, regulatory guidance deta hai aur need par\nliquidity support provide karta hai. [Q206]\n• Reserve Bank of India desh ka central bank hai. [Q207]\n• RBI ki major income Government securities aur foreign-exchange assets/transactions se aati hai; pension-fund management, private-\ncompany lending aur currency printing income sources nahi hain. [Q208]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Q192 — J&K Banking Arrangement Changed in 2011\n▸ Jammu & Kashmir Government ka RBI agreement January 2011 mein sign hua aur 1 April 2011 se effective hua. J&K Bank RBI agent\nbana; reorganisation ke baad dono Union Territories ka government banking business bhi J&K Bank RBI agent ke roop mein conduct karta\nhai.\n[Q192]\n\n\n\n\n10. Monetary Policy, MPC & Credit-Control Instruments\n\n• Monetary policy fiscal policy ko complement karti hai; RBI/MPC framework money, liquidity, interest rates aur credit conditions ko influence\nkarta hai. [Q209]\n• Monetary policy ka central objective 'growth with price stability' hai: inflation ko reasonable range mein rakhte hue sustainable economic\ngrowth support karna. [Q210]\n• Equitable distribution of income/assets monetary policy ka direct primary objective nahi hai; price stability, growth aur monetary/financial\nstability key objectives hain. [Q211]\n• India mein monetary policy RBI framework mein operate hoti hai; 2016 se policy-rate decisions statutory Monetary Policy Committee (MPC)\nleti hai. [Q212]\n• Price stability aur inflation control ke liye RBI monetary-policy tools operate karta hai. [Q213]\n• MPC six-member statutory committee hai: RBI Governor Chairperson, RBI ke 2 other members aur Government-nominated 3 external\nmembers; policy repo rate jaise decisions MPC leti hai. [Q214]\n• India mein money aur credit conditions ko RBI quantitative aur qualitative/selective credit-control tools se regulate karta hai. [Q215]\n• Variable Reserve Ratio qualitative/selective nahi, quantitative credit-control instrument hai. [Q216]\n• Quantitative credit-control tools system-wide liquidity ko affect karte hain; example, CRR badhne par banks ke lendable funds aur money-\ncreation capacity generally reduce hoti hai. [Q217]\n• Credit rationing qualitative/selective credit-control method hai; Bank Rate, reserve-ratio changes aur Open Market Operations quantitative\nmethods hain. [Q218]\n• Open Market Operations RBI ka quantitative credit/liquidity-control instrument hai. [Q219]\n• Bank Rate aur Open Market Operations monetary-policy instruments hain; public debt aur public revenue fiscal-policy domain se related\nhain. [Q220]\n• Open Market Operations (OMO) mein RBI Government Securities buy/sell karta hai; purchase se liquidity inject hoti hai aur sale se liquidity\nabsorb hoti hai. [Q221]\n• RBI dwara Government Securities ki purchase aur sale ko Open Market Operations kaha jata hai. [Q222]\n• Variable reserve ratios aur Open Market Operations quantitative monetary-policy instruments hain jo system-wide credit aur liquidity\nregulate karte hain. [Q223]\n• RBI securities purchase kare to liquidity badhti hai, sale kare to liquidity absorb hoti hai; OMO sterilisation/liquidity-management ka\nimportant tool bhi ho sakta hai. [Q224]\n• Operation Twist mein RBI long-term Government bonds buy aur short-term securities sell karke yield curve/liquidity conditions influence\nkarta hai; India mein 2020 mein bhi aise operations use hue. [Q225]\n\n\n11. CRR, SLR, Repo, Bank Rate & Policy Transmission\n\n• Central bank Government Securities public se purchase kare ya Government ko direct liquidity support de to money supply expand ho sakti\nhai; securities sale ya public currency deposit is direction mein expansionary nahi hote. [Q226]\n• Inflation restrain karne ke liye RBI Bank Rate/CRR jaise rates-reserve ratios raise karke liquidity aur credit creation reduce kar sakta hai;\nGovernment Securities purchase contractionary step nahi hai. [Q227]\n• High inflation mein RBI Government Securities sell karke liquidity absorb kar sakta hai; forex market mein dollar sale rupee depreciation\npressure ko moderate karne mein help kar sakti hai. [Q228]\n• Statutory reserve requirements ka monetary-policy effect banks ki lendable funds aur credit-creation capacity ko influence karna hai. [Q229]","• SLR mein banks NDTL ka prescribed share liquid assets, including approved Government Securities, mein maintain karte hain; isse\nGovernment securities demand bhi support hoti hai. [Q230]\n• SLR requirement lower hone par banks ke paas lending ke liye relatively more funds available ho sakte hain; SLR cash, gold aur approved\nsecurities jaise liquid assets mein maintain hota hai. [Q231]\n• Bank liquidity requirement ke context mein Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR) key statutory ratio hai. [Q232]\n• Prime Lending Rate commercial bank apne lending framework mein decide karta hai; Bank Rate, CRR aur SLR-related regulatory\nframework RBI se governed hote hain. [Q233]\n• Marginal Standing Facility (MSF) RBI ki overnight liquidity facility hai, 2011-12 monetary-policy framework mein introduce hui aur 9 May\n2011 se effective hui; NDTL banking liabilities ka key base term hai. [Q234]\n• Expansionary monetary policy mein central bank money supply/liquidity badhakar demand aur economic activity support karta hai. [Q235]\n• Interest rates lower hone se borrowing cost girti hai, jisse consumption aur capital investment generally increase ho sakte hain. [Q236]\n• Indian financial-sector reforms mein CRR/SLR reduction, interest-rate deregulation aur insurance sector ko private participation ke liye\nopen karna shamil tha. [Q237]\n• Reform period ke baad CRR aur SLR dono earlier high levels se progressively reduce kiye gaye, jisse banks ke lendable resources aur\nmarket-based allocation ko support mila. [Q238]\n• SLR NDTL ka prescribed proportion hai jo banks liquid assets mein maintain karte hain; earlier 25% statutory floor 2007 amendment ke\nbaad remove hua, while maximum statutory ceiling 40% tak framework mein thi. [Q239]\n• Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR) banks ke NDTL ka prescribed percentage hai jo cash form mein RBI ke paas maintain karna hota hai. [Q240]\n• Banks ko apne NDTL ka prescribed share RBI ke paas Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR) ke roop mein maintain karna hota hai. [Q241]\n• CRR badhne par banks ke lendable funds kam hote hain, isliye credit creation aur system liquidity generally reduce hoti hai. [Q242]\n• CRR reduce hone par banks ke paas more lendable liquidity aati hai; credit, investment aur money supply expand ho sakte hain. [Q243]\n• CRR reduction commercial banks ke available lending funds badhati hai aur credit creation ko support karti hai. [Q244]\n• CRR increase se banks ke lendable resources kam hote hain; ye inflation control ke liye contractionary monetary measure ho sakta hai.\n[Q245]\n• CRR badhne par commercial banks ke paas lending ke liye comparatively less money bachta hai. [Q246]\n• CRR increase credit-creation capacity reduce karta hai kyunki banks ko larger cash reserve RBI ke paas maintain karna padta hai. [Q247]\n• Repo transaction securities ke sale/purchase ke saath future reverse transaction agreement hota hai; RBI repo se liquidity inject aur\nreverse-repo/SDF-type operations se liquidity absorb karta hai. [Q248]\n• Repo money-market liquidity aur short-term credit conditions manage karne ka RBI tool hai; rupee par forex-market selling pressure\nmanage karna iska direct purpose nahi. [Q249]\n• Repo Rate RBI/MPC monetary-policy framework mein set hota hai. [Q250]\n• Repo Rate woh policy rate hai jisme RBI eligible collateral ke against banks ko short-term funds lend karta hai. [Q251]\n• RBI commercial banks ko short-term liquidity jis policy rate par provide karta hai, use Repo Rate kehte hain. [Q252]\n• Reverse Repo mein RBI banks se short-term funds absorb karta hai; historically RBI securities sell karke future repurchase agreement ke\nthrough liquidity absorb karta tha. [Q253]\n• Repo Rate RBI ke short-term lending rate se related hai; Gini Coefficient income/wealth distribution inequality ka measure hai. [Q254]\n• Bank Rate traditionally RBI ka rediscounting/lending reference rate hai aur monetary-policy stance ka important indicator raha hai. [Q255]\n• Repo Rate ya SLR increase contractionary effect de sakta hai aur inflation pressure reduce kar sakta hai; reverse repo/Bank Rate reduction\ncomparatively expansionary effect create kar sakti hai. [Q256]\n• Central bank Bank Rate raise kare to borrowing cost badhti hai, credit demand/expansion slow hota hai; ise tight ya dear-money policy\nsignal maana jata hai. [Q257]\n• Bank Rate reduction banks ke funding cost ko lower kar sakti hai, credit expansion aur liquidity ko support karti hai. [Q258]\n• RBI eligible bills/securities ko rediscount kar sakta hai; Bank Rate concept historically isi central-bank rediscounting function se linked hai.\n[Q259]\n• Bank Rate central bank ka official lending/rediscounting reference rate hai. [Q260]\n• Bank Rate RBI dwara commercial banks ko specified lending/rediscounting transactions par charged official rate se related hai. [Q261]\n• 2011-12 monetary-policy framework mein reverse repo rate ko repo rate se 1 percentage point below set karne ka automatic corridor\napproach adopt kiya gaya tha. [Q262]\n• 5 December 2019 ko Repo 5.15% aur Reverse Repo 4.90% tha; February 2026 context mein Repo 5.25%, SDF 5.00%, fixed Reverse\nRepo 3.35%, Bank Rate/MSF 5.50% the. [Q263]\n• 30 September 2014 ki RBI policy review ke time Repo Rate 8.00% thi. February 2026 context mein Repo 5.25%, SDF 5.00%, Bank\nRate/MSF 5.50%, SLR 18%, fixed Reverse Repo 3.35% aur CRR 3.00% the. [Q264]\n• October 2001 mein Bank Rate 7% se reduce karke 6.5% kiya gaya; February 2012 se Bank Rate ko MSF rate ke saath align kiya gaya.\n[Q265]\n• 29 September 2015 ko Bank Rate 7.75% tha; February 2026 context mein 5.50% tha. [Q266]\nEXAM TRAP — CRR Reduction Effects\n▸ CRR reduce hone par banks ke paas liquidity badhti hai, lending/investment aur money supply increase ho sakte hain; real investment\nrate fall hona necessary effect nahi hai. Isliye effects I, II aur III valid hain, IV nahi.\n▸ Reverse Repo Rate ya Bank Rate reduce karna expansionary effect de sakta hai aur inflationary pressure badha sakta hai; Repo Rate ya\nSLR increase karna generally contractionary hota hai.\n[Q243, Q256]\n\n\n\n\n12. RBI Governors, Legal Framework & Development Financial Institutions","• RBI Governor chronology: I. G. Patel (1977-82) -> Manmohan Singh (1982-85) -> C. Rangarajan (1992-97) -> D. Subbarao (2008-13).\n[Q267]\n• RBI Act, 1934 ke Section 8(1)(a) ke under Central Government Governor aur maximum four Deputy Governors appoint karti hai. [Q268]\n• Raja J. Chelliah RBI Governor nahi rahe; Manmohan Singh, C. Rangarajan aur Vimal Jalan RBI Governors rahe, aur Sanjay Malhotra 11\nDecember 2024 se Governor hain. [Q269]\n• Sanjay Malhotra ne 11 December 2024 ko RBI Governor ka office assume kiya; Shaktikanta Das 2018-24 aur Urjit Patel 2016-18 mein\nGovernor rahe. [Q270]\n• Committee on Financial Sector Assessment ko RBI Deputy Governor aur Government of India Finance Secretary jointly co-chair karte the;\n2009 self-assessment report public domain mein aayi. [Q271]\n• RBI ka accounting year ab April-March hai; pehle July-June financial year follow hota tha. [Q272]\n• RBI ne apna financial year July-June se align karke April-March kar diya. [Q273]\n• Negotiable Instruments Act 1881 mein pass hua aur 1 March 1882 se effective hua; Act ko time-to-time amend kiya gaya, including 2018\namendment. [Q274]\n• UTI mutual-fund/investment institution thi; Canbank Financial Services public-sector bank ki subsidiary thi; SBI commercial bank hai; aur\nU.P. State Financial Corporation State public-sector financial institution hai. In alag classification bases ki wajah se single unambiguous odd-\none-out nahi banta. [Q275]\n• Company limited by shares mein shareholders ki liability generally unke unpaid share amount tak limited hoti hai. [Q276]\n• LLP separate body corporate aur perpetual succession entity hai; partners ki maximum number par traditional partnership-style 20-member\ncap apply nahi hota, aur internal governance LLP agreement se decide hoti hai. [Q277]\n• India ka first International Financial Services Centre GIFT City, Gandhinagar, Gujarat mein SEZ framework ke under 2015 mein\noperationalise hua. [Q278]\n• IFCI 1 July 1948 ko IFCI Act, 1948 ke under establish hua; ye India ki first development financial institution thi aur industrial/infrastructure\nfinance provide karti thi. [Q279]\n• IDBI 1 July 1964 ko Industrial Development Bank of India Act, 1964 ke under establish hua; iska mandate industrial long-term finance,\ndevelopment support, coordination aur refinance tha. [Q280]\n• Institutional chronology: IFCI 1948, ICICI 1955, UTI February 1964 aur IDBI July 1964. [Q281]\n• Institutional chronology: IFCI 1948, ICICI January 1955, IDBI July 1964 aur NABARD July 1982. [Q282]\n• Pairs: IFCI - 1948; ICICI - 1955; IDBI - 1964; SIDBI - 1990. [Q283]\n• Pairs: ICICI - 1955; IDBI - 1964; EXIM Bank - 1982; BIFR - 1987. EXIM Bank Export-Import Bank of India Act, 1981 ke under 1982 mein\nestablish hua. [Q284]\n• Bank-establishment pairs: Allahabad Bank - 1865; Alliance Bank of Simla - 1874; Oudh Commercial Bank - 1881; Punjab National Bank -\n1894. Allahabad Bank ab Indian Bank mein merged hai. [Q285]\n• SAIL industrial/marketing organisation hai; SEBI, SIDBI aur NABARD respectively securities regulation, MSME development finance aur\nagriculture/rural development ke apex institutions hain. [Q286]\n• SBI commercial bank hai; NABARD, SIDBI aur State Cooperative Banks apne respective domains mein apex/development institutions ke\nroop mein function karte hain. [Q287]\n• Chronology: LIC 1956 -> UTI February 1964 -> IDBI July 1964 -> SEBI 12 April 1988. [Q288]\nEXAM TRAP — Current Governor & Ambiguous Classification\n▸ Sanjay Malhotra 11 December 2024 se RBI Governor hain. Shaktikanta Das 12 December 2018 se 10 December 2024 aur Urjit Patel 4\nSeptember 2016 se 11 December 2018 tak Governor rahe.\n▸ UTI mutual-fund/investment institution thi; Canbank Financial Services PSU-bank subsidiary thi; SBI commercial bank hai; U.P. State\nFinancial Corporation State public-sector financial institution hai. Different classification bases ki wajah se single unambiguous odd-one-out\nnahi banta.\n▸ Establishment chronology: LIC 1956 → UTI February 1964 → IDBI July 1964 → SEBI 12 April 1988. Numerical order 1 → 4 → 2 → 3\nbanta hai.\n[Q270, Q275, Q288]\n\n\n\n\n13. Insurance, IRDA, Provident Fund, SEBI & Mutual Funds\n\n• Life Insurance Corporation of India (LIC) 1956 mein LIC Act, 1956 ke under establish hua. [Q289]\n• R. N. Malhotra Committee (1993) ne insurance sector ke liye independent regulator recommend kiya; IRDA Act 1999 ke baad IRDA April\n2000 mein statutory regulator bana. [Q290]\n• India ke insurance sector ko Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India (IRDAI) regulate karta hai. [Q291]\n• IRDAI insurance companies aur insurance-sector regulation/supervision ka statutory authority hai. [Q292]\n• IRDAI ne October 2020 mein P. Umesh ki chairmanship mein panel banaya jo standard cyber-liability insurance product ki need examine\nkare. [Q293]\n• Actuaries insurance sector se closely associated professionals hain; wo uncertain future events ke financial risk/cost ko statistical methods\nse assess karte hain. [Q294]\n• Indemnity principle measurable loss ki compensation par based hai; health, motor, fire aur marine jaise non-life insurance generally\nindemnity contracts hote hain, life insurance nahi. [Q295]\n• Provident Fund contractual/statutory saving ka form hai; employee contribution future benefit ke liye accumulate hota hai aur\nGovernment/fund ke liye liability hota hai, ordinary income nahi. [Q296]\n• State Provident Fund receipts Public Account mein credit hote hain, kyunki Government un funds ko fiduciary/liability capacity mein hold\nkarti hai. [Q297]\n• 2010 ke context mein Pension Funds ki relative importance sabse zyada decline hui thi. [Q298]","• Forward Markets Commission (FMC) 1953 se commodity futures regulator tha; 28 September 2015 ko FMC SEBI ke saath merge ho gaya.\n[Q299]\n• SEBI mutual funds aur securities market regulate karta hai; 12 April 1988 ko non-statutory body ke roop mein start hua aur 1992 mein\nstatutory status mila. [Q300]\n• SEBI Act 1992 mein enact hua. [Q301]\n• Stock-market functioning aur securities trading ko SEBI regulate karta hai. [Q302]\n• Stock exchanges ka principal securities-market regulator SEBI hai. [Q303]\n• Capital market aur securities-market regulation SEBI ke jurisdiction mein aata hai. [Q304]\n• Stock-market operations ko SEBI regulate/supervise karta hai. [Q305]\n• Business Responsibility and Sustainability Report (BRSR) SEBI ka corporate sustainability-disclosure framework hai, RBI ka nahi. [Q306]\n• Private-sector mutual funds ko 1993 mein permission mili; Kothari Pioneer July 1993 mein registered first private-sector mutual fund tha,\nlater Franklin Templeton mein merge hua. [Q307]\n• 1998 mein UTI Unit Scheme-1964 ko stock-market slump ke karan major crisis face karna pada; scheme ke market value mein significant\nerosion hua. [Q308]\n• Unit Trust of India 1 February 1964 ko UTI Act, 1963 ke under establish hua, jiska objective small savings ko mobilise karke productive\ninvestment/industrial development support karna tha. [Q309]\n• 31 December 2025 AUM data mein SBI Mutual Fund India ka largest mutual-fund house tha; older period mein UTI dominant institution\nraha. [Q310]\n\n\n14. Stock Exchanges, Sensex, Capital Market & Financial Instruments\n\n• National Stock Exchange (NSE) November 1992 mein Pherwani Committee ki recommendation par establish hua; headquarters Mumbai\nmein hai. [Q311]\n• NSE 1992 mein establish hua; Government-backed major financial institutions including IDBI, IFCI, LIC, GIC aur SBI promoters mein\nshamil the. [Q312]\n• BSE Sensex 30 major stocks par based hai, jabki NSE Nifty 50 major stocks par based index hai. [Q313]\n• Sensex BSE ka 30-stock sensitive index hai; Sensex rise ka matlab index constituent shares ka overall weighted market value increase\nhona hai. [Q314]\n• BSE Sensex 30 major blue-chip companies par based index hai. [Q315]\n• BSE Sensex mein 30 constituent companies/stocks hote hain. [Q316]\n• BSE-GREENEX index mein 25 companies included hain. [Q317]\n• BSE-GREENEX 25-company index hai. [Q318]\n• 2000-01 mein NSE ka turnover Indian exchanges mein highest tha; March 2025 tak bhi NSE turnover ke basis par leading Indian stock\nexchange raha. [Q319]\n• Indian stock-market fluctuations par foreign fund flows, monetary/fiscal policy, global markets, industrial conditions aur domestic liquidity\njaise factors major impact daalte hain. [Q320]\n• Bombay Stock Exchange (BSE) Dalal Street, Mumbai, Maharashtra mein located hai. [Q321]\n• Bull rising share prices expect karta hai, jabki Bear falling share prices expect karta hai. [Q322]\n• Jo investor/speculator kisi security ka price girne ki expectation rakhta hai use Bear kaha jata hai. [Q323]\n• Traditional stock-market speculator types Bull, Bear, Stag aur Lame Duck hain; broker profession hai, speculator category nahi. [Q324]\n• Insider Trading mein connected person unpublished price-sensitive/internal information use karke securities trading se unfair advantage\nleta hai. [Q325]\n• Beta kisi stock/security ki market ke relative price volatility measure karta hai; Beta >1 generally market se higher sensitivity dikhata hai.\n[Q326]\n• Financial instruments contractual claims/obligations hote hain jo monetary asset, debt, equity ya derivative rights represent karte hain aur\ntrade/settle kiye ja sakte hain. [Q327]\n• Capital Market long-term funds ka market hai, jahan equity aur long-term debt instruments ke through capital raise hota hai. [Q328]\n• Call Money aur Treasury Bill market Money Market ka part hain; Government Bond aur Stock Market Capital Market ka part hain. [Q329]\n• Alternative Investment Fund (AIF) SEBI AIF Regulations, 2012 ke under privately pooled investment vehicle hai jo investors se funds collect\nkarke defined investment policy ke basis par invest karta hai. Hedge Funds Category III AIF aur Venture Capital Funds Category I AIF hote\nhain; Bonds debt securities aur Stocks company-ownership units hain, isliye is set mein sirf Hedge Funds aur Venture Capital AIFs hain.\n[Q330]\n• CCIL ne Collateralised Borrowing and Lending Obligation (CBLO) 20 January 2003 se introduce kiya; ye collateral-backed money-market\nborrowing/lending instrument tha. [Q331]\n• Money Market short-term funds/securities ka market hai, Capital Market long-term funds ka; Call Money extremely short-term/on-demand\nfinance hai. [Q332]\n• Money-market ecosystem mein institutions/instruments jaise banks, money-market mutual funds aur short-term securities participate karte\nhain; RBI regulator/monetary authority hai, ordinary market component nahi. [Q333]\n• Commercial Paper short-term unsecured promissory instrument hai, generally 7 days se 1 year maturity range mein issue hota hai. [Q334]\n• Commercial Paper corporate sector ka short-term unsecured credit instrument hai; India mein 1990 se use ho raha hai aur maturity 7 days\nse 1 year tak ho sakti hai. [Q335]\n• Company-investment risk hierarchy mein bondholders generally stockholders se lower risk face karte hain, kyunki debt claims ko equity se\npriority milti hai. [Q336]\n• Convertible Bond holder ko specified terms par bond ko issuer company ki equity shares mein convert karne ka option deta hai. [Q337]\n• 2023 mein Indian exchanges par equity-index options trading volume extremely high tha, around 84.3 billion contracts, jo global derivatives\nactivity ka major share tha. [Q338]","• Corporate Bonds companies ke debt instruments hain jo expansion, project finance ya working capital ke liye funds raise karte hain; mutual\nfunds, insurers aur pension funds major investors ho sakte hain. [Q339]\n• Bond debt instrument hai jisme investor issuer ko fixed period ke liye funds lend karta hai; Bond Yield investor ko bond/G-Sec par milne\nwala effective return hai. [Q340]\n• Inflation-Indexed Bond (IIB) principal/interest ko inflation se link karke purchasing power protect karta hai; India mein RBI ne 2013 mein\nWPI-linked IIBs issue kiye. [Q341]\n• CRISIL Ratings ke December 2023 estimate mein India ka corporate-bond market FY2023 ke around Rs 43 lakh crore se FY2030 tak Rs\n100-120 lakh crore range mein expand ho sakta hai. [Q342]\n• Blue-chip shares well-established, financially strong companies ke shares hote hain jinka earnings/dividend record aur market reputation\ngenerally strong hota hai. [Q343]\n• Sweat Equity Shares employees/directors ko concessional/special terms par unke know-how, intellectual property ya value contribution ke\nrecognition mein issue kiye ja sakte hain. [Q344]\n• Gilt-edged market Government aur high-grade semi-government securities ka market hai; in securities ko high safety aur comparatively\nstable value ke liye jaana jata hai. [Q345]\n• Government securities ko 'gilt-edged securities' bhi kaha jata hai. [Q346]\n• Sensex, BSE aur Nifty stock market se related terms hain; Structural Adjustment Programmes macroeconomic reforms/imbalances\ncorrection se related the, India mein 1991 reforms ke context mein. [Q347]\n• Nikkei 225 Tokyo Stock Exchange/Japan ka major stock-price index hai. [Q348]\n• S&P 500 U.S. exchanges par listed 500 large companies ka major stock-market index hai. [Q349]\n• Hang Seng Hong Kong ka stock index hai; South Korea ka major index KOSPI hai. [Q350]\n• Singapore ka major index Straits Times Index (STI) hai; Shanghai Stock Exchange Composite Index (SHCOMP) China ke Shanghai market\nse associated hai. [Q351]\n• International crude-oil benchmark prices mein Brent major reference index/benchmark hai. [Q352]\n• Recent years mein India jaise markets mein stock-market capitalization GDP ke 100% se upar bhi gaya hai; market-cap/GDP ratio\nvaluation aur market-depth context mein use hota hai. [Q353]\n• Company dividend subscribed/paid-up share capital ke eligible shareholders ko declare karti hai, kyunki wahi actually subscribed ownership\ncapital represent karta hai. [Q354]\n• Debenture holders company ke creditors hote hain, owners/shareholders nahi; unhe specified interest/payment terms milte hain. [Q355]\n• Company debenture transferable security ho sakta hai, jabki traditional life-insurance policy, bank fixed deposit aur Kisan Vikas Patra\nindividual holder ke naam se linked instruments hote hain. [Q356]\n• Balance Sheet kisi specific date par company ki financial position dikhati hai aur assets, liabilities aur equity ki size/composition show karti\nhai. [Q357]\n\n\n15. Cooperative Credit, Land Development Banks & Rural Finance\n\n• India ka first Land Development Bank 1920 mein Jhang, Punjab mein establish hua; Land Development Banks agriculture ke long-term\nimprovement loans se associated hain. [Q358]\n• Land Development Banks farmers ko land development, permanent agricultural improvements aur other long-term investment ke liye long-\nterm credit provide karte hain. [Q359]\n• Land Development Bank ka principal function farmers ko long-term agricultural loans provide karna hai. [Q360]\n• Agricultural sector ke long-term institutional credit mein Land Development Banks important role play karte hain. [Q361]\n• Land Development Banks cooperative credit structure ka part hain; State Cooperative Banks, Central Cooperative Banks/PACS aur land-\ndevelopment institutions rural cooperative finance mein role play karte hain. [Q362]\n• Traditional cooperative credit structure three-tier hai: State Cooperative Bank at State level, Central Cooperative Bank at district level aur\nPACS at village/local level. [Q363]\n• Cooperative credit-society structure traditionally three-tier model follow karta hai. [Q364]\n• Central Cooperative Banks district level par operate karte hain. [Q365]\n• Urban Cooperative Banks par dual-control framework raha hai: banking functions RBI regulate karta hai, jabki cooperative\nmanagement/registration State/Central cooperative authorities ke jurisdiction mein aate hain. [Q366]\n• March 2012 mein India mein Urban Cooperative Banks ki number 1,618 thi; consolidation ke chalte ye count time ke saath change hota\nraha. [Q367]\n• Consumer cooperative stores members dwara establish hote hain aur Registrar of Cooperative Societies ke under registered/regulatory\nframework follow karte hain. [Q368]\n• NABARD agriculture aur rural development ka apex refinance institution hai; ordinary public ko generally direct retail loans nahi deta. [Q369]\nEXAM TRAP — Urban Cooperative Bank Count / Direct Finance\n▸ March 2012 mein Urban Cooperative Banks ki sankhya 1,618 thi; consolidation ke process ke saath ye count time ke saath change hua.\n▸ NABARD apex refinance institution hai aur ordinarily general public ko direct loans nahi deta; RRBs aur commercial banks direct rural-\ncredit channels ke roop mein kaam karte hain.\n[Q367, Q369]\n\n\n\n\n16. NABARD, Regional Rural Banks & SHG-Bank Linkage\n\n• NABARD 12 July 1982 ko Sixth Five-Year Plan period mein establish hua. [Q370]","• NABARD ka full form National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development hai; ye agriculture/rural development refinance ka apex\ninstitution hai. [Q371]\n• Agriculture aur rural credit refinance ka apex institution NABARD hai. [Q372]\n• NABARD 12 July 1982 ko establish hua. [Q373]\n• NABARD 1982 mein establish hua. [Q374]\n• NABARD ka headquarters Mumbai mein hai. [Q375]\n• Agriculture aur rural-development credit needs ke liye NABARD apex development-finance institution hai. [Q376]\n• NABARD 12 July 1982 ko establish hua. [Q377]\n• NABARD Act, 1981 ke under NABARD 12 July 1982 ko establish hua; agriculture ke saath rural development, cottage industries aur non-\nfarm rural sectors ko bhi credit/refinance support deta hai. [Q378]\n• NABARD ka major role agriculture aur rural-development institutions ko refinance provide karna hai. [Q379]\n• Agriculture financing ka apex development institution NABARD hai. [Q380]\n• Agricultural credit system ka apex institution NABARD hai. [Q381]\n• Agriculture aur rural development ka apex development bank NABARD hai. [Q382]\n• Agricultural finance aur refinance system mein NABARD apex institution hai. [Q383]\n• NABARD = National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development. [Q384]\n• NABARD scheduled commercial banks, RRBs aur rural/cooperative credit institutions ko refinance provide karta hai; EXIM Bank ko\nrefinance karna iska normal function nahi. [Q385]\n• Rural Infrastructure Development Fund (RIDF), 1995-96, ko implement/manage karne ki nodal agency NABARD hai. [Q386]\n• India ke first Regional Rural Banks 2 October 1975 ko establish hue. [Q387]\n• Regional Rural Banks India mein 1975 mein establish hue. [Q388]\n• RRBs ka function rural deposits, credit aur development-oriented banking hai; Agricultural Refinance Corporation ke functions take over\nkarna RRB function nahi tha, ye NABARD formation se related institutional transfer tha. [Q389]\n• RRBs aur Land Development Banks rural households/farmers ko direct credit de sakte hain; NABARD apex refinance institution hai aur\ngenerally direct retail lending nahi karta. [Q390]\n• 'One State, One RRB' initiative ke under 26 RRBs ka merger 11 States/UTs mein announce hua aur 1 May 2025 se effective hua. [Q391]\n• Chronology: RRBs 1975 -> NABARD 1982 -> SHG-Bank Linkage Programme 1992-93 -> Kisan Credit Card 1998. [Q392]\n• SHG-Bank Linkage Programme NABARD ne 1992-93 mein initiate kiya; direct linkage/lending commercial banks, RRBs aur cooperative\nbanks ke through hota hai. [Q393]\n• SHG-Bank Linkage Programme NABARD ne start kiya, SBI ne nahi; SHG model mein group members collective responsibility lete hain aur\nRRBs/scheduled commercial banks SHGs ko support karte hain. [Q394]\n\nEXAM TRAP — NABARD vs RRB vs SHG Roles\n▸ NABARD apex refinance/development institution hai; RRBs aur other banks direct rural credit mein role play karte hain. SHG-Bank\nLinkage questions mein institution-role wording ko carefully distinguish karo.\n[Q370, Q371, Q372, Q373, Q374, Q375, Q376, Q377, Q378, Q379, Q380, Q381, Q382, Q383, Q384, Q385, Q386, Q387, Q388, Q389, Q390, Q391, Q392, Q393, Q394]\n\n\n\n\n17. Financial-Sector Committees, FSDC & Disinvestment\n\n• Bhandari Committee (1994) Regional Rural Banks ke restructuring/reform se related thi. [Q395]\n• Narasimham Committee-I (1991) ne financial-sector reforms ke under CRR aur SLR ko reduce/rationalise karne ki recommendation di.\n[Q396]\n• Narasimham Committee Indian banking-sector aur financial-sector reforms se associated hai. [Q397]\n• Narasimham Committees 1991 aur 1998/second reform phase mein banking-system efficiency, prudential norms aur structural reforms par\nmajor recommendations di gayi. [Q398]\n• Planning Commission ne 2007 mein Dr. Raghuram Rajan ki chairmanship mein Committee on Financial Sector Reforms banayi; report\n2008 mein submit hui. [Q399]\n• Raghuram Rajan Committee financial-sector reforms se related thi. [Q400]\n• RBI ne new-bank licence applications scrutinise karne ke liye former Governor Bimal Jalan ki chairmanship mein high-level panel banaya;\npanel ne February 2014 mein report submit ki. [Q401]\n• Disinvestment Commission 1996 mein G. V. Ramakrishna ki chairmanship mein constitute hua; disinvestment mein Government PSU/PSE\nki equity holding ka ek hissa sell karti hai. [Q402]\n• Government ne 1993 mein Rangarajan Committee constitute ki, jo public-sector enterprises ke disinvestment policy se related thi. [Q403]\n• Rangarajan Committee disinvestment policy/reforms se associated thi. [Q404]\n• 2007 mein Prof. Abhijit Sen ki chairmanship mein WPI series revise karne ke liye Working Group bana; base year, item selection, weights\naur price methodology par recommendations di gayi. [Q405]\n• Financial Stability and Development Council (FSDC) December 2010 mein establish hua; objectives financial stability, financial-sector\ndevelopment aur regulators ke beech coordination strengthen karna hain. [Q406]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Committees are Topic-Specific\n▸ Narasimham, Raghuram Rajan, Bimal Jalan, Rangarajan aur WPI Working Group alag financial-sector, disinvestment aur market topics\nse linked hain; committee name ko generic reform tag na samjho.\n[Q395, Q396, Q397, Q398, Q399, Q400, Q401, Q402, Q403, Q404, Q405, Q406]","18. Financial-Inclusion Schemes, KCC, Ombudsman & Credit Rating\n\n• Swabhimaan financial-inclusion programme 10 February 2011 ko launch hua; focus unbanked rural areas mein banking services doorstep\ntak pahunchana tha. [Q407]\n• Swabhimaan Scheme rural banking aur financial inclusion se related thi. [Q408]\n• Swabhimaan Scheme February 2011 mein unbanked villages ko banking access dene ke liye launch hui; Business Correspondents/Bank\nMitras village-bank interface ke roop mein kaam karte the. [Q409]\n• Union Budget 2015-16 mein Gold Monetisation Scheme aur Sovereign Gold Bond Scheme propose kiye gaye, taaki idle gold ko financial\nsystem mein mobilise kiya ja sake. [Q410]\n• Kisan Credit Card Scheme 1998-99 Budget mein announce hui aur August 1998 mein launch hui; farmers ko consumption aur\ncrop/investment credit access commercial banks, cooperative banks aur RRBs ke through milta hai. [Q411]\n• Kisan Credit Card (KCC) Scheme 1998-99 mein implement hui. [Q412]\n• Banking Ombudsman RBI appoint karta tha aur NRI account-related complaints bhi hear kar sakta tha; Ombudsman award/order ke\nagainst RBI Appellate Authority ke paas appeal possible thi, isliye order automatically final-and-binding nahi tha. [Q413]\n• India mein Credit Rating Agencies ko SEBI, SEBI (Credit Rating Agencies) Regulations, 1999 ke under regulate karta hai. [Q414]\n• CRISIL, CARE aur ICRA India ki major Credit Rating Agencies hain. [Q415]\n• Establishment years: CRISIL 1987; ICRA 1991; CARE 1993. [Q416]\n• CRISIL credit-rating agency hai jo companies/instruments ki creditworthiness aur default-risk assess karti hai. [Q417]\n\n\n19. Money/Banking Terms, Markets & Business-Economics Basics\n\n• Phillips Curve short run mein inflation aur unemployment ke beech generally inverse relationship show karta hai; A. W. Phillips ne 1958\nmein is relationship ko highlight kiya. [Q418]\n• Traditional banking terminology mein 'Smart Money' credit-card/smart-card based electronic payment context se associated raha hai. [Q419]\n• Plastic Money cash alternative payment cards ko refer karta hai; examples credit card, debit card, smart card aur ATM/debit cards hain.\n[Q420]\n• Plastic Money term mainly bank-issued credit/debit/payment cards ke liye use hota hai. [Q421]\n• UPI ka full form Unified Payments Interface hai; ek mobile app se multiple participating bank accounts ko link karke instant\npayments/transfer possible karta hai. [Q422]\n• Banking mein PIN ka full form Personal Identification Number hai; ATM/debit-card transactions mein customer authentication ke liye\nnumeric secret code use hota hai. [Q423]\n• Credit card prepaid payment instrument nahi hai; ye post-paid credit facility hai jisme cardholder purchases karta hai aur issuer ko later\npayment karta hai. [Q424]\n• Market woh mechanism/place hai jahan buyers aur sellers goods/services exchange karte hain aur price demand, supply, cost aur market\nconditions se determine hota hai. [Q425]\n• Market-oriented economy consumer choice aur consumption ko encourage kar sakti hai; 'consumerism' market/capitalist consumption\npattern se associated concept hai. [Q426]\n• Buyer's Market mein sellers/supply buyers/demand se zyada hote hain; excess supply ke karan buyers ki bargaining power strong aur\nprices lower rehne ki tendency hoti hai. [Q427]\n• Accounting ko 'language of business' kaha jata hai kyunki ye business operations ke financial results ko owners, managers, investors aur\nother stakeholders tak communicate karta hai. [Q428]\n• Contract law mein condition contract ke main purpose ke liye essential stipulation hoti hai; condition breach par aggrieved party ko contract\nrepudiate karne ka right mil sakta hai. [Q429]\n• Fixed Capital long-term productive assets jaise land/building, machinery, equipment aur tools ko refer karta hai jo multiple production cycles\nmein use hote hain. [Q430]\n• Key ratios: Stock Turnover = COGS/Average Stock; Current Ratio = Current Assets/Current Liabilities; Acid-Test Ratio = Liquid\nAssets/Current Liabilities; ROCE context mein operating profit ko capital employed se compare kiya jata hai. [Q431]\n• Auction/dynamic-pricing model B2B aur C2C dono e-commerce contexts mein operate kar sakta hai. [Q432]\n• B2B mein transaction two businesses ke beech hota hai; B2C mein business directly consumer ko sell karta hai. Product-based FMCG\ncompany B2C model ka example ho sakti hai. [Q433]\n• Very short-period/daily market mein supply immediately increase nahi ho pati, isliye price par demand ka strong influence hota hai;\nvegetables/fish jaise perishables typical examples hain. [Q434]\n• Supply-side economics producer/supplier perspective se incentives, production, tax rates aur aggregate supply par focus karta hai. [Q435]\n• Jab Total Product maximum aur constant point par hota hai, Marginal Product zero hota hai. [Q436]\n• Consumer equilibrium tab hota hai jab given income aur prices ke under consumer available budget se maximum possible satisfaction\nachieve karta hai. [Q437]\n• Law of Demand mein own price aur quantity demanded generally inverse related hote hain; substitute ki price rise demand badha sakti hai,\ncomplement ki price rise demand ghata sakti hai, aur inferior good ki demand income rise par fall kar sakti hai. [Q438]\n• Demand constant ho aur supply increase ho to normal market mein commodity price generally fall karta hai; supply aur price ka\nrelationship, other things equal, inverse direction mein hota hai. [Q439]\n• Production fall, effective demand rise ya money supply expansion general price level ko upward pressure de sakte hain. [Q440]\n• Money supply unchanged ho aur money demand increase ho to interest rate generally rise karta hai. [Q441]\n• MRP packaged commodity ka legally declared maximum retail price hai, taxes inclusive; seller MRP se lower price charge kar sakta hai,\nhigher nahi. [Q442]","• General price rise se consumers ki purchasing power girti hai; traders/producers ko some conditions mein benefit mil sakta hai, while\nconsumers higher cost bear karte hain. [Q443]\n• P/V Ratio = Contribution/Sales = (Sales - Variable Cost)/Sales; ise 1 - Variable Cost/Sales ya Change in Profit/Change in Sales ke form\nmein bhi express kiya ja sakta hai. [Q444]\n• Shadow Price ya imputed price public goods/capital-project appraisal mein economic opportunity value show karta hai; concept J.\nTinbergen aur Hollis se associated bataya gaya hai. [Q445]\n• Public Goods ordinary market exchange se efficiently provide nahi hote; national defence, public administration aur some public\ninfrastructure examples hain, jabki car private good hai. [Q446]\n• Government kisi good ko consumer ko free provide kare tab bhi resource cost vanish nahi hoti; financing tax-paying public bear kar sakta\nhai, isliye opportunity cost society/taxpayers par shift hoti hai. [Q447]\n• Trade goods/services ke buying-selling/exchange ko refer karta hai, jiska purpose buyers aur sellers ke beech mutual benefit create karna\nhai. [Q448]\n• Commerce broad concept hai: Trade plus trade ko assist karne wali activities, jaise transport, banking, insurance, warehousing aur\ncommunication. [Q449]\n\n\n                                    Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · Money & Banking Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0457","t":"10 Human Development","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["HUMAN DEVELOPMENT — FULL MIND MAP\n                              Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\nTopics in this chapter:\n 1. Development — Meaning, Stages & Measures\n 2. PQLI — Physical Quality of Life Index\n 3. UNDP, HDI Origin & Capability Approach\n 4. HDI — Dimensions, Components & Methodology\n 5. Global HDI — Report-Year & Ranking Facts\n 6. Nutrition & Human-Centred Development Warnings\n 7. Gender Inequality & Comparative Human Development\n 8. Gross National Happiness & World Happiness Report\n 9. Universal Declaration of Human Rights — Key Rights\n 10. India — State Human Development Reports & Rankings\n 11. Bare Necessities Index (BNI)\n 12. State Health Index — NITI Aayog\n 13. Education Development & School Enrolment\n\n\n 1. Development — Meaning, Stages & Measures\n\n• Auguste Comte ne apni book 'The Positive Philosophy' mein social development ka three-stage law diya: theological →\n metaphysical → positive stage. [Q1]\n• Development ka matlab sirf income growth nahi, balki standard of living aur welfare mein improvement bhi hai. Per-capita income\n ka increase akela sufficient measure nahi hai; Income growth welfare improvement ka ek hissa hai, complete definition nahi. [Q2]\n• Economic Survey 2018-19 ke wording mein Indian states ki HDI aur SDG rankings ke beech positive correlation hai, kyunki SDG ke\n basic dimensions education aur health se closely interrelated hain. [Q3]\n• Development measures ka chronological order: Per Capita Income → PQLI → HDI. HDI ko UNDP ki Human Development Report\n mein 1990 se publish kiya gaya. [Q4]\n\n\n 2. PQLI — Physical Quality of Life Index\n\n• PQLI Morris D. Morris ne develop kiya. Isme life expectancy, infant mortality rate aur literacy ko combine kiya jata hai. [Q5, Q6]\n• PQLI scale ka maximum 100 aur minimum 1 hai; 100 ki taraf movement better physical quality of life ko indicate karta hai. [Q5]\n\n\n 3. UNDP, HDI Origin & Capability Approach\n\n• Human Development Report (HDR) UNDP publish karta hai. Pehli HDR 1990 mein aayi aur isi report ke under HDI ka first version\n develop/publish hua. [Q7, Q8, Q11]\n• HDI development ka leadership credit Pakistani economist Mahbub-ul-Haq ko diya jata hai; Prof. Amartya Sen aur other economists\n ne collaborate kiya. HDI development ka standard attribution Mahbub-ul-Haq ke leadership aur Amartya Sen sahit multiple\n economists ke contribution ko deta hai. [Q10, Q11]\n• Prof. Amartya Sen se 'Capability Approach' associated hai. UNDP ne development ke human aspect par early focus kiya aur\n income ke saath health aur education ko bhi important maana. [Q12, Q13]\n• UNDP ne HDR 1996 mein Capability Poverty Measure (CPM) introduce kiya. Isme deprivation in healthy/nourished life, healthy\n reproduction aur education/knowledge measure ki jati hai; child malnutrition aur 15+ female illiteracy relevant indicators hain. [Q9]\n\n\n 4. HDI — Dimensions, Components & Methodology\n\n• HDI ke three core dimensions health, education aur standard of living/income hain. Current methodology mein health = life\n expectancy at birth; education = mean years + expected years of schooling; living standard = per-capita GNI on PPP basis. [Q14,\n Q19, Q22]\n• 2010 se pehle HDI income dimension mein per-capita GDP use hota tha. older methodology mein literacy + life expectancy + real\n per-capita GDP adjusted for purchasing power relevant combination tha. [Q14, Q15]\n• Older HDI methodology mein life-expectancy goalposts minimum 25 years aur maximum 85 years liye jate the. [Q16]\n• Social inequality traditional HDI ke three direct components mein nahi thi. Isi tarah 'health and nutrition' separate component ke\n roop mein nahi tha aur urbanization bhi 1990 HDI calculation ka component nahi tha. [Q17, Q18, Q20, Q21]\n• HDI ranking improvement context mein more children going to school + more adults becoming literate (3 and 4) relevant\n combination tha, kyunki education dimension improve hota hai. [Q30]\n\n\n 5. Global HDI — Report-Year & Ranking Facts","• HDR 2021-22 (2021 data) mein Hong Kong rank 4, Australia 5, Japan 19, Russia 52 aur India 132 tha. HDR 2025 ke HDI 2023 data\nmein Australia 7, Hong Kong 8, Japan 23, Russia 64 aur India 130 hai. [Q23]\n• HDR 2021-22 mein India ka rank 2020 ke 130 se 2021 mein 132 hua. HDR 2025 mein HDI 2023 ke liye India rank 130 hai; 2022\nrank revised 133 diya gaya hai. Report year aur data year ko alag rakhein. [Q24]\n• HDI 2023 ranks: China 78, Brazil 84, South Africa 106 aur India 130. HDI rankings ko hamesha relevant report/data year ke saath\npadhein. [Q25]\n• Human Development Report 2020 ke historical mein India ka HDI rank 131 diya gaya tha. [Q26]\n• HDR 2015 data: India 130th among 188 countries; HDI life expectancy, education aur per-capita income par based; India BRICS\ncountries mein lowest. [Q27]\n• HDR 2018 mein Norway HDI ranking mein top tha. HDR 2025 ke HDI 2023 data mein Iceland first, Norway aur Switzerland joint-\nsecond, South Sudan 193rd aur India 130th hai. [Q28]\n\nEXAM TRAP — HDI Rank/Year Context\n▸2014 aur 186 countries ka combination relevant HDR editions se match nahi karta.\n▸HDR 2015 (2014 data): India rank 130/188; HDR 2014 (2013 data): India rank 135/187. Report-year aur data-year ko saath padhein.\n▸Historical entry disputed hai, kyunki printed year/country-count relevant HDR editions ke saath align nahi karta.\n[Q29]\n\n\n\n\nEXAM TRAP — HDI Rank — Report Year vs Data Year\n▸HDR release-year aur HDI data-year alag ho sakte hain; ranking ko hamesha dono context ke saath padhein.\n▸HDR 2025 / HDI 2023 ke updated ranks older report-year figures ko historical context mein hi chhodte hain.\n[Q23, Q24, Q25, Q26, Q27, Q28, Q29]\n\n\n\n\n6. Nutrition & Human-Centred Development Warnings\n\n• Human Development Report 1997 ke fact 4 saal se kam age ke Indian children mein 53% ko proper nutrition nahi milta tha. [Q31]\n• HDR 1996 mein UNDP ne developing countries ke economic-development path ke undesirable forms ko 'jobless, rootless, ruthless,\nvoiceless and futureless' kaha aur policy-makers ko in issues par dhyan dene ko kaha. [Q32]\n\n\n7. Gender Inequality & Comparative Human Development\n\n• Gender Inequality Index (GII) UNDP ki Human Development Report 2010 mein introduce hua. [Q33]\n• Asian-country table se clear hai ki higher adult education har case mein automatically lower infant mortality ensure nahi karta;\nMalaysia aur Indonesia dono 84% adult education ke bawajood IMR 11 vs 47 the. [Q34]\n• Usi table mein Korea ka human-development performance best tha: life expectancy 71.7 years, adult education 98% aur infant\nmortality 6 — displayed countries mein favourable combination. [Q35]\n• HDR 2025 India ki life expectancy at birth 1990 ke 58.6 years se badhkar 2023 mein 72 years hui; men 70.5 aur women 73.6 years\ndiye gaye hain. [Q36]\n\n\n8. Gross National Happiness & World Happiness Report\n\n• Bhutan ne 1972 mein King Jigme Singye Wangchuck ki initiative par Gross National Happiness (GNH) ko progress/well-being\nindicator ke roop mein choose kiya. GNH/happiness measurement context mein life satisfaction, life expectancy aur environment ko\nelements batata hai. [Q37, Q38, Q39]\n• World Happiness Report 2024 mein India 143 countries mein 126th tha. China, Nepal, Pakistan aur Myanmar India se upar the; Sri\nLanka, Bangladesh aur Afghanistan neeche. 2025 report mein India 118/147 tha. [Q40]\n• World Happiness Report 2023 mein India 126th out of 137 tha. World Happiness Report 2025 mein Finland, Denmark aur Iceland\ntop three hain; India 118th hai, aur Afghanistan, Sierra Leone aur Lebanon bottom three hain. [Q41]\n\n\n9. Universal Declaration of Human Rights — Key Rights\n\n• Universal Declaration of Human Rights mein Article 26(1)—right to education; Article 21(2)—equal access to public service; Article\n25(1)—right to food/basic standard of living se related hai. [Q42]\n\n\n10. India — State Human Development Reports & Rankings\n\n• State-level Human Development Report release karne wala India ka first state Madhya Pradesh tha; first report 1995 mein release\nhui. [Q43]","• Indian Human Development Report ke sample-village data framework mein education-related, health-related aur\ninfrastructure/amenities indices included the; unemployment-related index included nahi tha. [Q44]\n• 2001 HDI data mein Kerala highest among these States tha; data 2019 context mein bhi Kerala ko top aur Bihar ko bottom batata\nhai. 'Most developed state' context mein bhi Kerala top tha. [Q45, Q46]\n• 2001 HDI descending order among compared states: Kerala → Punjab → Uttar Pradesh → Bihar. Data 2019 ranking context mein\nKerala 1st, Punjab 4th, UP 28th, Bihar 29th diya gaya. [Q47]\n• Kerala HDI ranking mein first tha aur 2011 Census framing mein unemployment rate highest tha; high unemployment Kerala ke\nHDI-top rank ka direct cause nahi hai. [Q48]\n• IHDR 2011 ke 2008 data mein Uttar Pradesh rank 18, Jharkhand 19, Bihar 21 aur Odisha 22 tha. 2019 ranking context mein Uttar\nPradesh 28th tha. [Q49]\n• 2019 mein Bihar HDI value 0.574 thi — Indian states mein lowest; Kerala 0.782 ke saath top tha. [Q50]\n\n\n11. Bare Necessities Index (BNI)\n\n• Economic Survey 2020-21 ne rural, urban aur all-India levels par Bare Necessities Index (BNI) framework outline kiya. BNI 26\nindicators ko five dimensions — water, sanitation, housing, micro-environment, other facilities — par evaluate karta hai. [Q51]\n• BNI all states ke liye 2012 aur 2018 dono years ke liye NSO 69th aur 76th round data se prepare hua; BNI 2012 aur 2018 dono\nrounds use karta hai; six-dimension description BNI framework se align nahi karti. [Q51]\n\n\n12. State Health Index — NITI Aayog\n\n• NITI Aayog ne State Health Index ka fourth edition (2019-20) December 2021 mein release kiya. Overall 'Small States' category\nmein Mizoram top tha; Updated figures mein Tripura next diya gaya. [Q52]\n• Annual incremental performance mein large states ke top three Uttar Pradesh, Assam, Telangana; small states mein Mizoram aur\nMeghalaya ne maximum incremental progress record ki. [Q52]\n\n\n13. Education Development & School Enrolment\n\n• Educational Development Index (February 2011) mein Kerala, Tamil Nadu, Punjab aur Delhi top four the. School Education Quality\nIndex 2019 mein large states mein Kerala first aur Tamil Nadu second tha; Jharkhand bottom par tha. Small states mein Tripura aur\nUTs mein Chandigarh top the. [Q53]\n• 6-14 age group mein school enrolment earlier data mein around 93% tha; ASER Rural 2024 mein enrolment 98.1% report hua.\n[Q54]\n\n\n\n                             Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · Human Development Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0458","t":"11 Employment and Welfare Schemes","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["EMPLOYMENT & WELFARE SCHEMES — FULL MIND MAP\n                                 Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\nTopics in this chapter:\n 1. Flagship Welfare, Health Insurance & Public Health\n 2. Nutrition, POSHAN, ICDS & Scheme Chronology\n 3. Skill Development, Innovation & Education Initiatives\n 4. Rural Services, Financial Inclusion, DBT & Basic Amenities\n 5. Women, Girl Child, Maternity & Empowerment Schemes\n 6. Rural Infrastructure, Bharat Nirman, Jal Jeevan & AMRUT\n 7. Food, Disability, Accessibility & Rural Infrastructure\n 8. Employment & Rural Anti-Poverty Programmes — Historical Evolution\n 9. MGNREGA, Employment Guarantee & Urban Employment\n 10. Community Development, Extension & Antyodaya\n 11. Pension, Insurance & Social-Security Schemes\n 12. Swachh Bharat, Sanitation & WaterCredit\n 13. NSAP, Minimum Needs, SWAMITVA & Land Records\n 14. Housing Schemes — PMAY, Indira Awas & Rajiv Awas\n 15. School Education, Literacy, Mid-Day Meal & Inclusive Education\n 16. Livelihood Training, Minority/Tribal Initiatives, MPLADS & SAGY\n 17. International Days, Labour, Social Change & Rural Institutions\n 18. Aadhaar, PURA, Rehabilitation, Disability Institutions & Youth\n\n\n 1. Flagship Welfare, Health Insurance & Public Health\n\n• Atmanirbhar Bharat Abhiyaan ki announcement Government ne 12 May 2020 ko ₹20 lakh crore ke special economic package ke roop mein\nki. Iska objective country ko self-reliant banana aur local manufacturing par focus badhana tha. [Q1]\n• NITI Aayog ka Aspirational Districts Programme January 2018 mein Prime Minister Narendra Modi ne launch kiya. Iska aim country ke 112\nunderdeveloped districts ka rapid aur effective transformation hai. [Q2]\n• Garib Kalyan Rojgar Abhiyaan Prime Minister Narendra Modi ne 20 June 2020 ko Bihar ke Khagaria district se video conferencing ke\nthrough launch kiya. 125-day campaign ka focus large-scale employment aur rural public works par tha. [Q3]\n• PM CARES Fund (Prime Minister's Citizen Assistance and Relief in Emergency Situations Fund) ko COVID-19 jaise emergencies ke liye\ndedicated fund aur affected persons ko relief dene ke purpose se public charitable trust ke roop mein establish/register kiya gaya. [Q4]\n• Prime Minister Narendra Modi ne 27 September 2021 ko video conferencing ke through Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission (ABDM) launch\nkiya. Ismein citizens ko Ayushman Bharat Health Account number mil sakta hai, jise digital health records se link kiya ja sakta hai. [Q5]\n• Constitution ke under health State subject hai; isliye har State ka apna health-care delivery system hota hai jisme public aur private\ninstitutions dono shamil ho sakte hain. [Q6]\n• Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana, jise ab Ayushman Bharat Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana (ABPM-JAY) kaha jata hai, September\n2018 mein launch hui. [Q7]\n• Ayushman Bharat PM-JAY ke under health-insurance cover up to ₹5 lakh per family diya jata hai, per person nahi. Centre-State\ncontribution ratio 60:40 hai; Himalayan aur North-Eastern States ke liye 90:10 hai. [Q8]\n• Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana ko Prime Minister Narendra Modi ne 23 September 2018 ko Ranchi, Jharkhand mein launch kiya.\nABPM-JAY ke under 12 crore se zyada families ko per year up to ₹5 lakh free-treatment cover diya jata hai. [Q9]\n• Ayushman Bharat Pradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana 23 September 2018 ko launch hui. Scheme empanelled public/private hospitals mein\nsecondary aur tertiary treatment ke liye per family ₹5 lakh annual cover deti hai; National Health Authority implementing agency hai. [Q10]\n• Rashtriya Swasthya Bima Yojana originally Ministry of Labour and Employment ki health-insurance scheme thi. Iski announcement October\n2007 mein hui aur implementation 1 April 2008 se start hua. [Q11]\n• Rashtriya Swasthya Bima Yojana unorganised-sector workers ko cover karti thi. 1 April 2015 se implementation responsibility Ministry of\nHealth and Family Welfare ko transfer hui; usse pehle Ministry of Labour and Employment handle karta tha. [Q12]\n• Hospitalisation package mein admitted disease ke tests, medicines aur consultation ka cost admission se 5 days pehle aur discharge ke 10\ndays baad tak cover hota hai. Rajasthan ki Mukhyamantri Chiranjeevi Swasthya Bima Yojana 1 May 2021 se start hui; initial ₹5 lakh family\ncover ko Budget 2023-24 mein ₹25 lakh tak raise kiya gaya. [Q13]\n• Ayushman Bharat-Mahatma Gandhi Rajasthan Swasthya Bima Yojana 30 January 2021 se effective hua, Bhamashah Swasthya Bima\nYojana aur Ayushman Bharat PM-JAY ke integration ke baad. 13 December 2019 se ordinary illnesses ke liye ₹50,000 aur serious illnesses\nke liye ₹4.50 lakh cover tha; Rajasthan Budget 2023-24 mein cover ₹25 lakh tak raise hua. 1 May 2021 se expanded form Mukhyamantri\nChiranjeevi Swasthya Bima Yojana ke naam se chala aur February 2024 mein Mukhyamantri Ayushman Arogya Yojana naam diya gaya.\n [Q14]\n• Ayushman Sahakar 19 October 2020 ko launch hua. Scheme NCDC ne Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers Welfare ke under formulate ki,\n jabki launch Government of India ke finance-related framework se hua. [Q15]\n• Mission Indradhanush ko Ministry of Health and Family Welfare ne 25 December 2014 ko children aur pregnant women ke vaccination ke\n liye launch kiya. [Q16]\n• National Rural Health Mission (NRHM) 12 April 2005 ko rural population ko effective health facilities aur universal public-health access\n dene ke liye launch hua; original programme period 2012 tak tha. [Q17]\n• National Rural Health Mission 2005 mein launch hua, jab Tenth Five-Year Plan (2002-07) chal raha tha; ise Eleventh Plan mein start hua\n nahi maana ja sakta. [Q18]\n• National Rural Health Mission April 2005 mein launch hua, jab Tenth Five-Year Plan (2002-07) chal raha tha. [Q19]\n• NRHM ka infant-mortality target 2012 tak 28 per 1,000 live births tha. [Q20]\n• NRHM ke under trained community health worker ko ASHA—Accredited Social Health Activist—kaha jata hai. NRHM 12 April 2005 ko\n Ministry of Health and Family Welfare ne launch kiya tha. [Q21]\n• ASHA ki functions mein women ko antenatal check-up ke liye health facility tak accompany karna, pregnancy-testing kit ka use aur\n nutrition/immunisation information dena shamil hai; childbirth conduct karna ASHA ka prescribed function nahi hai. [Q22]\n• U-WIN (Universal Immunization-Win) Ministry of Health and Family Welfare ka digital platform hai, jo Universal Immunisation Programme\n ke vaccination services ko digitise karta hai. [Q23]","• India ki National Health Policy ka earlier version 1983 mein announce hua. Later Ministry of Health and Family Welfare ne health-sector\nreforms ke liye National Health Policy 2017 announce ki. [Q24]\n• Seven priority areas mein Swachh Bharat Abhiyaan; balanced nutritious diet + regular exercise; tobacco, alcohol aur narcotic substances\nse prevention; rail-road accident deaths reduce karna; Nirbhaya Nari ke through women harassment par action; workplace stress/safety\nimprovement; aur indoor-outdoor air pollution reduction include the. [Q25]\n• 2009-10 mein India ka public-health expenditure 1.09% of GDP tha—Centre 0.39% aur States 0.70%. National Health Policy 2017 ka target\nhealth expenditure ko roughly 1.2% se 2.5% of GDP tak 2025 by raise karna tha. [Q26]\n• NFHS-3 (2005-06) mein private medical sector urban households ke ~70% aur rural households ke ~63% ka main healthcare source tha;\nNFHS-5 (2019-21) mein ye shares 51.8% aur 46.4% rahe. [Q27]\n• Economic Survey 2025-26 ke 2019-23 data mein India ki life expectancy at birth 70.3 years thi—males 68.5 years aur females 72.5 years.\nOlder data mein male 65.8 aur female 68.1 years jaise figures milte hain; year-context alag rakhein. [Q28]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Health Scheme Facts & Targets\n▸PM CARES ke listed points mein clear factual inconsistency nahi milti.\n▸NRHM ka infant-mortality target 2012 tak 28 per 1,000 live births tha.\n[Q4, Q20]\n\n\n\n\n2. Nutrition, POSHAN, ICDS & Scheme Chronology\n\n• National Nutrition Mission/POSHAN Abhiyaan ko 8 March 2018 ko Jhunjhunu, Rajasthan se launch kiya gaya aur Ministry of Women and\nChild Development implement karta hai. Focus children, adolescent girls, pregnant women aur lactating mothers ki nutrition improve karna\nhai. [Q29]\n• September ko har saal Rashtriya Poshan Maah ke roop mein observe kiya jata hai. POSHAN Abhiyaan 8 March 2018 ko Jhunjhunu,\nRajasthan se launch hua aur nutrition ko national-development agenda mein priority dene wala multi-ministerial convergence mission hai.\n[Q30]\n• Poshan Pakhwada April 2025 mein POSHAN Abhiyaan ke under two-week programme tha. Main objective women aur children ke liye\nhealthy/nutritious diet promote karke malnutrition reduce karna tha. [Q31]\n• National Oral Health Programme nutrition programme nahi hai; baaki three programmes National Nutrition Programmes hain. [Q32]\n• Mitti Cafe ek non-profit social initiative aur cafe chain hai jo persons with disabilities ko training aur employment dekar livelihood aur dignity\npromote karta hai. Cafes ko physical, intellectual aur mental disabilities wale adults manage karte hain. [Q33]\n• Shabari Sankalp Abhiyaan National Nutrition Mission, ab Mission POSHAN 2.0, se related hai. Iska objective malnutrition eradicate karna\nhai aur programme Uttar Pradesh mein implement ho raha hai. [Q34]\n• Census 2011 mein India mein 10-19 years age-group ke 253 million adolescents the, yani total population ka one-fifth se slightly zyada.\nMinistry of Health and Family Welfare ne adolescent development ke liye 7 January 2014 ko Rashtriya Kishor Swasthya Karyakram (RKSK)\nlaunch kiya. [Q35]\n• Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS) 2 October 1975 ko launch hua flagship Government programme hai. Ye 0-6 year children\naur pregnant/lactating mothers ko six services deta hai—supplementary nutrition, pre-school non-formal education, nutrition-health\neducation, immunisation, health check-up aur referral services; family planning in six services mein nahi hai. [Q36]\n• Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS) Scheme 2 October 1975 se Ministry of Women and Child Development ke through\nimplement hui. [Q37]\n• Central Government ne Integrated Child Development Services (ICDS) programme 2 October 1975 ko start kiya. [Q38]\n• ICDS mein free books aur school uniforms distribute karna included nahi hai. October 1975 se programme 0-6 year children aur\npregnant/lactating mothers ko six core services provide karta hai. [Q39]\n• Important launch-year pairs hain: National Programme for Control of Blindness - 1976; Integrated Child Development Scheme - 1975;\nNational Leprosy Eradication Programme - 1983; National Rural Health Mission - 2005. [Q40]\n• Launch years: PM Jan Arogya Abhiyaan—2018; PM Vaya Vandana Yojana—2017; PM Rojgar Protsahan Yojana—2016; Swachh Bharat\nAbhiyaan—2014. PM Rojgar Protsahan Yojana 2016 mein launch hui, 2015 mein nahi. [Q41]\n• Important scheme-year pairs hain: Pradhan Mantri Garib Kalyan Package - 2020; Muskaan Scheme - 2021; Mission Indradhanush - 2014;\nPradhan Mantri Jan Arogya Yojana - 2018. [Q42]\n• Launch timeline mein: Swachh Bharat Mission - 2 October 2014; Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana - 15 July 2015; Pradhan Mantri\nGarib Kalyan Yojana - 2016; Pradhan Mantri Mahila Shakti Kendra Yojana - November 2017. [Q43]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Scheme Launch Years\n▸National Oral Health Programme nutrition programme nahi hai.\n▸PM Rojgar Protsahan Yojana 2016 mein launch hui; 2015 timeline correct nahi hai.\n[Q32, Q41]\n\n\n\n\n3. Skill Development, Innovation & Education Initiatives\n\n• India aur Australia ne 23 May 2023 ko Migration and Mobility Partnership Arrangement (MMPA) agreement kiya. Ye bilateral framework\ntwo-way migration/mobility promote karta hai aur illegal/irregular migration ko address karta hai. [Q44]\n• Department of Higher Education, Ministry of Education ne YUVA 2.0 ko 2 October 2022 ko launch kiya. Aim reading-writing/book culture\npromote karna, Indian writing ko globally present karna aur below-30 young authors ko train karna hai. [Q45]\n• STRIDE—Scheme for Trans-disciplinary Research for India's Developing Economy—UGC ne launch ki. Ye socially relevant, local-need\nbased aur nationally/globally significant research projects ko support karti hai. [Q46]\n• Skill Development Initiative Ministry of Labour and Employment ne May 2007 mein launch ki. [Q47]","• Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY) Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship ki flagship scheme hai, Ministry of\nLabour and Employment ki nahi. PMKVY Guidelines 2016-20 mein short-term training school/college dropouts aur unemployed Indian\nnationals ke liye targeted thi. [Q48]\n• Construction workers ke traditional routes se acquired skills ki certification Recognition of Prior Learning (RPL) se judi hai. National Skill\nPolicy 2009 ne RPL ka framework envisage kiya tha, lekin us samay organised implementation nahi hua; 2015 se Skill India Mission ki\nflagship scheme PMKVY ke under ise regular implementation mila. [Q49]\n• Union Budget 2023-24 mein 30 Skill India International Centres establish karne ki announcement hui, taaki youth ko international\nopportunities ke liye skill kiya ja sake. Initiative PMKVY 4.0 ke under hai. [Q50]\n• NSQF ke under learner formal, non-formal ya informal learning ke through required competency level ka certification le sakta hai. NSQF\nqualifications alignment se vocational aur general education ke beech mobility support hoti hai. [Q51]\n• National Career Service (NCS) Ministry of Labour and Employment, Government of India ki initiative hai. Prime Minister Narendra Modi ne\nise 20 July 2015 ko mission-mode project ke roop mein launch kiya. [Q52]\n• Skill India Mission July 2015 mein Prime Minister ne launch ki. Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship long-term aur short-term\ntraining ke through youth ko employable skills dene par focus karta hai. [Q53]\n• Skill India Mission data mein March 2016 tak 76 lakh youth trained the aur 26 lakh ko jobs mili thi; Ministry data ke hisaab se July 2021 tak\nroughly 556.1 lakh persons ko Skill India ecosystem mein training mili. [Q54]\n• Global Skills Park ka foundation stone 3 July 2017 ko Bhopal mein rakha gaya. Iska naam Sant Shiromani Ravidas Global Skills Park\n(SSRGSP) hai aur design Singapore ke Institute of Technical Education model par based tha. [Q55]\n• UN General Assembly ne 2014 mein 15 July ko World Youth Skills Day declare kiya. 2021 theme 'Reimagining Youth Skills Post Pandemic'\naur 2025 theme 'Youth Empowerment through AI and Digital Skills' tha. [Q56]\n• Us samay ke HRD Minister Ramesh Pokhriyal 'Nishank' ne 10 October 2019 ko Pradhan Mantri Innovative Learning Programme - DHRUV\n- launch kiya. Launch venue ISRO headquarters, Bengaluru tha. [Q57]\n• Atal Innovation Mission NITI Aayog ki flagship initiative hai. Iska objective India mein innovation aur entrepreneurship promote karna hai.\n[Q58]\n• Atal Innovation Mission (AIM) NITI Aayog ke under establish hua. Iska aim schools, universities, research institutions, MSME sector aur\nindustry mein innovation aur entrepreneurship ka better ecosystem create karna hai. [Q59]\n• Department of Science and Technology ne NIDHI—National Initiative for Developing and Harnessing Innovations—ideas/innovations ko\nsuccessful startups mein convert karne ke liye launch ki. Programme ka aim har year 20 student startups ko financial support dena hai. [Q60]\n• SWAYAM ka full form Study Webs of Active-Learning for Young Aspiring Minds hai. Ministry of Human Resource Development ka ye web\nportal citizens ko affordable, quality education ke MOOCs free access ke saath provide karta hai. [Q61]\n• Unnat Bharat Abhiyan rural development se related hai. Iska objective higher-education institutions ko local communities/rural-development\nactivities se connect karke appropriate technology ke through development challenges solve karna hai. [Q62]\n• Vidyanjali Ministry of Education ki initiative hai; iska aim private sector aur community participation ke through government schools mein\neducation quality improve karna hai. [Q63]\n• ULLAS—Understanding of Lifelong Learning for All in Society, jise New India Literacy Programme bhi kaha jata hai—adult aur lifelong\nlearning ko NEP 2020 ke framework mein support karta hai. DIKSHA NCERT/Ministry of Education ka national digital school-education\nplatform hai. [Q64]\n\n\n4. Rural Services, Financial Inclusion, DBT & Basic Amenities\n\n• Rajasthan Government ne January 1991 mein Apna Gaon Apna Kaam scheme launch ki. Iske aims village-use public assets banana, rural\nemployment generate karna, self-confidence/self-reliance badhana aur living standards improve karna the. [Q65]\n• Apna Gaon Apna Kaam type rural-employment approach ka objective villages mein useful public assets banate hue employment generate\nkarna, poverty reduce karna, self-reliance encourage karna aur living standard improve karna tha. [Q66]\n• Pradhan Mantri Sahaj Bijli Har Ghar Yojana—Saubhagya—September 2017 mein launch hui. Aim all rural households aur urban poor/BPL\nhouseholds ko electricity connection dena tha. [Q67]\n• Saubhagya, ya Pradhan Mantri Sahaj Bijli Har Ghar Yojana, Ministry of Power ki scheme hai jo September 2017 mein launch hui. Iska aim\nuniversal household electrification tha aur implementation mein Centre-State cooperation important tha. [Q68]\n• Pradhan Mantri Jan-Dhan Yojana financial inclusion ki national mission hai. Har family ko kam-se-kam ek bank account aur banking access\ndena iska core objective hai; announcement 15 August 2014 aur formal launch 28 August 2014 ko hua. [Q69]\n• Pradhan Mantri Jan-Dhan Yojana 28 August 2014 ko formally launch hui. Iska purpose universal banking access ke through financial\ninclusion hai. [Q70]\n• Pradhan Mantri Jan-Dhan Yojana launch ke time applicable insurance cover ₹1 lakh accident insurance aur ₹30,000 life insurance tha,\nscheme conditions ke subject mein. [Q71]\n• Important launch dates hain: PM Awas Yojana-Gramin - 20 November 2016; Jal Jeevan Mission - 15 August 2019; Swachh Bharat Mission-\nGramin - 2 October 2014; PM Ujjwala Yojana - 1 May 2016. [Q72]\n• Important launch years/dates hain: Aam Aadmi Bima Yojana - 2007; PM Atmanirbhar Swasth Bharat Yojana - 2021; PM Jan Arogya Yojana\n- 2018; PM Suraksha Bima Yojana - 9 May 2015. PMSBY ko Prime Minister Narendra Modi ne Kolkata mein launch kiya. [Q73]\n• Chronology: PM Jan-Dhan Yojana - 28 August 2014 -> Make in India - 25 September 2014 -> Sukanya Samriddhi Yojana - 22 January\n2015 -> Atal Pension Yojana - 9 May 2015. [Q74]\n• Relevant launch dates hain: PM Jan-Dhan Yojana - 28 August 2014; Digital Gender Atlas for Advancing Girls' Education - 9 March 2015;\nMUDRA Bank - 8 April 2015; PM Suraksha Bima Yojana - 9 May 2015. [Q75]\n• Rashtriya Vayoshri Yojana ka actual launch 1 April 2017 ko Nellore district, Andhra Pradesh se hua. Scheme Union Budget 2015-16 mein\nannounce ki gayi thi. [Q76]\n• Atal Jyoti Yojana, 2013 ke under then Madhya Pradesh CM Shivraj Singh Chouhan ne rural agricultural sector ke liye 10 hours electricity\nsupply ka provision diya. [Q77]\n• 2015 mein Ministry of Petroleum and Natural Gas ko PAHAL ke liye Guinness World Records certificate mila as world's largest direct-\nbenefit cash-transfer scheme. PAHAL LPG subsidy ka DBT programme hai. [Q78]\n• PAHAL ke under LPG subsidy Direct Benefit Transfer ke through beneficiary ke bank account mein transfer hoti hai. First phase 54 districts\nmein 15 November 2014 se start hua aur 1 January 2015 se scheme all districts tak extend hui. [Q79]","• Welfare-scheme associations hain: Nai Roshni - women empowerment; DISHA - IT training; PM Jan-Dhan Yojana - financial inclusion;\nSwavalamban - unorganised-sector workers ke liye pension support. [Q80]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Chronology & Launch Place\n▸PMSBY ka launch 9 May 2015 ko hua.\n▸Chronology: PMJDY → Make in India → Sukanya Samriddhi → Atal Pension.\n▸Rashtriya Vayoshri Yojana 1 April 2017 ko Nellore district, Andhra Pradesh se launch hui.\n[Q73, Q74, Q76]\n\n\n\n\n5. Women, Girl Child, Maternity & Empowerment Schemes\n\n• Government of India ne Swavalamban Scheme 2010 mein unorganised-sector workers ke liye launch ki. [Q81]\n• Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana Prime Minister Narendra Modi ne 1 May 2016 ko Ballia, Uttar Pradesh se 'Clean Fuel, Better Life' tagline ke\nsaath launch ki. Initial target BPL households ki women ko 5 crore free LPG connections dena tha. [Q82]\n• Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana 1 May 2016 ko Ballia, Uttar Pradesh se launch hui. Scheme ka aim eligible poor households ko LPG\nconnection ke through clean cooking fuel dena hai. [Q83]\n• Tejaswini Rural Women's Empowerment Programme Madhya Pradesh mein 2007 se IFAD assistance ke saath implement hua. Groups ke\nthrough women ka economic, social aur political empowerment iska objective hai. [Q84]\n• Madhya Pradesh Government ne Panchdhara Yojana November 1991 mein rural aur tribal women ke welfare/development ke liye launch\nki. Isme Vatsalya, Gramya, Ayushmati, Social Security Pension aur Kalpavriksha—five schemes shamil thi. [Q85]\n• Beti Bachao, Beti Padhao 22 January 2015 ko Panipat, Haryana mein launch hui. Aim declining child-sex ratio ke against awareness create\nkarna, female foeticide rokna aur girls ki safety/education ensure karna hai. [Q86]\n• Ministry of Women and Child Development ne Dhan-Lakshmi Scheme March 2008 mein launch ki. Aim girl-child birth ke against negative\nattitude badalna, discrimination/neglect reduce karna aur girls ko equal opportunities dena tha. [Q87]\n• Maharashtra Government ne Bhagyashree Scheme 8 March 2015 ko BPL-family girls ke liye launch ki; isne State ki earlier Sukanya\nScheme ko replace kiya. [Q88]\n• West Bengal Government ne Rupashree Prakalpa 1 April 2018 ko launch kiya. Economically weaker families ki 18+ age eligible girls ki\nmarriage ke liye one-time ₹25,000 assistance milti hai. [Q89]\n• Amma Scooter Scheme mein 50% subsidy (maximum Rs 25,000) di gayi; rural aur urban working women dono cover the. Scheme 24\nFebruary 2018 ko Jayalalithaa ki 70th birth anniversary par launch hui. [Q90]\n• Development of Women and Children in Rural Areas (DWCRA) 1982 mein rural BPL women ko self-employment opportunities dene ke liye\nstart hua. Later ise 1 April 1999 ko launch Swarnjayanti Gram Swarozgar Yojana mein merge kar diya gaya. [Q91]\n• February 2025 mein Ministry of Skill Development and Entrepreneurship ne NITI Aayog ke collaboration se Assam, Meghalaya aur\nMizoram mein women-entrepreneurship programme 'Swavalambini' launch kiya. [Q92]\n• Swavalamban Programme, earlier NORAD/Women's Economic Programme, 1982-83 mein nationwide launch hua. Women ko\ntraditional/non-traditional occupations mein training aur skills dekar employment/self-employment enable karna aim tha. [Q93]\n• Swavalamban Programme women ko traditional aur non-traditional occupations mein training/skills dekar employment ya self-employment\nke liye prepare karta tha. [Q94]\n• Government of India ne Swayamsiddha July 2001 mein self-help groups ke through comprehensive women empowerment ke focus se\nlaunch ki. Swadhar difficult circumstances mein reh rahi women ko assistance provide karne ke liye hai. [Q95]\n• Swadhar Ministry of Women and Child Development ki scheme hai, jo 2001-02 mein start hui. Difficult circumstances, natural disaster se\nhomeless, released women prisoners aur other destitute/affected women ko shelter aur vocational training di ja sakti hai. [Q96]\n• Rajasthan Government ne Indira Mahila Shakti Nidhi 18 December 2019 ko launch ki. Women empowerment/self-reliance ke liye women\naur SHGs ko fund se up to ₹1 crore loan support ka provision tha. [Q97]\n• Women self-help groups ko financially strengthen karne ke liye National Rural Livelihoods Mission ke under changes approve hue. 5 May\n2013 announcement mein eligible SHGs ko 7% interest rate par bank loans available karane ka provision tha. [Q98]\n• Mahila Samakhya Programme 1988 mein start hua tha, 1989 mein nahi. [Q99]\n• Scheme-purpose pairs hain: Rashtriya Mahila Kosh - poor women ki credit needs; Mahila Samriddhi Yojana - rural women mein savings;\nIndira Mahila Yojana - women empowerment; Mahila Samakhya Programme - education for women's equality. [Q100]\n• Mahila Samriddhi Yojana 2 October 1993 ko launch hui. Aim women mein saving habit, self-confidence aur self-reliance develop karna tha;\n12 July 2001 ko Mahila Samriddhi Yojana aur Indira Mahila Yojana ko Swayamsiddha programme mein merge kiya gaya. [Q101]\n• Maternity Benefit (Amendment) Act, 2017 ne Maternity Benefit Act, 1961 amend kiya. Main provisions 1 April 2017 se aur crèche provision\n1 July 2017 se effective hua. [Q102]\n• Janani Suraksha Yojana 12 April 2005 ko National Rural Health Mission ke under centrally sponsored scheme ke roop mein launch hui.\nAim poor/BPL women mein institutional delivery encourage karna tha; isne earlier National Maternity Benefit Scheme ko replace kiya. [Q103]\n• Janani Suraksha Yojana 12 April 2005 ko National Rural Health Mission ke under launch hui. Aim poor pregnant women mein institutional\ndelivery promote karke maternal aur neonatal mortality reduce karna hai. [Q104]\n• Janani Suraksha Yojana 12 April 2005 ko all States/UTs ke liye NRHM ke under launch hui. Special focus low-performing States—UP,\nUttarakhand, Bihar, Jharkhand, MP, Chhattisgarh, Odisha, Assam, Rajasthan aur Jammu & Kashmir—par tha. [Q105]\n• Janani Suraksha Yojana NRHM ke under safe-motherhood intervention hai. Poor pregnant women mein institutional delivery promote karke\nmaternal aur neonatal mortality reduce karna iska main objective hai. [Q106]\n• Government of India ne Janani-Shishu Suraksha Karyakram 1 June 2011 ko pregnant women aur infants ko free healthcare facilities dene\nke liye launch kiya. Programme ka inauguration Mewat, Haryana se Sonia Gandhi ne kiya. [Q107]\n• Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana 1 January 2017 ko pregnant aur lactating women mein better health-related behaviour promote\nkarne ke liye launch hui. [Q108]\n• Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan (PMSMA) ka objective designated days par sabhi pregnant women ko assured, comprehensive\naur quality antenatal care dena hai. [Q109]","• Ministry of Health and Family Welfare ne MAA - Mothers Absolute Affection - August 2016 mein breastfeeding promote karne ke liye launch\nkiya; programme ka aim under-five child mortality reduce karne mein contribution dena hai. [Q110]\n• Integrated Rural Energy Programme (IREP) ka main focus selected rural areas mein cooking, heating aur lighting ki minimum domestic-\nenergy needs ko meet karna hai, especially renewable energy ke through. [Q111]\n• ABC Index—Achievements of Babies and Children Index—Jean Drèze, Reetika Khera aur Sudha Narayanan ne develop kiya. Ye child\ndevelopment ko survival, immunisation, nutrition aur school attendance ke average performance se assess karta hai aur health-related index\nke roop mein use hota hai. [Q112]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Amma Scooter Scheme\n▸Amma Scooter Scheme mein 50% subsidy (maximum Rs 25,000) thi; rural aur urban working women dono cover the. Scheme 24 February 2018\nko Jayalalithaa ki 70th birth anniversary par launch hui.\n[Q90]\n\n\n\n\n6. Rural Infrastructure, Bharat Nirman, Jal Jeevan & AMRUT\n\n• Gujarat Government ne Gokul Gram Yojana 1995-96 mein launch ki. Five-year scheme (1995-2000) ka aim Gujarat ke sabhi villages ko\nbasic infrastructure aur better environment dena tha. [Q113]\n• Union Cabinet ne 4 April 2025 ko Vibrant Villages Programme-II (VVP-II) approve kiya. Focus international land borders ke paas villages ka\nsocio-economic development, basic facilities strengthen karna aur livelihood opportunities create karna hai. [Q114]\n• Bharat Nirman December 2005 mein rural infrastructure development ke objective se launch hua. Action plan mein rural electricity, roads,\ndrinking water, telecommunications, irrigation aur poor households ke housing components shamil the. [Q115]\n• Bharat Nirman ke six major rural-infrastructure areas the—rural housing, drinking water, roads, irrigation, electrification aur\ntelecommunications. Rural sanitation in six components mein nahi tha. [Q116]\n• Bharat Nirman mein water supply, irrigation, electrification, roads, housing aur telecommunications shamil the. Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan\neducation programme tha, Bharat Nirman ka component nahi. [Q117]\n• Bharat Nirman ke components mein rural electrification, rural housing aur rural telephony shamil the; agro-based industries iska component\nnahi tha. [Q118]\n• Bharat Nirman mein rural housing, rural water supply aur rural electrification shamil the; rural hospitals iska component nahi the. [Q119]\n• Bharat Nirman rural infrastructure-development programme tha; iske 6 components irrigation, roads, housing, drinking water, electrification\naur rural telecommunications the. [Q120]\n• Bharat Nirman December 2005 mein four-year period 2005-09 ke liye launch hua; second phase later 2012 tak extend hua. Targets mein\nadditional 1 crore hectare assured irrigation aur remaining villages ko telephone connectivity dena shamil tha. [Q121]\n• Bharat Nirman ka ek target programme period mein additional 10 million hectares ko assured irrigation ke under lana tha. [Q122]\n• Bharat Nirman originally 2005-09 ke four-year period ke liye launch hua tha; initial targets 2009 ke end tak complete hone the. [Q123]\n• Government of India ne Accelerated Rural Water Supply Programme 1972-73 mein start kiya. 1 April 2009 se iska naam National Rural\nDrinking Water Programme (NRDWP) hua; ye Bharat Nirman ke six components mein se ek tha. [Q124]\n• Prime Minister Narendra Modi ne 15 August 2019 ko Jal Jeevan Mission launch kiya. Union Budget 2019-20 mein States ke saath milkar\n2024 tak all rural households ko piped water supply—'Har Ghar Jal'—dene ka objective rakha gaya. [Q125]\n• National Urban Innovation Stack Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs ki strategic initiative hai jo urban governance/innovation ke liye\ncommon digital infrastructure create karti hai. NUIS blueprint MoHUA aur NIUA ne jointly develop kiya. [Q126]\n• AMRUT Prime Minister Narendra Modi ne June 2015 mein New Delhi se launch kiya. Ye 1 lakh se zyada population wale 500 cities/towns\nmein implement hua aur 60% se zyada urban population cover karta tha. [Q127]\n• AMRUT primarily urban-infrastructure mission hai. Iska focus water supply, sewerage, transport, drainage, green spaces aur other basic\nurban services par hai. [Q128]\n• AMRUT ke core areas water supply, sewerage/septage management, storm-water drainage, public/non-motorised transport and parking,\naur green spaces/parks/recreation centres hain. [Q129]\n• Urban-policy timeline: JNNURM—3 December 2005; National Urban Housing and Habitat Policy—December 2007; AMRUT—25 June\n2015. [Q130]\n\n\n7. Food, Disability, Accessibility & Rural Infrastructure\n\n• Uttarakhand ne Atal Khadyanna Yojana 2011 mein launch ki. APL families ko per month 10 kg wheat ₹4/kg aur 10 kg rice ₹6/kg; BPL\nfamilies ko 10 kg wheat ₹2/kg aur 10 kg rice ₹3/kg diya jata tha. [Q131]\n• Listed States mein Tamil Nadu sabse pehle ₹1 per kg rice scheme launch karne wala State tha; baad mein Madhya Pradesh, Odisha,\nAndhra Pradesh aur other States ne similar schemes implement ki. [Q132]\n• Persons with Disabilities Act, 1995 ke Section 26 mein children with disabilities ko at least 18 years age tak free aur compulsory education\nka provision tha. Section 43 concessional/preferential land allotment jaise housing aur business purposes se related tha. [Q133]\n• Sugamya Bharat Abhiyan (Accessible India Campaign) December 2015 mein Department of Empowerment of Persons with Disabilities ne\nlaunch kiya. Ye persons with disabilities ke liye universal accessibility aur equal opportunities promote karne wala nationwide campaign hai.\n[Q134]\n• Sangam Scheme 15 August 1996 ko persons with disabilities ko support karne ke liye launch hui. Rural areas mein beneficiaries ko\ngroups/Sangams mein organise karke each group ko economic activities ke liye ₹15,000 assistance di jati thi. [Q135]\n• Sangam Scheme persons with disabilities, especially rural areas mein, welfare aur economic empowerment ke liye hai. [Q136]\n• Sankalp Project HIV/AIDS ko combat/eliminate karne se related hai. Isse Hindustan Latex Limited aur Employees' State Insurance\nCorporation jointly operate karte hain. [Q137]\n• Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) 25 December 2000 ko launch hui. Aim plains mein 500+ population aur hilly/tribal/desert\nareas mein 250+ population wale eligible villages ko all-weather roads se connect karna tha. [Q138]","• PMGSY ka core objective eligible unconnected rural habitations ko good all-weather/pucca roads se road network ke saath connect karna\nhai. [Q139]\n• PMGSY un rural habitations ko all-weather roads se connect karta hai jo adequate road connectivity se deprived hain; broader aim village\ncommunity life ko improve karna hai. [Q140]\n• PMGSY 25 December 2000 ko launch hui. FY 2001-02 allocation ₹2,500 crore tha; 2023-24 actual expenditure ₹15,380 crore, 2024-25 RE\n₹14,500 crore aur 2024-25/2025-26 budget allocations ₹19,000 crore each diye gaye. [Q141]\n• Pradhan Mantri Gramodaya Yojana 2000-01 mein rural basic needs ko time-bound manner mein meet karne ke liye launch hui. Priority\nareas primary education, health, drinking water, housing aur rural roads the. [Q142]\n• Prime Minister Narendra Modi ne Shyama Prasad Mukherji Rurban Mission February 2016 mein Rajnandgaon, Chhattisgarh se launch\nkiya. Mission rural clusters ke integrated development ke liye economic activities, skill development, local entrepreneurship aur infrastructure\nimprove karta hai. [Q143]\n• Shyama Prasad Mukherji Rurban Mission February 2016 mein launch hua tha, 2015 mein nahi. [Q144]\n• Etawah Pilot Project 1947 mein conceived aur September 1948 mein launch hua. Lt. Col. Albert Mayer ke guidance mein project initially 64\nvillages cover karta tha aur later 97 villages tak expand hua. [Q145]\n• Etawah Pilot Project 1947 mein conceive hua aur Lt. Col. Albert Mayer ke guidance mein September 1948 mein launch hua. [Q146]\n• Etawah Pilot Project rural development ke liye September 1948 mein Mahewa village se start hua. India mein project initiate karne ka credit\nAmerican military officer Albert Mayer ko diya jata hai. [Q147]\n• Prime Minister Narendra Modi ne Deen Dayal Upadhyaya Gram Jyoti Yojana 25 July 2015 ko Patna mein launch ki. Main objective rural\nareas mein agricultural aur non-agricultural consumers ko judicious electricity supply ensure karna hai. [Q148]\n\nEXAM TRAP — DDUGJY — Primary Objective\n▸DDUGJY ka primary objective rural agricultural aur non-agricultural consumers ko judicious electricity supply dena hai; education, health, banking\naur empowerment broader effects ho sakte hain.\n[Q148]\n\n\n\n\n8. Employment & Rural Anti-Poverty Programmes — Historical Evolution\n\n• Employment Guarantee Scheme sabse pehle Maharashtra Government ne 28 March 1972 ko launch ki. [Q149]\n• Employment Assurance Scheme 2 October 1993 ko 1,778 identified backward blocks mein start hui. Aim rural BPL families ko employment\nsupport dena tha; later phases mein scheme all blocks tak extend hui. [Q150]\n• TRYSEM, Jawahar Rozgar Yojana aur IRDP rural-development programmes hain. CRY—Child Rights and You—child-rights NGO hai,\nrural-development scheme nahi. [Q151]\n• Programme timeline: IRDP—1978-79; RLEGP—1983; Prime Minister's Rozgar Yojana—1993; Rural Employment Generation Programme\n—1995. [Q152]\n• Employment-scheme chronology: TRYSEM—August 1979; RLEGP—15 August 1983; JRY—April 1989; Employment Assurance Scheme\n—1993. [Q153]\n• TRYSEM—Training of Rural Youth for Self Employment—August 1979 mein IRDP ke supporting component ke roop mein launch hua.\nSC/ST ko preference thi; April 1999 mein ise Swarnjayanti Gram Swarozgar Yojana mein merge kiya gaya. [Q154]\n• RLEGP, IRDP aur NREP rural employment/poverty reduction programmes the. MRTP Act, 1969 private sector mein monopolistic/restrictive\ntrade practices control karne ke liye tha; ye anti-poverty programme nahi tha. [Q155]\n• Government of India ne Rural Infrastructure Development Fund (RIDF) NABARD ke under 1995-96 mein ₹2,000 crore initial corpus ke\nsaath establish kiya. [Q156]\n• RIDF 1995-96 mein NABARD ke under establish hua, jiska purpose State governments/State-owned corporations ko incomplete rural-\ninfrastructure projects complete karne ke liye funds dena hai. Rural water supply, roads aur electrification covered hain; rural industries listed\ncoverage mein nahi hain. [Q157]\n• RIDF 1995-96 mein rural infrastructure finance karne ke liye establish hua; iska financial management NABARD handle karta hai. [Q158]\n• Then PM Rajiv Gandhi ne Jawahar Rozgar Yojana 1 April 1989 ko rural unemployed ko employment dene ke liye announce/launch ki. Ye\nSeventh Five-Year Plan (1985-90) period mein start hui. [Q159]\n• Jawahar Rozgar Yojana April 1989 mein NREP aur RLEGP ko merge karke bani. Scheme-generated employment ka 30% women ke liye\nreserved tha; main objective rural unemployed/underemployed ko village ke paas additional gainful employment dena tha. [Q160]\n• Jawahar Rozgar Yojana ka objective rural employment create karna aur rural economic/social infrastructure strengthen karna dono tha.\n[Q161]\n• Jawahar Rozgar Yojana ka central emphasis rural areas mein additional wage-employment opportunities create karna tha; productive\ncommunity assets ke through rural poverty alleviation bhi support hoti thi. [Q162]\n• Jawahar Rozgar Yojana (JRY) 1 April 1989 ko launch hui, April 1995 mein nahi. JRY ne NREP aur RLEGP ko combine kiya. [Q163]\n• IRDP 1978-79 mein limited basis par start hua aur 2 October 1980 se all blocks mein implement hua. Main objective rural BPL families ko\ncredit aur self-employment opportunities dekar poverty line se upar lana tha. [Q164]\n• IRDP 1978-79 mein experimental basis par introduce hua aur 2 October 1980 se nationwide implement hua. [Q165]\n• SGSY 1 April 1999 ko six programmes—IRDP, TRYSEM, DWCRA, Million Wells Scheme, SITRA aur Ganga Kalyan Yojana—merge karke\nlaunch hui; JRY in six mein nahi tha. Later SGSY ko NRLM mein restructure kiya gaya aur March 2016 mein DAY-NRLM naam diya gaya.\n[Q166]\n• IRDP 1978-79 mein experimental start hua aur 1980 mein nationwide extend hua. NREP 1980 mein Food for Work ke successor ke roop\nmein rural unemployment reduce karne ke liye start hua; DWCRA 1982-83 mein IRDP sub-component ke roop mein start hua. [Q167]\n• SGSY 1 April 1999 ko six earlier schemes ke merger se start hui. Aim rural poor ko self-employment opportunities dekar assisted families\nko roughly three years mein poverty line se upar lana tha. [Q168]\n• Swarnjayanti Gram Swarozgar Yojana 1 April 1999 ko launch hui. [Q169]\n• Swarnjayanti Gram Swarozgar Yojana ko June 2011 mein National Rural Livelihoods Mission (NRLM) ke roop mein restructure kiya gaya.\nMission 1 April 2013 se fully effective hua aur later DAY-NRLM naam mila. [Q170]","• NRLM, ab DAY-NRLM, 2011 mein launch hua aur 1 April 2013 se fully effective hua. Rural poor/BPL households ki poverty reduce karne\nke liye SHGs strengthen karna aur skill-development facilities dena iska objective hai. [Q171]\n• Swarnjayanti Shahari Rozgar Yojana (SJSRY) 1 December 1997 ko urban unemployed ke liye launch hui, rural unemployed ke liye nahi.\nBharat Nirman December 2005 ka rural-infrastructure programme tha, social-security measure nahi. [Q172]\n• Nehru Rozgar Yojana 11 October 1989 ko Jawahar Rozgar Yojana ke urban counterpart ke roop mein launch hui. Aim urban areas mein\nlagbhag 10 lakh annual employment opportunities create karna tha. [Q173]\n• Swarnjayanti Shahari Rozgar Yojana 1 December 1997 ko launch hui; iske liye NRY, Urban Basic Services Programme aur PM Integrated\nUrban Poverty Eradication Programme jaise 3 earlier urban employment/poverty programmes ko merge kiya gaya. [Q174]\n• Programme years hain: Prime Minister's Rozgar Yojana - 1993; Rural Employment Generation Programme - 1995; Pradhan Mantri\nGramodaya Yojana - 2000-01. [Q175]\n• Important scheme-year pairs hain: Agricultural Labourers Social Security Scheme - 2001; Swarnjayanti Gram Swarozgar Yojana - 1999;\nEmployment Assurance Scheme - 1993; PM Gramodaya Yojana - 2000. [Q176]\n• IRDP limited introduction 1978-79 ke baad 1980 mein nationwide implement hua; SGSY 1999 mein launch hui; rural employment\nguarantee law 2005 mein enact aur 2006 se implement hua. [Q177]\n• Launch dates: SGSY—1 April 1999; SJSRY—1 December 1997; Jawahar Rozgar Yojana—1 April 1989. [Q178]\n• Important pairs hain: National Rural Health Mission - 2005; National Social Assistance Programme - 1995; SGSY - 1999; Sampoorna\nGrameen Rozgar Yojana - 2001. [Q179]\n• Timeline: Janshree Bima Yojana—10 August 2000; National Rural Health Mission—April 2005; Aam Aadmi Bima Yojana—2 October 2007.\nNREGA 2005 mein enact hua, 2 February 2006 se implement hua aur 2 October 2009 ko MGNREGA rename hua. [Q180]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Urban Employment vs Bharat Nirman\n▸SJSRY urban unemployed ke liye tha, rural ke liye nahi; Bharat Nirman rural-infrastructure programme tha, social-security measure nahi.\n[Q172]\n\n\n\n\n9. MGNREGA, Employment Guarantee & Urban Employment\n\n• Rashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan (RMSA) March 2009 mein secondary education access/quality improve karne ke liye launch hua; ye\nprimarily employment programme nahi tha. Implementation 2009-10 session se hua. [Q181]\n• National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (NREGA), 2005 August 2005 mein pass, September 2005 mein notify aur 2 February 2006 se\nimplement hua. 2 October 2009 ko iska naam MGNREGA kiya gaya. [Q182]\n• NREGS legislation 2005 mein pass aur 2 February 2006 se implement hua. Initially 200 districts cover hue; second phase mein 130 aur\ndistricts add hokar total 330 hua. [Q183]\n• NREGS/MGNREGA initially 200 districts mein implement hua, phir later phases mein coverage expand hui. [Q184]\n• Employment-guarantee scheme 1 April 2008 se country ke all districts tak extend ho gayi. [Q185]\n• National Rural Employment Guarantee Act ka naam 2 October 2009 ko Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act kiya\ngaya. [Q186]\n• Original MGNREGA framework eligible rural household ko per financial year 100 days unskilled public-work employment ki legal guarantee\ndeta tha. [Q187]\n• NREGA sirf scheme nahi, employment ka legal entitlement tha. National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme 2 February 2006 ko\nAnantapur, Andhra Pradesh se launch hui aur ordinary schemes se isliye different thi kyunki ye legal right/guarantee create karti thi. [Q188]\n• MGNREGA ke older statutory framework mein eligible rural household ko per financial year 100 days employment guarantee thi. Later VB-\nGRAMG Act, 2025 framework mein guarantee 125 days ki gayi. [Q189]\n• NREGA mein transparency aur accountability ke specific provisions the. 0.274 year lagbhag 100 days ke barabar hota hai (0.274 × 365 ≈\n100). [Q190]\n• Relevant context mein allocation ₹39,000 crore tha. Later figures: 2024-25 actual ₹85,834 crore; 2025-26 BE ₹86,000 crore, RE ₹88,000\ncrore; 2026-27 BE ₹30,000 crore. [Q191]\n• Budget estimates comparison: MGNREGA ₹60,000 crore (2019-20) se ₹61,500 crore (2020-21) hua; Mid-Day Meal ₹11,000 crore par\nunchanged raha; National Ganga Plan ₹750 crore se ₹800 crore hua. [Q192]\n• Percentage comparison mein Atmanirbhar Bharat Rozgar Yojana ki reduction MGNREGA se greater thi; Reform-Linked Distribution\nScheme ka increase Jal Jeevan Mission se greater tha. [Q193]\n• NREGA eligible rural household ko per year 100 days employment ki guarantee deta tha; guarantee household ke har adult member ko\nseparately 100 days nahi thi. [Q194]\n• NREGA/MGNREGA ke positive outcomes mein rural poor ki purchasing power badhna, workers ki bargaining power improve hona aur\ncities ki taraf migration reduce hona shamil hai. [Q195]\n• Rajasthan mein scheme September 2022 mein then CM Ashok Gehlot ne launch ki. 1 April 2023 se employment entitlement 100 se\nbadhkar 125 days hua. [Q196]\n\nEXAM TRAP — MGNREGA — 100 Days vs Later Update\n▸MGNREGA employment guarantee ke saath durable assets, water management aur rural livelihoods jaise objectives bhi cover karta hai.\n▸Older MGNREGA framework 100 days ka tha; VB-GRAMG Act, 2025 update mein 125 days ka provision diya gaya. Dono periods alag rakhein.\n[Q182, Q187, Q188, Q189, Q194]\n\n\n\n\n10. Community Development, Extension & Antyodaya\n\n• Community Development Programme India mein 1952 mein Ford Foundation assistance ke saath start hua; India aisa programme launch\nkarne wala first country tha. Goal self-reliant aur economically/socially just social order build karna tha. [Q197]","• Community Development Programme 2 October 1952 ko experimental basis par launch hua. Objectives agriculture/communications\nimprove karna aur rural health, sanitation aur education mein substantial improvement lana the. [Q198]\n• Community development ka aim community ka all-round physical, social, moral aur cultural development hai. S. K. Dey ne 'live and let live'\nko iska foundation bataya; agriculture, irrigation, education, health, village industries, Panchayats aur local participation iske major\ndimensions hain. [Q199]\n• Programme timeline mein: Community Development Programme - 2 October 1952; Drought Prone Areas Programme - 1973-74; Food for\nWork Programme - 1977-78. [Q200]\n• Key Village Scheme 1950 mein cattle development ke liye launch hui; Community Development Programme 2 October 1952 ko launch hua;\nBalwant Rai Mehta Committee ne 1957 mein democratic decentralisation recommend ki. [Q201]\n• Community Development Programme India mein 2 October 1952 ko launch hua. [Q202]\n• Community Development Programme 1952 mein launch hua. [Q203]\n• Community Development Programme ne Panchayati Raj institutions ke organisation aur development ka path prepare kiya. [Q204]\n• S.K. Dey ko India mein community development ka principal architect maana jata hai. [Q205]\n• Community Development Programme 1952 limited funds ki wajah se whole country tak extend nahi ho saka; iske baad 1953 mein National\nExtension Service launch ki gayi. [Q206]\n• Community Development Programme 1952 mein launch hua; National Extension Service 1953 mein launch hua. [Q207]\n• Antyodaya Programme sabse pehle Rajasthan mein 2 October 1977 ko launch hua. [Q208]\n• Welfare-scheme facts period-specific hain; launch, coverage aur beneficiary figures ko same year/context ke saath read karein. [Q209]\nEXAM TRAP — Community Development — Broad Scope\n▸Community Development ka scope agriculture, communication, health, sanitation, education aur overall social/economic/cultural development tak\nbroad hai.\n[Q198, Q199]\n\n\n\n\n11. Pension, Insurance & Social-Security Schemes\n\n• Atal Pension Yojana Union Budget 2015-16 mein announce hui aur Prime Minister ne 9 May 2015 ko Kolkata mein formally launch ki;\nscheme 1 June 2015 se effective hui. Isne earlier Swavalamban scheme ko replace kiya aur iska main focus unorganised-sector workers par\nhai. [Q210]\n• NPS Government of India ne 1 January 2004 se introduce kiya. State Government employees ke liye respective State-notified joining date\napply hoti hai; eligible Indian citizens 18-70 years age mein voluntary provisions ke under join kar sakte hain. [Q211]\n• NPS ko Pension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority (PFRDA) regulate karta hai. Government-employee framework mein\nspecified Central Government employees ke liye NPS mandatory tha (Armed Forces excluded); State Government employees aur other\neligible citizens bhi applicable rules ke under join kar sakte hain. [Q212]\n• Eligible retail traders, shopkeepers aur self-employed persons ke liye pension scheme voluntary/contributory hai. General eligibility 18-40\nyears age aur annual turnover up to ₹1.5 crore hai; age 60 ke baad minimum assured pension ₹3,000 per month milti hai. [Q213]\n• Pradhan Mantri Kisan Maandhan Yojana small aur marginal farmers ke liye old-age/social-security pension scheme hai. 12 September\n2019 ko launch hui; generally 18-40 age aur up to 2 hectare cultivable land wale eligible farmers scheme conditions ke under cover hote\nhain. [Q214]\n• Pradhan Mantri Shram Yogi Maandhan Union Budget 2019-20 mein unorganised-sector workers ke liye announce hua, jinki monthly\nincome up to ₹15,000 ho. Age 60 ke baad minimum assured pension ₹3,000 per month hai. [Q215]\n• PM-SYM unorganised-sector workers ke liye voluntary aur contributory pension scheme hai. Entry age 18-40 years hai, 21-40 nahi; eligible\nmonthly income up to ₹15,000 hai. [Q216]\n• Ashraya Bima Yojana 10 October 2001 ko launch hui. Iska objective job/employment lose karne wale workers ko social-security support\ndena hai. [Q217]\n• Aam Aadmi Bima Yojana 2 October 2007 ko rural BPL landless workers ke liye launch hui. Rural landless household ka head ya 18-59 age\nka ek earning member scheme ke under insured ho sakta tha. [Q218]\n• Eligible insured person landless rural household ka head ya one earning member tha. Eligible age 18-59 years thi, 30-65 nahi; Classes 9-\n12 mein padhne wale up to two children ke liye scholarship provision tha. [Q219]\n• Aam Aadmi Bima Yojana 2 October 2007 ko launch hui. [Q220]\n• Employees' State Insurance (ESI) Indian workers ke liye self-financing social-security aur health-insurance scheme hai. ESI Act 1948 mein\nenact hua aur practical implementation 25 February 1952 ko Kanpur aur Delhi se start hua. [Q221]\n• Unorganised Workers' Social Security Act Parliament ne 2008 mein pass kiya; Presidential assent ke baad 30 December 2008 ko notify\nhua. [Q222]\n• Major social-protection measures mein Aam Aadmi Bima Yojana, Rashtriya Swasthya Bima Yojana, Unorganised Workers' Social Security\nAct 2008 aur National Social Security Fund included hain. Swarnjayanti Shahari Rozgar Yojana primarily urban unemployment/poverty-\nalleviation programme tha. [Q223]\n• Skill-development programmes vocational education/training se related hain, social-protection instrument nahi; baaki listed measures\nsocial-protection instruments hain. [Q224]\n• PMSBY personal accident insurance scheme hai jisme eligible age 18-70 years hai. Original annual premium ₹12 tha; 18-50 age condition\nPMSBY ke liye sahi nahi hai. [Q225]\n• Pairs: Pradhan Mantri Shram Yogi Maandhan—₹55 to ₹200 per month depending on entry age; Pradhan Mantri Jeevan Jyoti Bima Yojana\n—₹436 per year; Pradhan Mantri Suraksha Bima Yojana—₹20 per year; Atal Pension Yojana—contribution beneficiary/subscriber ki age aur\nchosen pension level par depend karta hai. [Q226]\n• MGNREGA, NRLM aur Swarnjayanti Shahari Rozgar Yojana employment-generation/poverty-alleviation programmes hain. National Social\nSecurity Fund July 2011 mein unorganised-sector workers ki social-security schemes support karne ke liye constitute hua. [Q227]\n• Important full forms hain: HRIDAY - Heritage City Development and Augmentation Yojana; SEHAT - Social Endeavour for Health and\nTelemedicine; AMRUT - Atal Mission for Rejuvenation and Urban Transformation. [Q228]","• Swachh Bharat Mission universal sanitation se related hai, pure drinking water supply se nahi. 2014 mein launch Mission ka focus\nsanitation efforts accelerate karna aur open defecation eliminate karna tha. [Q229]\n\n\n12. Swachh Bharat, Sanitation & WaterCredit\n\n• Swachh Bharat Abhiyan 2 October 2014 ko launch hua. Original target Mahatma Gandhi ki 150th birth-anniversary year 2019 tak Clean\nIndia achieve karna tha. [Q230]\n• Swachh Bharat Mission (Gramin) 2 October 2014 ko launch hua. [Q231]\n• Swachh Bharat Mission (Gramin) Phase II February 2020 mein 2020-21 se 2024-25 period ke liye announce hui; later two one-year\nextensions ke baad implementation period 2026-27 tak extend hua. [Q232]\n• Department of Drinking Water and Sanitation, Ministry of Jal Shakti ne 27 September 2019 ko ten-year Rural Sanitation Strategy launch ki.\n2019-29 strategy ka focus SBM-Gramin se aaye sanitation behaviour change ko sustain karna hai. [Q233]\n• Swachh Bharat Mission 2 October 2014 ko launch hua aur community-level collective behaviour change par focus karta hai. Open\ndefecation ko routine/social practice se shift karna Mission ka important sanitation objective raha hai. [Q234]\n• Prime Minister Narendra Modi ne 15 September 2018 ko Swachhata Hi Seva campaign launch kiya. Campaign ke conclusion par 29\nSeptember-2 October 2018 tak New Delhi mein Mahatma Gandhi International Sanitation Convention hua. [Q235]\n• Swachh Survekshan 2022 mein Surat, Bhopal, Mysuru, Navi Mumbai, Visakhapatnam aur Tirupati ko five-star Garbage Free City\ncertification mila; Indore India ka first seven-star Garbage Free City bana. [Q236]\n• Swachh Survekshan 2021 mein 1 lakh+ population cities category mein Indore first, Surat second aur Vijayawada third tha; Tirupati top\nthree mein nahi tha. [Q237]\n• 100 se zyada Urban Local Bodies wale States category mein Uttar Pradesh Swachh Survekshan 2021 mein sixth rank par tha. [Q238]\n• Rural Sanitation Survey 2016 mein listed States mein Sikkim first tha. Later surveys ko 2016 ke dated figure ke saath mix nahi karna. [Q239]\n• Rural population ke kuch segments ne traditionally open defecation ko health threat nahi maana; religious orthodoxy aur purity/impurity\nnotions ne ghar ke andar toilets banane ko bhi discourage kiya. [Q240]\n• Nirmal Bharat Abhiyan ka earlier naam Total Sanitation Campaign tha. Rural areas mein individual/community/village/school/Anganwadi\ntoilets aur open-defecation elimination iske main sanitation objectives the. [Q241]\n• Government of India ne Nirmal Gram Puraskar October 2003 mein Total Sanitation Campaign accelerate karne ke liye launch kiya.\nComplete sanitation aur open-defecation-free status achieve karne wale Gram Panchayats, blocks aur districts ko reward milta tha. [Q242]\n• Rashtriya Garima Abhiyan manual scavenging eliminate karne aur manual-scavenging workers ko rehabilitate karne ka campaign hai.\nMaila Mukti Yatra 30 November 2012 ko Bhopal se start hui aur awareness ke liye 18 States ke 200 districts cover kiye. [Q243]\n• Villages aur towns mein sanitary/flush toilets construct karne ke liye Government grants/loans support deti thi. Aim communicable diseases\nreduce karna aur universal toilet availability promote karna tha; is drive ko 'Toilet Revolution' bhi kaha gaya. [Q244]\n• Integrated Low Cost Sanitation Scheme Government of India 1980 se implement kar raha hai. Financing pattern: Central subsidy 75%,\nState subsidy 15% aur beneficiary contribution 10%. [Q245]\n• World Toilet Organization 2001 mein establish global non-profit organisation hai jo worldwide toilets aur sanitation improve karne par kaam\nkarta hai; ye UN agency nahi hai. 2013 mein UN ECOSOC consultative status mila. [Q246]\n• WaterCredit water aur sanitation ke liye microfinance approach use karta hai, taaki low-income households apni water/sanitation needs ko\nfinance kar saken. [Q247]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Swachh Survekshan — Five-Star Cities\n▸Surat, Bhopal aur Navi Mumbai tino Swachh Survekshan 2022 ki five-star Garbage Free City list mein the.\n[Q236]\n\n\n\n\n13. NSAP, Minimum Needs, SWAMITVA & Land Records\n\n• NSAP 15 August 1995 ko launch hua. Iske major schemes: Indira Gandhi National Old Age Pension Scheme, Indira Gandhi National\nWidow Pension Scheme, Indira Gandhi National Disability Pension Scheme, National Family Benefit Scheme aur Annapurna Scheme.\nMahila Kisan Sashaktikaran Pariyojana NRLM/DAY-NRLM ka sub-component hai, NSAP ka nahi. [Q248]\n• National Social Assistance Programme vulnerable poor households ko social assistance deta hai; old-age pension/support iska major\ncomponent hai. [Q249]\n• IGNOAPS 19 November 2007 ko launch hua; 1995 se 2007 tak programme ka naam National Old Age Pension Scheme tha. Shuruat mein\nBPL category ke 65+ elderly persons ko Centre se Rs 200 per month milta tha; 1 April 2011 se minimum age 65 se ghata kar 60 years kar di\ngayi. [Q250]\n• Minimum Needs Programme rural aur urban population ko health, education aur drinking-water jaise basic infrastructure dene ke liye tha;\nbroader purpose poverty alleviation process accelerate karna tha. [Q251]\n• Minimum Needs Programme 1975 mein launch hua aur human resources/human capital mein investment iska principal objective tha.\nComponents mein primary/adult education, rural health, water supply, roads, rural electrification, rural housing, urban-slum improvement aur\nnutrition-related environmental improvements shamil the. [Q252]\n• Minimum Needs Programme mein rural water supply, primary education aur urban-slum improvement shamil the; social forestry listed\ncomponent nahi tha. [Q253]\n• Economic liberalisation direct redistributive social measure nahi hai. Minimum Needs Programme, taxation aur land reforms income\ninequality reduce karne ke tools ho sakte hain; liberalisation primarily economic-reform approach hai. [Q254]\n• SWAMITVA ka full form Survey of Villages Abadi and Mapping with Improvised Technology in Village Areas hai. Ye Central Sector Scheme\nMinistry of Panchayati Raj, State revenue departments aur Survey of India ke cooperation se implement hoti hai. [Q255]\n• National Land Records Modernization Programme 2008 mein Centrally Sponsored Scheme ke roop mein approve hua; 1 April 2016 se ise\n100% Central funding wale Central Sector DILRMP ke roop mein restructure kiya gaya. [Q256]","14. Housing Schemes — PMAY, Indira Awas & Rajiv Awas\n\n• Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana (Urban) 2015 mein launch hui; original 'Housing for All' target year 2022 tha. [Q257]\n• Government ke Housing for All mission ka original target year 2022 tha. [Q258]\n• Indira Awas Yojana 1985-86 mein RLEGP ki sub-scheme ke roop mein start hui. Aim poorest SC/ST households aur freed bonded\nlabourers ke liye low-cost housing dena tha; later structure mein plain areas ke liye ₹70,000 aur hilly/difficult areas ke liye ₹75,000\nassistance noted thi. [Q259]\n• Rajiv Awas Yojana June 2011 mein launch hui aur 2022 tak extend ki gayi. Vision 'slum-free cities' tha jahan basic civic infrastructure,\nsocial amenities aur adequate shelter available ho. [Q260]\n• Rajiv Awas Yojana ka aim slum dwellers ke housing aur basic civic/social infrastructure improve karke slum-free cities/India create karna\ntha. [Q261]\n• Rajiv Awas Yojana ka core objective improved urban housing aur infrastructure ke through slum-free India tha. [Q262]\n\n\n15. School Education, Literacy, Mid-Day Meal & Inclusive Education\n\n• Central Schools/Kendriya Vidyalayas 1962 mein establish hue aur 1963 mein CBSE se affiliate hue; 1963 affiliation year hai. [Q263]\n• Kasturba Gandhi Balika Vidyalaya Scheme Government of India ne August 2004 mein launch ki. Aim educationally backward blocks mein\ndisadvantaged groups ki girls ke liye residential upper-primary schools establish karna tha. [Q264]\n• Education for All goals ko 2015 tak achieve karne ka target tha. [Q265]\n• Samagra Shiksha pre-school se Class 12 tak integrated school-education programme hai. Isme Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan, Rashtriya\nMadhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan aur Teacher Education ko merge kiya gaya. [Q266]\n• Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan 2001 mein 6-14 age children ke universal elementary education ke liye launch hua. Milestones: all districts\ncoverage by 2002, five years schooling by 2007 aur eight years elementary education completion/retention by 2010. [Q267]\n• Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan 2001 mein launch hua aur 6-14 age children ko free, useful aur quality elementary education dene ka target 2010\ntha; five years primary schooling completion milestone 2007 tha. [Q268]\n• Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan ka target 6-14 years age group ke children ke liye universal elementary education tha. [Q269]\n• Relevant allocation ₹10,041 crore tha. Samagra Shiksha ke later figures: BE 2025-26 ₹41,250 crore, RE ₹38,000 crore aur BE 2026-27\n₹42,100 crore. [Q270]\n• Saakshar Bharat September 2009 mein National Literacy Mission restructure karke launch hua. Female literacy major focus thi; target 15+\nage ke 7 crore adults, including 6 crore women, ko literate banana tha. [Q271]\n• Right to Education Act, 2009 1 April 2010 se effective hua. Ye 6-14 years children ke liye free aur compulsory elementary education ka right\ndeta hai. [Q272]\n• Free and compulsory elementary education expenditure mein Central Government ka proposed share 68% tha. Right 6-14 years age group\nko cover karta hai. [Q273]\n• Mid-Day Meal Scheme, Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan aur NRHM social-development programmes hain. Look East Policy South-East Asia ke\nsaath relations/trade strengthen karne wali foreign/economic policy hai, social-development programme nahi. [Q274]\n• National Programme of Nutritional Support to Primary Education 15 August 1995 ko launch hua. Aim school enrolment,\nretention/attendance aur child nutrition improve karna tha; initially 2,408 blocks se start hokar 1997-98 tak all blocks tak expand hua. [Q275]\n• Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs ne 29 September 2021 ko PM-POSHAN approve kiya. Scheme ne earlier Mid-Day Meal Scheme\nko replace kiya. [Q276]\n• National Programme of Nutritional Support to Primary Education (NP-NSPE) 15 August 1995 ko launch hua; October 2007 mein iska naam\nNational Programme of Mid-Day Meal in Schools hua, popularly Mid-Day Meal Scheme. [Q277]\n• Mid-Day Meal Scheme 1995 mein launch hui. [Q278]\n• NP-NSPE 15 August 1995 ko Centrally Sponsored Scheme ke roop mein launch hua. Objectives school enrolment/attendance badhana\naur children ka nutritional status improve karna the. [Q279]\n• Mid-Day Meal Scheme, ab PM-POSHAN, 1995 mein centrally sponsored scheme ke roop mein launch hui. Cooking cost sharing\nCentre:Northeastern States 90:10 aur Centre:other States/UTs 75:25 tha. [Q280]\n• Mid-Day Meal Scheme, ab PM-POSHAN, Central Government ne 1995 mein launch ki; Uttar Pradesh mein bhi isi period se implementation\nstart hua. [Q281]\n• Mid-Day Meal Scheme/PM-POSHAN contractors ke through initiate/manage nahi hoti. School-management bodies, Panchayati Raj\nInstitutions aur self-help groups implementation mein role play karte hain. [Q282]\n• Akshaya Patra Foundation 2000 mein Bengaluru, Karnataka mein establish Indian NGO hai jo school students ko free mid-day meals\nprovide karta hai. [Q283]\n• Rajasthan mein Mid-Day Meal implementation se Adamy Chetana Trust, Havells India Ltd., Hindustan Zinc Ltd. aur D.S.C.L. Kota (Shriram\nGroup) jaise trusts/corporates associated rahe hain. [Q284]\n• School Chalo Abhiyan Uttar Pradesh mein July 2000 mein 6-14 age children ke elementary education promotion ke liye launch hua. [Q285]\n• KALP Scheme Uttar Pradesh Government ne 1998 mein launch ki aur ye primary/elementary education se related thi. [Q286]\n• KALP ek educational scheme thi. [Q287]\n• Shiksha Mitra Scheme 2000-01 mein implement hui. Objectives mein prescribed teacher-pupil ratio maintain karna bhi shamil tha. [Q288]\n• District Primary Education Programme 1994 mein launch hua. Aim universal primary education, dropout aur social/gender disparities\nreduce karna aur children ki practical understanding improve karna tha. [Q289]\n• Proceeds of the education cess levied by the Central Government. The Prarambhik Shiksha Kosh was established by the Central\nGovernment in 2005 to collect revenue received from the education cess. [Q290]\n• Operation Blackboard 1987 mein launch hua. Iska objective country ke all primary schools mein minimum essential facilities aur services\nprovide karna tha. [Q291]\n• Inclusive Education for Disabled at Secondary Stage (IEDSS) 2009-10 mein launch hui aur earlier IEDC Scheme ko replace kiya. Classes\nIX-XII ke children with disabilities ke inclusive education ko support karti hai; 2013 se RMSA ke under include hui. [Q292]\n• Important pairs: Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan—2001; Saakshar Bharat Mission—2009; Operation Blackboard—1987. [Q293]","• Andragogy adult education ka doosra naam hai. [Q294]\n\n\n16. Livelihood Training, Minority/Tribal Initiatives, MPLADS & SAGY\n\n• RUDSET Institutes unemployed rural youth ko skill aur entrepreneurship training dete hain, taaki wo apna enterprise establish kar saken.\n[Q295]\n• Then Rural Development Minister Jairam Ramesh ne June 2013 mein Naxal-affected districts ke rural/tribal youth ke liye skill-development\nprogramme 'Roshni' launch kiya, livelihood improve karna iska aim tha. [Q296]\n• Minorities welfare ke liye Prime Minister's 15-Point Programme hai; 20-Point Programme poverty alleviation se associated hai. [Q297]\n• Nalanda Project Ministry of Minority Affairs ka programme tha, minority higher-educational institutions ke faculty development ke liye.\nProgramme 4 March 2014 ko launch hua aur initial implementation Aligarh Muslim University se start karne ka plan tha. [Q298]\n• 27 June 2025 ko Ministry of Tribal Affairs conference 'Adi Anveshan' ke concluding session mein Union Minister Jual Oram ne Adi\nKarmayogi Beta Version—A Responsive Governance Initiative—launch kiya. [Q299]\n• MPLADS December 1993 mein launch Central Government scheme hai. MPs apne area mein development works recommend kar sakte\nhain; annual entitlement ₹5 crore per MP hai aur Rajya Sabha MPs bhi covered hain. [Q300]\n• Sansad Adarsh Gram Yojana (SAGY) 11 October 2014 ko Loknayak Jayaprakash Narayan ki birth anniversary par launch hui. Aim villages\nmein values, participation aur model-village development promote karna hai. [Q301]\n\n\n17. International Days, Labour, Social Change & Rural Institutions\n\n• 1981 International Year of Disabled Persons tha. Listed later years mein 2023 International Year of Millets aur 2024 International Year of\nCamelids raha. [Q302]\n• World Literacy Day 8 September ko observe kiya jata hai. [Q303]\n• International Women's Day 8 March ko observe kiya jata hai. [Q304]\n• Social Empowerment Remembrance Day 20 March ko observe kiya jata hai. [Q305]\n• World Consumer Rights Day 15 March ko observe kiya jata hai. [Q306]\n• Bonded Labour System (Abolition) Act 1976 mein pass hua; Section 1(3) ke under ise 25 October 1975 se deemed effective maana gaya.\n[Q307]\n• Bonded Labour System (Abolition) Act 1976 mein pass hua. [Q308]\n• Modern science and technology, social planning, population growth aur material prosperity—sab Indian society mein rapid social change ke\nimportant factors ho sakte hain. [Q309]\n• Kurukshetra rural development focused magazine hai, jise Publications Division, Ministry of Information and Broadcasting publish karta hai.\n[Q310]\n• August 2011 mein Planning Commission expert panel ne transaction tax impose karne ke against recommendation di aur all citizens ko\nfree health services fund karne ke objective se taxable income par Health Surcharge recommend kiya. [Q311]\n• National Children's Fund 1979 mein establish hua. [Q312]\n• National Institute of Rural Development Hyderabad, Telangana mein located hai. [Q313]\n• CAPART ka full form Council for Advancement of People's Action and Rural Technology hai. 1 September 1986 ko constitute hua; Ministry\nof Rural Development ke direction mein rural-welfare programmes ko assistance/evaluation support deta hai aur headquarters New Delhi\nhai. [Q314]\n• CAPART Ministry of Rural Development ke under function karta tha. [Q315]\nEXAM TRAP — Rapid Social Change Factors\n▸Modern science & technology, social planning, population growth aur material prosperity rapid social change ke major factors hain.\n[Q309]\n\n\n\n\n18. Aadhaar, PURA, Rehabilitation, Disability Institutions & Youth\n\n• Aadhaar UIDAI ka identity programme hai. Ye 12-digit unique identification number demographic aur biometric information—photo,\nfingerprints, iris scan—par based hota hai aur infants sahit individuals ko identity provide karta hai. [Q316]\n• 12-digit unique identification number ka brand name Aadhaar hai. [Q317]\n• September 2010 mein Maharashtra ke Nandurbar district ka Tembhli village news mein tha kyunki UIDAI Aadhaar rollout sabse pehle\nwahin launch hua. [Q318]\n• Tembhli village, Nandurbar (Maharashtra) Aadhaar enrolment rollout ka first village tha. [Q319]\n• Vision 2020 for India former President Dr A.P.J. Abdul Kalam se associated document tha, jiska focus India ko 2020 tak developed nation\nbanane ki vision par tha. [Q320]\n• PURA—Providing Urban Amenities to Rural Areas—concept former President Dr A.P.J. Abdul Kalam ne popularise kiya aur 54th Republic\nDay eve par public ke saamne present kiya. [Q321]\n• PURA ka full form Providing Urban Amenities in Rural Areas hai; is policy ko A.P.J. Abdul Kalam ne strongly advocate kiya. [Q322]\n• PURA concept ko A.P.J. Abdul Kalam ne popularise/present kiya. [Q323]\n• Pradhan Mantri Shram Awards Ministry of Labour and Employment confer karta hai. Eligible categories mein Central/State public-sector\nenterprises aur selected private manufacturing units ke employees shamil hote hain. [Q324]\n• National Rehabilitation and Resettlement Policy, 2007 project ke liye land acquisition se affected persons ke saath other involuntarily\ndisplaced persons/families ko bhi cover karti hai. Policy Ministry of Rural Development ne formulate ki. [Q325]","• National Handicapped Finance and Development Corporation, jise ab National Divyangjan Finance and Development Corporation\n(NDFDC) kaha jata hai, Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment ke under 24 February 1997 ko establish hua. [Q326]\n• Major disability sectors ke liye national institutes establish kiye gaye hain. National Institute for Empowerment of Persons with Visual\nDisabilities Dehradun mein located hai. [Q327]\n• Persons with disabilities ke liye India ki first exclusive university Chitrakoot, Uttar Pradesh mein establish hui; headquarters bhi wahin hai.\nIska naam Jagadguru Rambhadracharya Divyang State University hai. [Q328]\n• India ki first Open University August 1982 mein Hyderabad mein establish hui aur Dr B.R. Ambedkar ke naam par rakhi gayi. Us time\nHyderabad Andhra Pradesh mein tha; ab Telangana mein hai. [Q329]\n• Tarun Bharat Sangh Dr Rajendra Singh ne found kiya voluntary organisation hai jo Rajasthan mein sustainable development par kaam\nkarta hai. [Q330]\n• National Youth Policy 2014 youth ko 15-29 years age group ke roop mein cover karti hai. [Q331]\n\n                            Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · Employment & Welfare Schemes Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0459","t":"12 Poverty & Unemployment","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["POVERTY & UNEMPLOYMENT — FULL MIND MAP\n                               Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\nTopics in this chapter:\n 1. Poverty — Meaning, Types & Estimation Methods\n 2. Poverty-Line Committees, Calorie Norms & Methodologies\n 3. Inequality, Poverty Concepts & Estimation Institutions\n 4. National Poverty Estimates, Hunger & Historical Trends\n 5. State-wise Poverty — Ratios, Numbers & Regional Patterns\n 6. Poverty Eradication, DRDA, PDS & Rural Programmes\n 7. Human Poverty Index & Multidimensional Poverty Index\n 8. Causes, Social Indicators, SHGs & Poverty-Alleviation Tools\n 9. Employment Growth, Educated Unemployment & Rural–Urban Link\n 10. Employment Structure & Organised-Sector Trends\n 11. Disguised / Hidden Unemployment & Usual Status\n 12. Measuring Unemployment — NSSO, WPR, LFPR & Elasticity\n 13. PLFS, Women’s Employment & Recent Unemployment Trends\n\n\n 1. Poverty — Meaning, Types & Estimation Methods\n\n• Poverty line society/country mein minimum consumption/income level ko indicate karti hai, jo basic needs meet karne ke liye necessary\nmaana jata hai. [Q1]\n• Poverty ka basic meaning fundamental needs ko fulfil na kar pana hai. Iske two major forms Absolute Poverty aur Relative Poverty hain;\nSubjective Poverty bhi ek recognised form hai. [Q2]\n• 1995 ke World Summit for Social Development, Copenhagen mein United Nations ne poverty ko two forms—Absolute Poverty aur Overall\nPoverty—mein define kiya. [Q3]\n• India mein poverty line household consumption expenditure par based determine hoti hai. Head Count Ratio aur calorie-intake approach\nbhi poverty estimation mein use hue hain. [Q4]\n• Planning Commission ne below-poverty-line population estimate karne ke liye expert group constitute kiya; poverty estimation mein\nNational Sample Survey Office (NSSO) ke household consumption-expenditure data ka use hua. [Q5]\n• Poverty estimation ke household surveys NSSO/NSS ke through conduct kiye jate the. [Q6]\n• India mein unemployment aur poverty estimates historically NSS/NSSO household consumption and employment surveys par based rahe\nhain. [Q7]\n• India ki poverty line determination ka base consumption data hai. [Q8]\n• India mein poverty estimation ka base household consumption expenditure hai. [Q9]\n• Urban poverty estimation mein CPI-IW use hota tha, jabki rural/agricultural-labour poverty estimation mein CPI-AL/RL relevant price index\ntha. [Q10]\n• NSSO 61st Round (2004-05) mein poverty estimation ke liye URP aur MRP methods use hue. URP mein consumption items ke liye\nuniform 30-day recall period use hota tha. [Q11]\n• Literacy poverty-line estimation ka direct indicator nahi hai; other listed consumption/price-related indicators use hote hain. [Q12]\n\n\n 2. Poverty-Line Committees, Calorie Norms & Methodologies\n\n• 1977 mein Dr. Y.K. Alagh ki chairmanship mein 'Minimum Needs Programme and Effective Consumption Demand' Task Force constitute\nhui aur 1979 mein report submit ki. Poverty line ko nutritional requirement/calorie norm par base kiya gaya—rural ~2,400 kcal aur urban\n~2,100 kcal per-capita per day. [Q13]\n• Poverty line ka concept V.M. Dandekar aur N. Rath ne 1971 ke fundamental study mein diya. [Q14]\n• Traditional Planning Commission poverty-line approach mein urban area ke liye 2,100 kcal aur rural area ke liye 2,400 kcal per-capita per\nday norm use kiya gaya. [Q15]\n• Rural poverty-line estimation ke historical calorie norm mein 2,400 kcal per person per day use kiya gaya. [Q16]\n• Traditional poverty-line approach calorie intake se linked raha hai. [Q17]\n• Poor households minimum prescribed food basket ke entitlement se related hote hain. [Q18]\n• Suresh Tendulkar was the chairman of the committee constituted by the Government of India in 2005 to determine new standards for\nestimating the population living below the poverty line. [Q19]\n• States mein price levels differ karte hain. Suresh Tendulkar Committee (2005) ne essential consumption goods/services aur State-wise\nexpenditure ko use karke rural aur urban poverty lines estimate ki. [Q20]\n• NSSO household consumer expenditure survey ke data par Tendulkar Committee recommendations ko use karke Planning Commission ne\n2011-12 ka poverty ratio aur poverty line update kiya; ye poverty estimates July 2013 mein release hue. [Q21]\n• Tendulkar Committee ke 2012/NSSO-based context mein poverty ratio 33.35% diya gaya tha; figure ko relevant survey/report context ke\nsaath padhein. [Q22]\n• Rangarajan Committee ne rural aur urban poverty line ke liye alag consumption-expenditure thresholds liye: rural areas mein Rs 32 per\ncapita per day (Rs 972 monthly) aur urban areas mein Rs 47 per capita per day (Rs 1,407 monthly). [Q23]\n\n EXAM TRAP — Tendulkar vs Rangarajan — Poverty Line Numbers\n ▸2011-12 Tendulkar estimate: rural ₹816/month (₹26.32/day), urban ₹1,000/month (₹32.25/day).\n ▸Rangarajan methodology: rural Rs 972/month (~Rs 32/day), urban Rs 1,407/month (~Rs 47/day), poverty ratio 29.5%; Tendulkar estimate 21.9%.\n Dono methodologies ke thresholds alag hain.\n [Q21, Q23]","3. Inequality, Poverty Concepts & Estimation Institutions\n\n• Lorenz Curve Dr. Max O. Lorenz ne income aur wealth inequality study karne ke liye propound ki. Isi economist ke naam par ise Lorenz\nCurve kaha jata hai. [Q24]\n• Income distribution ko Lorenz curve se represent kiya jata hai. [Q25]\n• Gini coefficient Corrado Gini ne 1912 mein propound kiya. Ye society mein income inequality aur national-income distribution ka\nmathematical measure hai. [Q26]\n• 'Culture of Poverty' concept American anthropologist Oscar Lewis se associated hai; persistent poverty ke saath cultural/behavioural\npatterns generation-to-generation reproduce ho sakte hain. [Q27]\n• Economist Ragnar Nurkse ne 1953 ki book 'Problems of Capital Formation in Underdeveloped Countries' mein Vicious Circle of Poverty\nexplain kiya: poor countries poverty ke self-reinforcing cycle ki wajah se poor bane reh sakte hain. [Q28]\n• Cyclical poverty mein people baar-baar poverty mein enter aur exit karte rehte hain. Ye agriculture aur industry dono contexts mein mil sakti\nhai. [Q29]\n• National aur State poverty-line estimation ka nodal role pehle Planning Commission ke paas tha; 1 January 2015 se Planning Commission\nki jagah NITI Aayog aaya. [Q30]\n• National aur State level poverty estimation ke liye Planning Commission nodal agency thi. [Q31]\n• 1989 mein D. T. Lakdawala ki chairmanship mein expert group constitute hua, jiska kaam 'Proportion and Number of Poor' estimate karna,\npoverty-estimation methodology review karna aur zarurat par poverty line redefine karna tha; report July 1993 mein submit hui. [Q32]\n• Suresh Tendulkar Committee aur Lakdawala Committee dono India mein poverty estimation se associated rahe hain. [Q33]\n• Sixth Five-Year Plan period se Planning Commission national/State poverty estimation coordinate karta raha; methodology review ke liye\ntime-to-time expert committees constitute hui. [Q34]\n• Urban poor identification ke liye 2010 mein Prof. S. R. Hashim ki chairmanship mein expert committee constitute hui, jisne December 2012\nmein report submit ki. [Q35]\n• Poverty line se neeche rehne wale logon ko identifiable group ke roop mein classify karne se programme agencies benefits aur\ninterventions ko accurately target kar sakti hain. [Q36]\n• Malnutrition poor aur rich dono groups mein ho sakta hai—poor ke nutrition options limited ho sakte hain, jabki rich group mein\nexcessive/unhealthy diet problem ho sakti hai. [Q37]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Poverty Estimation Committees\n▸Suresh Tendulkar Committee aur Lakdawala Committee dono poverty estimation se associated hain.\n▸Historical poverty ratios methodology aur year ke saath change hote hain; figures ko same-series context mein compare karein.\n[Q33]\n\n\n\n\n4. National Poverty Estimates, Hunger & Historical Trends\n\n• Tendulkar estimate mein 2004-05 poverty ratio 37.2% tha; Planning Commission ke 2011-12 update mein ye 21.9% tha. [Q38]\n• Tendulkar methodology ke 2011-12 estimate mein India ka poverty ratio 21.9% tha. [Q39]\n• NFHS-5 based multidimensional-poverty data mein Jammu & Kashmir 4.80%, Himachal Pradesh 4.93% aur Uttar Pradesh 22.93% tha.\nStates ke comparison mein Himachal Pradesh relevant hai, kyunki Jammu & Kashmir Union Territory hai. [Q40]\n• Planning Commission/Tendulkar series mein poverty ratio 2004-05 mein 37.2%, 2009-10 mein 29.8% aur 2011-12 mein 21.9% tha. NITI\nAayog National MPI 2023 mein multidimensional poverty 2015-16 ke 24.85% se 2019-21 mein 14.96% tak ghati. [Q41]\n• Planning Commission ke 2009-10 estimate mein poverty ratio lagbhag 30% tha, jo earlier 37.2% estimate se lower tha. [Q42]\n• Planning Commission ke 2004-05 URP estimate mein 27.5% population poverty line ke neeche thi. [Q43]\n• Historical estimate mein India ki 26.1% population poverty line ke neeche thi; Tendulkar methodology ke 2011-12 data mein poverty ratio\n21.9% tha. [Q44]\n• Eleventh Five-Year Plan mein 7 crore new employment opportunities create karke poverty ratio 10% reduce karne ka target tha; Twelfth\nPlan mein bhi five years mein 10% reduction ka target rakha gaya. [Q45]\n• World Development Report 2005 ke context mein India ki 80% population $2/day se kam income par thi. World Bank ne September 2022\nmein international poverty line $1.90/day (2011 PPP) se revise karke $2.15/day (2017 PPP) ki. [Q46]\n• FAO ke 2003-era assessment mein world hungry population mein India ka share lagbhag 30% bataya gaya tha. Later UN/WFP context\nmein income-poverty aur undernourishment dono significant rahe; National/Global MPI updates mein 2019-21 India multidimensional-poverty\nfigure 231.38 million (16.4%) diya gaya. [Q47]\n• Global Hunger Index 2017 mein India score 31.4 ke saath 119 countries mein rank 100 par tha. Later ranks: 2020—94/107, 2021—\n101/116, 2022—107/121, 2023—111/125, 2024—105/127, 2025—102/123. [Q48]\n• Global Hunger Index hunger aur malnutrition ko multidimensional indicators se compare karta hai; historical editions IFPRI ke saath partner\norganisations dwara publish hoti thi. [Q49]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Poverty Figures — Historical vs Updated\n▸Historical figures: 2004-05 Tendulkar 37.2%, 2009-10 29.8%, 2011-12 Tendulkar 21.9%; multidimensional-poverty updates alag series hain.\n▸GHI rank aur World Bank international poverty-line figures bhi year-specific hote hain; later update ko old-year figure ke saath mix nahi karein.\n[Q38, Q39, Q41, Q43, Q44, Q46, Q48]\n\n\n\n\n5. State-wise Poverty — Ratios, Numbers & Regional Patterns","• 1990s ke official data mein Bihar, Orissa, Madhya Pradesh aur Maharashtra ke tribal groups mein poverty incidence other social groups ke\ncomparison mein bahut high tha. [Q50]\n• NCAER survey ke relevant State comparison mein Bihar mein poverty-line ke neeche population ka percentage highest tha. [Q51]\n• Bihar ki backwardness se social, economic aur political factors jude hain—low education, income inequality, social orthodoxies, industrial\ndeficiency aur weak political will jaise factors important rahe hain. [Q52]\n• Planning Commission ke 1999-2000 poverty data mein Odisha ka poverty ratio 47.1% tha, jo States mein highest tha. [Q53]\n• Planning Commission ke 2011-12 estimates mein listed States ke comparison mein Uttar Pradesh mein poverty line ke neeche rehne wale\npeople ki number highest thi. [Q54]\n• Tendulkar methodology ke 2011-12 estimate mein Rajasthan ka poverty ratio 14.71% tha, yani roughly 15%. [Q55]\n• Rangarajan methodology ke 2011-12 estimates mein all-India poverty ratio 29.5% tha; Chhattisgarh 47.9%, Goa 6.3% aur Rajasthan\n21.7%. Highest-poverty group: Chhattisgarh 47.9, Manipur 46.7, Odisha 45.9, MP 44.3, Jharkhand 42.4; lowest group mein Andaman-\nNicobar 6, Goa 6.3, Lakshadweep 6.5, Puducherry 7.7, Himachal Pradesh 10.9%. [Q56]\n• Based on both the Tendulkar methodology and the Rangarajan methodology, state-wise comparison mein, the lowest percentage of\npopulation below the poverty line in 2011-12 was found in Himachal Pradesh. [Q57]\n• 2009-10 ke official poverty-percentage data mein Goa ka poverty ratio States mein minimum tha. [Q58]\n• Poverty ratios 2009-10 → 2011-12: Kerala 12.0% → 7.05%; Punjab 15.9% → 8.26%; Andhra Pradesh 21.1% → 9.20%; Gujarat 23.0% →\n16.63%. [Q59]\n• World Bank ke relevant assessment mein Bihar, Uttar Pradesh, Madhya Pradesh aur Odisha mein milkar country ke 50% se zyada poor\npeople rehte the. [Q60]\n• Poverty ratios 1993-94 → 2011-12: Punjab 11.77% → 8.26%; Bihar 54.96% → 33.74%; Uttar Pradesh 40.85% → 29.43%; Rajasthan\n27.41% → 14.71%. [Q61]\n• Planning Commission estimate mein Bihar ka poverty ratio 1993-94 mein 54.96% tha; 2011-12 mein ye 33.74% tha. [Q62]\n• India State Hunger Index (ISHI) 2008 mein Madhya Pradesh ki condition worst thi; malnutrition aur infant-mortality situation Ethiopia/Chad\njaise African-country levels se compare ki gayi thi. [Q63]\n• Madhya Pradesh ko severe malnutrition ke historical context mein kabhi-kabhi ‘Ethiopia of India’ kaha gaya. [Q64]\nEXAM TRAP — Poverty-Ratio Pairing\n▸1993-94 poverty ratios: Punjab 11.77%, Bihar 54.96%, Uttar Pradesh 40.85%, Rajasthan 27.41%.\n[Q61]\n\n\n\n\n6. Poverty Eradication, DRDA, PDS & Rural Programmes\n\n• Poverty eradication ke important methods mein population control, higher saving/capital formation aur productive activities mein more\ninvestment shamil hain. [Q65]\n• India mein poverty extent ke estimates mein clear uniformity nahi thi: Seventh Plan estimate ne 1987-88 mein 29.9% population ko poor\ndikhaya, jabki expert-group estimate 39.3% tha. [Q66]\n• District Rural Development Agencies (DRDA) ka main role rural poverty reduce karne wali programmes ko effectively implement karna aur\ninter-sectoral/inter-departmental coordination ensure karna hai. [Q67]\n• Public Distribution System 1960s mein price-support programme ke roop mein start hua. Main objective essential commodities subsidised\nprices par provide karna tha. [Q68]\n• PDS ka objective poor households ko lower prices par food security dena, hoarding/black marketing rokna aur excessive pricing control\nkarna hai. [Q69]\n• Rural-poverty programme timeline: TRYSEM—15 August 1979; IRDP—2 October 1980 nationwide implementation; NREP—October 1980.\nIn programmes ka broad goal rural poverty aur unemployment reduce karna tha. [Q70]\n• Mitigating Poverty in Western Rajasthan (MPOWR) programme IFAD, Rajasthan State Government aur Ratan Tata Trust ke cooperation se\nimplement hua. Programme Jodhpur district mein operate hua aur December 2017 mein complete hua. [Q71]\n\n\n7. Human Poverty Index & Multidimensional Poverty Index\n\n• UNDP poverty ko broadly Income Poverty aur Human/Multidimensional deprivation ke forms mein dekhta hai. Income poverty quality-of-life\nnecessities ki kami se related hoti hai. [Q72]\n• UNDP ne Human Poverty Index (HPI) first time Human Development Report 1997 mein introduce kiya. 2010 se Human Development\nReport mein HPI ki jagah 10 deprivation indicators based Multidimensional Poverty Index (MPI) publish hona start hua. [Q73]\n• UNDP approach mein sirf income measure karke poverty ka complete estimate nahi milta; education, health aur standard-of-living\ndimensions ko bhi measure karna necessary hai. [Q74]\n• Human Poverty Index 1997 mein introduce hua. [Q75]\n• Global Multidimensional Poverty Index (Global MPI) 100+ developing countries mein acute multidimensional poverty measure karta hai.\nCore dimensions health, education aur standard of living hain. [Q76]\n• Global Multidimensional Poverty Index 2010 mein OPHI ne UNDP ke collaboration se develop kiya aur Human Development Report 2010\nmein introduce hua. [Q77]\n• National MPI mein 3 equal dimensions hain - Health, Education aur Standard of Living. Health mein nutrition, child/adolescent mortality aur\nmaternal health; Education mein years of schooling aur school attendance; Standard of Living mein cooking fuel, sanitation, drinking water,\nhousing, electricity, assets etc. include hote hain. [Q78]\n• Multidimensional Poverty Index (MPI) 2010 mein OPHI aur UNDP ne develop kiya. MPI 3 dimensions—Health, Education aur Standard of\nLiving—ke 10 indicators use karta hai: infant mortality, nutrition, years of schooling, school attendance, cooking fuel, sanitation, drinking\nwater, electricity, housing aur assets. [Q79]","• Global MPI 100+ developing countries ke acute multidimensional poverty ko education, health aur standard-of-living deprivations ke\nthrough measure karta hai. [Q80]\n• Developing countries mein poverty intensity measure karne ke liye MPI useful hai, kyunki ye Health, Education aur Standard of Living jaise\nmultiple dimensions capture karta hai. [Q81]\n• OPHI-UNDP Multidimensional Poverty Index household level par Education, Health, Assets aur Services se related deprivations ko capture\nkarta hai. [Q82]\n• Earlier multidimensional-poverty figure 53.7% tha; Human Development Report 2025 ke 2022 estimate mein India ki multidimensionally\npoor population 16.4% (233.67 million; survey year 2019-21 mein 231.83 million) thi. [Q83]\n\nEXAM TRAP — HPI vs MPI — 1997 and 2010\n▸Human Poverty Index (HPI) HDR 1997 mein introduce hua.\n▸2010 se HPI ki jagah MPI aaya; Global MPI OPHI + UNDP collaboration se health, education aur standard-of-living deprivations measure karta hai,\nsirf income line nahi.\n[Q73, Q74, Q75, Q76, Q77, Q79, Q80]\n\n\n\n\n8. Causes, Social Indicators, SHGs & Poverty-Alleviation Tools\n\n• Same income-class ke earners mein time ke saath inequality badhe to iska matlab rich-poor gap widen ho raha hai—rich relatively richer\naur poor relatively poorer ho rahe hain. [Q84]\n• Poverty ke major causes inadequate economic growth, high population growth aur unemployment hain; increasing investment rate poverty\nka cause nahi, generally reduction ka support factor hai. [Q85]\n• Illiteracy, healthcare access ki kami aur employment opportunities ki kami social indicators of poverty hain; limited transport means ko is\nspecific set mein social poverty indicator nahi maana gaya. [Q86]\n• Poverty ke social indicators mein illiteracy, malnutrition se low immunity, health services ki kami, safe drinking water/sanitation access ki\nkami aur employment opportunities ki kami shamil hain. [Q87]\n• Differential Rate of Interest (DRI) Scheme Central Government ne 1972 mein introduce ki; iska objective weaker sections ko concessional\nrates par loans provide karna tha. [Q88]\n• May 2013 mein DAY-NRLM ke under Women Self Help Groups (SHGs) ko financially strengthen karne aur rural women empowerment ke\nliye Rs 3 lakh tak ke bank loans par 7% interest-rate support announce kiya gaya. [Q89]\n• Dr. A.P.J. Abdul Kalam ko '2008 Hoover Medal' ke liye select kiya gaya; announcement March 2009 mein New York mein hui. [Q90]\n• Self-Help Group idea Nobel Peace Prize winner Muhammad Yunus se associated hai; unhone 1976 mein Bangladesh mein Grameen Bank\nstart kiya. SHGs/microfinance poverty alleviation, financial inclusion aur women empowerment ke important tools bane. [Q91]\n• SAARC poverty-alleviation ministerial meeting April 2002 mein Islamabad, Pakistan mein hui; fourth ministerial meeting 2015 mein\nThimphu, Bhutan mein organise hui. [Q92]\n• Indian social structure ke major features mein high rural population, religious diversity, caste/sub-caste diversity, cultural diversity aur sex-\nratio patterns shamil hain. Poverty socio-economic condition ko reflect karti hai, social-structure feature ke roop mein classify nahi ki gayi.\n[Q93]\n\n\n\n9. Employment Growth, Educated Unemployment & Rural–Urban Link\n\n• NITI Aayog paper 'Changing Structure of Rural Economy of India: Implications for Employment and Growth, 2017' mein rural aur urban\nworker productivity increase hui. Rural income per worker 2004-05 mein 37,273 se 2011-12 mein 1,01,755 hua. [Q94]\n• India mein employment-growth rate historical context mein roughly 2.0% per annum ke around raha. [Q95]\n• Unemployment badhne se income source unavailable hota hai, isliye poverty-line ke neeche jane wale logon ki sankhya badh sakti hai.\n[Q96]\n• Educated unemployment ka highest percentage Kerala mein reported raha hai. [Q97]\n• High development rate ke saath employment create hota hai, lekin vocational education/skill mismatch ki wajah se educated persons bhi\nunemployed reh sakte hain. [Q98]\n• Financial help unemployed youth ko minimum requirements meet karne aur future plans execute karne mein support kar sakti hai; second\nassumption is logic se necessary nahi hai. [Q99]\n• Rural areas mein low education/skills ki wajah se villages se migrate karne wale log cities mein aksar low-level jobs tak limited reh jate\nhain. [Q100]\n\n\n10. Employment Structure & Organised-Sector Trends\n\n• PLFS 2023-24 ke relevant State comparison mein descending order Assam > West Bengal > Nagaland > Meghalaya tha. [Q101]\n• Economic Survey 2009-10 context mein 1994-2007 ke dauran organised-sector employment growth mein decline record hua, mainly public\norganised-sector employment ki decline ki wajah se. [Q102]\n• Developing/underdeveloped economies mein structural unemployment common hai. Main cause available skills aur jobs ke beech\nmismatch hota hai; backward economic structure, limited capital aur labour abundance bhi important causes hain. [Q103]\n\n\n11. Disguised / Hidden Unemployment & Usual Status\n\n• Disguised unemployment mein labour ki marginal productivity zero hoti hai; worker ko remove karne par total output mein reduction nahi\nhota. [Q104]","• Disguised/hidden unemployment tab hota hai jab jitne workers se job ho sakti hai usse zyada persons employed hon. Economics mein term\nka early use Joan Robinson se associated hai; technical sense mein marginal productivity near zero hoti hai. [Q105]\n• Disguised unemployment mein additional worker ki marginal productivity zero ya near-zero hoti hai. [Q106]\n• India mein disguised unemployment mainly primary sector—especially agriculture/allied activities—mein milta hai, kyunki land/natural\nresources par population pressure high hota hai. [Q107]\n• India mein disguised unemployment mainly agricultural sector mein milti hai. [Q108]\n• Rural India mein unemployment ke two common forms seasonal unemployment aur disguised unemployment hain. [Q109]\n• Usual Status Unemployment pichhle 365 days ke work-status ko dekhta hai. Agar person year ke major part (183 days ya usse zyada)\nmein work nahi karta, to use 'usual status unemployment' category mein rakha jata hai. [Q110]\n\n\n12. Measuring Unemployment — NSSO, WPR, LFPR & Elasticity\n\n• NSS unemployment measurement ke three methods: Usual Principal Status—previous year ke major period (183+ days) mein work na\nmilna while available/seeking work; Current Weekly Status—survey week mein gainful work na milna; Current Daily Status—day-wise\nemployment availability ko measure karta hai. [Q111]\n• India mein employment/unemployment data collection aur publication mein NSS/NSSO ka historically major role raha hai; current labour-\nmarket estimates PLFS framework se aate hain. [Q112]\n• Employment elasticity (1999-2000 to 2009-10): Construction 1.13; Finance & Real Estate 0.66; Manufacturing 0.09; Agriculture 0.04; All\nSectors 0.19. [Q113]\n• Rapid population growth, skill deficiency aur manpower planning ki kami unemployment ke important causes hain; per-capita income\nincrease unemployment ka direct cause nahi hai. [Q114]\n• Worker Population Ratio (WPR) total population mein employed persons ka percentage hota hai; 100 people mein 40 employed hon to\nWPR 40% hoga. [Q115]\n• Labour Force Participation Rate working-age population mein employed + work-seeking/willing persons ka ratio hai. Working-age\npopulation commonly 15-64 years, kuch Indian contexts mein 15-59 years, li jati hai. [Q116]\n\n\n13. PLFS, Women’s Employment & Recent Unemployment Trends\n\n• PLFS 2023-24 mein rural women ka LFPR 2021-22 ke 27.2% se badhkar 2023-24 mein 35.5% hua; rural increase urban women ke\ncomparison mein zyada tha. [Q117]\n• Bihar mein working women ka sabse bada share self-employed category mein hai; bahut si women household enterprises mein helper ke\nroop mein kaam karti hain. PLFS employment ko self-employed, regular wage earners aur casual labourers categories mein classify karta\nhai; PLFS 2023-24 data mein Bihar ki working women mein self-employment dominant tha. [Q118]\n• PLFS annual report July 2023-June 2024 mein 15+ age group ke Usual Status context mein male aur female labour-force participation\nrates mein increase dikhaya gaya. [Q119]\n• MoSPI ke October 2023 data mein female labour-force participation rate 2017-18 ke 23.3% se badhkar 2022-23 mein 37.0% ho gaya.\n[Q120]\n• NSS 2011-12 mein Current Daily Status unemployment rate 5.6% aur Usual Status unemployment rate 2.2% thi. [Q121]\n• Employment-Unemployment Survey 2011-12 (68th Round) ke Current Daily Status basis par urban unemployment 5.5% aur rural\nunemployment 5.9% tha. [Q122]\n• PLFS July 2023-June 2024 ke Usual Status (PS+SS) basis par 15+ age persons ki unemployment rate 3.2% thi. [Q123]\nEXAM TRAP — Female Labour Participation — Year Context\n▸Older period mein female labour participation decline dikhaya gaya tha; PLFS 2023-24 mein later rise dikhai deta hai.\n▸FLFPR: 23.3% (2017-18) → 37.0% (2022-23) → 41.7% (2023-24, Economic Survey 2024-25 context).\n[Q117, Q119, Q120]\n\n\n\n\n                                Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · Poverty & Unemployment Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0460","t":"13 International Trade","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["INTERNATIONAL TRADE — FULL MIND MAP\n                                Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\nTopics in this chapter:\n 1. Rules of Origin, SCRI & Foreign Trade Policy Basics\n 2. India's Trade Openness, Trade-to-GDP & Global Share\n 3. Regional & Country-wise Trading Partners\n 4. Trade Balance, Net Exports, BoP & Current Account\n 5. Import Composition, Energy Imports & Import Procedure\n 6. Export Composition — Manufactured & Engineering Goods\n 7. Commodity Exports, Destinations & Export Growth\n 8. Services, Invisible Exports, Entrepot & Trade Duties\n 9. EXIM / Foreign Trade Policies & Export Excellence\n 10. Globalization, Liberalization & 1991 Reforms\n 11. Free Trade Zones, SEZ & EPZ\n 12. Export Promotion Institutions & Schemes\n 13. Remittances, Emerging Markets & Tourism\n 14. Bilateral Trade Agreements, Trade Barriers & IITF\n\n\n 1. Rules of Origin, SCRI & Foreign Trade Policy Basics\n\n• Rules of Origin kisi product ka national source determine karne ke criteria hain; import duties aur restrictions kai cases mein origin/source\n par depend karte hain, isliye international trade mein ye important hote hain. [Q1]\n• India, Japan aur Australia ke trade ministers ne 27 April 2021 ko Supply Chain Resilience Initiative formally launch ki; aim Indo-Pacific\n supply chains ko resilient aur diversified banana hai. [Q2]\n• Ministry of Commerce and Industry, Government of India Foreign Trade Policy se related hai. Piyush Goyal Union Minister of Commerce\n and Industry hain. [Q3]\n• Department of Commerce ka long-term vision India ko world trade mein major player/leadership role tak le jana tha. 2019-20 tak goods +\n services exports ko US$ 900 billion tak le jane ka target bhi diya gaya tha. [Q4]\n\n 2. India's Trade Openness, Trade-to-GDP & Global Share\n\n• Historical FY 2011-12 data mein India ka total external trade ₹38,11,360 crore tha. [Q5]\n• FY 2011-12 ke historical data mein India ka total external trade ₹38,11,360 crore tha. [Q6]\n• FY 2011-12 ke historical data mein India ka total external trade ₹38,11,360 crore tha. [Q7]\n• 2000 se 2019 ke beech India ka trade-to-GDP ratio 27% se badhkar 40% hua; 2008, 2011, 2012 aur 2013 mein ratio 50% se upar gaya.\n [Q8]\n• 2012-13 mein India ka merchandise trade-to-GDP ratio 40.6% tha. World Bank data mein ye ratio 2021: 30.6%, 2022: 35.1%, 2023: 30.4%\n aur 2024: 29.7% raha. [Q9]\n• 1990-91 se 2011-12 ke beech goods exports + imports ka GDP share almost three times hua. World Bank 2024 data mein merchandise\n trade/GDP 29.7% tha; WITS 2023 data mein goods-services exports 21.45% aur imports 23.54% of GDP the. [Q10]\n• 2010-11 mein India ke goods-services exports GDP ka 22% the. WITS 2023 data mein exports 21.45% aur imports 23.54% of GDP the.\n [Q11]\n• 2020, 2021 aur 2022 mein India ka global goods-export share respectively 1.6%, 1.8% aur 1.8% tha; commercial-services export share\n roughly 4.1%, 4.0% aur 4.4% raha. [Q12]\n• WTO Global Trade Outlook and Statistics Update, October 2025 ke 2024 data mein India ka global merchandise-export share 1.8% (rank\n 18) aur import share 2.9% (rank 8) tha. Older 0.60% figure ko relevant historical context ke saath padhein. [Q13]\n• WTO Global Trade Outlook and Statistics Update, October 2025 ke 2024 data mein India ka global merchandise-export share 1.8% (rank\n 18) aur merchandise-import share 2.9% (rank 8) tha. [Q14]\n• 2018 mein worldwide exports mein India ka share 1.7% tha. WTO Global Trade Outlook and Statistics Update (October 2025) ke 2024\n merchandise-trade data mein India ka export share 1.8% tha, rank 18; merchandise imports mein share 2.9%, rank 9 tha. [Q15]\n• 2014 mein commercial-services exports mein India rank 8th tha. WTO Global Trade Outlook and Statistics Update, October 2025 ke 2024\n data mein services-export rank 8th aur import rank 9th tha. [Q16]\n• WTO Global Trade Outlook and Statistics, April 2024 ke 2023 data mein commercial-services exports ke top contributors USA, UK,\n Germany, Ireland aur China the. [Q17]\n• Global merchandise trade mein China leading exporter hai; 2024 mein uska world merchandise-export share lagbhag 14.6% tha. [Q18]\n EXAM TRAP — External Trade & Trade-to-GDP — Year Context\n ▸FY 2011-12 ka historical total-trade figure ₹38,11,360 crore tha.\n ▸World Bank context mein trade-to-GDP ratio 2000 ke 27% se 2019 mein 40% hua; 2008, 2011, 2012 aur 2013 mein 50% se upar tha.\n [Q6, Q7, Q8]\n\n\n 3. Regional & Country-wise Trading Partners","• Economic Survey 2025-26 ke 2024-25 aur Apr-Nov 2025-26(P) data mein Asia India's gross trade ka largest regional partner tha; Asia +\nASEAN ka combined participation bhi other regions se higher tha. [Q19]\n• Economic Survey 2025-26 mein April-March 2024-25 aur April-November 2025-26(P) ke import data mein Asia/Oceania region ka\ncombined contribution sabse dominant tha. [Q20]\n• Economic Survey 2025-26 ke 2024-25 aur April-November 2025-26(P) import data mein Asia India ka largest import region tha; Asia +\nASEAN ka combined participation other regions se higher tha. [Q21]\n• Economic Survey 2025-26 ke import data mein Asian countries ka share 2024-25 mein 61.6% aur April-November 2025-26(P) mein 60.7%\ntha—largest regional share. [Q22]\n• Historical comparison mein maximum import trade OPEC group ke saath dikhaya gaya tha; Economic Survey 2025-26 ke updated regional\ndata mein Asia largest import region hai. [Q23]\n• 2011 mein India ke international trade mein China-UAE pair ka percentage share sabse bada tha. 2024-25 mein major trade-partner order\nUSA > China > UAE > Russia tha; top five countries ka total exports mein 35.19% aur imports mein 43.87% share tha. [Q24]\n• 2024-25 mein United States India ka largest trading partner tha; listed order mein uske baad China, UAE aur Russia the. [Q25]\n• 2024-25 mein India ka sabse zyada total trade USA ke saath tha; China second aur United Arab Emirates third major trading partner the\n(June 2025 trade data). [Q26]\n• 2024-25 mein India ka maximum trade USA ke saath hua; China aur United Arab Emirates respectively second aur third major trading\npartners the (June 2025 trade data). [Q27]\n• 2024-25 mein USA India ka largest total-trade partner tha, China second aur UAE third. [Q28]\n• 2024-25 aur 2023-24 mein given major countries ke comparison mein India ka most favourable trade balance/surplus USA ke saath tha;\nexports imports se higher the. [Q29]\n• Historical current-account comparison mein India ka USA ke saath trade surplus tha. Ministry of Commerce October 2025 ke FY 2024-25\ndata mein top trading partners order USA, China, UAE, Russia aur Saudi Arabia tha. [Q30]\n• 2024-25 mein India ke imports by value mein China sabse bada source tha: China US$113,447.34 million, Russia US$63,811.44 million,\nUAE US$63,403.01 million aur USA US$45,625.20 million. [Q31]\n• India ke overall imports ka largest source China tha; 2024-25 mein China se imports US$113,447.34 million the, Russia, UAE aur USA se\nhigher. [Q32]\n• India-Sri Lanka bilateral trade decade mein fluctuate hua: 2011—US$4.88 bn; 2013—3.73 bn; 2017—5.23 bn; 2019—4.59 bn; 2020—3.61\nbn. [Q33]\n• Updated trade figures mein India ka maximum import source China hai, jabki arms supply ka major source Russia raha hai. [Q34]\n• 2024-25 mein India ke exports ka sabse bada destination USA tha; UAE second major destination tha. [Q35]\n• 2009-10 mein India ke exports mein UAE ka share sabse high tha. [Q36]\n• USA ka India ke total exports mein share 2024-25 mein 19.77% aur April-November 2025-26 mein 20.22% tha. [Q37]\nEXAM TRAP — Trading Partners — Historical vs Updated\n▸Historical questions mein China jaise old-period values mil sakte hain; 2024-25 updated data mein USA India ka largest trading partner tha.\n▸Trading-partner rank ko relevant financial year ke saath padhein; later data old-period ranking ko historical context se erase nahi karta.\n[Q25, Q26, Q28, Q35, Q36, Q37]\n\n\n4. Trade Balance, Net Exports, BoP & Current Account\n\n• December 2022 provisional goods-services trade balance -US$11.98 billion tha. April-March 2024-25 mein total exports US$820.93 billion\naur total imports US$915.19 billion record hue. [Q38]\n• 1949-50 se 2024-25(P) tak India ka trade balance lagbhag poore period negative raha; sirf 1972-73 aur 1976-77 mein trade balance\npositive tha. [Q39]\n• India ka trade balance 1949-50 se 2024-25(P) tak almost poore period negative raha; sirf 1972-73 aur 1976-77 mein positive tha. [Q40]\n• IMF Balance of Payments Manual 5 framework mein India's trade deficit 2012-13 mein US$195.66 billion ke peak par tha; 2013-14 ke\nUS$147.6 billion se ghatkar 2016-17 tak US$112.44 billion ho gaya. Petroleum oil aur non-oil products ki prices mein broad decline ne is\nreduction mein major role play kiya. [Q41]\n• Economic Survey 2023-24: multinational companies ke Global Capability Centres (GCCs) ke preferred destination ke roop mein India ki\nposition strong hui, jisse software aur business-services exports ko boost mila. Globally digitally delivered services exports mein India ka\nshare 2019 ke 4.4% se badhkar 2023 mein 6% ho gaya. [Q42]\n• Trade Balance kisi period mein total exports aur imports ki value ka difference hota hai; exports zyada hon to trade surplus aur imports\nzyada hon to trade deficit hota hai. [Q43]\n• Net Exports = given period mein total exports ki value minus total imports ki value. [Q44]\n• Foreign trade mein imports aur exports dono shamil hote hain. Balance of Payments ek specified period, usually one year, ke country ke\ninternational transactions ka record hota hai. [Q45]\n• Balance of Payments (BoP) specified period mein country residents ke rest of world ke saath international economic transactions ka\nsystematic account hota hai. [Q46]\n• Balance of Payments (BoP) ek statement/account hai jisme usually 1 year ke period mein country ke residents aur rest of the world ke\nresidents ke beech hone wale international economic transactions - imports, exports aur other cross-border transactions - record kiye jate\nhain. [Q47]\n• Balance of Payments (BoP) country ke residents aur rest of the world ke beech ek period, usually 1 year, mein hone wale international\neconomic transactions ka complete record hota hai. [Q48]\n• Balance of Payments (BoP) mein visible trade, invisible trade aur debt-related transactions shamil hote hain. Visible trade goods\nimports/exports aur invisible trade services imports/exports se related hai. [Q49]","• BoP current account mein visible transactions (exports-imports) aur invisible transactions include hote hain. Exports aur imports ke\ndifference ko balance of trade kaha jata hai. [Q50]\n• Current Account mein visible aur invisible items ke exports-imports record hote hain; imports zyada hon to Current Account Deficit (CAD)\naur exports zyada hon to surplus hota hai. [Q51]\n• Current Account Deficit tab hota hai jab visible + invisible imports ki value corresponding exports se zyada ho. [Q52]\n5. Import Composition, Energy Imports & Import Procedure\n\n• 2000-01 mein pearls aur precious stones compared imports mein high-value item the; 2024-25 mein chemical elements and compounds ka\nimport value in items se higher tha. [Q53]\n• India ka major import-related foreign-exchange outgo petroleum/POL par hota raha hai. Economic Survey 2025-26 mein 2024-25 aur April-\nSeptember 2025-26(P) dono periods mein POL top imported item tha. [Q54]\n• Economic Survey 2025-26 ke import composition mein petroleum/POL aur edible oils ka combined importance bahut high raha. [Q55]\n• Economic Survey 2025-26 ke import values (US$ million): Chemical elements & compounds - 41,236 (2024-25), 24,448 (Apr-Sep 2025-\n26P); Fertilizers - 10,226, 7,716; POL - 185,779, 92,116; Edible oils - 17,366, 9,992; Iron & steel - 17,400, 8,327. [Q56]\n• Economic Survey 2025-26 ke 2024-25 aur April-September 2025-26(P) data mein capital goods aur petroleum products ka combined\nimport value major high-value component tha. [Q57]\n• Economic Survey 2025-26 mein 2024-25 aur Apr-Sep 2025-26(P) ke imports mein petroleum products ka share sabse bada tha. Apr-Sep\n2025-26(P) mein POL imports US$92,116 million the, jo total imports US$376,483 million ka around 24.47% tha. [Q58]\n• Economic Survey 2025-26 mein import-share order 2024-25 / Apr-Sep 2025-26(P) ke liye: Fuel 30.1%/28.2% > Capital Goods\n12.3%/12.2% > Gold & Silver 8.7%/7.9% > Chemicals 6.3%/7.0%. [Q59]\n• Economic Survey 2025-26 mein total imports mein POL share 2024-25 mein 25.8% aur April-September 2025-26(P) mein 24.5% tha. [Q60]\n• Economic Survey 2025-26 import values (US$ million): POL—185,779/92,116; Capital Goods—96,541/50,390; Chemical elements &\ncompounds—41,236/24,448; pearls/precious stones etc. ko bhi major import group ke roop mein list kiya gaya. [Q61]\n• Updated figures mein India ko maximum LNG supply Qatar se milti hai. [Q62]\n• West Texas Intermediate (WTI) crude oil ka grade hai, jise Texas Light Sweet bhi kaha jata hai. [Q63]\n• Haldia Port ke major imports mein petroleum products, iron & steel aur machinery/heavy machine tools shamil hain; petroleum products,\niron & steel aur machinery/heavy machine tools tino major imports hain. [Q64]\n• Import process mein importer required foreign exchange ke liye RBI/authorised channel se approval arrange karta hai; approval ke baad\nindent/order ke through exporter ko supply order diya jata hai. [Q65]\n• International trade mein Letter of Credit (L/C) payment ensure karne wala documentary credit hai, jo importer ke request par financial\ninstitution issue karta hai. [Q66]\n• Letter of Credit (L/C) importer ke request par financial institution ke through issue documentary credit hai, jiska purpose international-trade\npayment ensure karna hai. [Q67]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Haldia Port — Major Imports\n▸Haldia ke major imports mein petroleum products, iron & steel aur machinery/heavy machine tools tino shamil hain.\n[Q64]\n\n\n6. Export Composition — Manufactured & Engineering Goods\n\n• India ke total exports mein manufactured goods ka share highest raha; export structure mein manufactured products ki dominance\nmodernization/industrialisation trend ko reflect karti hai. [Q68]\n• Economic Survey 2025-26 ke data mein manufactured goods ka total exports mein largest share tha—2024-25 mein 71% aur April-\nSeptember 2025-26(P) mein 72.1%. [Q69]\n• Export structure mein manufactured products ka rising share economy ke modernization ki taraf structural transformation ka important\nindicator hai. [Q70]\n• 2024-25 export composition mein engineering goods leading group tha, followed by petroleum products, electronic goods aur\ndrugs/pharmaceuticals. [Q71]\n• 2024-25 mein engineering goods export value agriculture-allied, minerals/ores aur handicrafts se kaafi zyada thi. [Q72]\n• 2024-25 export composition mein engineering goods leading export group tha; earlier periods mein product ranking alag thi. [Q73]\n• Ministry of Commerce and Industry ke 2023-24 aur 2024-25 data mein major merchandise export categories mein engineering goods top\npar the. [Q74]\n• 2023-24 aur 2024-25 merchandise-export data mein engineering goods major export category ke roop mein highest value par rahe. [Q75]\n• DGFT Monthly Bulletin October 2024 ke April-September 2024-25 merchandise-export data mein petroleum products top major export\ncommodities mein the. [Q76]\n• 2025-26 ke compared export groups mein agriculture aur allied products ka export-earnings share gems & jewellery se zyada tha. [Q77]\n• 2023-24 se 2024-25 ke data mein gems & jewellery ka export US$32,703.30m se US$29,802.06m raha; readymade garments\nUS$14,517.87m se US$15,974.61m; leather US$4,281.17m se US$4,363.25m; organic/inorganic chemicals US$29,343.80m se\nUS$28,631.01m. [Q78]\n• Economic Survey 2025-26 mein export shares 2024-25 -> Apr-Sep 2025-26(P): Gems & jewellery 6.8% -> 6.5%; Readymade garments\n3.7% -> 3.5%; Electronic goods 8.3% -> 9.5%; Tea & coffee 0.6% -> 0.7%. [Q79]\n• Economic Survey 2025-26 mein Indian exports mein gems & jewellery share 2024-25 mein 6.8% aur April-September 2025-26(P) mein\n6.5% tha. [Q80]","• Economic Survey 2025-26 mein textile industry major employment generator hai. Indian apparel-textile industry lagbhag US$179 billion,\nGDP ka ~2% aur manufacturing GVA ka ~11% contribute karti hai; India cotton area aur jute production mein leading hai aur cotton/silk/man-\nmade fibres production mein global top ranks mein hai. [Q81]\n• India imported diamonds ko cut/polish karke re-export karta hai; skilled diamond-cutting/polishing experts ki availability ki wajah se\ndomestic diamond availability limited hone ke baad bhi India large diamond exports karta hai. [Q82]\n\n7. Commodity Exports, Destinations & Export Growth\n\n• India Central aur Middle-East Asian countries se fruits, dry fruits aur palm-related products import karta hai. [Q83]\n• Tiruppur ko 'Manchester of South India' kaha jata hai; ise Dollar City, Knit City aur Cotton City bhi kaha jata hai. Readymade-garment\nexports ko promote karne ke liye July 2003 mein Tiruppur, Tamil Nadu mein first textile park ka foundation stone rakha gaya; yahan se\ngarments multiple countries ko export hote hain. [Q84]\n• Older period mein India textile exports mein fourth rank par tha; 2024-25 annual-report context mein India textiles-apparel ka sixth-largest\nexporter tha. [Q85]\n• Ministry of Textiles Annual Report 2024-25 ke hisaab se USA Indian textiles ka largest importer hai. [Q86]\n• 2024-25 mein exports contribution ke top 5 states Gujarat, Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu, Karnataka aur Uttar Pradesh the. Top shares: Gujarat\n29.88%, Maharashtra 16.92%, Tamil Nadu 13.38%, Karnataka 7.83%. [Q87]\n• Economic Survey 2025-26 mein total exports growth 2024-25 mein 0.1% aur April-September 2025-26(P) mein 2.9% record hui. [Q88]\n• Japan Indian iron ore ka major importer hai; Bailadila mine ka iron ore Japan export kiya jata hai. [Q89]\n• 2003-04 mein India ka highest steel export China ko tha; April-October 2025(P) finished-steel export destinations mein Italy 16.4%, Belgium\n16.1%, Spain 9.4% aur UAE 6.7% the. [Q90]\n• India ke leather aur leather-goods exports ka sabse bada market USA hai. Council for Leather Exports data (Apr-Oct 2025) mein major\nmarkets: USA 21.77%, Germany 11.01%, UK 9.11%, Italy 6.24% aur France 4.77%. [Q91]\n• 1994-95 mein India export growth lagbhag 18% tha. FY 2025-26 April-January estimates mein total exports US$720.76 bn, growth 6.15%;\ntotal imports US$823.41 bn, growth 6.54% thi. [Q92]\n• Economic Survey 2007-08 mein given years ke among Indian exports ki highest growth 2004-05 mein 30.8% thi. Feb 2026 data mein Apr-\nJan 2025-26 goods + services exports growth Apr-Jan 2024-25 ke comparison mein 6.15% thi. [Q93]\n• USDA February 2026 estimate mein 2026 rice-export volume ke hisaab se India top par aur Thailand next tha; India 2025 mein bhi top\nexporter tha. [Q94]\n• Updated figures mein Kenya duniya ka largest tea-exporting country hai. Data from the Tea Board of India: the top four tea exporting\ncountries in the years 2023 and 2024 are- Kenya > China > Sri Lanka > India. [Q95]\n• 2021 mein Switzerland ne about US$86.7 billion gold export kiya aur world ka largest gold exporter tha. [Q96]\n• Japan ke Mikimoto Kokichi ne cultured pearls production technique develop ki; iske baad Japan mein cultured-pearl industry rapidly\ndevelop hui. [Q97]\n• Recent long-staple cotton data mein Egypt leading producer aur exporter raha; earlier period mein USA leading position par tha. [Q98]\n8. Services, Invisible Exports, Entrepot & Trade Duties\n\n• Economic Survey 2025-26 mein services exports India ke external sector ka major pillar hain. GDP share FY2023-FY2025 average 9.7%\nraha vs pre-pandemic 7.4%; FY2026 first half mein share 10.0% tak gaya. [Q99]\n• Year 2000 se pahle ke 5 years mein India's software exports ne average lagbhag 50% growth record ki. Government policy incentives aur\ncost advantage ne software-export sector ki competitiveness ko support kiya. [Q100]\n• RBI Survey on Export of Computer Software and IT Enabled Services 2024-25 ke estimate mein Indian companies ke software-services\nexports 7.3% grow karke US$204.7 billion rahe, foreign commercial presence sales ko exclude karke. [Q101]\n• Foreign nationals ka India aakar Commonwealth Games dekhna economic accounting mein tourism-service export maana jayega; tourism\ninvisible export item hai. [Q102]\n• Economy mein services exports ko invisible exports kaha jata hai; tourism, insurance aur trade-related services is category mein aate hain.\n[Q103]\n• Entrepot trade mein goods import karke re-export kiye jate hain. [Q104]\n• Tariff border cross karne wale goods par lagne wala tax/duty hai; practical usage mein ye mainly import/customs duty ke sense mein use\nhota hai aur higher tariff trade volume reduce kar sakta hai. [Q105]\n• Duty Drawback export purpose ke imported inputs/goods par paid customs ya excise duty ki full ya partial refund hai, jo exporters ko diya\njata hai. [Q106]\n• Kisi country ka export goods ki quality, price competitiveness aur specific product characteristics par depend karta hai. [Q107]\n9. EXIM / Foreign Trade Policies & Export Excellence\n\n• Central Government ki 1992 import-export policy five-year period ke liye thi. Foreign Trade Policy 2015-20 April 2015 se start hui aur\noriginal end date 31 March 2020 thi. [Q108]\n• Export-Import Policy 2002-07 ka target India ke exports double karna aur global merchandise trade mein share 1% tak badhana tha. [Q109]\n• Foreign Trade Policy 2004-09 ka period 2004-2009 tha; iska focus world trade mein India's share badhana aur employment-generating\neconomic growth ko promote karna tha. [Q110]\n• Foreign Trade Policy 2004-09 August 2004 mein announce hui; target average 20% export growth ke through 2009 tak world trade mein\nIndia ka share double karna tha. [Q111]\n• Foreign Trade Policy 2004-09 ka target 2009 tak world trade mein India's share 1.5% tak le jana tha. Foreign Trade Policy 2015-20 mein\ntarget world exports share ko 2% se 3.5% tak badhana tha. [Q112]","• Foreign Trade Policy 2009-14 ko Commerce Minister Anand Sharma ne August 2009 mein announce kiya. Major targets: 2020 tak global\ntrade mein India's share double karna; 2014 tak goods + services exports double karna; 2011 tak annual exports US$200 billion tak le jana\nwith 15% annual growth; aur remaining 3 years mein around 25% annual export growth. [Q113]\n• Registered exporters ko previous-years export performance ke basis par ascending categories—Export House, Trading House, Star\nTrading House aur Super Star Trading House—mein classify kiya jata tha. [Q114]\n• EXIM Policy reforms ne foreign trade ko more liberal banaya; GATT aur later WTO framework ne trade liberalisation mein important role\nplay kiya. [Q115]\n• Tripura State hai, Town of Export Excellence nahi; Panipat, Ludhiana aur Madurai relevant Towns of Export Excellence list mein included\nthe. [Q116]\n\n10. Globalization, Liberalization & 1991 Reforms\n\n• Closed economy foreign trade se isolated hoti hai; ismein other economies ke saath exports aur imports nahi hote. [Q117]\n• Globalization ka core idea countries ke beech goods, services, capital aur intellectual property ka relatively free/unrestricted exchange hai.\nIske liye international-trade barriers ko reduce/remove karna important hai. [Q118]\n• Globalization countries ke beech economic relations ke restrictions reduce karke trade, capital aur labour mobility ko facilitate karta hai;\ncore elements free trade, capital movement aur labour mobility hain. [Q119]\n• Economic globalization free flow of capital, goods, services aur labour ko support karta hai. Public-sector equity disinvestment broader\nliberalization/privatization reforms se related ho sakta hai. [Q120]\n• Globalization primarily foreign investment aur large-company integration se linked hai; small entrepreneurs ke liye benefits automatic nahi\nhote, isliye universal optimism assume nahi kiya ja sakta. [Q121]\n• 1991 ke baad New Economic Policy ka major component liberalisation tha. Isme regulations ko ease karke economic activities ko market\nforces ke basis par operate karne ki greater freedom di gayi; capital-goods import duties reduction technology upgradation ko support karta\nhai. [Q122]\n• India mein selected sectors ka liberalisation 1980s mein start ho chuka tha, lekin Prime Minister P. V. Narasimha Rao ke tenure mein\nintroduced reform policies ka scope kaafi zyada comprehensive tha. [Q123]\n• India mein multinational companies ko promote karna liberalization, privatization aur globalization policy framework se related hai. [Q124]\n• 1991 mein agriculture ka GDP share lagbhag 30% tha; 2017 tak ye around 17% raha aur recent years mein current-price national income\nka roughly one-fifth contribute karta hai. [Q125]\n• World Development Indicators mein agriculture ka India GDP share 1991 ke 30% se 2017 mein 17% tak ghata. Economic Survey 2025-26\nmein agriculture & allied activities current prices par national income ka roughly one-fifth contribute karte hain. [Q126]\n• Globalization trade, investment aur information technology ke through countries ka social, cultural aur economic integration hai; India mein\nservice sector ko iska major benefit mila. [Q127]\n• India mein 1991 economic reforms Balance of Payments crisis ke background mein start hue; foreign-loan/forex stress important trigger\ntha. [Q128]\n• Globalisation trade, investment aur information technology ke through countries ki social-cultural-economic integration process hai. Isse\ngoods/services trade aur FDI flow badh sakta hai; exports ki growth imports se necessarily higher ho, ye universal feature nahi hai. [Q129]\n\n11. Free Trade Zones, SEZ & EPZ\n\n• Free Trade Area mein member countries aapas ke tariffs/trade barriers remove karte hain, lekin non-member countries ke against apni\nseparate tariff aur commercial policies maintain karte hain. [Q130]\n• Free Trade Zone export industries promote karne wala special zone hai jahan manufacturing, export aur processing facilities available hoti\nhain. [Q131]\n• Free Trade Zone mein export-oriented industries ko duty/excise concessions milte hain aur manufacturing/processing mainly exports ke liye\nhoti hai. [Q132]\n• SEZ Policy April 2000 mein clearance/control problems, weak world-class infrastructure aur unstable conditions ki constraints address\nkarne aur FDI attract karne ke purpose se announce hui. [Q133]\n• Government of India ne SEZ Policy April 2000 mein announce ki. SEZ duty-free economic zone hota hai jahan trade operations, duties aur\ntariffs mein significant exemptions mil sakti hain. [Q134]\n• SEZ Act Parliament ne May 2005 mein pass kiya; Presidential assent 23 June 2005 ko mila aur Act 10 February 2006 se effective hua.\n[Q135]\n• SEZ Act May 2005 mein pass, 23 June 2005 ko Presidential assent aur 10 February 2006 se effective hua. [Q136]\n• SEZ Act 2006 se effective hua. Iske main objectives additional economic activity generate karna, goods aur services exports promote\nkarna, domestic aur foreign investment attract karna, employment create karna aur infrastructure develop karna hain. [Q137]\n• SEZ Act, 2005 ke under SEZ notification aur implementation mein additional economic activity, exports, domestic/foreign investment,\nemployment aur infrastructure development jaise objectives guide karte hain. [Q138]\n• SEZ ka objective domestic aur foreign investment attract karna, additional economic activity/employment generate karna aur new\ntechnology ko promote karna hai; foreign investment discourage karna SEZ objective nahi hai. [Q139]\n• Asia ka first Export Processing Zone (EPZ) India ne 1965 mein Kandla mein establish kiya. Exim Policy effective 1 April 2000 ke under\ninternational-standard competitive export environment ke liye 8 EPZs - Kandla, Surat, Santacruz, Chennai, Noida, Falta, Visakhapatnam aur\nKochi - ko SEZs mein convert kiya gaya. [Q140]\n• Falta, West Bengal ek Export Processing Zone/SEZ hai. [Q141]\n• India ne 1965 mein Kandla mein Asia ka first Export Processing Zone (EPZ) establish kiya. Exim Policy effective 1 April 2000 ke under\nKandla, Surat, Santacruz, Chennai, Noida, Falta, Visakhapatnam aur Kochi ke 8 EPZs ko Special Economic Zones (SEZs) mein convert kiya\ngaya. [Q142]\n• 1965 mein Kandla mein establish EPZ Asia ka first Export Processing Zone tha. 1 April 2000 se effective Exim Policy ke under country ke 8\nEPZs - Kandla, Surat, Santacruz, Chennai, Noida, Falta, Visakhapatnam aur Kochi - SEZs mein convert hue. [Q143]","• Thiruvananthapuram ko older Free Trade Zone comparison mein non-FTZ city maana gaya tha; current status latest SEZ/FTZ notifications\npar depend karta hai. [Q144]\n• Diamond mining Madhya Pradesh ke Panna district mein hoti hai. [Q145]\n• Export Processing Zones (EPZs) manufactured-goods exports ko promote karne ke liye use kiye gaye, jahan export units ko internationally\ncompetitive environment milta hai. 2000 mein Surat mein Gems and Jewellery ke liye EPZ establish hua, jo India's first private-sector EPZ\ntha. [Q146]\n• Nandigram, West Bengal SEZ-policy context mein Salim Group ko gems and jewellery related project permission se joda gaya tha. [Q147]\n• SEZ-unit exports mein 2006-07 se strong growth dekhi gayi: ₹34,615 crore (2006-07), ₹2,20,711 crore (2009-10), ₹4,94,077 crore (2013-\n14), ₹7,96,669 crore (2019-20); 2020-21 mein -4.66% contraction bhi record hua. [Q148]\n• Undivided Andhra Pradesh historical approved/operational SEZ count mein leading tha. 31 December 2025 status: Uttar Pradesh 23\nnotified/14 operational; Andhra Pradesh 30/25; Tamil Nadu 58/49; Maharashtra 41/36; Telangana 53/37. [Q149]\n• SEZ India mobile app January 2017 mein Ministry of Commerce and Industry ne launch kiya; Department of Commerce ke SEZ Division ne\nise Special Economic Zones related services/information ke liye develop kiya. [Q150]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Kandla EPZ/SEZ Timeline\n▸Kandla EPZ 1965 mein establish hua aur later EPZ se SEZ mein convert hua; city/year ko historical timeline ke saath padhein.\n[Q140]\n\n\n12. Export Promotion Institutions & Schemes\n\n• Covered four institutions India ke imports/exports aur foreign-trade promotion se linked hain. [Q151]\n• EXIM Bank ki establishment Union Budget 1981-82 mein announce hui aur bank 1982 mein constitute hua; objective exporters/importers\nko finance dekar foreign trade promote karna hai. [Q152]\n• ECGC export-credit risk cover karke foreign trade promote karta hai. STC aur MMTC ka objective bhi foreign trade facilitate/promote karna\nhai. [Q153]\n• Export Credit Guarantee Corporation (ECGC) 1957 mein Export Risk Insurance Corporation (ERIC) naam se establish hua; 1964 mein\nnaam ECGC hua. Ye export financing aur insurance/credit-risk cover se related hai. [Q154]\n• ECGC 1957 mein Export Risk Insurance Corporation (ERIC) naam se establish hua aur 1964 mein ECGC naam adopt kiya; iska role\nexport credit-risk cover aur related insurance support hai. [Q155]\n• Export Credit Guarantee Corporation 1957 mein ERIC naam se start hua; 1964 mein ECGC naam hua. Corporation export trade ke credit-\nrisk insurance se related hai. [Q156]\n• 1957 mein Export Risk Insurance Corporation (ERIC) ke roop mein established institution ka naam 1964 mein ECGC hua; ye exporters ko\ncredit-risk insurance support deta hai. [Q157]\n• EPCG ka full form Export Promotion Capital Goods hai. Scheme capital-goods imports simplify karke goods/services ki export capacity aur\ncompetitiveness badhane ka aim rakhti hai. [Q158]\n• Budget 2020-21 mein NIRVIK scheme small exporters ke benefit ke liye launch hui—export-insurance coverage badhana, premium reduce\nkarna aur claim process simplify karna iske aims the. [Q159]\n• India Brand Equity Foundation (IBEF) Ministry of Commerce and Industry ne 1996 mein establish ki; main objective 'Made in India' brand ko\nglobal markets mein promote karna tha. [Q160]\n\n13. Remittances, Emerging Markets & Tourism\n\n• Remittance ranking: India–1st, Mexico–2nd, China–3rd, Philippines–4th; World Bank ke 2024 estimate mein bhi in four countries ka yehi\norder raha. [Q161]\n• Morgan Stanley ke 1998 emerging-market assessment mein Brazil rank 1, Mexico rank 2 aur India rank 3 tha. [Q162]\n• India ke tourism potential ke comparison mein infrastructure historically inadequate raha; basic tourism infrastructure ki kami sector ke\nexpansion ki major constraint rahi hai. [Q163]\n• India Tourism Data Compendium 2025 ke 2024 global-tourism data mein France most-visited destination tha. [Q164]\n• Relevant historical aur updated data dono mein France top tourist-destination country raha. [Q165]\n• Older-period context mein Singapore relevant tha; India Tourism Data Compendium 2025 ke 2024(P) data mein UAE ko India se\ndepartures ka highest share 25.21% mila. [Q166]\n• Foreign Tourist Arrivals series mein 2009-10 historical decline tha; India Tourism Data Compendium 2025 ke 2011-2024 series mein major\ndeclines 2020 (-74.93%) aur 2021 (-44.53%) mein aaye. 2024 foreign-tourist-arrival leading States/UT order Maharashtra, West Bengal,\nUttar Pradesh, Gujarat, Rajasthan tha. [Q167]\n• India Tourism Data Compendium 2025 ke 2024 data mein foreign tourist arrivals ke top States/UT order mein Maharashtra, West Bengal,\nUttar Pradesh, Gujarat aur Rajasthan the. [Q168]\n• India Tourism Data Compendium 2025 ke 2024 data mein domestic tourist visits ke top States Uttar Pradesh, Tamil Nadu, Karnataka,\nAndhra Pradesh aur Rajasthan the. [Q169]\n• Travel + Leisure survey 2009 mein Udaipur world's best city select hua tha. World's Best Awards 2025 mein San Miguel de Allende\n(Mexico) rank 1; Indian cities Jaipur 5th, Mumbai 16th aur Agra 20th top-25 mein the. [Q170]\n\n14. Bilateral Trade Agreements, Trade Barriers & IITF\n\n• Nathu La Pass Sikkim ko Tibet se connect karta hai aur historical Silk Route ka part tha. 1962 India-China war ke baad route close hua;\ncross-border trade ke liye 2006 mein reopen kiya gaya. [Q171]\n• Australia Oceania region mein India ka important trading partner hai. India-Australia Economic Cooperation and Trade Agreement (ECTA)\n29 December 2022 se effective hai. [Q172]","• India-Australia Economic Cooperation and Trade Agreement (ECTA) 2 April 2022 ko sign hua. [Q173]\n• India-Japan Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement/FTA Tokyo mein 16 February 2011 ko sign hua. [Q174]\n• India ne Malaysia ke saath Comprehensive Economic Cooperation Agreement (CECA) 18 February 2011 ko Putrajaya, Malaysia mein sign\nkiya. [Q175]\n• 31 May 2022 ko Israel aur United Arab Emirates ne Free Trade Agreement sign kiya; Israel ka kisi Arab country ke saath ye first FTA tha.\n[Q176]\n• January 2002 mein China aur Bangladesh ke beech bilateral trade agreement sign hua, jisme economic cooperation aur trade main\nsubjects the; 2001-2003 ke liye cultural agreement bhi linked tha. [Q177]\n• World Tariff Profiles 2025 ke 2024 anti-dumping measures count mein top countries: USA 538, India 145, Türkiye 131, Brazil 130. [Q178]\n• Super-301 US Trade and Competitiveness Act, 1988 ka section hai, jiske under USA unfair-trade practices ke against economic action le\nsakta hai. [Q179]\n• India International Trade Fair (IITF) 2021 November 2021 mein Pragati Maidan, New Delhi mein organise hua, Jaipur mein nahi. 40th\nedition ka theme 'Aatmanirbhar Bharat' tha. [Q180]\n• Import Quota physical control ka measure hai: government ek fixed period, usually 1 year, ke liye import quantity limit set karti hai;\nprescribed quota se zyada import allowed nahi hota. [Q181]\n• Important associations hain: Open General License - Foreign Trade; TRYSEM - Employment; Wholesale Price Index - Inflation; Cash\nReserve Ratio - Credit Control. [Q182]\n\n                                   Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · International Trade Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0461","t":"14 Foreign Exchange, FDI, External Debt","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["FOREIGN EXCHANGE, FDI, EXTERNAL DEBT — FULL MIND MAP\n                                  Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\nTopics in this chapter:\n1. Exchange Rate Basics & Rupee Convertibility\n2. Devaluation — Meaning, History & Effects\n3. International Liquidity, Currency Crisis & BoP Defence\n4. FDI — Sector-wise & State-wise Inflows\n5. Portfolio Flows, Hot Money & Tobin Tax\n6. FDI/FII Basics, Sources, P-Notes & Sectoral Policy\n7. External Debt, Debt Trap & External Commercial Borrowing\n8. Green Bonds, NRI Bonds, Masala Bonds & Oil Pool Account\n9. Foreign Exchange Reserves & Import Cover\n10. NEER/REER, Exchange Value, Appreciation & Depreciation\n11. Euro, Eurodollar & Important World Currencies\n12. FEMA, FERA & Foreign-Exchange Regulation\n13. Hawala, PMLA & Money Laundering\n14. Monetary Trade Cycle & External-Sector Linkages\n\n\n 1. Exchange Rate Basics & Rupee Convertibility\n\n • Flexible exchange rate market forces, yani demand aur supply, se determine hota hai; monetary authorities exchange rate ko influence\n karne ke objective se intervene nahi karti. [Q1]\n • Rupee convertibility ka matlab hai ki rupee ko foreign currencies mein aur foreign currencies ko rupee mein freely convert kiya ja sake. Full\n convertibility current aur capital dono accounts par restrictions hata deti hai, jisse foreign-capital inflow/outflow aasaan hota hai. [Q2]\n • Rupee convertibility ka matlab hai rupee ko major foreign currencies mein aur foreign currencies ko rupee mein freely convert kar pana. [Q3]\n • Rupee current-account ke sabhi transactions ke liye foreign currency mein convertible hai. [Q4]\n • Indian rupee ko current account par fully convertible 1994 mein banaya gaya. [Q5]\n • Indian rupee current account par 1994 se fully convertible hua. [Q6]\n • Indian rupee August 1994 se current account par convertible hua. [Q7]\n • March 1994 se rupee current account par convertible hua. [Q8]\n • Union Budget 1992-93 mein us waqt ke Finance Minister Dr. Manmohan Singh ne Liberalized Exchange Rate Management System\n (LERMS) announce kiya tha. [Q9]\n • Rupee convertibility ka matlab rupee ko foreign currencies mein aur foreign currencies ko rupee mein bina intervention freely convert kar\n pana hai. Current aur capital account par full convertibility ka matlab foreign capital ke India mein inflow aur outflow par restriction na hona\n hai. [Q10]\n • Indian rupee current account par fully convertible hai; capital account par full convertibility nahi hai aur gold mein full convertibility bhi\n applicable nahi hai. [Q11]\n • Capital-account convertibility ka matlab financial assets ke liye Indian rupee ko market-determined exchange rate par major foreign\n currencies mein bina restriction exchange kar pana hai; ismein funds/financial assets ka country mein inflow aur outflow include hota hai.\n [Q12]\n • Capital account mein foreign loans, FDI aur portfolio investment jaise flows include hote hain; private remittances capital account ka part\n nahi hoti. [Q13]\n • 20 March 2006 ko RBI ne former Deputy Governor S. S. Tarapore ki chairmanship mein six-member committee banayi thi, jiska kaam rupee\n ki full capital-account convertibility ke liye roadmap tayyar karna tha. [Q14]\n • Indian rupee ki full capital-account convertibility examine karne ke liye Tarapore Committee constitute ki gayi thi. [Q15]\n EXAM TRAP — Current vs Capital Account Convertibility\n ▸ Current-account convertibility aur capital-account convertibility alag concepts hain. Rupee current account par 1994 se fully convertible hai, jabki\n full capital-account convertibility ko Tarapore Committee ne examine kiya.\n ▸ Liberalized Exchange Rate Management System (LERMS) ka announcement Union Budget 1992-93 mein hua; ye 1991 BoP crisis ke baad\n exchange-rate reform ka transitional step tha.\n [Q2–Q15]\n\n\n\n 2. Devaluation — Meaning, History & Effects\n\n • Devaluation mein government domestic currency ki foreign currencies ke against official value kam karti hai. Indian rupee ka devaluation\n 1949, 1966 aur 1991 mein hua; 1991 mein two-step devaluation kiya gaya tha. [Q16]\n • Kisi currency ki value ka other countries ki currencies ke comparison mein kam hona currency-value decline ko show karta hai. [Q17]\n • India mein rupee ka first devaluation 1949 mein hua. [Q18]\n • 1991 mein first devaluation 1 July ko aur second devaluation 3 July ko hua tha. [Q19]\n • Indian rupee ka two-stage devaluation 1991-92 financial-year context mein hua. [Q20]\n • Devaluation ka major effect ye hai ki country's exports relatively cheaper ho jaate hain aur foreign markets mein domestic exports ki\n competitiveness improve hoti hai. [Q21]","• Devaluation se domestic currency ki value foreign currencies ke comparison mein girti hai; exports cheaper aur imports costlier ho jaate\nhain. Isliye generally exports badhte aur imports ghatte hain. [Q22]\n• Devaluation external value of currency ko reduce karke balance-of-payments adjustment mein help kar sakta hai. [Q23]\n• Currency devaluation generally exports ko competitive banati aur imports ko costlier banati hai, isliye exports badhne aur imports ghatne ka\npressure banta hai. [Q24]\n• Devaluation/depreciation ke baad imports costlier aur exports relatively cheaper ho jaate hain. [Q25]\n• Devaluation mein country ki currency ki external value reduce ki jaati hai; Balance of Payments mein fundamental disequilibrium hone par\ntrade balance aur external position improve karne ke liye ye policy use ki ja sakti hai. [Q26]\n• Devaluation importing country mein deflation cause nahi karta; imports costly hone se general price level badh sakta hai. [Q27]\n• 1991 reforms ke baad export growth mein substantial rise hua aur devaluation ne export competitiveness improve karne mein role play kiya.\nDevaluation episodes ko unke historical period ke saath hi read karna chahiye. [Q28]\n\nEXAM TRAP — Devaluation vs Market-led Depreciation\n▸ Devaluation currency ki official value reduction hoti hai; India ke major episodes 1949, 1966 aur 1991 se linked hain. 1991 mein devaluation two-\nstep process mein hua.\n▸ Market forces se exchange rate Rs.60/$ se Rs.65/$ ho jaaye to rupee depreciation hota hai; appreciation iska opposite hai.\n[Q16–Q28, Q114–Q116]\n\n\n\n3. International Liquidity, Currency Crisis & BoP Defence\n\n• International liquidity mein central bank ke internationally acceptable reserve assets—hard currencies, gold, SDR holdings aur other reserve\nassets—include hote hain. Inka use BoP deficit manage karne mein hota hai. [Q29]\n• Late 1990s ke Southeast Asian crisis mein Indonesia, Thailand, Malaysia aur South Korea ki local currencies ka Western currencies ke\ncomparison mein long-term overvaluation major cause tha; monetary mismanagement aur currency speculation ne crisis ko aur badhaya.\n[Q30]\n• 1997-98 Southeast Asian crisis mein in countries ki currencies ki value gir gayi; Indian exports inke products se compete karte the, isliye\ncurrency fall ka India ke exports par adverse impact pada. [Q31]\n• Future global financial crisis ke against short-term foreign debt par kam dependence India ko protection de sakti hai; foreign-bank exposure\nbadhne se global-financial risk bhi badh sakta hai. [Q32]\n• Currency crisis tab hoti hai jab fixed exchange rate ko forex reserves ke support se maintain karne ki central bank ki capacity par serious\ndoubt ho. Ye situation usually severe BoP deficit aur inadequate reserves ke time dikhti hai. [Q33]\n• Rupee ko strengthen karne ke measures mein non-essential imports control karna, exports promote karna, Masala Bonds issue karna aur\nECB conditions ease karna shamil ho sakta hai. Expansionary monetary policy money supply badha kar interest rates ghata sakti hai, jisse\ncurrency weak aur inflation/forex outflow badh sakta hai. [Q34]\n• BoP position improve karne ke liye import substitution, rupee devaluation aur imports par higher tax useful ho sakte hain; exports par higher\ntax exports ko discourage karke BoP ko worsen kar sakta hai. [Q35]\n• Current Account Deficit (CAD) tab hota hai jab goods, services aur unilateral transfers ko mila kar current-account payments receipts se\nzyada ho jayein. Domestic currency ka devaluation exports ko support aur imports ko discourage karke CAD kam karne mein help kar sakta\nhai. [Q36]\n\n4. FDI — Sector-wise & State-wise Inflows\n\n• Cumulative FDI inflows mein Services sector leading sector raha; telecommunications older-period comparisons mein top sector tha. [Q37]\n• Earlier period mein Services sector India ka highest FDI-equity-inflow sector tha. [Q38]\n• Earlier FDI data mein Services sector top inflow sector tha. [Q39]\n• 2007-08 mein services sector ko India mein largest direct foreign-capital inflow mila. [Q40]\n• Relevant decade mein Services sector ne compared sectors mein highest FDI inflow attract kiya. [Q41]\n• 1997-2000 aur updated cumulative period mein India ke FDI inflows mein services sector ka share sabse bada raha; sector-wise inflows ko\nUS$ million terms mein compare kiya jata hai. [Q42]\n• April 2000-December 2025 ke cumulative FDI data mein leading sectors ka order tha: Services > Computer Software & Hardware > Trading\n> Telecommunications > Automobile Industry. [Q43]\n• April 2000-December 2025 tak FDI inflow approx Hotels & Tourism US$18,930.76 million, Hospitals & Diagnostic Centres US$12,726.93\nmillion, Textiles US$5,017.15 million aur Leather-related sector US$396.42 million raha. [Q44]\n• April-September 2022 mein listed states mein Karnataka ko highest FDI equity inflow mila. 2024-25 mein leading states/UTs Maharashtra,\nKarnataka, Delhi, Gujarat, Tamil Nadu the; April-December 2025-26 mein order Maharashtra, Karnataka, Gujarat, Tamil Nadu, Haryana raha.\n[Q45]\n• Updated figures mein the services sector attracted the highest FDI equity inflow in Bihar. [Q46]\n5. Portfolio Flows, Hot Money & Tobin Tax\n\n• 2005-06 mein India ke foreign-exchange inflow ka major source portfolio investment tha. RBI ke 27 June 2025 release mein forex reserves\nke change ke updated sources ko US$ billion mein report kiya gaya. [Q47]\n• Foreign portfolio investment aur NRI deposits interest-rate differentials se attract hote hain; investors ka main objective in differences se\nreturn/profit earn karna hota hai. [Q48]\n• Rapid movement wali foreign currency funds ko hot money kaha jaata hai. Investors short-term interest-rate differential aur exchange-rate\nchanges se gain ke liye in funds ko countries ke beech quickly shift karte hain. [Q49]","• Tight monetary policy mein central bank interest rates badhakar money supply ko control karta hai. Rapid inflation ke time aisi policy\ninflationary pressure ko kam karne ke liye use hoti hai. [Q50]\n• James Tobin ne 1972 mein currency-market transactions par ek chhota tax propose kiya tha, jise Tobin Tax kaha jata hai. Iska objective\nsharp foreign-exchange fluctuations ko discourage karna tha. [Q51]\n\n6. FDI/FII Basics, Sources, P-Notes & Sectoral Policy\n\n• FDI mainly non-debt-creating capital flow hai. Listed Indian company mein fully diluted paid-up equity capital ka 10% ya usse zyada\ninvestment, ya unlisted Indian company ke capital instruments mein non-resident investment, FDI category mein aata hai. [Q52]\n• Foreign company ka another country mein subsidiary establish karna, foreign majority equity holding aur completely foreign-financed\ncompany direct investment ke forms hain. [Q53]\n• Non-resident foreign deposits FDI ka part nahi hote; baaki listed investment items FDI ke under include hote hain. [Q54]\n• FCNR(A), jo 1975 se exist karta tha, discontinue karke 15 May 1993 se FCNR(B) se replace kiya gaya. [Q55]\n• East Asian aur Latin American crises mein excessive foreign portfolio investment ek major factor tha, kyunki portfolio flows highly volatile\nhote hain; FDI comparatively stable hota hai aur long-term development mein zyada useful hota hai. [Q56]\n• FII broad capital availability badhata hai, jabki FDI specific sectors mein aata hai. FDI relatively permanent hota hai aur management\nskills/technology transfer bhi la sakta hai; FII ka focus mainly capital flow aur secondary market par hota hai. [Q57]\n• Foreign pharmaceutical company ke bonds ya stocks purchase karna FDI nahi, portfolio-type investment hai; direct ownership/control wale\ncases FDI ke forms hote hain. [Q58]\n• Participatory Notes (P-notes) domestic market mein indirectly invest karne ka instrument hain. SEBI-registered foreign institutional/portfolio\ninvestors inhe un foreign clients ko issue karte hain jo Indian market mein directly register nahi karte. [Q59]\n• P-notes SEBI-registered Foreign Portfolio Investors foreign investors ko issue karte hain, jisse wo direct registration ke bina Indian stock\nmarket mein participate kar sakte hain. [Q60]\n• Union Budget 2020-21 mein outstanding corporate bonds mein portfolio-investment limit 9% se badha kar 15% ki gayi; FY 2024-25 tak ye\nlimit 15% hi rahi. [Q61]\n• Recent FDI data mein Singapore leading source country hai; earlier periods mein Mauritius top source raha. [Q62]\n• April 2000-December 2025 cumulative FDI data mein Singapore leading source country tha; Mauritius historically major source raha hai.\n[Q63]\n• 1991-95 mein India mein highest FDI lane wale major countries USA aur Japan the; December 2025 tak ke 2023-24 aur 2024-25\ncomparison mein bhi USA-Japan relevant pair raha. [Q64]\n• Earlier period mein Mauritius major FDI source tha; 2023-24 aur 2024-25 mein Singapore India ka largest FDI source raha. [Q65]\n• India-Mauritius Double Taxation Avoidance Agreement (DTAA) Mauritius-route FDI ka major reason raha. Investors low-tax Mauritius route\nuse karke India mein investment par tax benefit lete the. [Q66]\n• April-March 2023-24 aur 2024-25 mein Singapore India ka largest FDI source raha. India-Mauritius tax treaty ke 2016 amendment ke baad\npreferential benefits 2017-18 se 2019-20 ke beech phase out hue. [Q67]\n• Economic Survey 2023-24 ke context mein global FDI slowdown ka impact India par bhi pada; net FDI inflow FY2023 ke US$42.0 billion se\nghatkar FY2024 mein US$26.5 billion hua. [Q68]\n• India-USA Foreign Account Tax Compliance Act (FATCA) agreement 9 July 2015 ko sign hua aur 30 September 2015 se effective hua.\n[Q69]\n• Government of India ki tea-sector policy mein foreign firm ko five years ke andar 26% equity Indian partner ke favour mein divest karni thi.\n[Q70]\n• Calendar year 2005 mein FIIs ka net capital investment lagbhag US$10.7 billion tha. Updated NSDL data FPI net investment ko US$ million\nmein show karta hai. [Q71]\n• January 2003 mein Foreign Investment Promotion Board (FIPB) ko President ke order se Department of Economic Affairs, Ministry of\nFinance mein transfer kiya gaya; baad mein FIPB abolish kar diya gaya. [Q72]\n• Single-brand retail mein FDI 2006 se permitted hai, jabki multi-brand retail mein 2012 se permission mili. Online retail of goods/services\nmein 100% FDI automatic route se permitted ki gayi. [Q73]\n• Consumer Protection (E-Commerce) Rules, 2020 mein e-commerce entity wahi person/entity hai jo electronic-commerce platform own,\noperate ya manage kare; apne hi goods/services sell karne wala seller is definition mein include nahi hota. [Q74]\n• Insurance sector mein FDI limit 26% se 49% badhane ka proposal October 2012 mein aaya; 2015 Act ke baad automatic route ke under\nlimit 49% ho gayi. [Q75]\n• Budget 2021-22 mein insurance-sector FDI limit 49% se 74% ki gayi. Union Budget 2025-26 mein ise 74% se 100% tak raise karne ka\nannouncement hua. [Q76]\n• August 1991 ke Small-Scale Industries Policy Statement mein small-scale industries ke liye foreign capital investment limit 24% rakhi gayi\nthi; later MSME sector mein automatic route ke under limit 100% tak badhayi gayi. [Q77]\n\nEXAM TRAP — FDI Data is Period-specific\n▸ FDI rankings period-specific hoti hain; historical aur current figures ko alag rakho, updated ranking ko older period par apply mat karo.\n▸ Mauritius/Singapore source-country data, sector-wise inflows aur state-wise inflows ko exact year/period ke saath read karna zaroori hai.\n[Q37–Q46, Q62–Q68]\n\n\n\n7. External Debt, Debt Trap & External Commercial Borrowing\n\n• Debt trap tab hota hai jab outstanding debt ka interest chukane ke liye country ko naya loan lena pade. [Q78]\n• End-2010 tak India ka external debt US$300 billion cross kar chuka tha. December 2025 release ke hisab se end-September 2025 par\nexternal debt US$746 billion tha, jo end-June 2025 ke US$746.7 billion se US$0.7 billion kam tha. [Q79]","• India ke external debt ka major part non-government entities ka hai. End-September 2025(P) par total external debt US$746 billion tha—\ngovernment debt US$168.8 billion aur non-government debt approx US$577.2 billion. [Q80]\n• 2017 ke context mein commercial credit external debt ka major component tha; end-September 2025 mein loans sabse bada component\nthe—US$258.3 billion, yani total external debt ka 34.6%. [Q81]\n• Commercial borrowings aur NRI deposits badhne se external debt increase ho sakta hai; end-September 2025(P) ke data mein loans\nexternal debt ka largest component the. [Q82]\n• End-September 2023 par India ka external debt US$635.3 billion, GDP ka 18.61% tha; end-December 2023 par US$648.2 billion, GDP ka\n18.7% tha. [Q83]\n• India mein External Commercial Borrowing (ECB) ko RBI regulate karta hai. [Q84]\n• Older listed order Russia, China, Turkey, Brazil, India tha. September 2025 'India's External Debt: A Status Report 2024-25' ke end-\nDecember 2024 data mein highest gross external-debt stock wale five countries USA, UK, France, Germany aur Japan the. [Q85]\n• Gross external-debt stock ke basis par USA sabse heavily indebted country raha. End-December 2024 ranking mein USA ke baad UK,\nFrance, Germany aur Japan the. [Q86]\n• External debt ki size aur composition ke basis par India ko less-indebted country category mein treat kiya gaya; September 2025 ki 'India's\nExternal Debt: A Status Report 2024-25' mein India world ke top 20 debtor countries ki list se bahar tha. [Q87]\n• India's External Debt: A Statistics Report 2024-25 ke end-December 2024 data mein emerging/developing economies ke top five external-\ndebt holders China, Brazil, India, Mexico aur Türkiye the. Inmein listed comparison mein Brazil ka debt sabse zyada tha. [Q88]\n• US government ka debt default payment obligations ko time par meet na kar pana hota hai; isse US Treasury bondholders ke legal claims\nautomatically cancel nahi hote. [Q89]\n• 2001 mein India ne Tajikistan ko drought situation se deal karne ke liye five lakh million dollars dene ka proposal diya tha. [Q90]\n• 2001 mein German Chancellor Gerhard Schroder ne Uttarakhand ke Tehri Dam project ke liye US$32 million loan sanction kiya tha. [Q91]\nEXAM TRAP — External Debt: Stock, Component & Ratio\n▸ External debt ka stock, components aur debt-to-GDP ratio date/year-specific hote hain; historical aur later figures ko mix mat karo.\n▸ Debt trap ka core concept: old outstanding debt ka interest pay karne ke liye naya borrowing karna pade.\n[Q78–Q89]\n\n\n\n8. Green Bonds, NRI Bonds, Masala Bonds & Oil Pool Account\n\n• Green Bonds environment-friendly projects—renewable energy, clean transport, sustainable water management—ko finance karne ke liye\nearmarked bonds hote hain. First major green bonds European Investment Bank ne 2007 aur World Bank ne 2008 mein issue kiye. [Q92]\n• State Bank of India ne NRI aur foreign corporate bodies se forex raise karne ke liye 21 October 2000 ko India Millennium Deposits launch\nkiya; scheme 15 din open rahi aur 5-year deposits dollars, pounds sterling aur euros mein accept hue. [Q93]\n• SBI ne 5 August 1998 ko five-year maturity wale Resurgent India Bonds US dollar, pound sterling aur Deutsche Mark mein issue kiye. [Q94]\n• Masala Bonds se Indian institutions foreign markets se foreign currency ki jagah rupee-denominated funds raise kar sakte hain. July 2016\nmein HDFC London Stock Exchange par Masala Bond issue karne wali first Indian company bani. [Q95]\n• Masala Bond India ke bahar rupees mein issue hone wala corporate bond hai. IFC Masala Bond International Finance Corporation issue\nkarta hai, jo World Bank Group ka part hai. [Q96]\n• Oil-sector Administered Price Mechanism aur Oil Pool Account 1 April 2002 se abolished hue. [Q97]\n9. Foreign Exchange Reserves & Import Cover\n\n• Import cover batata hai ki country's international reserves kitne months ke imports finance kar sakte hain. Economic Survey 2025-26 mein\nforex reserves 16 January 2026 tak US$701.4 billion the, end-March 2025 ke US$668.3 billion se higher. [Q98]\n• End-December 2024 tak India ke foreign-exchange reserves lagbhag US$640.3 billion the, jo end-March 2024 se higher the; global ranking\nfourth thi, seventh nahi. [Q99]\n• Older rupee-value figure Rs.28,55,882 crore tha; RBI ke 27 February 2026 release mein 20 February 2026 ko India's forex reserves\nUS$723,608 million the. [Q100]\n• March 2019 mein India ke forex reserves US$412.9 billion the; 23 May 2025 ko reserves US$692.7 billion the. [Q101]\n• RBI weekly data ke hisab se 27 December 2024 ko end hue week mein India's foreign-exchange reserves US$640.3 billion the; ise end-\nDecember 2024 ka official reserve level maana jata hai. [Q102]\n• India ke forex reserves mein Foreign Currency Assets, Gold, Special Drawing Rights (SDR) aur IMF Reserve Position—chaar components\ninclude hote hain. [Q103]\n• 28 March 2025 ko India ke forex reserves mein Foreign Currency Assets ka share sabse bada tha, uske baad Gold; SDR third aur IMF\nReserve Position ka share sabse chhota tha. [Q104]\n• Forex reserves ke four major components hain: Foreign Currency Assets, Gold, SDRs aur IMF Reserve Position. 20 February 2026 ke\nweekly data mein FCA largest component tha. [Q105]\n• India ke forex reserves mein Foreign Currency Assets, RBI-held Gold, SDRs aur IMF Reserve Position include hote hain; external-sector\nstrength assess karne mein reserves important indicator hain. [Q106]\n• RBI ke foreign-exchange reserves mein Foreign Currency Assets (FCA), Gold, Special Drawing Rights (SDR) aur IMF Reserve Position\ninclude hote hain. [Q107]\n• Tata Statistical Outline of India 2004-05 ke historical data mein 10 December 2004 ko India's forex reserves US$129.7 billion the. [Q108]","EXAM TRAP — Forex Reserves: Components vs Dated Levels\n▸ Foreign-exchange reserves ke four components hain: Foreign Currency Assets, Gold, SDRs aur IMF Reserve Position. FCA generally largest\ncomponent hota hai.\n▸ Forex-reserve levels date-specific hote hain; later reserve figure ko historical dated value ki jagah use mat karo.\n[Q98–Q108]\n\n\n\n10. NEER/REER, Exchange Value, Appreciation & Depreciation\n\n• NEER aur REER external competitiveness ke indicators hain. NEER domestic currency ke bilateral nominal exchange rates ka weighted\naverage hai; REER relative price differences se adjusted weighted exchange-rate index hai aur PPP concept se linked hai. [Q109]\n• International market mein kisi country ki currency value determine karne mein World Bank ka direct role nahi hota; government stability aur\ncountry ke goods/services ki demand jaise factors currency value ko affect karte hain. [Q110]\n• Raghuram Rajan ne high inflation, high fiscal deficit aur high crude-oil prices ko rupee ki exchange value affect karne wale key factors\nbataya. [Q111]\n• Important dollar currencies: US Dollar, Canadian Dollar, New Zealand Dollar aur Hong Kong Dollar; inke rupee exchange rates date-specific\nhote hain. [Q112]\n• 2002-06 exchange-rate series mein rupee pound sterling, euro aur yen ke against dono directions mein move hua, jabki US dollar ke\nagainst continuous strengthening dikhayi gayi. [Q113]\n• Market forces se domestic currency ki value girti hai to depreciation hota hai; jaise Rs.60 per dollar se Rs.65 per dollar. Government ke\nofficial intervention se value kam ki jaye to devaluation kehte hain. Devaluation government-driven hota hai, depreciation market-driven.\n[Q114]\n• Ek currency ki value doosri currency ke comparison mein badhe to appreciation kehte hain. Jaise 1 US dollar Rs.80 se Rs.75 ho jaye, to\nrupee appreciate hua kyunki ek dollar ke liye kam rupees chahiye. [Q115]\n• Exchange rate 1$ = Rs.60 se 1$ = Rs.58 ho jaye to rupee dollar ke against appreciate karta hai aur dollar rupee ke against depreciate karta\nhai. [Q116]\n• 1 March 2026 ko selected currency values mein 1 Euro approx Rs.107.63 aur 1 Pound Sterling approx Rs.124.12 tha; currency values date-\nspecific hoti hain. [Q117]\n• Jo currency circulation mein nahi hoti aur accounting purpose ke liye use hoti hai use artificial currency kaha ja sakta hai. SDR (Special\nDrawing Rights) IMF transactions mein use hone wali aisi accounting unit hai. [Q118]\n\n11. Euro, Eurodollar & Important World Currencies\n\n• Maastricht Treaty ke basis par European Union ki single currency Euro create hui; Euro formally January 1999 mein create hua aur notes-\ncoins ke form mein January 2002 se circulation mein aaya. [Q119]\n• European Monetary Union ki currency Euro hai. [Q120]\n• Euro electronic form mein 1999 se issue hua aur 1 January 2002 se circulation mein aaya; current context mein EU ke 27 countries mein se\n21 ne Euro adopt kiya hai. [Q121]\n• Euro 1999 mein common currency ke roop mein introduced hua; euro notes aur coins 2002 mein circulation mein aaye. [Q122]\n• Older euro-area composition mein 12 EU States included the. [Q123]\n• Euro ab 21 European countries mein circulation mein hai. Britain, Sweden aur Denmark ne euro adopt nahi kiya; Croatia January 2023 mein\n20th aur Bulgaria January 2026 mein 21st euro-area member bana. [Q124]\n• Eurodollar ka matlab United States ke bahar—especially Europe—circulate/deposit hone wala US dollar hai. [Q125]\n• Seychelles ki currency Seychelles Rupee hai; Bhutan ki Ngultrum, Malaysia ki Ringgit aur Maldives ki Rufiyaa hai. [Q126]\n• Bangladesh ki currency Taka hai; Nepal ki Rupee, Iraq ki Dinar aur Italy ki historical currency Lira thi, jise Euro ne replace kar diya. [Q127]\n• Thailand ki currency Baht hai; Türkiye ki Turkish Lira, Vietnam ki Dong aur Iran ki Rial hai. [Q128]\n• China ki official currency Renminbi hai aur uski unit Yuan hai; Italy ki historical currency Lira thi jise Euro ne replace kar diya, Japan ki\ncurrency Yen aur India ki Rupee hai. [Q129]\n• Yuan China ki currency hai. [Q130]\n• Sudan mein Dinar historical currency rahi aur Sudanese Pound bhi use hota hai; former Yugoslavia mein New Yugoslav Dinar, Tunisia mein\nDinar aur UAE mein Dirham currency hai. [Q131]\n• Malaysia ki currency Ringgit, Thailand ki Baht, Indonesia ki Rupiah aur South Korea ki Won hai. [Q132]\n• Important currency pairs: Mexico—Peso; Austria—historically Schilling, ab Euro; Japan—Yen; Saudi Arabia—Riyal. [Q133]\n12. FEMA, FERA & Foreign-Exchange Regulation\n\n• FEMA ke under forex-rule violation criminal offence nahi raha; FERA-1973 ko replace karke FEMA 1 June 2000 se effective hua. Violations\ngenerally monetary penalties ke through deal kiye jaate hain, imprisonment-based criminal treatment nahi. [Q134]\n• FEMA 1 June 2000 se effective hua. FERA ka scope sirf foreign debt tak limited nahi tha; ye forex payments, foreign exchange/securities\ntransactions aur forex resources ke conservation/regulation ko cover karta tha. [Q135]\n• Foreign Exchange Management Act (FEMA) ne 1 June 2000 se FERA ko replace kiya; iska objective external trade/payments facilitate\nkarna aur foreign-exchange market ka orderly development promote karna hai. [Q136]\n• FERA ko FEMA ne replace kiya. [Q137]\n• FEMA Act 1999 mein pass hua, 2000 se operate karna start hua aur older disputes transition period mein FERA ke under continue hue.\nExternal trade/payments facilitate karna aur forex market ka orderly development/stability FEMA ka core objective hai. [Q138]","• Foreign Exchange Management Act (FEMA) 1999 mein enacted/existence mein aaya; iska operational commencement 2000 mein hua.\n[Q139]\n• Goods import ke liye importers RBI-authorized exchange banks/dealers ke through foreign exchange approval obtain kar sakte hain. [Q140]\n• FERA se FEMA transition ke baad current-account forex restrictions reduce hue. Foreign Currency Assets end-March 1999 ke US$38,036\nmillion se badhkar end-March 2001 mein US$42,281 million hue. [Q141]\n• Important Act-year pairs: Minimum Wages Act—1948; Industries (Development and Regulation) Act—1951; MRTP Act—1969; FERA—\n1973. [Q142]\n\nEXAM TRAP — FEMA: Passed vs Effective Date\n▸ FEMA 1999 mein pass hua aur FERA ko replace karke 1 June 2000 se effective hua; passing aur effective date separate events hain.\n▸ FEMA timeline mein 1999 enactment aur 1 June 2000 enforcement ko alag dates ke roop mein note karo.\n[Q134–Q142]\n\n\n\n13. Hawala, PMLA & Money Laundering\n\n• Hawala official banking/dealer channel ke bahar rupee ke against foreign currency ya foreign currency ke against rupee transfer ka\nunauthorized system hai. Official forex transactions RBI-authorized dealers ke through hone chahiye; hawala networks black-money transfer\nke liye misuse ho sakte hain. [Q143]\n• Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002 ko 1 July 2005 se enforce kiya gaya. [Q144]\n• Money-laundering framework mein financial institutions ke maintained records ko call karne ki power Director of Money Laundering ke paas\nnahi hoti. Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002 legal framework ka core hai aur Act/rules 1 July 2005 se effective hue. [Q145]\n\n14. Monetary Trade Cycle & External-Sector Linkages\n\n• Economist Hawtrey ne trade cycle ki Pure Monetary Theory di. Is theory mein trade cycle ko monetary problem maana gaya aur money-\ndemand flow mein changes ko prosperity aur depression se link kiya gaya. [Q146]\n• Forex reserves increase hone se domestic monetary expansion ka direct effect ho sakta hai; India ki industries imported goods aur\nmachinery par significant dependence rakhti hain. [Q147]\n                        Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · Foreign Exchange, FDI, External Debt Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0462","t":"15 International Organizations","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["INTERNATIONAL ORGANIZATIONS — FULL MIND MAP\n                                   Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\n📑 Topics in this chapter:\n1. World Bank Group, AIIB & Development Finance\n2. IMF, Bretton Woods & International Monetary System\n3. WTO — Basics, Membership & Ministerial Conferences\n4. WTO Agriculture, GATT, Dunkel, TRIMS & Trade Facilitation\n5. TRIPS, IPR, GI & WTO-linked Agreements\n6. G20, Global Tax Issues & NAMA-11\n7. World Economic Forum & Global Indices\n8. ADB, AIIB, European Union & Trade-Technology Cooperation\n9. UN Agencies, FAO, ILO & UN System\n10. APTA, TPP & Non-Aligned Movement\n11. OPEC, SAARC & UNICEF\n12. BRICS & New Development Bank\n13. SCO, GCC & Mekong-Ganga Cooperation\n14. BIMSTEC, Benelux & Regional Trade Arrangements\n15. G-15, G7/G8, IORA & Developing-Country Coalitions\n16. ASEAN, RCEP & East Asia Summit\n17. CDRI & Voice of Global South\n\n\n 1. World Bank Group, AIIB & Development Finance\n\n • Asian Infrastructure Investment Bank (AIIB) ek multilateral development bank hai jiska headquarters Beijing mein hai. 2016 mein 57 founding\n members the; 28 February 2026 tak 111 approved members the. [Q1]\n • First World Summit for Social Development March 1995 mein Copenhagen, Denmark mein held hua tha. [Q2]\n • World Bank 1944 mein establish hua, jabki institution ki membership 1945 se effective hui; current note mein World Bank ke 189 member countries\n hain. [Q3]\n • International Development Association (IDA) 1960 mein establish hui aur World Bank/IBRD ke under operate karti hai. Concessional lending ki wajah\n se ise World Bank ka 'Soft Loan Window' bhi kaha jata hai. [Q4]\n • Global Infrastructure Facility World Bank ka collaboration platform hai jo private-sector aur institutional capital ko mobilize karke complex\n infrastructure aur PPP projects ki preparation/structuring ko support karta hai, especially emerging aur developing economies mein. [Q5]\n • World Development Report (WDR) IBRD/World Bank publish karta hai aur ye World Bank Group ka flagship report hai. [Q6]\n • World Development Report 2001 ka central theme 'Attacking Poverty: Opportunity, Empowerment and Security' tha; report ka main focus poverty\n problem par tha. [Q7]\n • World Development Report 1994 mein US$725 ya usse kam per-capita income wali economies ko low-income category mein rakha gaya tha. World\n Bank 1 July ko per-capita GNI ke basis par classification update karta hai; FY2026 thresholds: Low Income <=US$1,135; Lower-Middle US$1,136-\n 4,495; Upper-Middle US$4,496-13,935; High Income >US$13,935. [Q8]\n • World Bank ke 2024 current-price data mein listed countries ke beech Luxembourg ka per-capita GDP sabse zyada, US$137,781.7 tha. [Q9]\n • Ease of Doing Business ke sub-indices mein 'Maintaining law and order' include nahi tha; September 2021 mein World Bank ne Ease of Doing\n Business Report discontinue karne ka announcement kiya. [Q10]\n • World Bank ki October 2017 Ease of Doing Business report mein India rank 100 par tha; Doing Business 2020 mein 14-rank jump ke saath India 190\n countries mein rank 63 par pahunch gaya. [Q11]\n • Ease of Doing Business Index World Bank 2003 se annually issue karta tha; ranking business-friendly reforms aur regulatory changes ke basis par\n hoti thi. [Q12]\n • Ease of Doing Business Index World Bank Group/IFC publish karte the. Doing Business 2020 mein India 190 countries mein rank 63 par tha. [Q13]\n • Ease of Doing Business Index 2020 mein New Zealand top par tha aur India 63rd position par; 2019 report mein India 77th rank par tha. [Q14]\n • Ease of Doing Business 2020 mein India 63rd rank par tha, previous edition ke 77th rank se improvement; New Zealand first tha. [Q15]\n • BRAP 2019 ranking, jo 5 September 2020 ko release hui: Andhra Pradesh 1st, Uttar Pradesh 2nd, Telangana 3rd aur Madhya Pradesh 4th. [Q16]\n • India mein states ki Ease of Doing Business ranking DPIIT, Ministry of Commerce & Industry issue karta hai; DPIIT business-environment reforms\n coordinate karne wala nodal department hai. [Q17]\n • World Bank Group 'Global Economic Prospects' report aam taur par saal mein do baar—January aur June—release karta hai. [Q18]\n • World Bank 2024 current-price per-capita GDP data: China US$13,303.1; Sri Lanka US$4,515.6; Indonesia US$4,925.4; India US$2,694.7. [Q19]\n • World Bank ke 2024 current-price GDP data mein USA world's largest economy tha; uske baad China, Germany, Japan aur India ka order tha. [Q20]\n • 2024 nominal GDP ranking mein Germany world ki third-largest economy thi; USA first aur China second the. [Q21]\n • World Bank ke December 2025 data ke mutabik 2024 current-price GDP mein India duniya ki 5th-largest economy tha; PPP terms mein India China\n aur USA ke baad 3rd position par tha. [Q22]\n • 2024 PPP ranking mein China first, USA second aur India third tha; older PPP references mein India fourth position par tha. [Q23]\n • Older PPP data mein India fourth rank par tha; World Bank December 2025 release ke 2024 PPP data mein China > USA > India > Russia > Japan\n order tha. [Q24]\n • Purchasing Power Parity (PPP) exchange rate identical goods aur services ki prices ko different countries mein compare karke calculate ki jati hai;\n ye currencies ki relative purchasing power ko show karti hai. [Q25]\n • 2019 mein India ka GDP lagbhag US$2.875 trillion (about ₹209 lakh crore) tha. [Q26]\n • 2024 current-price GDP ke basis par top five economies ka order tha: USA > China > Germany > Japan > India. [Q27]\n • 2019 mein Central Government ne Indian economy ko US$5 trillion banane ka target 2024 ke liye announce kiya tha; Economic Survey 2019-20\n mein target year 2025 mention hua. [Q28]\n • International agencies aur Government of India ke agreements ke through milne wali assistance mainly development-oriented hoti hai; World\n Bank/ADB support mein technical assistance, concessional loans, grants aur short/long-term financing include ho sakta hai. [Q29]\n • World Bank ne 1958 mein India Consortium/Aid-India Consortium Group establish kiya, jiska later naam Indian Development Forum (IDF) hua. World\n Bank Indian states ko transport, irrigation, water supply, power aur roads jaise infrastructure ke liye long-term finance deta raha hai. [Q30]","• Indian Development Forum (IDF) ka earlier name India Aid Club tha. [Q31]\n• 2008 Kosi flood se affected Bihar ke millions of people ki condition improve karne ke liye January 2011 mein Government of Bihar aur World Bank ke\nbeech US$220 million ka agreement hua. [Q32]\n• November 2007 ke agreement mein World Bank ne India ki rural cooperative structure ko strengthen karne ke liye four years mein US$600 million\nloan propose kiya tha. [Q33]\n• November 2007 mein World Bank President Robert B. Zoellick ne India's rural cooperative credit system ke liye US$944 million loan approve kiya. Is\npackage mein vocational training aur community-based water management included the; rural roads aur sanitation included nahi the. [Q34]\n• National Agricultural Innovation Project (NAIP) ICAR ne World Bank support ke saath July 2006 mein launch kiya. Iske four components the: ICAR ki\ncatalytic role strengthen karna; consumer-system oriented agricultural-production research; sustainable rural livelihood research; aur basic/strategic\nagricultural-science research. [Q35]\n• World Bank ne emerging-economy companies ki Carbon Efficiency dikhane ke liye environment-friendly stock-market index 'Green Index' develop\nkiya. [Q36]\n• World Bank Group ke five institutions hain: IBRD, IDA, IFC, MIGA aur ICSID. IBRD 1944 aur IDA 1960 mein establish hue. [Q37]\n• World Bank Group five institutions—IBRD, IDA, IFC, MIGA aur ICSID—se milkar bana hai; IBRD ki sthapna 1944 mein hui. [Q38]\n• World Bank formally 1944 mein establish hua; membership December 1945 se effective hui. Iska headquarters Washington, D.C. mein hai. [Q39]\n• World Bank ka headquarters Washington, D.C. mein hai. [Q40]\n• IBRD middle-income aur creditworthy low-income countries ko loans, guarantees aur related financial support provide karta hai. [Q41]\n• International Development Association (IDA) 24 September 1960 ko World Bank ki supplementary concessional-lending arm ke roop mein establish\nhui. Ye poorest countries ko poverty aur inequality kam karne, development aur living standards improve karne ke liye loans/grants deti hai. [Q42]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — World Bank Rankings & Data are Period-specific\n▸ Ease of Doing Business, GDP-size aur PPP rankings report-year specific hain; later ranking ko older-year figure par apply mat karo.\n▸ World Bank Group mein IBRD, IDA, IFC, MIGA aur ICSID ke roles alag hain; institution-function pairing exact rakho.\nQ Coverage: Q3–Q42\n\n\n\n2. IMF, Bretton Woods & International Monetary System\n\n• IMF Bretton Woods framework ke under 1944 mein World Bank ke saath establish hua; member-country membership 27 December 1945 se\neffective hui. IMF lending member countries ko hi karta hai. [Q43]\n• IMF loans sirf member countries ko deta hai; IMF aur World Bank dono 1944 Bretton Woods framework se linked institutions hain. [Q44]\n• IMF ka core role member countries ko Balance of Payments problems solve karne mein financial assistance dena hai. [Q45]\n• IMF temporary Balance of Payments deficit ko finance karne ke liye member countries ko loans provide karta hai. [Q46]\n• IMF aur IBRD dono 1944 Bretton Woods agreement se bane, isliye inhe 'Bretton Woods Twins' kaha jata hai. Dono ka headquarters Washington,\nD.C. mein hone ki wajah se 'Washington Twins' bhi kaha jata hai. [Q47]\n• IMF aur World Bank/IBRD ko 1944 Bretton Woods Conference mein conceive/approve kiya gaya, isliye inhe 'Bretton Woods Twins' kaha jata hai.\n[Q48]\n• Bretton Woods institutions IMF aur World Bank hain; United Nations aur World Trade Organization Bretton Woods institutions nahi hain. [Q49]\n• July 1944 ka United Nations Monetary and Financial Conference hi commonly Bretton Woods Conference ke naam se jana jata hai. [Q50]\n• International Monetary Fund (IMF) Bretton Woods Agreement ke under establish hua. [Q51]\n• IMF ke 191 member countries hain; Liechtenstein October 2024 mein 191st member bana. [Q52]\n• India IMF ka founding member hai aur 27 December 1945 se IMF membership rakhta hai. [Q53]\n• India IMF ka founding member hai. [Q54]\n• February 2026 tak IMF mein India ka SDR share 2.75% aur voting share 2.63% tha. [Q55]\n• Gold Tranche ya Reserve Tranche IMF members ke liye credit-access facility hai; member country apne contributed reserve portion ko zarurat,\nespecially emergency, mein use kar sakta hai. [Q56]\n• IMF ka Rapid Financing Instrument immediate Balance of Payments financing need ke time member countries ko quick support deta hai; ise different\nfinancing needs meet karne ke broader reform ke part ke roop mein introduce kiya gaya. [Q57]\n• IMF ke Special Drawing Rights (SDR) ko 'Paper Gold' kaha jata hai. SDR ki value currency basket ke basis par determine hoti hai aur IMF iska\nreview five-year interval par karta hai. [Q58]\n• IMF ka Special Drawing Right (SDR) 'Paper Gold' ke naam se jana jata hai; IMF ne SDR arrangement 1969 mein introduce kiya. [Q59]\n• IMF Executive Board ke 30 November 2015 decision ke baad Chinese Yuan/Renminbi 1 October 2016 se SDR basket ki fifth currency bana; ye SDR\nbasket mein include hone wali first developing-country currency thi. [Q60]\n• World Economic Outlook IMF publish karta hai, jabki Human Development Report UNDP publish karta hai. [Q61]\n• IMF World Economic Outlook, April 2025 mein India's 2025 nominal GDP approx US$4.187 trillion estimate hua; projection tha ki India 2025 mein\nJapan ko overtake karke world's 4th-largest economy ban sakta hai. [Q62]\n• Global Financial Stability Report IMF ka semiannual report hai jo global financial system aur markets ki stability/risks assess karta hai. [Q63]\n• Global Financial Stability Report (GFSR) IMF ki report hai aur generally saal mein do baar—April aur October—issue hoti hai. [Q64]\n• Gita Gopinath October 2018 mein IMF ki Economic Counsellor aur Research Department Director appointed hui; January 2022 se wo IMF ki First\nDeputy Managing Director bani. [Q65]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — Bretton Woods & IMF Facts\n▸ IMF aur IBRD Bretton Woods twins hain aur dono ka headquarters Washington, D.C. mein hai. United Nations aur WTO Bretton Woods institutions nahi\nhain.\n▸ India IMF ka founding member hai; membership 27 December 1945 se effective hui.\nQ Coverage: Q47–Q60\n\n\n\n3. WTO — Basics, Membership & Ministerial Conferences","• World Trade Organization (WTO) international trade ko regulate karne wali intergovernmental organization hai. Ye Marrakesh Agreement ke under 1\nJanuary 1995 ko establish hui. [Q66]\n• GATT system mein reforms ki need ke baad WTO 1 January 1995 ko establish hua; ye multilateral international trade framework ko institutional form\ndeta hai. [Q67]\n• WTO ka predecessor General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT) tha. [Q68]\n• World Trade Organization (WTO) ka headquarters Geneva, Switzerland mein hai. [Q69]\n• December 1999 Seattle WTO Ministerial mein developing countries ne labour aur environmental issues ko WTO agenda mein include karne ke US\nproposal oppose kiya; consensus na banne se conference fail hui. [Q70]\n• First WTO Ministerial Conference December 1996 mein Singapore mein hua, jahan Information Technology products par agreement/initiative ko\nmajor outcome mana gaya. [Q71]\n• WTO ka fourth Ministerial Conference November 2001 mein Doha, Qatar mein hua; Doha Qatar ki capital hai. [Q72]\n• WTO ka 13th Ministerial Conference 26 February-2 March 2024 Abu Dhabi, UAE mein hua; final declaration mein WTO reform agenda prominently\ninclude tha. [Q73]\n• WTO ki 13th Ministerial Conference (MC13) 26 February-2 March 2024 ko Abu Dhabi, UAE mein held hui. Ministerial Conference WTO ka highest\ndecision-making body hai. [Q74]\n• International food-safety standards ke context mein WTO Codex Alimentarius Commission ke standards ko reference point ke roop mein use karta\nhai. Codex Commission FAO aur WHO ne 1963 mein establish ki thi. [Q75]\n• World Trade Organization (WTO) 1 January 1995 se operational hai aur member countries ke beech non-discriminatory free trade promote karta hai.\n[Q76]\n• WTO ka main objective member countries ke beech non-discriminatory aur rules-based free trade ko promote karna hai; WTO 1 January 1995 se\noperational hai. [Q77]\n• Roberto Azevedo ne 1 September 2013 ko WTO ke 6th Director-General ke roop mein Pascal Lamy ko replace kiya. March 2021 se Ngozi Okonjo-\nIweala WTO ki 7th Director-General bani. [Q78]\n• India 1 January 1995 se WTO ka founding member hai; currently WTO ke 166 member nations hain. [Q79]\n• Nepal 23 April 2004 ko World Trade Organization ka 147th member bana. [Q80]\n• WTO international trade ke fair aur smooth conduct ke rules/policies banata aur review karta hai. 1 January 1995 se operational WTO ke 166\nmembers hain; Comoros August 2024 mein 165th aur Timor-Leste 166th member bana; Afghanistan July 2016 mein 164th member bana tha. [Q81]\n• WTO Uruguay Round se emerge hua aur trade liberalization, trade disputes resolve karna, trade capacity develop karna aur trade monitoring uske\nmajor functions hain. [Q82]\n• Doha Round negotiations ka major issue agricultural subsidies aur agricultural imports se related rules hain; Doha Round 2001 mein start hua. [Q83]\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — WTO Facts: Historical vs Current\n▸ WTO establishment, India membership, headquarters, Director-General succession aur Ministerial Conferences alag years ke facts hain; MC13 February-\nMarch 2024 ko separately yaad rakho.\n▸ Historical membership counts aur joining dates ko exact period ke figure ke saath read karo.\nQ Coverage: Q66–Q83\n\n\n\n4. WTO Agriculture, GATT, Dunkel, TRIMS & Trade Facilitation\n\n• WTO ke Agreement on Agriculture mein anti-dumping aur countervailing duties three main pillars ka part nahi hain; major pillars Market Access,\nDomestic Support aur Export Competition hain. [Q84]\n• WTO subsidy framework mein Green Box, Blue Box aur Amber Box important categories hain. Green Box subsidies minimally trade-distorting hoti\nhain, Blue Box production-limiting conditions se linked hoti hain aur Amber Box trade-distorting support ko cover karta hai. [Q85]\n• Amber Box, Blue Box aur Green Box WTO ke agricultural-subsidy disciplines se related terms hain. [Q86]\n• WTO Agreement on Agriculture ke standard subsidy boxes mein Red Box aur White Box categories nahi hain. [Q87]\n• Agricultural subsidies direct aur indirect categories mein divide hoti hain; India mein fertilizer jaise input subsidies indirect farm subsidies ke under\naati hain. [Q88]\n• Special Safeguard Mechanism (SSM) WTO framework mein developing countries ko cheap imports se domestic economy harm hone par additional\nsafeguard duty lagane ki flexibility deta hai. [Q89]\n• GATT ka Most Favoured Nation (MFN) principle kehta hai ki ek member ko di gayi special trade facility normally sab GATT/WTO members ko bhi\nextend ki jaaye. [Q90]\n• GATT ka full form General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade hai. October 1947 Geneva agreement ke basis par 1 January 1948 se operational hua;\nobjective trade expand karna aur tariffs reduce karna tha. [Q91]\n• GATT office 1948 mein Geneva mein establish hua. [Q92]\n• Uruguay Round negotiations December 1990 Brussels mein stall hue. December 1991 mein GATT DG Arthur Dunkel ne final draft present kiya, jise\nDunkel Draft kaha gaya; ismein services, investment aur TRIPS jaise issues include the. [Q93]\n• GATT Uruguay Round ki talks December 1990 mein Brussels mein break down/stall hui thi. [Q94]\n• GATT ke 8th round, Uruguay Round, ki negotiations mein deadlock aaya tha; ise break karne ke liye then GATT Director-General Arthur Dunkel ne\nproposal present kiya, jise Dunkel Draft ke naam se jana gaya. [Q95]\n• GATT negotiations ka 8th round 1986 mein Punta del Este, Uruguay se start hua. Uruguay Round GATT ke sabse extensive aur controversial\nnegotiation rounds mein se ek tha. [Q96]\n• TRIMs ka full form Trade-Related Investment Measures hai aur ye WTO agreement framework ka part hai. [Q97]\n• WTO countries ke beech trade rules se concerned global organization hai; WTO agreements member countries negotiate/sign karte hain aur\ndomestic ratification ke through adopt hote hain. [Q98]\n• Cross-border goods movement mein unnecessary delays, especially SMEs ke liye, major concern the. Isi problem ko reduce karne ke liye WTO ka\nTrade Facilitation Agreement (TFA) 2013 Bali Ministerial mein conclude hua. [Q99]","⚠ EXAM TRAP — WTO Agriculture Boxes\n▸ WTO agriculture mein Green, Amber aur Blue Box standard categories hain; Red Box aur White Box standard agriculture-subsidy boxes nahi hain.\n▸ Agreement on Agriculture ke three pillars aur direct/indirect farm subsidies ke roles ko clearly distinguish karo.\nQ Coverage: Q84–Q99\n\n\n\n5. TRIPS, IPR, GI & WTO-linked Agreements\n\n• WTO ka TRIPS Agreement intellectual-property rights cover karta hai—copyright/related rights, trademarks, geographical indications, industrial\ndesigns, patents, integrated-circuit layout designs aur trade secrets; labour standards TRIPS ka part nahi hain. [Q100]\n• TRIPS framework patents, geographical indications, copyrights, integrated-circuit layout designs aur trademarks jaise IPRs ko protect karta hai; plant\nvarieties ke liye protection ka approach separate provisions/sui generis system se linked hai. [Q101]\n• Brand recognition, intellectual property aur client mailing list intangible investment hain; inventory tangible investment hai. [Q102]\n• Government of India ne May 2016 mein National Intellectual Property Rights Policy approve ki; iska slogan 'Creative India, Innovative India' hai.\n[Q103]\n• International Intellectual Property Index 2024 ke 12th edition mein India 55 economies mein 42nd tha. Index U.S. Chamber of Commerce publish\nkarta hai. 2025 ke 13th edition mein India 43rd rank par tha, score 36.45%; top three USA, UK aur France the. [Q104]\n• National Intellectual Property Rights Policy May 2016 mein approve hui aur iska slogan 'Creative India, Innovative India' hai. [Q105]\n• India ka IPR framework WTO/TRIPS obligations se linked hai. Important laws mein Geographical Indications of Goods Act 1999, Trade Marks Act\n1999 aur Patents Act 1970 include hain. [Q106]\n• Geographical Indication (GI) sirf agricultural products ya handicrafts tak limited nahi hai; manufactured goods ko bhi GI protection mil sakti hai. [Q107]\n• WTO agriculture commitments mein developed countries ko six years mein agricultural subsidies about 20% reduce karni thi, jabki developing\ncountries ko ten years mein about 13% reduction karna tha; base period 1986-88 tha. [Q108]\n• Agreement on Agriculture, Agreement on Sanitary and Phytosanitary Measures aur Peace Clause WTO framework se related hain. [Q109]\n• WTO ke paas trade-dispute settlement mechanism hai; isliye 'WTO has no mechanism for resolving trade disputes' statement correct nahi hai. WTO\npar other issues ko lekar criticism alag se hota raha hai. [Q110]\n• International Grains Council (IGC) ek intergovernmental organization hai jo Grains Trade Convention 1995 ki implementation oversee karta aur\nglobal grain-trade cooperation promote karta hai. [Q111]\n• Broad-based Trade and Investment Agreement (BTIA) India-EU free-trade agreement negotiations ka framework hai; negotiations 2007 mein start\nhui. [Q112]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — TRIPS, IPR & GI\n▸ TRIPS trademarks, industrial designs, geographical indications aur other IPR areas ko cover karta hai; labour standards TRIPS protection list ka part nahi\nhain.\n▸ National IPR Policy aur International IP Index ke facts policy/report year ke context ke saath read karo.\nQ Coverage: Q100–Q112\n\n\n\n6. G20, Global Tax Issues & NAMA-11\n\n• G20 mein 19 countries ke saath European Union aur African Union members hain; African Union ko 2023 India G20 Summit mein permanent\nmember banaya gaya. [Q113]\n• 1997-98 Asian financial crisis ke baad 1999 mein G20 forum establish hua. G20 mein 19 countries ke saath European Union aur African Union\nregional bodies ke roop mein include hain. [Q114]\n• Argentina, Mexico, South Africa aur Türkiye G20 members hain. G20 major economies ka forum hai jahan finance ministers, central-bank governors\naur leaders economic cooperation par coordinate karte hain; forum 1999 mein establish hua. [Q115]\n• G20 mein 19 countries ke saath European Union aur African Union shamil hain. Iska permanent secretariat nahi hai; presidency annually rotate hoti\nhai. [Q116]\n• Historical context mein notified year 2022 tha; India ne G20 Presidency 1 December 2022 ko assume ki aur 30 November 2023 tak hold ki. [Q117]\n• India ne 1 December 2022 ko Indonesia se G20 Presidency li. India ki presidency ke under first G20 Development Working Group meeting 13-16\nDecember 2022 ko Mumbai mein hui. [Q118]\n• First G20 Leaders' Summit 2008 mein Washington, D.C., USA mein held hua. [Q119]\n• 2023 ka G20 Summit 9-10 September 2023 ko New Delhi mein held hua; ye G20 ka 18th Leaders' Summit tha. [Q120]\n• 18th G20 Summit 9-10 September 2023 ko Bharat Mandapam, New Delhi mein held hua; official theme 'Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam - One Earth, One\nFamily, One Future' thi. [Q121]\n• Unsustainable debt problem address karne ke liye G20 countries ne 2020 mein Common Framework agree kiya; debt relief debtor ki repayment\ncapacity aur essential expenditure needs ko dhyan mein rakhkar design hota hai. [Q122]\n• Base Erosion and Profit Shifting (BEPS) tax-avoidance strategy hai jisme MNCs tax-rule loopholes use karke profits high-tax se low/no-tax\njurisdictions mein shift karti hain. OECD aur G20 ne September 2013 mein 15-point BEPS Action Plan adopt kiya. [Q123]\n• NAMA-11 developing countries ka WTO coalition hai. NAMA ka matlab Non-Agricultural Market Access hai aur iska focus non-agricultural products\npar tariffs/non-tariff barriers reduce karne ki negotiations par hai. [Q124]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — G20 Membership & Presidency\n▸ G20 origin, membership representation, first summit, India presidency aur 2023 New Delhi Summit alag events/years se linked hain; country-year pairing\nexact rakho.\n▸ BEPS aur NAMA-11 ko G20/WTO-linked global economic cooperation ke correct institutional context mein yaad rakho.\nQ Coverage: Q113–Q124\n\n\n\n7. World Economic Forum & Global Indices\n\n• UNCTAD ne first Digital Economy Report September 2019 mein issue ki; second Digital Economy Report 2021 mein aayi. [Q125]","• World Economic Forum 1971 mein establish hua; iske founder University of Geneva ke Professor Klaus Schwab the. [Q126]\n• Recent WEF Davos annual-meeting themes: 2026—'A Spirit of Dialogue'; 2025—'Collaboration for the Intelligent Age'; 2024—'Rebuilding Trust';\n2023—'Cooperation in a Fragmented World'. [Q127]\n• World Economic Forum Annual Meeting 2016, 20-23 January ko Davos, Switzerland mein held hui; theme 'Mastering the Fourth Industrial\nRevolution' thi. [Q128]\n• WEF Annual Meeting 2018, 23-26 January ko Davos mein held hui. PM Narendra Modi ne 23 January speech mein climate change, terrorism aur\nprotectionism ko major global challenges bataya. [Q129]\n• '100 Million Farmers' WEF platform hai jiska aim net-zero, nature-positive food-water systems aur climate-resilient farming ko accelerate karna hai;\ntarget 100 million farmers tak climate/nature-friendly practices scale karna hai. [Q130]\n• Global Talent Competitiveness Index 2020 mein Switzerland rank 1, USA 2, Singapore 3 aur India 72 par tha. 2025 index mein top three Singapore,\nSwitzerland aur Denmark the; India rank 100 par raha. [Q131]\n• Global Competitiveness Report World Economic Forum (WEF) publish karta tha. [Q132]\n• Geneva-based World Economic Forum Global Competitiveness Report issue karta tha. [Q133]\n• World Economic Forum Global Gender Gap Report issue karta hai; Economic Participation & Opportunity aur Educational Attainment jaise\nparameters ismein include hote hain. [Q134]\n• Global Gender Gap Index WEF 2006 se annually publish karta hai. India 2024 index mein 146 countries mein 129th tha; 2023 mein rank 127th thi.\n[Q135]\n• Global Gender Gap Index gender equality measure karta hai aur World Economic Forum ise 2006 se annually issue karta hai. [Q136]\n• WEF ke Global Gender Gap Index 2020 mein 153 countries cover hue aur India ki rank 112th thi. [Q137]\n• UNDP ne March 2020 mein first Gender Social Norms Index release kiya. Ye index politics, work aur education jaise areas mein gender equality ko\naffect karne wale social norms measure karta hai. [Q138]\n• Travel and Tourism Competitiveness Index (TTCI) 2007 se prepare hua aur travel-tourism sector ke sustainable development factors/policies\nmeasure karta tha; 2021 se WEF ne Travel and Tourism Development Index (TTDI) publish kiya. [Q139]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — WEF Report vs Publisher\n▸ Global Competitiveness, Global Gender Gap, Travel & Tourism Competitiveness aur Global Talent Competitiveness jaise indices ke publisher ko exact pair\nmein yaad rakho; WEF multiple major indices publish karta hai.\n▸ Rank-based facts year-specific hote hain; 2020 ranking ko later-year ranking se replace mat karo.\nQ Coverage: Q125–Q139\n\n\n\n8. ADB, AIIB, European Union & Trade-Technology Cooperation\n\n• India Asian Development Bank ka member 1966 se aur Colombo Plan ka member 1951 se hai; India APEC aur OECD ka member nahi hai. [Q140]\n• Asian Development Bank ka Indian Resident Mission 1992 mein open hua aur New Delhi mein located hai. [Q141]\n• Nature Solutions Finance Hub for Asia and the Pacific ki initiative/launch Asian Development Bank (ADB) ne ki. [Q142]\n• AIIB ek multilateral development bank hai jiska aim Asia mein infrastructure, productive sectors aur connectivity ke through sustainable economic\ndevelopment promote karna hai. AIIB ne 2016 mein operations start kiye. [Q143]\n• Treaty of Rome (1957) ke under European Economic Community (EEC) establish hui, jiska institutional centre Brussels se linked tha. 1993 mein\nEuropean Union banne ke baad EEC ko European Community (EC) ke naam se integrate kiya gaya. [Q144]\n• Switzerland European Union (EU) ka member nahi hai. [Q145]\n• Poland 2004 se European Union ka member hai; older pre-2004 references mein Poland EU ke bahar tha. [Q146]\n• European Stability Mechanism (ESM) euro-area countries ko financial crisis ke time assistance deta hai; ye Luxembourg-based permanent eurozone\nfinancial-stability institution hai. [Q147]\n• EU ka Stability and Growth Pact sound public finances aur fiscal-policy coordination ke rules set karta hai; member countries ke fiscal deficit aur\npublic debt par limits prescribed hain. [Q148]\n• Digital Single Market Strategy European Union se related hai; aim EU countries ke beech digital-trade barriers remove karna aur future\ntrade/employment opportunities expand karna hai. [Q149]\n• June 2021 Brussels US-EU Summit mein US President Joe Biden aur European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen ne US-EU Trade and\nTechnology Council establish karne ka announcement kiya. [Q150]\n\n9. UN Agencies, FAO, ILO & UN System\n\n• UNOPS ke Sustainable Investments in Infrastructure and Innovation Initiative mein affordable housing, renewable energy aur health infrastructure\ntargeted areas hain; mass rapid transport is listed target set ka part nahi hai. [Q151]\n• FAO ne 2002 mein Globally Important Agricultural Heritage Systems (GIAHS) programme launch kiya. Iska aim biodiversity, genetic resources,\ntraditional knowledge, culture aur landscapes ko preserve karne wale agricultural systems identify, support aur conserve karna hai. [Q152]\n• ILO ki 100th session, Geneva, 16 June 2011 mein domestic workers/labourers ke interests protect karne ka decision hua; related rules 5 September\n2013 se effective hue. [Q153]\n• ILO Convention 138 minimum age for employment se related hai, jabki Convention 182 worst forms of child labour se related hai. [Q154]\n• United States developing countries par labour standards ko lekar pressure dalta raha hai; US compliance-sensitivity wala second claim is\nexplanation ko support nahi karta. [Q155]\n• Earlier 'World Social Security Report' ILO issue karta tha; ab report 'World Social Protection Report' ke naam se publish hoti hai, jiska latest cited\nedition 2024-26 hai. [Q156]\n• World Social Security Report ILO/International Labour Office issue karta tha; later reporting 'World Social Protection Report' ke naam se continue\nhui. [Q157]\n• Global Wage Report 2024-25 International Labour Organization (ILO) ka flagship report hai. [Q158]\n• World Economic Forum ke Crystal Awards un artists ko diye jaate hain jo inclusive aur sustainable global change ke liye notable commitment dikhate\nhain. [Q159]\n• UNSC ka full form United Nations Security Council hai. Ismein 5 permanent members—China, USA, France, Russia, UK—aur 10 non-permanent\nmembers hote hain. [Q160]","• UNSC ka full form United Nations Security Council hai. [Q161]\n• Important organization-function pairs: WTO—quantitative trade restrictions ko generally prohibit/reduce karna; IMF—Balance of Payments imbalance\ncorrect karne ke liye finance provide karna. [Q162]\n• International Monetary and Financial Committee (IMFC) IMF Board of Governors ki recommendation par establish hua; Board ko IMFC aur\nDevelopment Committee ministerial-level advice dete hain. [Q163]\n• Important report-publisher pairs: Human Development Report—UNDP; World Economic Outlook—IMF; World Investment Report—UNCTAD;\nEmissions Gap Report—UNEP. [Q164]\n• Important headquarters pairs: UNO—New York; WTO—Geneva; ILO—Geneva; FAO—Rome. [Q165]\n• Organization-headquarters pairs: UNO—New York; WTO—Geneva; ILO—Geneva; FAO—Rome. [Q166]\n• UN membership earlier period mein 191 thi; Montenegro aur South Sudan ke admission ke baad current membership 193 hai. [Q167]\n• First three institutions World Bank Group se associated hain aur UN system se linked hain; Bank for International Settlements UN se associated nahi\nhai. [Q168]\n• UNCTAD classification mein Brazil, Mexico aur South Africa emerging economies mein include kiye jaate hain. [Q169]\n• Asia-Pacific Ministerial Conference on Housing and Urban Development 13-16 December 2006 ko New Delhi mein held hui; theme 'A Vision for\nSustainable Urbanization in Asia-Pacific by 2020' thi. [Q170]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — UN Membership: Historical vs Current Count\n▸ UN membership figures date-specific hain; older 191-member figure ko later membership count se replace nahi karna chahiye.\n▸ FAO, ILO, UNOPS, UNSC, UNCTAD aur UN ke headquarters/reports ko organization-function/report ke exact pair ke saath yaad rakho.\nQ Coverage: Q151–Q170\n\n\n\n10. APTA, TPP & Non-Aligned Movement\n\n• 12 Trans-Pacific countries ne 5 October 2015 ko Atlanta, USA mein Trans-Pacific Partnership (TPP) agreement conclude kiya. TPP ka aim EU-style\nlarge integrated trade market create karna tha. [Q171]\n• APTA originally 1975 mein Bangkok Agreement ke naam se sign hua aur 2005 mein Asia Pacific Trade Agreement naam mila. Members:\nBangladesh, China, India, South Korea, Laos, Mongolia aur Sri Lanka; Myanmar member nahi hai. [Q172]\n• NAM ka 13th Summit February 2003 Kuala Lumpur mein, 16th Summit August 2012 Tehran mein aur 17th Summit September 2016 Margarita\nIsland, Venezuela mein hua. [Q173]\n• NAM Coordinating Bureau ki ministerial meeting 2019 mein Caracas, Venezuela mein hui. [Q174]\n11. OPEC, SAARC & UNICEF\n\n• OPEC ki sthapna 1960 mein hui; focus petroleum production aur oil-price coordination par hai. Currently 12 member countries hain, including Iran,\nIraq, Kuwait, Saudi Arabia, UAE, Algeria, Nigeria, Libya, Venezuela, Equatorial Guinea, Congo aur Gabon. [Q175]\n• China OPEC ka member nahi hai. [Q176]\n• SAARC 8 December 1985 ko establish hua aur iska headquarters Kathmandu mein hai. Iske 8 members India, Pakistan, Sri Lanka, Maldives,\nBangladesh, Nepal, Bhutan aur Afghanistan hain; Afghanistan April 2007 ke 14th summit mein 8th member bana. [Q177]\n• SAARC 8 December 1985 ko establish hua. [Q178]\n• Cambodia SAARC ka member nahi hai. [Q179]\n• Grain Storage Research and Training Centre 1958 mein Hapur mein establish hua; aaj ye Indian Grain Storage Management and Research Institute\n(IGMRI) ke naam se jana jata hai. [Q180]\n• India mein first SAARC Summit 16-17 November 1986 ko hua; first-ever SAARC Summit 1985 mein Dhaka, Bangladesh mein hua tha. [Q181]\n• UNICEF, IMF aur WHO global institutions hain, jabki SAARC eight South Asian countries ka regional organization hai. [Q182]\n• UNICEF ka headquarters New York, USA mein hai; full name United Nations Children's Fund hai. [Q183]\n12. BRICS & New Development Bank\n\n• 11th BRICS Summit 13-14 November 2019 ko Brasilia, Brazil mein held hua; previous summit 25-27 July 2018 ko Johannesburg, South Africa mein\nhua tha. [Q184]\n• 1 January 2024 se Russia BRICS chair bana; 16th BRICS Summit October 2024 mein Kazan, Russia mein held hua. [Q185]\n• 16th BRICS Summit 22-24 October 2024 ko Kazan, Russia mein Russia ki chairmanship mein held hua. [Q186]\n• BRICS ka original grouping Brazil, Russia, India, China aur South Africa se bana; South Africa ke join karne se pehle group ko BRIC kaha jata tha.\n[Q187]\n• Brazil, Russia, India, China aur South Africa ka emerging-economies group BRICS kehlata hai. [Q188]\n• South Africa ke inclusion ke saath BRIC grouping 2010 mein BRICS bani. [Q189]\n• First BRICS summit Russia mein held hua. [Q190]\n• South Africa ko BRIC grouping mein 2010 mein admit/invite kiya gaya; usne BRICS summit format mein 2011 se participate kiya. [Q191]\n• Canada BRICS ka member nahi hai. [Q192]\n• 9th BRICS Summit 3-5 September 2017 ko Xiamen, China mein held hua; theme 'BRICS: Stronger Partnership for a Brighter Future' thi. [Q193]\n• 2014 Fortaleza BRICS Summit mein New Development Bank (NDB) establish karne ka agreement sign hua; NDB ka headquarters Shanghai, China\nmein hai. [Q194]\n• 2014 Fortaleza BRICS Summit mein leaders ne New Development Bank (NDB) establish karne ka agreement sign kiya. [Q195]\n• BRIC term 2001 mein Goldman Sachs ke economist Jim O'Neill ne Brazil, Russia, India aur China ke liye coin kiya tha; South Africa ke judne ke\nbaad group BRICS bana. [Q196]\n• Economist Jim O'Neill ne BRIC term coin kiya, jo South Africa ke join karne ke baad BRICS bana. [Q197]\n• Indian banker K. V. Kamath 2015 mein New Development Bank/BRICS Bank ke first President bane. [Q198]\n• Current BRICS membership expansion ke baad group mein 10 full members ka context diya gaya hai; older sources/webpages par 11-country\nwording bhi mil sakti hai, isliye date-context important hai. [Q199]","• World Bank 2024 current-price data mein listed BRICS countries mein highest per-capita GNI Russia ka US$15,320 tha; order Russia > China >\nBrazil > South Africa > India. [Q200]\n• Expanded BRICS countries collectively global trade ka roughly 46% account karte hain. [Q201]\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — BRICS Membership Update\n▸ BRICS facts 2009 first summit, South Africa joining, 2019/2023/2024 summits aur later expansion ke different time-points cover karte hain; membership ko\ndate-context ke saath read karo.\n▸ 16th BRICS Summit Kazan, Russia mein held hua; Indonesia later full member bana. Theme wording mein 'Multilateralism' aur 'Multiculturalism' ko mix mat\nkaro.\nQ Coverage: Q184–Q201\n\n\n\n13. SCO, GCC & Mekong-Ganga Cooperation\n\n• SCO ke Astana Summit mein India aur Pakistan 9 June 2017 ko full members bane; Iran 2023 aur Belarus 2024 mein full member bana. [Q202]\n• Vietnam Shanghai Five ka member nahi tha; 15 June 2001 ko Shanghai mein Shanghai Cooperation Organisation (SCO) ki formation announce hui.\n[Q203]\n• India 2017 se Shanghai Cooperation Organization ka full member hai; isse pehle observer status mein tha. [Q204]\n• India 2017 se SCO ka full member hai; BASIC, G-4 aur BRICS ke saath bhi associated hai. [Q205]\n• Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC) 25 May 1981 ko Riyadh mein Bahrain, Kuwait, Oman, Qatar, Saudi Arabia aur UAE ke agreement se establish hua.\nIska aim member states ke beech coordination, integration aur close relations ko strengthen karna hai. [Q206]\n• Gulf Cooperation Council (GCC) 25 May 1981 ko Riyadh mein Bahrain, Kuwait, Oman, Qatar, Saudi Arabia aur UAE ne establish kiya. [Q207]\n• Mekong-Ganga Cooperation/Swarnabhoomi Project 2000 mein India aur eastern neighbours ke transport, tourism, culture, education aur\ncommunication cooperation ke liye start hua. Six members: India, Cambodia, Vietnam, Laos, Myanmar aur Thailand; Bangladesh included nahi hai.\n[Q208]\n• Mekong-Ganga Cooperation 2000 mein Vientiane, Laos mein establish hua; iska objective Ganga aur Mekong region ke countries ke beech mutual\ncooperation badhana hai. [Q209]\n• Mekong-Ganga Cooperation 2000 mein Vientiane, Laos mein establish hua. [Q210]\n14. BIMSTEC, Benelux & Regional Trade Arrangements\n\n• November 2025 tak BIMSTEC ke 7 member countries the. [Q211]\n• BIMSTEC ki sthapna June 1997 mein hui. Seven members—Bangladesh, Bhutan, Nepal, Sri Lanka, Myanmar, Thailand, India—hain; permanent\nsecretariat Dhaka mein hai. Main objective regional economic cooperation improve karna hai. [Q212]\n• BIMSTEC 1997 mein establish hua aur current seven members Bangladesh, Bhutan, India, Myanmar, Nepal, Sri Lanka aur Thailand hain; Indonesia\nBIMSTEC member nahi hai. [Q213]\n• BIMSTEC countries ke liye 'Climate Smart Farming Systems' seminar 11-13 December 2019 ko New Delhi mein organize hua; aim tropical\nsmallholder farming ko climate-change ke against more resilient banane ke experiences share karna tha. [Q214]\n• Benelux group Belgium, Netherlands aur Luxembourg se milkar banta hai. [Q215]\n• APEC 21-member Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation forum hai; iski sthapna November 1989 mein hui. [Q216]\n• NAFTA Canada, Mexico aur USA ka trilateral trade agreement tha, 1 January 1994 se effective. 2018 mein USMCA agreement conclude hua aur 1\nJuly 2020 se NAFTA ko USMCA ne replace kar diya. [Q217]\n• Regional blocs ki formation chronology mein COMECON-1949, ASEAN-1967, APEC-1989 aur NAFTA-1994 hain; inmein NAFTA newest tha aur 1\nJuly 2020 se USMCA ne NAFTA ko replace kiya. [Q218]\n• SAARC countries ne 11 April 1993 ko Dhaka mein SAPTA sign kiya, jiska aim mutual trade mein customs duties reduce karna tha; SAPTA ne later\nSAFTA ke liye groundwork prepare kiya. [Q219]\n• SAPTA 11 April 1993 ko Dhaka mein sign hua. Participating countries Bangladesh, Bhutan, India, Maldives, Nepal, Pakistan aur Sri Lanka the.\nSAFTA agreement 2004 mein sign hua aur January 2006 se effective hua. [Q220]\n• Trade-agreement chronology: SAPTA—signed 1993/effective 1995; India-Sri Lanka FTA—signed 1998/effective 2000; SAFTA—signed\n2004/effective 2006; India-Singapore CECA—signed 2005. [Q221]\n• APEC ka headquarters Singapore, ADB ka Manila (Philippines) aur ASEAN ka Jakarta (Indonesia) mein hai. [Q222]\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — BIMSTEC Founders, Declaration & Leadership\n▸ BIMSTEC seven-member organization hai; Bangkok Declaration 1997 se established hua. Nepal founding members mein included nahi tha.\n▸ BIMSTEC membership, Dhaka secretariat aur sub-sector leadership ko exact period ke context mein yaad rakho.\nQ Coverage: Q211–Q214\n\n\n\n15. G-15, G7/G8, IORA & Developing-Country Coalitions\n\n• G-15 developing countries ke cooperation ko promote karne wala informal group hai. Ye September 1989 mein Belgrade ke 9th Non-Aligned Summit\nke during establish hua. [Q223]\n• Spain G-8 ka member nahi tha. Historical G-8, G-7 countries plus Russia se bana tha; Russia 1997 mein group mein aaya aur 2014 se uski\nparticipation suspended rahi. [Q224]\n• Russia original G-7 ka member nahi tha; 1997 mein group se judne ke baad grouping ko G-8 kaha gaya. [Q225]\n• India G-8 ka member nahi tha. [Q226]\n• G-7 ke members Canada, France, Germany, Italy, Japan, United Kingdom aur United States hain; Russia G-7 ka member nahi hai. [Q227]\n• 37th G8 Summit 26-27 May 2011 Deauville, France mein hua; June 2020 ka proposed 46th Summit COVID-19 ki wajah se postpone raha. [Q228]\n• 36th G8 Summit Huntsville-Muskoka, Canada mein held hua. Muskoka Initiative maternal, newborn aur child health par focused tha; 2010-15 ke liye\nadditional US$5 billion commitment announce hua. [Q229]\n• 29-31 March 1995 ko Mauritius initiative par Indian Ocean countries ki economic-cooperation meeting hui. Australia, India, Kenya, Mauritius, Oman,\nSingapore aur South Africa 'Core Group States/M-7' ke roop mein participate kiye. [Q230]","• G-77 developing countries ka group hai jo international economic negotiations mein unke collective interests ko represent karta hai. Ye 15 June 1964\nko establish hua. [Q231]\n\n16. ASEAN, RCEP & East Asia Summit\n\n• ASEAN ki sthapna 1967 mein Bangkok mein hui aur headquarters Jakarta mein hai. Timor-Leste ke join karne ke baad ASEAN ke 11 member\ncountries hain. [Q232]\n• ASEAN ka full form Association of Southeast Asian Nations hai; organization 1967 mein Bangkok mein establish hua. [Q233]\n• South Korea ASEAN ka member nahi hai. [Q234]\n• Brunei ASEAN ka member hai. [Q235]\n• ASEAN ka headquarters Jakarta, Indonesia mein hai. [Q236]\n• ASEAN ne seven dialogue partners—China, Japan, South Korea, India, Australia, New Zealand aur Hong Kong—ke saath FTAs sign kiye hain.\n[Q237]\n• RCEP ASEAN countries aur five FTA partners—Australia, China, Japan, South Korea, New Zealand—ka mega trade arrangement hai. Negotiations\nNovember 2012 Cambodia ASEAN Summit mein formally launch hui. [Q238]\n• RCEP agreement 15 November 2020 ko sign hua; ismein 10 ASEAN members ke saath China, Australia, Japan, New Zealand aur South Korea\npartner countries hain; Pakistan RCEP member nahi hai. [Q239]\n• 21st ASEAN-India Summit 10 October 2024 Vientiane, Lao PDR mein hua; Laos ne ASEAN Chair ke roop mein host kiya aur Comprehensive\nStrategic Partnership, especially digital transformation, par cooperation discuss hua. [Q240]\n• FY2023-24 mein ASEAN countries ka India ke global trade mein approx 11% share tha; bilateral trade around US$121 billion raha. [Q241]\n• India East Asia Summit ka partner/participating country hai; East Asia Summit East aur South Asian countries ka regional strategic forum hai. [Q242]\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — ASEAN vs RCEP vs East Asia Summit\n▸ ASEAN membership/headquarters, ASEAN FTA partners, RCEP membership aur East Asia Summit mein India's status alag institutional facts hain; groups\nko interchange mat karo.\n▸ RCEP agreement 15 November 2020 ko 10 ASEAN members + 5 FTA partners ke framework mein sign hua; Pakistan member nahi hai.\nQ Coverage: Q232–Q242\n\n\n\n17. CDRI & Voice of Global South\n\n• Coalition for Disaster Resilient Infrastructure (CDRI) countries, UN agencies, multilateral development banks, private sector aur academic institutions\nka international coalition hai. Ye September 2019 mein Government of India ki leadership aur UNDRR support ke saath launch hua. [Q243]\n• Voice of Global South Summit chronology: 1st—12-13 January 2023; 2nd—17 November 2023; 3rd—17 August 2024; tino India ne host kiye. [Q244]\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — CDRI & Voice of Global South\n▸ CDRI India-led international coalition hai, UN agency nahi; headquarters New Delhi mein hai aur launch September 2019 mein hua.\n▸ Voice of Global South chronology mein 2024 ka third summit latest event tha; second summit November 2023 mein hua tha.\nQ Coverage: Q243–Q244\n\n\n\n                                Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · International Organizations Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0463","t":"16 Miscellaneous","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["MISCELLANEOUS — FULL MIND MAP\n                                    Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\n📑 Topics in this chapter:\n1. Schemes, International Years & National Awards\n2. Education, e-Governance & Social-Digital Concepts\n3. Competition Commission, Pay Commissions & Key Office-holders\n4. Committees, Commissions & Policy-Reform Bodies\n5. Business Houses, Industry Leaders & Economic Personalities\n6. Economists, Nobel Prize, Books & Economic Thought\n7. Governance, CAG, Indices & Public Accountability\n8. Consumer, Legal, Patent & Rights Framework\n9. Statistics, Institutes, Water & Resource Institutions\n10. Government Campaigns, Digital Platforms & Sectoral Schemes\n11. Development Concepts, Classical Economics & Gandhian Thought\n12. Digital Economy, Innovation, Benami Law & Market Concepts\n13. Climate Policy, Environment & Global Economic Crises\n14. Power Sector, Electricity Policy & Solar Schemes\n15. Regional Development, Corridors, Roads, Railways & Tourism\n16. Economic History, Labour & Arthashastra\n17. Telecom, Postal Services & Banking/Industrial Facts\n18. Quality Marks, GI, Eco Mark & Product Standards\n19. Services, Outsourcing, Aviation & Global Initiatives\n\n\n 1. Schemes, International Years & National Awards\n\n • Perform, Achieve and Trade (PAT) scheme 2012 mein start hui; Ministry of Power ke under Bureau of Energy Efficiency ise National Mission for\n Enhanced Energy Efficiency ke component ke roop mein implement karta hai. [Q1]\n • United Nations General Assembly ne 2023 ko International Year of Millets declare kiya tha, jiska main objective millets ke food security aur nutrition\n contribution par awareness badhana tha. [Q2]\n • UN General Assembly different global issues par attention focus karne ke liye International Years declare karta hai. Official list examples: 2025—\n International Year of Peace and Trust; 2026—International Year of the Woman Farmer; 2027—International Year of Sustainable and Resilient\n Tourism; 2029—International Year of Asteroid Awareness and Planetary Defence. [Q3]\n • March 2021 mein UN General Assembly ki 75th session ne 2023 ko 'International Year of Millets' declare kiya. [Q4]\n • Third National Water Awards 2020, jo January 2022 mein announce hue, mein Uttar Pradesh Best State category ka winner tha; Muzaffarnagar\n Northern Zone ka Best District declare hua. [Q5]\n\n 2. Education, e-Governance & Social-Digital Concepts\n\n • National e-Governance Plan (NeGP) ke Mission Mode Projects compilation ko 'Saaransh' kaha jata hai. [Q6]\n • India ki first National Education Policy 1968 mein aur second National Education Policy 1986 mein issue hui. [Q7]\n • June 2017 mein constituted National Education Policy panel ke Chairman K. Kasturirangan the. [Q8]\n • Karnataka ne higher-education level par NEP 2020 implement karne wala first state hone ka status liya; Union Education Minister ne August 2021\n mein Karnataka mein NEP 2020 ko virtually launch kiya. [Q9]\n • Union Cabinet ne 29 July 2020 ko National Education Policy (NEP) 2020 approve ki. Ye 21st century ki first education policy hai aur isne 1986 wali\n old National Education Policy ko replace kiya. [Q10]\n • Tamil Nadu School Education Department ne 26 April 2024 ko schools ke liye Guidelines for Elimination of Corporal Punishment (GECP) issue ki;\n focus students ki physical aur mental health protect karna hai. [Q11]\n • Madhya Pradesh was given the status of \"Achievers\". [Q12]\n • Collaborative filtering e-commerce mein personalized recommendations ke liye commonly used technique hai; Amazon, Flipkart aur Netflix jaise\n platforms is technology ka use karte hain. [Q13]\n • Robert E. Park ne 'Marginal Man' concept ko apne article 'Human Migration and the Marginal Man' mein propound kiya. [Q14]\n • 'Missing Women' concept economist Amartya Sen ne diya; 1990 mein unhone Europe aur Asia ke sex ratios compare karke is idea ko highlight kiya.\n [Q15]\n • Global Village concept transport aur communication development se linked hai; Marshall McLuhan ne aise global village ki idea di jahan members\n electronic nervous system ke through connected hote hain. [Q16]\n • Gartner e-governance maturity model ka order hai: Information > Interaction > Transaction > Transformation. Ye stages government services mein\n ICT use aur online-delivery maturity ko show karti hain. [Q17]\n • Social network ko generally graph ke roop mein model kiya jaata hai—vertices actors ko aur edges relationships ko represent karte hain. Tree graph\n ka special case hai, general social-network representation nahi. [Q18]\n • Mrs. Hansa Mehta renowned social worker, freedom fighter aur educationist thi. [Q19]\n ⚠ EXAM TRAP — NEP 2020: First State Depends on Level\n ▸ NEP 2020 implementation mein 'first state' level-specific hai: higher education ke context mein Karnataka first tha, jabki pre-primary implementation ke July\n 2022 context mein Uttarakhand first tha.\n ▸ 'First state' fact ko implementation level aur year ke saath read karo.\n Q Coverage: Q7-Q10\n\n\n\n 3. Competition Commission, Pay Commissions & Key Office-holders\n\n • October 1999 mein Government of India ne modern competition law suggest karne ke liye S.V.S. Raghavan ki chairmanship mein high-level\n committee banayi; committee ne MRTP Act/Commission replace karke new competition law aur Competition Commission of India (CCI) establish\n karne ki recommendation di. [Q20]","• Competition Commission of India (CCI) Competition Act, 2002 ke under 14 October 2003 ko constitute hui. [Q21]\n• Competition Commission of India ki Chairperson Ravneet Kaur hain; Ashok Chawla earlier Chairperson reh chuke hain. [Q22]\n• September 2024 mein CCI ne 'Market Study on Artificial Intelligence and Competition' ke liye Management Development Institute Society (MDIS)\nappoint ki; CCI Competition Act, 2002 ke under statutory authority hai. [Q23]\n• Sixth Pay Commission October 2006 mein Justice B. N. Srikrishna ki chairmanship mein constitute hua; report 24 March 2008 ko submit hui aur\nrecommendations 1 January 2006 se effective mani gayi. [Q24]\n• Justice Ashok Kumar Mathur February 2014 mein constituted Seventh Central Pay Commission ke Chairman the. January 2025 mein Central\nGovernment ne Eighth Pay Commission establish karne ko approve kiya. [Q25]\n• Gujarat Co-Operative Milk Marketing Federation Ltd. (GCMMF) milk market karta hai; iska famous brand/product Amul hai. [Q26]\n• Airports Authority of India (AAI) ne CSR initiative ke under Assam government ke saath Kamrup district ke villages mein sustainable human-\nsettlement development ke liye MoU sign kiya. [Q27]\n• FICCI ke 2025-26 President Anant Goenka hain; Omkar S. Kanwar earlier period mein FICCI President reh chuke hain. [Q28]\n• Dr Duvvuri Subbarao, jo Central Government mein senior finance/economic-affairs roles mein reh chuke the, September 2008 mein RBI Governor\nbane; Sanjay Malhotra 11 December 2024 se RBI Governor hain. [Q29]\n• Kristalina Georgieva October 2019 se IMF ki Managing Director aur Chairperson hain. Christine Lagarde July 2011-September 2019 tak IMF\nManaging Director/Chairperson rahi. [Q30]\n• Raghuram G. Rajan 1 October 2003-29 December 2006 tak IMF Research Department mein Chief Economist aur Director rahe. [Q31]\n• A. M. Khusro former UPSC Chairman rahe; T. S. Krishnamurthy February 2004-May 2005 tak Chief Election Commissioner of India rahe. [Q32]\n• Important person-association pairs: M. S. Swaminathan—Green Revolution; L. K. Jha—economic-administration reforms; Verghese Kurien—milk\nproduction; Morarji Desai—social control over banks. [Q33]\n• Rekhi Committee exports-imports simplification se related nahi thi; iska focus indirect taxes tha. Committee 1992 mein constituted hui aur report\n1993 mein submit hui. [Q34]\n• Kirit Parikh Committee ne November 2022 mein gas-pricing report submit ki. Recommendation mein 1 January 2026 se complete pricing freedom\naur old gas fields ke natural gas ke liye market-determined pricing ka proposal tha. [Q35]\n\n4. Committees, Commissions & Policy-Reform Bodies\n\n• Gadgil aur Kasturirangan Committee reports Western Ghats ke ecological conservation se related hain. Madhav Gadgil chaired committee ne 2011\nmein report submit ki, aur K. Kasturirangan chaired committee ne 2013 mein. [Q36]\n• Important committee-focus pairs: Sarkaria Commission—Centre-State relations; C. Rangarajan Committee—poverty-measurement methodology;\nParekh Committee—infrastructure financing; Narasimham Committee—banking-sector reforms. [Q37]\n• Tarapore Committee capital-account par rupee convertibility advise karne ke liye constitute hui thi, bank customer service ke liye nahi. [Q38]\n• Committee-year/focus pairs: Dutt Committee (1969)—industrial licensing; Wanchoo Committee (1971)—direct taxes; Rajamannar Committee (1971)\n—Centre-State relations; Chakravarty Committee (1985)—monetary system. [Q39]\n• Committee-focus pairs: Chakravarty—monetary/financial system; Narasimham—banking-sector reforms; Tendulkar—poverty assessment; Chelliah\n—tax reforms. [Q40]\n• Policy-expert pairs: Taxation—Parthasarathi Shome; Agriculture—Ashok Gulati; Monetary Policy—C. Rangarajan; economic planning/state reforms\n—Arvind Panagariya; Fiscal Policy—M. Govinda Rao. [Q41]\n• Committee-period pairs: 1949-51—Local Finance Enquiry Committee; 1953-54—Taxation Enquiry Commission; 1963-66—Rural-Urban Relationship\nCommittee; 1985-88—National Commission on Urbanisation. [Q42]\n• Important policy-year pairs: National Agriculture Policy—2000; Marine Fisheries Policy—2004; Foreign Trade Policy—2014; Seventh Finance\nCommission—1978. [Q43]\n• City-period associations: Kanchipuram—Ancient; Jaisalmer—Medieval; Kodaikanal—Colonial; Bhilai—Post-Independence. [Q44]\n• C. D. Deshmukh RBI ke first Indian Governor the; unka tenure August 1943-June 1949 raha. [Q45]\n• September 2014 mein Vimal Jalan Expenditure Management Commission ke first part-time Chairman bane; Commission ki formation Budget 2014-\n15 mein announce hui thi. [Q46]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — Q40 Committee Associations\n▸ Committee-reform mapping: Chakravarty—financial-sector reforms; Narasimham—banking-sector reforms; Tendulkar—poverty assessment; Chelliah—tax\nreforms.\n▸ In committee-reform pairs ko exact association ke saath yaad rakho.\nQ Coverage: Q40\n\n\n\n5. Business Houses, Industry Leaders & Economic Personalities\n\n• Older Forbes billionaire data mein Azim Premji aur Dhirubhai Ambani top-50 context se linked the; Forbes 2025 mein Mukesh Ambani 18th, Gautam\nAdani 28th aur Savitri Jindal & family 48th the. [Q47]\n• SEWA (Self-Employed Women's Association) 1972 se registered trade union hai; focus poor self-employed women workers par hai. [Q48]\n• Business-person pairs: Zia Mody—AZB & Partners; Anuradha J. Desai—Venkateshwara Hatcheries; Villoo Morawala-Patell—Avesta Genegre\nTechnologies; Meena Kaushik—Quantum Market Research. [Q49]\n• Suzlon Energy wind-power company hai, jo 1995 mein establish hui. [Q50]\n• TRYSEM aur SJRY employment-related terms hain; TRYSEM = Training of Rural Youth for Self Employment, SJRY = Swarna Jayanti Rojgar\nYojana. OGL ka full form Open General Licence hai. [Q51]\n• December 2004 mein Finance Minister P. Chidambaram ne three-member Investment Commission constitute kiya. Ratan Tata Chairman the;\nDeepak Parekh aur Ashok Ganguly members the. Kumar Mangalam Birla member nahi the. [Q52]\n• Policy decisions ko effectively influence karne wale organized group ko pressure group kehte hain. Inki effectiveness state/government structure,\npolitical culture aur public-politics relationship jaise factors se affect hoti hai. [Q53]\n• Prime Minister's Economic Advisory Council ke Chairman S. Mahendra Dev hain; Dr. C. Rangarajan earlier Chairman reh chuke hain. [Q54]\n• Nicholas Stern British economist aur academic hain; 2000-03 mein World Bank Vice-President rahe aur UK Government ke economic adviser bhi\nrahe. Unhone Kenya aur India ki Green Revolution par bhi work/books ki hain. [Q55]","• National Disaster Management Plan 1 June 2016 ko launch hua; ismein disaster prevention, mitigation, relief aur rescue operations cover hote hain.\n[Q56]\n• Tata Group ne Nano car factory Singur, West Bengal se shift karke Sanand, Gujarat mein set up karne ka decision liya. [Q57]\n• CII ke 2025-26 President Rajiv Memani hain; R. Sheshsayee earlier period mein CII President reh chuke hain. [Q58]\n• 'The Rich Report: 2004' mein Lakshmi Niwas Mittal ko Asia ke richest person ka title diya gaya tha. [Q59]\n• 1998 Nobel Prize in Economics Amartya Sen ko welfare economics mein contributions ke liye mila. [Q60]\n6. Economists, Nobel Prize, Books & Economic Thought\n\n• 2023 Nobel Prize in Economics Claudia Goldin ko mila. 2024 prize Daron Acemoglu, Simon Johnson aur James A. Robinson ko institutions aur\nprosperity par research ke liye mila; 2025 prize Joel Mokyr, Philippe Aghion aur Peter Howitt ko diya gaya. [Q61]\n• Angus Deaton ko 2015 ka Nobel Prize in Economics consumption, poverty aur welfare par unke work ke liye mila. [Q62]\n• 'India's Economic Policy: The Gandhian Blueprint' former Prime Minister Chaudhary Charan Singh ki book hai. [Q63]\n• Economist-book pairs: Gunnar Myrdal—Economic Theory and Underdeveloped Regions; A. O. Hirschman—The Strategy of Economic Development;\nN. Kaldor—Strategic Factors in Economic Development; Adam Smith—The Wealth of Nations. [Q64]\n• 'On the Principles of Political Economy and Taxation' David Ricardo ki book hai, Karl Marx ki nahi. [Q65]\n• Book-author pairs: Inequality Reexamined—Amartya Sen; The Price of Inequality—Joseph E. Stiglitz; Inequality: What Can Be Done—Anthony B.\nAtkinson; The Economics of Inequality—Thomas Piketty. [Q66]\n• Important tribes book-author pairs: The Tribes and Castes of the Central Provinces of India—R. V. Russell & R. B. Hiralal; Tribal Development in\nM.P.—Ajit Raizada; The Bhils—T. B. Naik; The Baiga—Verrier Elwin. [Q67]\n• Concept associations: Price discrimination—land monopoly; Land reforms—land consolidation; Vicious Circle of Poverty—Ragnar Nurkse; IBRD—\nBretton Woods. [Q68]\n• Annie Besant ne 1898 mein Banaras mein Central Hindu College establish kiya; Madan Mohan Malaviya ke efforts se 1916 mein ye Banaras Hindu\nUniversity bana. [Q69]\n\n7. Governance, CAG, Indices & Public Accountability\n\n• Jab India ko foreign banks ke paas gold pledge/keep karna pada, us period mein Chandrashekhar Prime Minister the. [Q70]\n• Jayant Patil Committee drought-affected areas ke development se related thi. Planning Commission member Dr. Jayant Patil iske Chairman the aur\ncommittee ko 25-year perspective plan prepare karna tha. [Q71]\n• Central Vigilance Commission ne 2001 mein 'Citizens Guide to Fighting Corruption' issue ki. [Q72]\n• Government of Rajasthan ne economic reforms par advice ke liye Economic Policy and Reforms Council constitute ki. [Q73]\n• CAG ki reports Parliament ki Public Accounts Committee examine karti hai; committee mein 22 members hote hain—15 Lok Sabha aur 7 Rajya\nSabha se—and report Parliament ko submit karti hai. [Q74]\n• Article 148 ke under CAG ko President appoint karta hai; Union/State accounts ka form CAG ki advice par prescribe hota hai aur CAG reports\nPresident/Governors ke through legislatures ke saamne rakhi jaati hain. CAG public finance ka guardian maana jata hai. [Q75]\n• Index of Government Economic Power (IGEP) 2009 mein India 112 economies mein 5th rank par tha; USA, China, Japan aur Germany 1st se 4th\npositions par the. [Q76]\n• Figure mein Italy aur France ke unemployment rates mein bahut chhota difference dikhaya gaya hai. [Q77]\n• Uttar Pradesh Planning Atlas 2005 ke composite development index mein top districts Gautam Buddha Nagar, Ghaziabad, Meerut, Kanpur Nagar\naur Lucknow the. 2005 CMS study mein 2002 ke comparison mein corruption decline report hua, jisme media coverage ko ek factor bataya gaya.\n[Q78]\n• Transparency International CPI mein India's ranks: 2014—85th, 2012—94th, 2011—95th, 2010—87th. CPI 2025, released February 2026 mein\nDenmark 1st, Finland 2nd aur Singapore 3rd tha. [Q79]\n• National Housing Bank ne residential land-price fluctuations track karne ke liye July 2007 mein RESIDEX launch kiya; pilot phase mein Bengaluru,\nBhopal, Delhi, Kolkata aur Mumbai include the. [Q80]\n• Residential housing land-price fluctuations track karne ke liye National Housing Bank (NHB) ne July 2007 mein RESIDEX index launch kiya. [Q81]\n8. Consumer, Legal, Patent & Rights Framework\n\n• Consumer Protection Act, 1986 consumer education aur protection provide karta hai; weaker sections ko goods/services par price discount dena\niska provision nahi hai. [Q82]\n• Trade and Merchandise Marks Act 1958 mein pass hua; aim goods par fraudulent marks prevent karna aur trademarks ko better\nprotection/registration framework dena tha. [Q83]\n• POCSO law children ko sexual offences se protect karne se related hai; full form Protection of Children from Sexual Offences hai. [Q84]\n• Land Acquisition amendment bill Lok Sabha mein 9 amendments ke saath pass hua; iska full name Right to Fair Compensation and Transparency in\nLand Acquisition, Rehabilitation and Resettlement (Amendment) Bill, 2015 tha. [Q85]\n• Marrakesh Treaty visually impaired aur print-disabled persons ko published works tak access improve karne se linked hai. India ne 30 June 2014 ko\ntreaty ratify ki aur ise ratify karne wala first country bana. [Q86]\n• Biodiversity conservation se linked Acts mein Indian Fisheries Act 1897, Destructive Insects and Pests Act 1914, Forest Act 1927, Agricultural\nProduce (Grading and Marking) Act 1937, Coffee Act 1942, Imports and Exports Control Act 1947, Rubber Act 1947, Tea Act 1953, MMDR Act 1957,\nPrevention of Cruelty to Animals Act 1960, Customs Act 1962 aur Cardamom Act 1965 include hain. [Q87]\n• 1957 mein Government of India ne patent law amendment examine karne aur recommendations dene ke liye Justice N. R. Ayyangar Committee\nappoint ki. [Q88]\n• Patent Second Amendment Act ko Parliament ne 2002 mein approve kiya; 15 March 2024 ko Central Government ne Patents Rules, 2003 amend\nkarke Patents (Amendment) Rules, 2024 notify kiye, jo same date se effective hue. [Q89]\n• World Consumer Rights Day har saal 15 March ko observe kiya jaata hai. [Q90]\n9. Statistics, Institutes, Water & Resource Institutions\n\n• Energy Statistics India annual publication National Statistical Office (MoSPI) publish karta hai; CSO iska predecessor institution tha. [Q91]","• CAG 2000 data mein listed states ka low per-capita kerosene allocation: Orissa 7.56 kg, Bihar 7.86 kg, Madhya Pradesh 8.05 kg, Rajasthan 8.22 kg,\nUttar Pradesh 8.47 kg. [Q92]\n• 'Bahini Darbar' Rewa district ke Dabhaura block se 2008 se publish hone wala handwritten monthly newspaper hai; Bagheli dialect mein women by\nwomen ke liye likha jaata hai. [Q93]\n• 'GHG Emissions of All World Countries 2025' report mein 2024 ke highest-emission countries ka order China, USA, India, Russia aur Indonesia tha.\nEU27 ko ek unit maana jaye to wo China, USA aur India ke baad 4th position par aata hai. [Q94]\n• Indian Institute of Carpet Technology Bhadohi district mein establish hua aur 2001 se functioning start hui. [Q95]\n• Nivesh Mitra Uttar Pradesh Government ka single-window clearance portal hai; industries ko approvals, NOCs aur licences time-bound online mode\nmein provide karta hai. [Q96]\n• V. V. Giri National Labour Institute Noida mein located hai. [Q97]\n• National Institute of Agricultural Economics and Policy Research (former NCAP) New Delhi mein hai; ICAR ne ise 1991 mein establish kiya. [Q98]\n• ICAR-NIAP New Delhi-based agricultural economics aur policy research institute hai; March 1991 mein former NCAP ke roop mein establish hua aur\nICAR ke under operate karta hai. [Q99]\n• P. C. Mahalanobis Indian Statistical Institute ke founder the. [Q100]\n• State Land Use Boards, National Land Use and Conservation Board aur National Land Resources Conservation and Wastelands Development\nCouncil ka main focus land ka proper utilization, cultivable land identify karna aur uska development hai. [Q101]\n• Finance Commission constitutional body hai, jabki Planning Commission non-constitutional body tha. [Q102]\n• Central Marine Fisheries Research Institute 1947 mein Kochi, Kerala mein establish hua; Central Sheep Breeding Farm Hisar, Haryana mein located\nhai. [Q103]\n• Roche Holding A.G. financial-services company nahi; ye global healthcare company hai. [Q104]\n• National Water Development Agency (NWDA) July 1982 mein Societies Registration Act, 1860 ke under autonomous society ke roop mein establish\nhui; 1990 se National Perspective Plan ke Himalayan Rivers Development works bhi NWDA ko diye gaye. [Q105]\n• Union Jal Shakti Minister C. R. Patil ne 31 December 2024 ko Annual Ground Water Quality Report 2024 release ki. [Q106]\n• Entrepreneurship Development Institute of India Ahmedabad, Gujarat mein 1983 mein autonomous non-profit institution ke roop mein establish hua.\n[Q107]\n• National Institute for Entrepreneurship and Small Business Development Noida, U.P. mein located Ministry of Skill Development & Entrepreneurship\nka premier institute hai; entrepreneurship-development institutions ko coordinate/monitor karta hai. [Q108]\n• Indira Gandhi Rashtriya Manav Sangrahalaya Bhopal, Madhya Pradesh mein located hai. [Q109]\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — Institute Location / NWDA Option Mismatch\n▸ Indian Institute of Carpet Technology Bhadohi district mein located hai.\n▸ National Water Development Agency (NWDA) July 1982 mein establish hua.\nQ Coverage: Q95, Q105\n\n\n\n10. Government Campaigns, Digital Platforms & Sectoral Schemes\n\n• Har Ghar Dastak 2.0 campaign 1 June 2022 ko COVID-19 vaccination completion ensure karne ke liye launch hua. [Q110]\n• Swadesh Darshan Scheme Ministry of Tourism ne January 2015 mein launch ki; iska focus theme-based tourist circuits aur pilgrimage destinations\nmein tourism infrastructure develop karna hai. [Q111]\n• PM Narendra Modi ne 24 October 2016 ko Varanasi mein Urja Ganga gas-pipeline project launch kiya; project UP, West Bengal, Bihar, Odisha,\nJharkhand aur Assam ke cities ko piped clean cooking fuel supply se connect karne ke liye tha. [Q112]\n• Korba Super Thermal Power Station, jo ab NTPC ke under hai, 1982-83 mein World Bank assistance se establish hua. [Q113]\n• Bureau of Energy Efficiency ka Standards & Labelling Programme consumers ko energy-saving aur appliance cost-saving potential ki information\ndeta hai; programme 2006 mein start hua. [Q114]\n• DigiLocker Digital India initiative hai jo voter card, driving licence, school certificates jaise documents ke liye digital storage aur continuous access\nprovide karta hai. [Q115]\n• DigiLocker ka basic purpose personal documents/certificates ke liye secure cloud/digital storage provide karna hai. [Q116]\n• November 2015 mein us waqt ke Union Urban Development Minister M. Venkaiah Naidu ne e-Lala online portal launch kiya. [Q117]\n• March 2024 mein Accenture ne California-based edtech platform Udacity acquire kiya; objective clients ke skill-development opportunities expand\nkarna tha. [Q118]\n• Semfex Ministry of Defence ki ex-servicemen ko loan support dene wali scheme hai; Rajasthan mein Rajasthan Financial Corporation (RFC) is\nscheme ke under loans provide karta hai. [Q119]\n• Biodiesel Mission ka nodal ministry Ministry of Rural Development hai; Jatropha seeds se biodiesel production ke experiments Chhattisgarh, Tamil\nNadu aur Andhra Pradesh jaise states mein hue. [Q120]\n• ITC Ltd. ne 2000 mein rural marketing mechanism 'e-Chaupal' start kiya; project ko UN Millennium Development Goals support se linked top 'World\nBusiness Award' bhi mila. [Q121]\n• Visa on Arrival scheme 1 January 2010 ko Japan, Singapore, Finland, Luxembourg aur New Zealand citizens ke liye tourism promote karne ke\npurpose se start hui; 1 January 2011 se Cambodia, Laos, Vietnam aur Philippines bhi add hue. [Q122]\n• Bhakra-Nangal Haryana, Punjab aur Rajasthan ka joint project hai. Sutlej River par Bhakra aur Nangal dams hydropower aur irrigation facilities\nprovide karte hain. [Q123]\n• Gujarat Government ne 2016 mein Surat mein India's first Textile University establish karne ka announcement kiya; aim textile sector ko technology,\nR&D aur skilled-manpower support dena tha. [Q124]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — Swadesh Darshan: All Listed Circuits Included\n▸ Heritage, Sufi, Ramayana aur Coastal—chaaron Swadesh Darshan ke identified theme-based circuits mein included hain.\nQ Coverage: Q111\n\n\n\n11. Development Concepts, Classical Economics & Gandhian Thought","• Economic globalisation expand hone ke saath uski limitations bhi visible hoti hain; isi reaction mein socialism jaise anti-globalisation/alternative ideas\nke supporters bhi badh sakte hain. [Q125]\n• Corruption economic development mein obstacle hai; ye development ko promote nahi karta. [Q126]\n• Coal mines economic infrastructure category mein include nahi hote; remaining infrastructure items include hote hain. [Q127]\n• Supermarket retail organization ka ek form hai jahan different product categories ka wide range available hota hai. [Q128]\n• Fashion/accessory pairs: Beret—cap/hat; Stilettos—women's shoe/sandal; Aviators—sunglasses; Chignon—hairstyle; Brogue—men's shoe. [Q129]\n• Adam Smith philosopher aur economist the; unhe Father of Modern Economics kaha jata hai. [Q130]\n• Classical-school economists generally limited government intervention aur market-based economy ko support karte the; Adam Smith, Ricardo, Mill,\nJ.B. Say, Pigou jaise names is tradition se linked hain. [Q131]\n• Adam Smith ki book 'An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations' 1776 mein publish hui aur classical economics ki foundational\nworks mein count hoti hai. [Q132]\n• Bina Agarwal ko June 2009 mein Leontief Prize recipient announce kiya gaya; wo Institute of Economic Development mein Professor of Economics\nthi. [Q133]\n• Money supply ko nation's wealth ke stock mein directly include nahi kiya jaata. [Q134]\n• 'The Accumulation of Capital' ki author Joan Robinson hain. [Q135]\n• Prisoner's Dilemma game theory ka classic problem hai. Modern game theory ko John von Neumann aur Oskar Morgenstern ke work se strongly\nassociate kiya jata hai. [Q136]\n• F. W. Taylor ko Father of Scientific Management kaha jaata hai. [Q137]\n• Mahatma Gandhi aur Karl Marx dono ultimate stateless/classless society ki baat karte the, lekin us goal tak pahunchne ke unke means alag the.\n[Q138]\n• Business property ki trusteeship principle Mahatma Gandhi ne propound ki; ye corporate governance aur capitalism-Marxism ke beech Gandhian\napproach se related hai. [Q139]\n• Gandhian economics trusteeship ideology par based hai; wealthy persons se expectation hoti hai ki wo resources ko common welfare ke trustee ke\nroop mein use karein. [Q140]\n• Trusteeship concept Mahatma Gandhi se associated hai. [Q141]\n• Gandhian economics trusteeship principle par based hai; Uttar Pradesh Zamindari Abolition and Land Reforms Act, 1950 Constitution ki Ninth\nSchedule ke Entry 11 mein include hua, jisse judicial protection mili. [Q142]\n\n12. Digital Economy, Innovation, Benami Law & Market Concepts\n\n• Prakash Javadekar ne 9 November 2016 ko Smart India Hackathon 2017 inaugurate kiya; aim students ko national problems ke innovative\ntechnology solutions develop karne ke liye challenge karna tha. [Q143]\n• Prohibition of Benami Property Transactions Act, 1988 ko 2016 mein amend kiya gaya; amendment ko Benami Transactions (Prohibition)\nAmendment Act, 2016 kaha gaya. [Q144]\n• June 2016 mein Himachal Pradesh ke then CM Virbhadra Singh ne State Data Centre (SDC) launch kiya; Himachal Pradesh is centre ko start karne\nwala first state tha. [Q145]\n• DACNET web portal e-Agriculture se related Department of Agriculture & Cooperation ka e-governance project hai; NIC ise agriculture services\nonline facilitate karne ke liye implement karta hai. [Q146]\n• Sweden ko world ki first cashless-economy country ke roop mein cite kiya jata hai. [Q147]\n• 'Age earthquake' population mein elderly people ka proportion badhne ko refer karta hai, child-age population ko nahi. [Q148]\n• 'Drishti 2025' food production increase karne se related initiative/vision hai. [Q149]\n• 12th day ki market recovery specifically non-multinational companies se linked dikhayi gayi; other proposed causes ko same recovery attribute karne\nke liye clear evidence nahi tha. [Q150]\n\n13. Climate Policy, Environment & Global Economic Crises\n\n• Carbon markets ko climate change solution ke ek policy tool ke roop mein decades se use/discuss kiya jaata hai. [Q151]\n• Intended Nationally Determined Contributions (INDC) climate change tackle karne ke liye countries ke action plans ko refer karta hai; UNFCCC\ncontext mein ye greenhouse-gas emissions reduce karne se linked hai. [Q152]\n• Domestic Content Requirement (DCR) domestic solar-power manufacturing/development ko support karne se linked concept hai. India ko major\nsolar-energy country banane ke objective se Jawaharlal Nehru National Solar Mission (JNNSM) January 2010 mein launch hua. [Q153]\n• IREDA ko 2024 mein Navratna status mila. IREDA 1987-established, Ministry of New & Renewable Energy ke administrative control wala\nNBFC/public-sector institution hai jo renewable energy aur energy-efficiency projects ko finance/promote karta hai. [Q154]\n• Compensatory Afforestation Fund Act, 2016 ke under CAMPA authorities national aur State dono levels par establish ki gayi hain. [Q155]\n• India mein Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR) e-Waste (Management and Handling) Rules, 2011 ka important feature bana; recycling aur e-\nwaste reduction ki responsibility producers/manufacturers par rakhi gayi. [Q156]\n• 'Strategic choice' women's empowerment ki specific international-policy achievement nahi; sociology/organization theory ka concept hai. [Q157]\n• 2008 subprime lending crisis ne Lehman Brothers ko bankruptcy tak pahunchaya; August 2008 tak global financial institutions ke losses lagbhag\nUS$501 billion tak pahunch gaye the. [Q158]\n• US economy ki subprime crisis domestic real-estate sector ke slowdown se start hui. [Q159]\n• Center for Disaster Philanthropy ki April 2024 report ke mutabik Venezuela long-running socio-political/economic crisis face kar raha tha; 2014 se\n7.72 million se zyada people country leave kar chuke the, making it Latin America's largest displacement/refugee crisis. [Q160]\n• Earlier aur updated data dono mein USA per-person annual domestic waste generation mein world mein first rank par tha. [Q161]\n• World ke water resources ka approx 4% India mein available hai. [Q162]\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — Dated Environment & Crisis Data\n▸ Carbon markets, NDCs, renewable-energy institutions, subprime crisis, Venezuela aur waste/water figures different years ke data hain; dated values ko later\nfigures se replace mat karo.\nQ Coverage: Q151-Q162","14. Power Sector, Electricity Policy & Solar Schemes\n\n• Older hydropower comparisons mein Uttar Pradesh leading State tha; Uttarakhand formation ke baad uske kai major hydro projects Uttarakhand\nmein aa gaye. [Q163]\n• 2023 mein India ne approx 44.6 TWh nuclear electricity generate ki, jo global nuclear-electricity generation ka around 1.71% tha. [Q164]\n• Earlier electricity-generation order Gujarat > Maharashtra > Uttar Pradesh > Tamil Nadu tha; updated National Power Portal data mein Uttar Pradesh\n> Maharashtra > Gujarat > Tamil Nadu order milta hai. [Q165]\n• India ka per-capita electricity consumption 2024-25 mein lagbhag 1,460 kWh tha; earlier data mein roughly 500 kWh figure milta hai. [Q166]\n• Pradhan Mantri Gramodaya Yojana ke five basic services the: primary health care, primary education, rural housing, nutrition aur drinking water.\nRural electrification later supporting service ke roop mein add hui. [Q167]\n• Rural Electrification Policy 23 August 2006 ko notify hui; major objective 2009 tak all rural households ko electricity access dena tha. [Q168]\n• Union Budget 2006-07 ka target March 2007 tak 15,000 MW additional generation capacity tha. CEA ke 30 November 2025 data mein total installed\nelectricity capacity 509,743 MW thi—Central 119,222 MW, State 111,958 MW aur Private 278,563 MW. [Q169]\n• December 2011 mein gross installed power capacity mein renewable share lagbhag 11% tha. 30 November 2025 tak CEA data mein renewable\ncapacity excluding hydropower 203,607 MW thi, yani total 509,743 MW ka 39.9%; hydropower include karne par share 49.8% hota hai. [Q170]\n• National Electricity Policy 2002-07 ka objective electricity ki per-unit price reduce karna nahi tha; baaki listed objectives policy mein include the.\n[Q171]\n• November 2015 mein Ministry of Power ne Ujjwal DISCOM Assurance Yojana (UDAY) launch ki; objective DISCOMs ki financial/operational\nefficiency improve karna aur interest burden, power cost aur AT&C losses reduce karna tha. [Q172]\n• UDAY ka purpose electricity distribution companies ka financial turnaround aur revival tha; Union Cabinet ne 5 November 2015 ko scheme approve\nki aur Ministry of Power ise implement karta hai. [Q173]\n• PM Surya Ghar: Muft Bijli Yojana 13 February 2024 ko launch hui. Scheme residential rooftop solar ko promote karti hai; targeted households ko\nconsumption-linked rooftop capacity/subsidy framework ke through support milta hai. [Q174]\n• PM Surya Ghar scheme ka target one crore residential homes/rooftops par rooftop solar install karna hai; MNRE installation, operation, maintenance\naur repair ke liye grassroots training programmes chalata hai. [Q175]\n• Primary energy natural resources mein conversion se pehle available energy hoti hai; examples coal, crude oil, sunlight, wind, flowing water,\nbiomass/vegetation aur uranium hain. Tidal power primary-energy source category mein aata hai. [Q176]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — Power-sector Figures are Period-specific\n▸ Per-capita electricity use, renewable share, rural-electrification targets aur generation-capacity targets period-specific figures hain; PM Surya Ghar ke later\nscheme facts ko separately read karo.\nQ Coverage: Q163-Q176\n\n\n\n15. Regional Development, Corridors, Roads, Railways & Tourism\n\n• Regional disparity ka matlab developed aur economically backward states/regions ke development levels mein difference hai. Punjab, Maharashtra,\nHaryana, Gujarat, West Bengal, Kerala, Tamil Nadu relatively advanced rahe, jabki MP, Assam, UP, Rajasthan, Odisha, Bihar comparatively\nbackward rahe. [Q177]\n• International North-South Transport Corridor (INSTC) India-Russia-Iran initiative hai. 12 September 2000 agreement ke through Indian\nOcean/Persian Gulf ko Iran, Caspian Sea aur aage Northern Europe se connect karne ka route develop kiya gaya. [Q178]\n• East-West Corridor Silchar (Assam) ko Porbandar (Gujarat) se aur North-South Corridor Srinagar ko Kanyakumari se connect karta hai; dono\ncorridors Jhansi par intersect karte hain. [Q179]\n• Golden Quadrilateral Delhi, Mumbai, Chennai aur Kolkata ko connect karta hai. Section length ka descending order Chennai-Kolkata > Kolkata-Delhi\n> Delhi-Mumbai > Mumbai-Chennai hai; project 2001 mein launch hua. [Q180]\n• Madhya Pradesh ke road-network figures strong hain, lekin road-density mein first-rank claim latest Basic Road Statistics ke saath consistent nahi\nhai. [Q181]\n• India ki first railway line jo PPP model ke under construct ki ja rahi thi, Gujarat mein thi. [Q182]\n• Railway Ministry/PIB ke 10 February 2023 data mein Amrit Bharat Station Scheme ke under 1,275 stations identify kiye gaye the, jisme Uttar\nPradesh ke 149 stations sabse zyada the. 26 July 2024 Parliament data mein total 1,324 stations the, jisme Uttar Pradesh ke 157 stations again\nhighest the. [Q183]\n• Indian tourism ka Golden Triangle Agra, Delhi aur Jaipur se banta hai; map par ye three cities triangle form karte hain. [Q184]\n• Historical period mein world crude-steel production ka more than one-third China se aata tha. World Steel Association 2025 data mein top five order\nChina > India > USA > Japan > Russia tha. [Q185]\n• World Steel Association February 2026 data mein crude-steel production mein China 1st, India 2nd, Japan 3rd aur USA 4th tha. [Q186]\n16. Economic History, Labour & Arthashastra\n\n• Kamaiya Nepal ka hereditary bonded/contract labour system tha jo generations tak continue hua; legal abolition ke baad bhi practice ke traces\ncontinue rahe. [Q187]\n• British colonial period mein India's economy raw-material supplier aur British manufactured goods ke market ke roop mein restructure hui. British\ndominance Bengal mein Battle of Plassey 1757 ke baad expand hui aur colonial rule 15 August 1947 tak raha. [Q188]\n• Dadabhai Naoroji ki 'Poverty and Un-British Rule in India' 1901 mein publish hui; book ne British policies ke through India se wealth drain ko highlight\nkiya. [Q189]\n• Dadabhai Naoroji ne India ki poverty line estimate karne ke liye 'cost of living in prison' concept use kiya. Unhone is approach ko apni book 'Poverty\nand Un-British Rule in India' mein discuss kiya aur poverty measurement ke early development mein major contribution diya. [Q190]\n• Kautilya ka Arthashastra political science/administration ka major treatise hai aur us period ke economic life par bhi important information deta hai.\n[Q191]\n• Kautilya ka Arthashastra total 15 Adhikaranas/Books mein divided hai. [Q192]\n• Vijayanagara Empire mein land-revenue department ko 'Athavana' kaha jaata tha; land categories ke basis par revenue assessment hota tha. [Q193]","17. Telecom, Postal Services & Banking/Industrial Facts\n\n• TRAI Parliament Act ke through 20 February 1997 ko establish hua. Ye statutory body telecom services aur tariffs ko regulate/revise karti hai. [Q194]\n• TRAI Parliament ke Act se 20 February 1997 ko establish hua; telecom services aur tariffs regulate/revise karna iska major role hai. Governing law\nTelecom Regulatory Authority of India Act, 1997 hai. [Q195]\n• India total 9 PIN regions mein divided hai; 8 regions states/UTs ke liye aur 9th region Army Post Office/Field Post Office ke liye hai. [Q196]\n• 31 March 2003 ko India mein 155,618 post offices the; 29 April 2024 tak country mein post offices ki sankhya 155,000 se zyada thi. [Q197]\n• Department of Posts ne Indian postal system mein long-term modernization ke liye April 2008 mein Project Arrow launch kiya. [Q198]\n• Project Arrow ka objective post offices ko new identity aur modern service standards dena tha; scheme April 2008 mein launch hui. [Q199]\n• IDBI ko treasury operations mein efficiency/effectiveness ke liye 'Asian Banking Award, 2001' mila tha. IDBI July 1964 mein establish hua. [Q200]\n• Petroleum Minister S. Jaipal Reddy ne 15 February 2011 ko Panipat, Haryana ka India's largest Naphtha Cracker Plant nation ko dedicate kiya.\n[Q201]\n• One barrel crude oil = 42 US gallons = approx 158.9873 litres, yani roughly 159 litres. [Q202]\n• Non-domestic LPG cylinder mein 19.0 kg LPG hota hai, jabki domestic cylinder mein 14.2 kg LPG hota hai. [Q203]\n• PVR ka full form Priya Village Roadshow hai. Priya Exhibitors aur Village Roadshow partnership ke through first PVR multiplex 1997 mein launch\nhua. [Q204]\n\n18. Quality Marks, GI, Eco Mark & Product Standards\n\n• Food Products Order (FPO) mark processed fruit products ke hygiene/food-safety standards se related certification mark hai; packaged fruit\nbeverages, jams, pickles, squash, dehydrated fruit products etc. par use hota tha. [Q205]\n• AGMARK India mein agricultural/food products ka quality certification mark hai. [Q206]\n• AGMARK agricultural aur food products par applied certification/quality mark hai. [Q207]\n• AGMARK agricultural products ke quality certification/assurance mark ke roop mein use hota hai. [Q208]\n• Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) India's national standards body hai. Organized standard-setting ka institutional base January 1947 mein Indian\nStandards Institution (ISI) ke establishment se start hua. [Q209]\n• ISO 14001 environment-management/pollution-control systems implement karne wali industrial units ke liye international certification standard hai.\n[Q210]\n• Lucknow Chikan, Banarasi Sari, Darjeeling Tea aur Allahabad Surkha Guava sab GI protection/registration paa chuke hain. [Q211]\n• Nizamabad, Azamgarh ki black clay pottery ko GI tag mila hai; iski specialty deep glossy black surface aur engraved silver patterns hain. [Q212]\n• Eco Mark completely environment-friendly Indian products ke liye certification mark hai; ise 1991 mein introduce kiya gaya. [Q213]\n• Bureau of Indian Standards (BIS) environment-friendly Indian products ko Eco Mark deta hai; Eco Mark ka logo earthen pot hai. [Q214]\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — GI Status of Listed Products\n▸ Lucknow Chikan, Banarasi Sari, Darjeeling Tea aur Allahabad Surkha Guava—sabhi GI protection/registration paa chuke hain.\nQ Coverage: Q211\n\n\n\n19. Services, Outsourcing, Aviation & Global Initiatives\n\n• Rajasthan ki geography, environment aur culture ko dekhte hue tourism sector ko sustainable aur inclusive development ke liye policy priority milni\nchahiye. [Q215]\n• Divided State of Bihar ke context mein agriculture main source of wealth tha. [Q216]\n• Outsourcing ka matlab work ko third party se karwana hai. India mein outsourcing growth ke reasons mein communications revolution ke saath USA-\nIndia time-zone difference bhi important raha. [Q217]\n• Packaging products ko safe, attractive aur reliable banane mein important role play karta hai. [Q218]\n• Air India aur Indian Airlines merger ke baad entity ka naam Air India hi raha. Air India ki establishment J. R. D. Tata ne 1932 mein ki thi; ab airline\nTata Group ke ownership mein hai. [Q219]\n• Supreme Court bench—Justice D. K. Jain aur Madan B. Lokur—ne January 2013 order mein airlines ko air fares par transaction fee charge karne se\nprohibit kiya. [Q220]\n• Universal Service Obligation Fund (USOF) telecom companies ki liabilities/obligations adjustment aur universal telecom access se related fund hai.\n[Q221]\n• Vietnam Southeast Asian country hai jahan Communist Party of Vietnam-led one-party political system hai; multi-party system nahi. [Q222]\n• Belt and Road Initiative/One Belt One Road China ka economic-strategic connectivity agenda hai, jiska aim China ko Europe, Africa aur Oceania ke\nroutes/networks se connect karna hai. [Q223]\n• O-SMART Ministry of Earth Sciences ki ocean-services umbrella scheme hai, covering marine technology, observations, modelling, resources,\necosystems, coastal research aur research vessels. 2018 mein 2017-18 to 2019-20 ke liye Rs.1,263 crore approve hua; November 2021 mein 2021-\n26 continuation Rs.2,177 crore ke saath approve hui. [Q224]\n• Yamuna Expressway ko Taj Expressway bhi kaha jata hai; ye Greater Noida aur Agra ko connect karta hai. [Q225]\n• Greenfield Airports Policy 2008 new greenfield airports develop karne ke liye banayi gayi. Government ne 21 airports ko in-principle approval diya,\nincluding Mopa, Navi Mumbai, Shirdi, Sindhudurg, Kalaburagi, Vijayapura, Hassan, Shivamogga, Jewar, Dholera, Rajkot, Bhogapuram, Pakyong,\nKannur aur Donyi Polo. [Q226]\n                                        Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · Miscellaneous Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0464","t":"17 India : Population","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["INDIA : POPULATION — FULL MIND MAP\n                                   Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\n📑 Topics in this chapter:\n1. Population Growth, Demographic Transition & Theories\n2. Census History, Census 2011 & National Population Register\n3. Population-Growth Phases & Demographic Divide\n4. Population Size, State Rankings & Distribution\n5. Decadal Growth & Population Characteristics\n6. Population Density & Spatial Distribution\n7. Literacy — India, States, Districts & Trends\n8. Sex Ratio, Child Sex Ratio & Gender Indicators\n9. Scheduled Castes, Scheduled Tribes & Tribal Demography\n10. National Population Policy, TFR & Fertility\n11. Death Rate, Birth Registration, IMR & Child Health\n12. Age Structure, Dependency & Demographic Dividend\n13. Religion, Household Size & Literacy Mission\n14. Population Pressure, Environment & Resource Regions\n\n 1. Population Growth, Demographic Transition & Theories\n • Natural population growth births aur deaths ke difference se aata hai; growth rate broadly crude birth rate minus crude death rate se express ki ja\n sakti hai. Demographic Transition Theory long-term population change ko high birth-high death stage se low birth-low death stage tak explain karti hai;\n Warren Thompson ne 1929 mein framework diya aur Frank Notestein ne later stages ko develop kiya. [Q1-Q4]\n • Stable population structure mein birth rate aur death rate dono low aur controlled hote hain. India ka population growth high raha hai lekin trend\n declining hai; historical demographic-transition context mein death rate birth rate se faster decline karne ki wajah se population rapidly increase hui.\n [Q5-Q7]\n • Malthus ke hisaab se population geometric progression mein aur food supply arithmetic progression mein badhti hai. Population checks\n positive/natural aur preventive dono ho sakte hain; Malthus moral restraint ko prefer karta tha. Theory mapping: Optimum Population—Edwin Cannan;\n Social Disequilibrium—Henry George; Demographic Transition—Thompson; Population-Food Supply—Malthus. [Q8-Q12]\n • Rapid population growth resource consumption, waste aur pollution ke through environmental pressure badha sakta hai. Demographic\n characteristics mein density, sex ratio, rural-urban distribution, literacy, age structure aur life expectancy aate hain; standard of living demographic\n characteristic nahi hai. High density industrial/metropolitan regions se strongly associated hoti hai; sterilization reluctance mein son preference, high\n infant mortality, low awareness aur poverty jaise factors important rahe hain. [Q13-Q16]\n\n 2. Census History, Census 2011 & National Population Register\n • India mein first non-synchronous Census 1872 mein Lord Mayo ke tenure mein complete hua; first regular, scientifically synchronized decennial\n Census 1881 mein Lord Ripon ke tenure mein hua. Iske baad Census generally every ten years conduct hota raha. [Q17-Q22]\n • Census decennial exercise hai aur India ki population generally continuously badhi; 1921 exceptional Census year tha jahan decline record hua.\n Census 2011 India ka 15th national Census tha aur motto 'Our Census, Our Future' tha. NPR process mein photograph, fingerprints aur iris imaging\n ke liye minimum age 15 years rakhi gayi; caste inclusion issue par GoM Pranab Mukherjee ki chairmanship mein bana. [Q23-Q26]\n • Census 2011 mein India ke 640 districts the; administrative reorganisation ke saath district count later badhta raha. [Q27]\n ⚠ EXAM TRAP — Census 1872 vs 1881\n ▸ 1872 Lord Mayo ke tenure mein first non-synchronous Census complete hua; 1881 Lord Ripon ke tenure mein first regular, synchronized decennial Census\n hua.\n ▸ 2021 Census COVID-19 ke karan delay hua; future Census schedule separate current-update fact hai; isliye historical first-Census fact ke saath current\n schedule mix na karein.\n Q Coverage: Q17-Q22\n\n\n 3. Population-Growth Phases & Demographic Divide\n • 20th-century population-growth phases: 1901-21 slow/stagnant; 1921-51 steady growth; 1951-81 rapid growth; 1981 onward high growth ke saath\n clear slowing trend. 1911-21 mein decadal growth -0.31% thi, jo minimum/negative decade tha. [Q28-Q31]\n • Census 2011 final figures mein 2001-11 decadal population growth 17.7% thi. Highest decadal percentage growth 1961-71 mein 24.80% record hui;\n 1971-81 24.66%, 1981-91 23.87%, 1991-2001 21.54% aur 2001-11 17.7% raha. [Q32-Q37]\n • 1921 ko India's demographic history ka 'Year of Great Divide' kaha jata hai, kyunki 1911-21 mein population growth negative thi aur uske baad\n continuous growth phase start hua. 1951 rapid-growth era ka important starting point hai, lekin Great Divide 1921 hi hai. [Q38-Q43]\n • Annual exponential growth aur density dono long-term mein sharply badhe, lekin population-growth rate recent decades mein slow hui. Historical\n statements jaise India ka world-population share, China ko overtake karne ki expectation aur poverty share ko unke specific year/context ke saath\n read karna chahiye. [Q44-Q47]\n\n ⚠ EXAM TRAP — Great Divide = 1921, Not 1951\n ▸ 1911-21 decadal growth -0.31% thi; isi break ke baad continuous population growth start hui, isliye 1921 Year of Great Divide hai.\n ▸ 1951 se rapid-growth phase strong hua, lekin demographic Great Divide 1921 hi rehta hai.\n Q Coverage: Q38-Q43\n\n\n 4. Population Size, State Rankings & Distribution\n • India ki population Census 1991 mein about 846.42 million, Census 2001 mein 1,028.74 million aur Census 2011 mein 1,210.85 million thi. India\n world land area ka around 2.4% rakhta hai lekin world population ka much larger share hold karta hai; Census 2011 provisional data mein female\n share roughly 48% ke around thi. [Q48-Q52]\n • Uttar Pradesh Census 2001 aur 2011 dono mein India ka most populous State raha. Census 2011 mein UP ka India population share about 16.5%\n tha; Maharashtra second, Bihar third aur West Bengal fourth large-population States the. [Q53-Q60]\n • Census 2011 population order broadly Uttar Pradesh > Maharashtra > Bihar > West Bengal > undivided Andhra Pradesh tha. Uttar Pradesh,\n Maharashtra aur Bihar milkar India ki population ka one-third se zyada share rakhte the. [Q61-Q68]\n • Maharashtra area ke hisaab se Rajasthan aur Madhya Pradesh ke baad third-largest State hai aur population mein second tha. Bihar ka Census\n 2011 population share roughly 8.6-9% tha. State/UT rankings mein year aur pre/post reorganisation context ko saath rakhna zaroori hai. [Q69-Q76]","• Small-population end par Sikkim least-populous State aur Lakshadweep least-populous Union Territory tha. Census 2011 state/UT comparisons\nmein Delhi high density aur Lakshadweep very small absolute population ke liye notable rahe. [Q77-Q83]\n\n5. Decadal Growth & Population Characteristics\n• Census 2011 mein States ke among highest decadal growth Meghalaya 27.9% thi; Bihar 25.4%, Arunachal Pradesh 26.0%, Rajasthan 21.3%,\nMadhya Pradesh 20.3% aur Uttar Pradesh 20.2% jaise figures rahe. 2001 Census context mein Nagaland ka growth rate exceptionally high tha. [Q84-\nQ92]\n• Census 2011 mein lowest State growth Nagaland -0.6% thi; Kerala 4.9%, Goa 8.2%, Andhra Pradesh 11.0% aur Sikkim 12.9% low-growth States\nmein the. Bihar listed BIMARU States mein high growth ke liye notable tha. [Q93-Q100]\n• UP ka 2001-11 final decadal growth 20.2% tha. Uttarakhand ke Pauri Garhwal aur Almora districts ne negative growth record ki. Kerala ka low\ngrowth high literacy, health outcomes aur lower fertility jaise demographic-transition factors se associated hai. [Q101-Q109]\n• Population size aur density ko mix nahi karna chahiye: UP most populous State tha, jabki Census 2011 density mein Bihar highest tha. Large-district\npopulation examples mein Allahabad/Prayagraj, Moradabad, Ghaziabad aur Lucknow high-population districts the. [Q110-Q119]\n\n6. Population Density & Spatial Distribution\n• Census 2011 State-density extremes: Bihar 1,106 persons/sq km highest; West Bengal 1,028, Kerala 860, Uttar Pradesh 829 aur Haryana 573 next\nhigh-density States the. Arunachal Pradesh 17 persons/sq km ke saath least-dense State tha. [Q120-Q126]\n• Density rankings Census year ke saath change hui: Census 2001 mein West Bengal 903 ke saath highest State tha, Census 2011 mein Bihar 1,106\nke saath top ho gaya. BIMARU group mein Bihar ki density highest thi; Bihar > Punjab > Jharkhand > Uttarakhand jaise listed orders Census 2011\ndata se bante hain. [Q127-Q136]\n• Low-density States Census 2011: Arunachal Pradesh 17, Mizoram 52, Sikkim 86, Nagaland 119 aur Himachal Pradesh 123. North-Eastern States ke\ndensity comparisons mein census-year context important hai. [Q137-Q143]\n• Union Territories mein Census 2011 density: Puducherry 2,547, Daman & Diu 2,191, Lakshadweep 2,149, Dadra & Nagar Haveli 700 aur Andaman\n& Nicobar Islands 46 persons/sq km. Andaman & Nicobar listed UTs mein lowest tha. [Q144-Q149]\n• India ki density 2001 mein about 324/325 aur 2011 mein 382 persons/sq km thi. Bihar ke districts mein Sheohar 1,880, Patna 1,823, Darbhanga\n1,728 aur Vaishali 1,717 persons/sq km high-density examples the. Effective literacy rate India mein age 7+ population ke liye calculate hoti hai.\n[Q150-Q155]\n\n7. Literacy — India, States, Districts & Trends\n• Census 2011 final literacy rate 73.0% thi; male 80.9% aur female 64.6%. Provisional overall figure 74.04% thi. Census 2001 literacy 64.8% thi; 1991-\n2001 mein literacy about 12.6 percentage points badhi. Kerala ne full literacy claim ki aur 2011 final literacy 94.0% thi;\nSerchhip/Aizawl/Mahe/Kottayam/Pathanamthitta top-literate districts mein the. [Q156-Q163]\n• Census 2011 State literacy leaders: Kerala 94.0%, Mizoram 91.3%, Goa 88.7%, Tripura 87.2%, Himachal Pradesh 82.8%. Mizoram overall literacy\naur urbanization dono mein very high rank rakhta hai. Historical 2001/1991 rankings ko same census context ke saath compare karna chahiye. [Q164-\nQ175]\n• Low-literacy States Census 2011: Bihar 61.8%, Arunachal Pradesh 65.4%, Rajasthan 66.1%, Jharkhand 66.4%, Andhra Pradesh 67.0. Male-female\nliteracy gap national level par roughly 16 percentage points tha; Meghalaya low gender-gap States mein aur some northern States high-gap group\nmein the. [Q176-Q185]\n• Female literacy mein Kerala highest State (92.1% final 2011) aur Mizoram second; Bihar female literacy roughly 51.5% ke saath lowest State level\npar tha. Union Territories mein Lakshadweep high overall/female literacy ke liye notable tha. [Q186-Q197]\n• Overall lowest State literacy Bihar thi. Union-Territory literacy rankings aur rural literacy ko Census year ke saath read karein; 1991/2001/2011\ncomparisons mein ranking change ho sakti hai. [Q198-Q205]\n• Uttar Pradesh Census 2011 literacy 67.7% thi; male 77.3% aur female 57.2%. Most-literate districts mein Gautam Buddha Nagar 80.1%, Kanpur\nNagar 79.7%, Auraiya 78.9% aur Etawah 78.4% the. Shravasti lowest overall literacy aur lowest female literacy districts mein tha. [Q206-Q215]\n• India ka sex ratio long-term mein fluctuate hua: 1901 mein 972, 1991 mein 927, 2001 mein 933 aur Census 2011 final 943 females per 1,000 males.\nLargest listed decline 1961-71 decade mein hua. [Q216-Q219]\n\n8. Sex Ratio, Child Sex Ratio & Gender Indicators\n• Sex ratio Census 1951 mein 946, 1991 mein 927, 2001 mein 933 aur 2011 final mein 943 tha; 2011 provisional figure 940 thi. Census 2011 listed\norder Kerala > Puducherry > Tamil Nadu > Andhra Pradesh tha. [Q220-Q228]\n• Kerala Census 2011 mein 1,084 females per 1,000 males ke saath highest State sex ratio rakhta tha; Puducherry 1,037 ke saath 1,000 se above UT\ntha. Tamil Nadu 996, Andhra Pradesh 993, Karnataka 973 jaise relatively high figures the; Jharkhand 949 national average se slightly above tha.\n[Q229-Q238]\n• Low-sex-ratio States Census 2011 mein Haryana 879 lowest tha; Jammu & Kashmir 889, Sikkim 890, Punjab 895 aur Uttar Pradesh 912. Daman &\nDiu 618 ke saath UTs mein extremely low sex ratio rakhta tha. [Q239-Q247]\n• 1991-2001 mein Daman & Diu ka sex ratio sharply 960 se 710 tak gira, jabki Arunachal Pradesh aur Uttarakhand jaise States mein improvement hui.\nUP sex ratio 2001 ke 898 se 2011 final 912 hua. Bihar districts mein Gopalganj 1,021 high sex-ratio district tha. [Q248-Q258]\n• District-level extremes Census 2011 mein Mahe (Puducherry) about 1,184 highest aur Daman about 534 lowest cited figure tha. Low sex ratio ke\ncauses social preferences ke saath biological birth pattern se bhi linked ho sakte hain. [Q259-Q260]\n• Child sex ratio (0-6) India mein long decline dikhata hai: 1961 mein 976, 2001 mein 927 aur 2011 final mein 919. Census 2011 child sex ratio\nexamples: Chhattisgarh 969, Bihar 935, UP 902, Rajasthan 888, Punjab 846, Haryana 834; rural overall sex ratio urban se higher tha. [Q261-Q268]\n\n9. Scheduled Castes, Scheduled Tribes & Tribal Demography\n• Census 2011 mein Scheduled Caste share Punjab 31.9% ke saath highest State tha; UP ka absolute SC population sabse bada tha, followed by\nWest Bengal, Bihar, Tamil Nadu aur Andhra Pradesh. North-East ke kuch States, jaise Nagaland/Arunachal Pradesh, mein SC population nil ya\nextremely small thi. [Q269-Q274]\n• Scheduled Tribes Census 2011 mein India ki total population ka 8.6% the. ST-share leaders mein Mizoram 94.4%, Nagaland 86.5%, Meghalaya\n86.1% aur UT Lakshadweep 94.8% tha. Absolute ST population Madhya Pradesh mein largest thi, followed by Maharashtra/Odisha/Rajasthan in\nCensus 2011 comparisons. [Q275-Q282]\n• UP ka ST share 0.6% ke around very low tha. Bhil Census 2011 mein largest Scheduled Tribe aur Gond second-largest major tribal group tha; PVTG\ncategory earlier Primitive Tribal Groups ke naam se known thi aur 2009 se Particularly Vulnerable Tribal Groups term use hua. Punjab ka SC share\naur Kerala sex ratio jaise State indicators ko mix nahi karna chahiye. [Q283-Q290]","10. National Population Policy, TFR & Fertility\n• National Population Policy 2000 February 2000 mein announce hui. Immediate objective contraception/health infrastructure needs meet karna;\nmedium-term goal replacement-level TFR 2.1 achieve karna; long-term goal sustainable population stabilization tha. Policy ne 2010 ke liye socio-\ndemographic goals set kiye aur original stabilization horizon 2045 diya gaya. [Q291-Q298]\n• Total Fertility Rate average children per woman ko measure karta hai; replacement-level fertility about 2.1 hai. India TFR NFHS-3 mein 2.7, NFHS-4\nmein 2.2 aur NFHS-5 (2019-21) mein 2.0 tak decline hui; SRS Statistical Report 2023 figure mein TFR 1.9, rural 2.1 aur urban 1.5 diya gaya. [Q299-\nQ304]\n• State TFR comparisons period ke saath change hote hain: NFHS-5 mein Bihar 3.0 aur Meghalaya 2.9 high the; SRS Statistical Report 2023 data\nmein Bihar 2.8, UP 2.6, MP 2.4, Rajasthan 2.3 aur Chhattisgarh 2.2 high side par the. Couple-protection rate younger couples mein increase hona\nbirth-rate reduction ke liye effective hai; working women ki fertility generally lower hoti hai. [Q305-Q309]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — NPP 2000 & Replacement-Level Fertility\n▸ NPP 2000 ka medium-term target replacement-level TFR 2.1 achieve karna tha; long-term goal population stabilization tha.\n▸ TFR data survey/year ke saath update hota hai—NFHS-5 2.0 aur SRS Statistical Report 2023 figure 1.9 hai; NPP target ko current observed TFR se mix na\nkarein.\nQ Coverage: Q291-Q304\n\n\n11. Death Rate, Birth Registration, IMR & Child Health\n• Population stabilization mein births aur deaths balance hote hain; migration include karne par births + in-migration = deaths + out-migration. SRS\nStatistical Report 2023 data mein India crude death rate 6.4 per 1,000 tha; State rates period ke saath vary karte hain. [Q310-Q313]\n• Infant Mortality Rate one year se pehle hone wali infant deaths per 1,000 live births hai. SRS Statistical Report 2023 update mein India IMR 25 tha;\nChhattisgarh, Madhya Pradesh aur Uttar Pradesh 37 ke saath high side par the. Birth Registration Act framework mein birth ko concerned authority ke\nsaath 21 days ke andar register karna hota hai. [Q314-Q320]\n• Historical IMR State comparisons mein Kerala/Goa low aur MP/UP/Odisha high rahe; later SRS data mein improvement hua. Eleventh Plan IMR\ntarget 28 aur Twelfth Plan target 25 per 1,000 live births tha. Child malnutrition, gender differences in infant mortality aur Youth in India reports ko\nyear-specific data ke saath read karna chahiye. [Q321-Q329]\n\n12. Age Structure, Dependency & Demographic Dividend\n• Census 2011 mein young population share very large tha; below-14 population roughly 29% aur below-35 population final figures mein about 65.6%\nthi. Working-age population ka share rising demographic structure India ke demographic transition ka important feature hai. [Q330-Q334]\n• Dependency ratio dependent population ko working-age population se compare karta hai. Indian context mein working age often 15-59 aur\ninternational convention mein 15-64 liya jata hai; children aur elderly dependants hote hain. Old-age dependency ratio long term mein rise kar raha\nhai, jabki overall dependency ratio demographic transition ke dauran decline hua. [Q335-Q336]\n• Demographic dividend tab milta hai jab working-age share dependants ke comparison mein high ho. Is potential ko economic growth mein convert\nkarne ke liye skill development, employment aur productivity essential hain. Population growth working population badha sakta hai, lekin land\nfragmentation, unemployment aur per-capita resource pressure adverse effects ho sakte hain. [Q337-Q344]\n• Census 2011 mein persons with disabilities about 2.1% population the. March 2018 Union Ministry data mein India mein 413,670 beggars count hue;\nWest Bengal highest aur Uttar Pradesh second-highest absolute count tha. [Q345-Q346]\n\n13. Religion, Household Size & Literacy Mission\n• Census 2011 religion data mein Christians about 27.8 million (2.3%) the; listed smaller communities ka population order Jain < Buddhist < Sikh <\nChristian tha. Muslim absolute population Uttar Pradesh mein highest thi; Christian population share Nagaland, Mizoram aur Meghalaya mein very\nhigh tha; Buddhist absolute population Maharashtra mein largest thi. [Q347-Q352]\n• Religion-wise sex ratio Census 2011 comparisons mein Christians 1,023, Buddhists 965, Jains 954 aur Muslims 951; Sikh community 903 ke saath\nlowest religious-group sex ratio cited thi. Jain community highest literacy religious groups mein tha. [Q353-Q355]\n• Household-size Census 2001/2011 comparisons mein Uttar Pradesh ka average household size relatively high aur Tamil Nadu low tha. Census\nnormal, institutional aur houseless households ko distinguish karta hai. National Literacy Mission 5 May 1988 ko launch hua; Andaman tribes ko\nNegrito affinities ke context mein discuss kiya gaya. [Q356-Q358]\n\n14. Population Pressure, Environment & Resource Regions\n• Rapid population growth food-production pressure, pesticide/fertilizer use, settlement expansion aur habitat loss ke through birds/wildlife ko affect kar\nsakta hai. Floods, pollution aur cultivable-land pressure population growth se intensify ho sakte hain; wildlife increase iska expected consequence nahi\nhai. [Q359-Q360]\n• Ganga Plain India aur world ke densely populated regions mein hai. Progressive population-resource regions ke examples mein West Bengal delta,\nMaharashtra-Gujarat Deccan Trap, Tamil Nadu, Punjab Plain/Ganga-Yamuna Doab aur south-eastern Karnataka plateau shamil hain. [Q361-Q362]\n                                      Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · India Population Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0465","t":"18 India : Urbanization","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["INDIA : URBANIZATION — FULL MIND MAP\n                                   Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\n📑 Topics in this chapter:\n1. Urbanization Basics, Causes & India’s Urban Transition\n2. Census Urban Definition, Urban Growth & Urban Share\n3. State/UT-wise Urban Population & Urbanization Rankings\n4. Rural–Urban Population, Children & Occupational Structure\n5. Town Classes, Million-Plus Cities & Urban Agglomerations\n6. Metropolitan Patterns, Migration & Urban Corridors\n7. Urban Problems, Slums & Urban Institutions\n8. Urban Infrastructure, JNNURM, Smart Cities & Committees\n\n  1. Urbanization Basics, Causes & India’s Urban Transition\n • Town aur village social values, family structure, lifestyle aur economic activities mein differ karte hain. Rural community relatively homogeneous aur\n primary activities oriented hoti hai, jabki urban life diversity, secondary/tertiary activities aur complex social structure se associated hai. [Q1-Q5]\n • Urbanization ke major drivers rural-to-urban migration, industrialization, education/employment opportunities aur urban services hain. India ka\n urbanization level developed/highly urbanized countries se lower hai; Census 2001 urban share 27.8% aur Census 2011 final share about 31.1% tha.\n Infrastructure urban growth ke proportional na bade to housing, water, sanitation, transport aur pollution problems intensify hote hain. [Q6-Q12]\n • India ki absolute urban population developed countries ke total population se bhi large ho sakti hai, lekin urbanization percentage comparatively\n lower rehta hai. 2011 mein urban population about 377.1 million thi. Urbanization birth rate reduce karne, organized services/consumption access aur\n secondary-tertiary employment expand karne mein role play karta hai; regional pattern uniform nahi hai. [Q13-Q20]\n\n  2. Census Urban Definition, Urban Growth & Urban Share\n • First Census 1872 aur regular decennial Census 1881 se linked hai. India mein 1931-61 ko moderate urbanization phase kaha gaya; urban\n population growth-rate 1971-81 decade mein about 46.1% ke saath peak par thi, while 2001-11 growth about 31.8% thi. [Q21-Q23]\n • Census Town ke three core criteria: minimum population 5,000; at least 75% male main workers non-agricultural work mein; population density\n minimum 400 persons/sq km. Statutory towns municipality/corporation/cantonment/notified-town status se urban count hote hain. Census 2011 final\n Census Towns 3,892 the, up from 1,362 in 2001. [Q24-Q26]\n • Census 2011 final India population 1.21085 billion thi: rural 833.75 million (68.9%) aur urban 377.11 million (31.1%). Provisional urban share 31.16%\n thi; absolute urban population roughly 37.7 crore thi. [Q27-Q31]\n\n ⚠ EXAM TRAP — Census Town Definition\n ▸ Census Town: population >=5,000; >=75% male main workers non-agricultural work mein; density >=400 persons/sq km.\n ▸ Municipality/corporation/cantonment/notified town statutory status se settlement urban count hota hai; 10 sq km minimum-area condition Census Town\n criterion nahi hai.\n Q Coverage: Q24-Q26\n\n\n  3. State/UT-wise Urban Population & Urbanization Rankings\n • Religious groups, States aur UTs ka urban share widely vary karta hai. Maharashtra aur Uttar Pradesh absolute urban-population leaders mein rahe;\n South/West India ke several States higher urbanization percentages show karte hain, jabki small North-Eastern States ka absolute urban population\n low ho sakta hai. [Q32-Q39]\n • Census 2011 mein Goa 62.2% ke saath most urbanized State tha; Mizoram, Tamil Nadu, Kerala aur Maharashtra high-urbanization group mein the.\n Maharashtra ka absolute urban population largest tha. State aur Union Territory category alag rakhna zaroori hai—Delhi/Chandigarh high-urbanization\n UTs hain, States nahi. [Q40-Q50]\n • Census 2011 State comparisons mein Tamil Nadu, Maharashtra, Gujarat, Kerala aur West Bengal relatively high urban shares dikhate hain;\n Himachal Pradesh around 10% ke saath least-urbanized State tha. Landlocked-State comparisons aur 2001-vs-2011 rankings ko census-year ke\n saath read karein. [Q51-Q60]\n • District/UT comparisons mein highly urban districts and UTs separate category hain. Chandigarh/Delhi/Daman-Diu/Lakshadweep jaise UTs ka\n urbanization level high ho sakta hai; village-count aur urban-share facts UT-specific hain. [Q61-Q64]\n\n ⚠ EXAM TRAP — State vs Union Territory Urbanization\n ▸ Census 2011 States mein Goa 62.2% ke saath most urbanized tha; Maharashtra absolute urban population mein leader tha.\n ▸ Delhi/Chandigarh jaise Union Territories ko State ranking ke saath mix na karein; State-vs-UT category answer change kar sakti hai.\n Q Coverage: Q40-Q50\n\n\n  4. Rural–Urban Population, Children & Occupational Structure\n • Census 2011 final rural share 68.9% aur urban share 31.1% tha; provisional figures 68.84/31.16 ke around the. Census 2001 rural share about\n 72.2% aur urban share 27.8% tha. Independence ke baad first time 2001-11 decade mein absolute population increase urban areas mein rural areas\n se higher hua. [Q65-Q66]\n • Rural aur urban population State-wise composition sharply differ karti hai. Children, sex ratio, literacy aur occupational structure bhi rural-urban\n transition se linked hain; rural workforce mein primary-sector employment ka share high, urban workforce mein secondary/tertiary activities ka share\n higher hota hai. [Q67-Q79]\n\n ⚠ EXAM TRAP — Rural vs Urban Share — Census 2011\n ▸ Census 2011 final share: rural 68.9%, urban 31.1%; provisional figures around 68.84% and 31.16% the.\n ▸ Census 2001 rural share about 72.2% thi. Final, provisional aur older Census figures ko ek hi value samajhkar mix na karein.\n Q Coverage: Q65-Q66\n\n\n  5. Town Classes, Million-Plus Cities & Urban Agglomerations\n • Census urban settlements population size ke basis par Class I, II, III etc. categories mein divide hote hain; Class I towns 100,000+ population rakhte\n hain. Urban agglomeration contiguous urban spread hota hai jo core town/city aur adjoining outgrowths ko include kar sakta hai. [Q80-Q89]\n • Million-plus urban agglomerations ki number 2001 mein 35 se badhkar 2011 mein 53 hui. Mumbai, Delhi, Kolkata, Chennai, Bengaluru, Hyderabad,\n Ahmedabad jaise major metropolitan centres high-population urban agglomerations rahe. [Q90-Q99]","• UP ke million-plus cities Census 2011 mein Kanpur, Lucknow, Ghaziabad, Agra, Varanasi, Meerut aur Allahabad/Prayagraj the. Listed 2011 urban-\npopulation comparisons mein Lucknow > Agra > Meerut > Allahabad; Ghaziabad major urban agglomeration tha. Kota 53 million-plus UAs list ke\nlower end par tha. [Q100-Q109]\n\n6. Metropolitan Patterns, Migration & Urban Corridors\n• Metropolitanization large urban centres mein population/economic activity concentration ko reflect karti hai. Philip M. Hauser ne population\nconcentration ko Population Implosion, ethnic mixing ko Displosion aur rapid population increase ko Explosion jaise terms se distinguish kiya. [Q110-\nQ116]\n• Lee migration theory push aur pull factors use karti hai. Rural-to-urban migration ko employment, education, health aur better urban facilities pull\nkarte hain; rural problems push kar sakti hain. India mein rural-to-rural migration overall largest stream raha, women rural-rural migration mein high\nshare rakhti hain, aur Delhi ko major migrants UP se mile. [Q117-Q123]\n• Urban corridors transport-network expansion ke saath urban activities ke linear/connected growth ko describe karte hain. Rapid\nurbanization/industrialization social dislocation, relative deprivation aur crime/family-value stress bhi create kar sakte hain. [Q124-Q126]\n\n7. Urban Problems, Slums & Urban Institutions\n• Rapid urban population concentration peri-urban settlements badhata hai aur service quality par pressure dalta hai. Rural migrants low-skill jobs aur\ninadequate housing ki wajah se urban poverty mein disproportionately represented ho sakte hain. [Q127-Q129]\n• Census 2011 mein Greater Mumbai slum-household share about 41.3% tha; India ki total slum population about 65.5 million, urban population ka\naround 17.4% thi. Slum-population share mein Maharashtra, Andhra Pradesh, West Bengal, Uttar Pradesh aur Tamil Nadu major contributors the;\nslum-reporting towns ki number Tamil Nadu mein highest thi. [Q130-Q133]\n• India Urban Observatory New Delhi mein Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs ke under located hai. India Sanitation Coalition 2015 mein FICCI\naegis ke under launch hui; NIUA 1976 mein establish national urban-planning/development platform hai. [Q134-Q135]\n\n8. Urban Infrastructure, JNNURM, Smart Cities & Committees\n• Urban infrastructure mein drinking water, sanitation, transport, sewerage, solid-waste systems aur related basic services major components hain.\nSwachh Bharat Mission, HRIDAY aur Smart Cities Mission urban-infrastructure improvement se related programmes hain; Digital India broader public-\nservice digitisation programme hai. [Q136-Q137]\n• Jawaharlal Nehru National Urban Renewal Mission (JNNURM) 2005 mein launch hua. Focus urban infrastructure/governance aur Basic Services to\nUrban Poor par tha; water, sanitation, sewerage, solid waste, roads/urban transport aur slum development key components the. Original seven-year\nperiod March 2012 tak tha, later ongoing projects ke liye extensions hui. [Q138-Q142]\n• Smart Cities Mission ka aim technology/data based smart solutions, local-area development, better infrastructure, economic growth aur quality of life\nimprove karna tha; first phase mein 100 smart cities target the. Urban-policy committee chronology: Local Finance Enquiry Committee 1949-51;\nTaxation Enquiry Commission 1953-54; Rural-Urban Relationship Committee 1963-66; National Commission on Urbanization 1985-88. [Q143-Q145]\n                                     Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · India Urbanization Full Mind Map"]},{"f":"./Crux-Tricks/viewer.html?id=ct0466","t":"19 World : Population & Urbanization","b":"Crux & Tricks / Ghatnachakra / Economics","x":["WORLD : POPULATION & URBANIZATION — FULL MIND MAP\n                                    Topic-wise · Fully Explained · Hinglish · SSC / BPSC / BSSC / Railway\n\n\n📑 Topics in this chapter:\n1. World Population History, Demography & World Population Day\n2. Population Distribution, Country Rankings & Global Shares\n3. Population Growth, Density, Sex Ratio, Religion & Literacy\n4. World Urbanization Levels, Regions & Countries\n5. Megacities, Technopolis & Urbanization Theory\n6. Migration, Fertility, Birth Rate, Mortality & Life Expectancy\n7. Family Planning & Biodiversity\n\n  1. World Population History, Demography & World Population Day\n • 10,000 BC world population ka single exact estimate nahi hai—different scholars ne around 1-10 million range ke estimates diye. Demography\n population size, distribution, composition, births, deaths, migration aur social mobility ka quantitative + qualitative study hai. Robert Redfield ne Folk-\n Urban Continuum Mexico ke community studies se develop kiya. [Q1-Q5]\n • UNFPA State of World Population report publish karta hai. World population 7 billion mark 31 October 2011 se associated hai; World Population Day\n 11 July ko observe hota hai, 1987 ke Five Billion Day ki memory mein. Themes year-specific hote hain—2015 Vulnerable Populations in Emergencies,\n 2017 Family Planning: Empowering People, 2024 To Leave No One Behind, Count Everyone, aur 2025 youth/family-choice empowerment theme.\n [Q6-Q11]\n\n ⚠ EXAM TRAP — 10,000 BC World Population — No Single Exact Estimate\n ▸ Different scholars ne 10,000 BC world population ke liye different estimates diye: roughly 2 million, 4 million aur 1-10 million range.\n ▸ Isliye is ancient-population fact ko ek single exact universally accepted number ke roop mein memorize nahi karna chahiye.\n Q Coverage: Q1\n\n\n  2. Population Distribution, Country Rankings & Global Shares\n • World population ka large concentration Northern Hemisphere aur 20°N-40°N belt mein hai. India Census 2011 context mein world population ka\n about 17.5% tha. UNFPA 2025 estimate: world population about 8.232 billion; India about 1.464 billion, China about 1.416 billion. World Population\n Prospects 2022 projection 2050 ke liye around 9.7 billion hai. [Q12-Q18]\n • UNFPA 2025 population ranking: India > China > United States > Indonesia > Pakistan > Nigeria > Brazil > Bangladesh > Russia > Mexico. Nigeria\n Africa ka most populous country aur Indonesia world ka most populous Muslim-majority country hai. Indonesia > Pakistan > Nigeria > Brazil listed\n order current estimates mein fit hota hai. [Q19-Q28]\n\n  3. Population Growth, Density, Sex Ratio, Religion & Literacy\n • Recent population-growth rate continents mein Africa highest aur Europe lowest/negative side par hai. Asia ke subregions mein Central/West Asia\n comparatively faster growth dikhate hain; Japan, South Korea aur Italy low fertility, ageing ya population-decline concerns ke liye notable hain. Low\n density automatically overpopulation imply nahi karti—carrying capacity and resources matter karte hain. [Q29-Q34]\n • Asia listed continents mein highest population density rakhta hai; Australia/Oceania lowest-density continent group mein hai. Country density\n extremes physical geography aur settlement pattern se linked hain; Nile Valley aur Java mein fertile land, water availability aur intensive agriculture\n high density ko support karte hain. [Q35-Q44]\n • Sex ratio countries mein highly variable hai; migration-heavy Gulf economies mein male-dominated ratios common hain, jabki some European\n countries female-majority ratios dikhate hain. Global religious-population order aur South Asian literacy rankings year aur dataset ke saath change ho\n sakte hain. [Q45-Q49]\n\n  4. World Urbanization Levels, Regions & Countries\n • World urbanization steadily increase hui: World Urbanization Prospects 2018 mein 2018 urban share 55.3% aur 2030 projection about 60.4% thi;\n World Bank 2024 around 58% urban share report karta hai. World urban population 2007 ke around rural population se exceed kar gayi. Urbanization\n curve ka rapid stage industrial/economic transition se linked hai. [Q50-Q53]\n • UN-Habitat socially aur environmentally sustainable towns/cities, adequate shelter, water, sanitation aur poverty reduction support karta hai; partners\n sirf governments tak limited nahi. City prosperity ko multiple economic, social, infrastructure aur environmental dimensions se assess kiya jata hai.\n [Q54-Q57]\n • Urbanization region/country-wise wide variation dikhata hai. Singapore/Hong Kong/Macao/Kuwait jaise places near-100% urban ho sakte hain; South\n America mein Uruguay very high urban share rakhta hai. South Asia mein 2024 World Bank figures roughly Bhutan 43%, Pakistan 39%, India 35%,\n Sri Lanka 20%; West Asia listed comparison mein Kuwait 100% urban tha. [Q58-Q66]\n\n ⚠ EXAM TRAP — World Urbanization Figures are Year-Specific\n ▸ World Urbanization Prospects 2018: 2018 urban share 55.3%, 2030 projection 60.4%, 2050 projection around 68.4%.\n ▸ World Bank/other newer reports later years ke different observed values dete hain; report-year aur projection-year ko saath padhein.\n Q Coverage: Q50-Q53\n\n\n  5. Megacities, Technopolis & Urbanization Theory\n • The World’s Cities in 2025 report ke updated ranking mein Jakarta largest city/urban agglomeration cited hai, followed by Dhaka, Tokyo aur New\n Delhi. India ke top-10 global-city list mein New Delhi aur Kolkata included hain. Technopolis technology/research-oriented city hota hai; Silicon Valley\n classic example aur Bengaluru India ka technopolis/Silicon Valley kaha jata hai. [Q67-Q70]\n • Industrialization historically urbanization ko drive karti hai. Developing countries mein urban growth economic development se faster ho to over-\n urbanization/slum pressure create ho sakta hai. Western urban gender patterns mechanised agriculture aur urban employment opportunities se\n influence hote hain; female labour-force participation countries mein vary karti hai. [Q71-Q74]\n\n  6. Migration, Fertility, Birth Rate, Mortality & Life Expectancy\n • World Migration Report 2024 mein international migrants about 281 million, world population ka 3.6% the. Migrant-population percentage regionally\n Oceania high hai; country shares mein UAE about 88%, Qatar 77% aur Kuwait 73% extremely high hain. Absolute migrant numbers Europe aur North\n America mein bhi very large hain. [Q75-Q77]\n • China ki fertility one-child policy period ke baad sharply decline hui; updated UN data around 1.0 TFR dikhata hai. Listed low-fertility countries mein\n China/Italy very low hain. 2025 crude birth-rate comparison mein Afghanistan high, Pakistan next, Bangladesh aur India lower side par the. [Q78-Q81]","• Life expectancy listed countries mein Japan among highest (~85 years in 2025). Japan negative natural-growth/ageing challenge face karta hai.\nSouth Asia maternal mortality and elderly shares country-wise vary karte hain; Sri Lanka elderly share relatively high hai. Around 80% world\npopulation historically adequate social-security protection se outside batayi gayi; South Asia poverty rankings report-year sensitive hain. [Q82-Q88]\n\n⚠ EXAM TRAP — Migration — Share vs Absolute Number\n▸ World Migration Report 2024: international migrants about 281 million, 3.6% of world population; Oceania region migrant-share percentage mein high hai.\n▸ Country migrant-share percentage mein UAE/Qatar/Kuwait top-side par hain, jabki absolute migrant population ka regional ranking alag ho sakta hai.\nQ Coverage: Q75-Q77\n\n\n7. Family Planning & Biodiversity\n• India ne 1952 mein world ka first official national family-planning programme launch kiya; objective birth rate ko reduce karke population ko\neconomy-compatible level par stabilize karna tha. [Q89-Q90]\n• Biodiversity estimates exact nahi hain: about 1.5-1.8 million species formally named hain, jabki estimated total species several million ho sakte hain;\ndifferent scientific estimates much higher totals bhi suggest karte hain. [Q91]\n                              Exam Fusion Prep · examfusionprep.com · World Population & Urbanization Full Mind Map"]}];
